const GW_DECKS = {"intro": {"name": "Module 1: Introduction", "n": 31}, "ivb": {"name": "Module 2: IV Bolus Administration", "n": 31}, "inf": {"name": "Module 3: Intravenous Infusions", "n": 20}, "cl": {"name": "Module 4: Clearance and Elimination", "n": 26}, "oral": {"name": "Module 5: Pharmacokinetics of Oral Absorption", "n": 24}, "rep": {"name": "Module 6: Repetitive IV Bolus and Intermittent IV Infusions", "n": 27}, "mo": {"name": "Module 6a: Multiple Oral Doses", "n": 21}, "ba": {"name": "Module 7a: Bioavailability and Bioequivalence", "n": 19}, "pd": {"name": "Module 7b: Pharmacokinetics and Pharmacodynamics", "n": 13}, "nonlin": {"name": "Module 8: Nonlinear Pharmacokinetics", "n": 22}};
const GUIDE_WALK = {"gobj-m1-1": {"title": "Pharmacokinetics and related disciplines", "segs": [{"t": "Module one, objective one. What pharmacokinetics is, and the disciplines around it. A drug given as a dosage form, such as a tablet, follows one path: it dissolves, enters the systemic circulation and moves to and from the tissues, where it produces its effect, and it is metabolized and excreted. Pharmaceutics covers the first stretch, from the dosage form up to the drug in solution. Pharmacokinetics, P K, covers everything after that point. She said, We don't consider the drug being into the body until it is in solution and able to pass through some of those membranes. So P K describes the rates at which the drug moves into, through and out of the body.", "s": "intro:3", "c": [[2, "dosage form → drug in solution → systemic circulation → tissues and back → effect"], [3, "pharmaceutics: dosage form → drug in solution"], [4, "pharmacokinetics (PK): drug in solution → circulation, tissues, metabolism, excretion → effect"], [6, "PK: the rates of drug moving into and out of the body"]]}, {"t": "In short, P K is the study of A D M E: absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion. Absorption is the passage of drug molecules from the administration site into the systemic circulation. Distribution is the reversible transfer of a drug to and from the site of measurement; it is reversible because drug moves back out of the tissues as well as into them. Metabolism is the conversion of one chemical species to another, also called biotransformation. Excretion is the removal of intact drug or metabolite from the body. Other faculty add a letter, an L for liberation or a T for toxicology, but in this course it is these four.", "s": "intro:4", "c": [[0, "PK = ADME"], [1, "absorption: administration site → systemic circulation"], [2, "distribution: reversible, to and from the site of measurement"], [3, "metabolism: one chemical species → another (biotransformation)"], [4, "excretion: removal of intact drug or metabolite"], [5, "this course: ADME, four letters"]]}, {"t": "The quickest way to separate P K from pharmacodynamics, P D, is what each one relates. P K follows concentration against time: after a dose, how the drug level changes from hour to hour. P D is the relationship between drug concentration at the site of action and the pharmacological response, so it follows concentration against response. The textbook gives the direction: higher doses produce higher drug concentrations, more drug at the receptor site and a more intense effect, up to a maximum effect. So the effect rises as the concentration rises, but not in proportion to it, because the effect cannot rise above that maximum. She said, Pharmacokinetics, we're looking at concentration and time. Pharmacodynamics, we're looking at concentration and effect.", "s": "intro:8", "c": [[1, "PK: concentration and time"], [2, "PD: concentration at the site of action → response"], [3, "higher dose → higher concentration → stronger effect, up to a maximum"], [4, "effect rises with concentration, but not in proportion: it stops at the maximum"]]}, {"t": "Several neighbouring disciplines use P K for a particular purpose, such as treating patients, comparing groups or judging safety. The first is biopharmaceutics: how the drug's physicochemical properties, the dosage form and the route of administration together affect the rate and extent of systemic absorption. These three matter because the drug must first dissolve and then pass through membranes, so its balance between lipid and water solubility, the product it is in and the place it is given change how much is absorbed and how fast. Rate means how fast the drug is absorbed, and extent means how much of the dose is absorbed.", "s": "intro:6", "c": [[1, "biopharmaceutics: properties + dosage form + route → rate and extent of absorption"], [2, "dissolve, then cross membranes: lipid and water solubility matter"], [3, "rate: how fast   extent: how much"]]}, {"t": "The other disciplines related to P K each have a one line definition. Clinical pharmacokinetics is applying pharmacokinetic methods to drug therapy. Population pharmacokinetics is the study of pharmacokinetic differences between population groups, such as older adults, children, pregnant women, immunocompromised patients and patients with a disease. Clinical toxicology is the study of adverse effects of drugs and toxic substances in the body. Toxicokinetics is applying pharmacokinetic principles to drug safety evaluation studies.", "s": "intro:8", "c": [[1, "clinical PK: PK methods applied to drug therapy"], [2, "population PK: differences between groups, e.g. geriatric, pediatric, pregnant, disease"], [3, "clinical toxicology: adverse effects"], [4, "toxicokinetics: PK in drug safety studies"]]}, {"t": "Her questions read out a definition almost word for word, and the options are bare discipline names, so you go from definition to name. In her poll the stem gave the biopharmaceutics definition, and the options were biopharmaceutics, toxicology, pharmacodynamics and pharmacology. The answer was biopharmaceutics, though a few students chose pharmacodynamics. P D is about response, not absorption, and pharmacology is not one of the disciplines this module defines. The key words decide it: absorption from dosage form and route means biopharmaceutics, response means P D, drug therapy means clinical P K, population groups means population P K, and safety studies means toxicokinetics. The E in A D M E is excretion; elimination, which also counts metabolism, is a separate term in objective three.", "s": "intro:6", "c": [[1, "stem: properties, dosage form, route → rate and extent of absorption"], [2, "biopharmaceutics ✓"], [3, "✗ PD: response, not absorption   ✗ pharmacology: not defined here"], [4, "absorption → biopharm   response → PD   drug therapy → clinical PK   groups → population PK   safety → toxicokinetics"], [5, "E in ADME = excretion, not elimination"]]}], "mins": 4}, "gobj-m1-2": {"title": "Types of pharmacokinetic models", "segs": [{"t": "Module one, objective two. The types of pharmacokinetic models. A pharmacokinetic model is a simplified description of the body that lets you predict drug levels and choose a dosing regimen. She said, We do this so that we can predict drug levels, that's what we do in pharmacokinetics. Models are also used to estimate accumulation, to link concentration to effect or toxicity, to compare formulations, and to explain how disease or drug interactions change P K. Comparing the rate or extent of availability between two formulations of a drug is called bioequivalence.", "s": "intro:13", "c": [[2, "model → predict drug levels, choose a dosing regimen"], [4, "also: accumulation, effect or toxicity, formulations, disease, interactions"], [5, "bioequivalence: rate or extent of availability, two formulations"]]}, {"t": "Pharmacokinetic models are of two broad types. Physiologic models, also called perfusion or blood flow models, are built from known anatomic and physiologic data, such as each tissue's blood flow and its drug concentration. Tissue samples are not available from human patients, so these models need more data than is usually on hand, and this course uses them mainly as a wrong answer choice. Compartment models treat the body as one or more boxes that the drug moves between at set rate constants. A compartment is not a real organ; it is a group of tissues with similar blood flow and drug affinity, and its concentration is an average for that group.", "s": "intro:14", "c": [[1, "physiologic (perfusion): anatomy, blood flow, tissue concentrations"], [2, "needs tissue data → rarely used in this course"], [3, "compartment: boxes joined by rate constants"], [4, "compartment: tissues with similar blood flow and affinity; average concentration"]]}, {"t": "How many compartments, or boxes, a compartment model needs depends on how the drug behaves in the body. The simplest is one compartment: the drug enters it, by injection or by absorption, and leaves it by the elimination rate constant, k. If the drug behaves differently in some tissues than in others, one compartment no longer fits the data, and a second compartment is added. Each added compartment brings more rate constants to estimate, and the textbook notes that as the number of parameters rises, the data needed rise with it. That is why this course keeps to one and two compartments, the models Module two compares.", "s": "intro:16", "c": [[1, "one compartment: drug in, out by k"], [2, "drug behaves differently in some tissues → add a compartment"], [3, "more compartments → more rate constants → more data needed"], [4, "this course: one or two compartments (Module 2)"]]}, {"t": "Three compartments can be joined in two ways. In a mammillary model there is a central compartment one, where the drug starts, and each other compartment is joined directly to it, for example two on one side and three on the other. Drug moves from one to two by k one two and back by k two one, and from one to three by k one three and back by k three one, so both outer boxes exchange with the centre at the same time. The subscripts read from source to destination: k one two is the transfer from one to two. A transfer rate constant is in reciprocal time, such as per hour. The mammillary model is the most common compartment model in pharmacokinetics.", "s": "intro:15", "c": [[1, "mammillary: every outer box joined to central 1"], [2, "k<sub>12</sub>, k<sub>21</sub>   k<sub>13</sub>, k<sub>31</sub>"], [3, "k<sub>12</sub>: 1 → 2   k<sub>21</sub>: 2 → 1"], [4, "k<sub>12</sub> in hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [5, "mammillary: the most common"]]}, {"t": "In a catenary model the compartments are joined in a chain, one to two to three. Drug reaches compartment three only by passing through two, by k one two and k two one, then k two three and k three two. The mammillary and the catenary model can both have three compartments, so what separates them is how the compartments are joined, not how many there are.", "s": "intro:15", "c": [[0, "catenary: 1 → 2 → 3"], [1, "k<sub>12</sub>, k<sub>21</sub>, then k<sub>23</sub>, k<sub>32</sub>"], [2, "mammillary vs catenary: how joined, not how many"]]}, {"t": "Her questions ask by how the compartments are joined, not by how many there are. Her poll asked which model has compartments joined to one another like the compartments of a train, with the options catenary, mammillary and physiologic. The answer is catenary. She said, You don't get to the third car without going through the second car. The trap is mammillary, which also has three compartments, but each is joined to the centre. So ask whether every outer box connects to the centre, which is mammillary, or whether they form a chain, which is catenary. Physiologic is not a compartment model, so it appears only as a distractor in these wiring questions.", "s": "intro:15", "c": [[1, "joined like the compartments of a train?"], [2, "catenary ✓"], [4, "✗ mammillary: also three compartments, all joined to 1"], [5, "all to the centre: mammillary   chain: catenary"], [6, "✗ physiologic: not a compartment model"]]}], "mins": 4}, "gobj-m1-3": {"title": "Fundamental pharmacokinetic terms", "segs": [{"t": "Module one, objective three. Fundamental pharmacokinetic terms, used in every later module. C p is the plasma concentration, the drug level measured in plasma. After an oral dose, C p rises from zero to a peak and then falls, because the drug has to get into the systemic circulation before it can be measured there. After an I V bolus, a single dose injected into a vein all at once, C p is highest at time zero and only falls. The aim of dosing is to keep C p above the level that works and below the level that harms; she said, Our goal is that we want to stay in between these two lines.", "s": "intro:9", "d": "Module one, objective three. Fundamental pharmacokinetic terms, used in every later module. C<sub>p</sub> is the plasma concentration, the drug level measured in plasma. After an oral dose, C<sub>p</sub> rises from zero to a peak and then falls, because the drug has to get into the systemic circulation before it can be measured there. After an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus, a single dose injected into a vein all at once, C<sub>p</sub> is highest at time zero and only falls. The aim of dosing is to keep C<sub>p</sub> above the level that works and below the level that harms; she said, Our goal is that we want to stay in between these two lines.", "c": [[2, "C<sub>p</sub>: plasma drug concentration, e.g. mg/L or mcg/mL"], [3, "oral: C<sub>p</sub> zero → peak → falls"], [4, "IV bolus: highest at time zero, then only falls"], [5, "aim: above the level that works, below the level that harms"]]}, {"t": "On her plot of drug concentration against time, the lower of the two dashed lines is the minimum effective concentration, M E C, the concentration that must be met or exceeded for the desired pharmacologic response. The upper line is the minimum toxic concentration, M T C, above which adverse effects start. So a level below the M E C gives no desired response, a level above the M T C gives adverse effects, and the range between them is the therapeutic window. The effect starts when the rising curve reaches the M E C and lasts until the falling curve drops back to it. A third term, the minimum inhibitory concentration, M I C, is used for antibiotics: the lowest concentration needed to kill or inhibit the bacteria.", "s": "intro:9", "c": [[0, "MEC: lower line; met or exceeded for the desired response"], [1, "MTC: upper line; adverse effects start above it"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> below MEC: no desired response   C<sub>p</sub> above MTC: adverse effects"], [2, "therapeutic window: between MEC and MTC"], [3, "onset: C<sub>p</sub> reaches MEC   duration: until C<sub>p</sub> falls back to MEC"], [4, "MIC: antibiotics, kill or inhibit bacteria"]]}, {"t": "Elimination is the irreversible loss of drug from the body by all routes, so metabolism and excretion are both forms of elimination. Disposition is everything that happens to a drug after it is absorbed into the systemic circulation: distribution and elimination. So the two terms differ by distribution: if distribution is included, the term is disposition. The first pass effect is the rapid metabolism of an oral drug, mainly by the liver, before it reaches the general circulation.", "s": "intro:5", "c": [[0, "elimination = metabolism + excretion"], [1, "disposition = distribution + elimination"], [2, "distribution included → disposition"], [3, "first-pass: oral drug metabolized, mainly by the liver, before the general circulation"]]}, {"t": "Bioavailability, F, is a measure of the systemic availability of a drug: the fraction of the dose that reaches the plasma, a number with no units. The more of an oral dose the liver metabolizes before the drug reaches the general circulation, which is the first pass effect, the less of the dose reaches the plasma, so F is lower. In her example, a thousand milligram tablet with only five hundred milligrams available for the body to use has an F of one half. F returns in objective five, where it comes from comparing areas under the curve.", "s": "intro:5", "c": [[0, "F: fraction of the dose reaching the plasma, no units"], [1, "more first-pass metabolism → lower F"], [2, "e.g. her tablet: {{frac:500 mg|1000 mg}} = 0.5"], [3, "F from AUC: objective 5"]]}, {"t": "Drug levels are measured in a fluid sample, most often from blood. Whole blood is venous blood with an anticoagulant such as heparin, with all its cells and proteins. Serum is the liquid left after blood clots and the clot is removed, so it has no cells, no fibrinogen and no other clotting factors. Plasma is the liquid after anticoagulated blood is centrifuged, so it has no cells but all the proteins, including albumin. Serum or plasma is used most, because they contain fewer components for the drug to interact with than whole blood does. A plasma level can stand for the tissues because drug in plasma is taken to be in equilibrium with them, so a change in plasma concentration reflects a change in tissue concentration.", "s": "intro:11", "c": [[1, "whole blood: anticoagulant, all cells and proteins"], [2, "serum: clotted, no cells, no fibrinogen"], [3, "plasma: anticoagulated, centrifuged, all proteins"], [4, "serum or plasma: fewer components to interact with"], [5, "plasma in equilibrium with tissues: C<sub>p</sub> change → tissue change"]]}, {"t": "Her questions word the stem as the property and ask for the name, and every option is a real term of the same kind. Her poll asked which concentration must be met or exceeded for the desired pharmacologic response, and offered steady state, minimum toxic, minimum effective and minimum inhibitory concentration. The answer is minimum effective concentration. Minimum toxic is the upper line where harm starts, minimum inhibitory is the antibiotic term, and steady state is the plateau reached when the rate of drug going in equals the rate going out.", "s": "intro:10", "c": [[1, "met or exceeded for the desired response?"], [2, "MEC ✓"], [3, "✗ MTC: harm   ✗ MIC: antibiotics   ✗ steady state: rate in = rate out"]]}, {"t": "Her second poll asked which sample is most commonly used for drug measurement: saliva, urine, whole blood, or serum or plasma. The answer is serum or plasma, although, as she said, all of these are options. In the notation, C p is the concentration in plasma, C s in serum, and a plain C is unspecified, so take it as whichever fluid applies. For elimination against disposition, the check is distribution: a stem that includes distribution describes disposition.", "s": "intro:11", "d": "Her second poll asked which sample is most commonly used for drug measurement: saliva, urine, whole blood, or serum or plasma. The answer is serum or plasma, although, as she said, all of these are options. In the notation, C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> is the concentration in plasma, C s in serum, and a plain C is unspecified, so take it as whichever fluid applies. For elimination against disposition, the check is distribution: a stem that includes distribution describes disposition.", "c": [[0, "most common sample?"], [1, "serum or plasma ✓ (all four possible)"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> plasma   C<sub>s</sub> serum   C unspecified"], [3, "includes distribution → disposition"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m1-4": {"title": "Orders of reaction and parameters from data", "segs": [{"t": "Module one, objective four. Orders of reaction, and parameters from a data set. The rate of a process is the amount or concentration lost per unit of time. The order says whether that rate depends on how much drug is present: in zero order it does not, and in first order it does. The rate constant, k, is a fixed number, not the rate itself, and the half-life is the time for the level to fall by half. An I V bolus, a single dose injected into a vein all at once, is taken as first order; a data table has to be tested.", "s": "intro:19", "d": "Module one, objective four. Orders of reaction, and parameters from a data set. The rate of a process is the amount or concentration lost per unit of time. The order says whether that rate depends on how much drug is present: in zero order it does not, and in first order it does. The rate constant, k, is a fixed number, not the rate itself, and the half-life is the time for the level to fall by half. An IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus, a single dose injected into a vein all at once, is taken as first order; a data table has to be tested.", "c": [[2, "rate = {{frac:dC|dt}}: concentration lost per time, e.g. mg/L per hr"], [3, "zero order: rate does not depend on C   first order: rate depends on C"], [4, "k: rate constant, fixed; not the rate"], [4, "t<sub>½</sub>: time to fall by half, e.g. hr"], [5, "IV bolus → first order   data table → decide"]]}, {"t": "In a zero order process the same amount or concentration is lost every hour, however much drug is left. She said, It doesn't matter if we start at a thousand milligrams or if we start at ten milligrams, the rate of elimination is going to be constant. Because a fixed amount is lost from a shrinking level, the fraction lost each hour grows as the level falls. On linear axes the fall is a straight line, and its slope is the rate. A lower starting level halves sooner, because less has to be lost at the same rate, so the half-life is not constant.", "s": "intro:20", "c": [[0, "zero order: same amount lost per hour"], [2, "fixed amount per hour → the fraction lost grows as C falls"], [2, "e.g. her table, 12 hr each: 206 → 134 mg/mL loses 72 (35%); 134 → 62 loses 72 (54%)"], [3, "linear axes: straight line; slope = rate"], [4, "t<sub>½</sub> shorter from a lower start"], [4, "e.g. 6 (mg/mL)/hr: 350 → 175 takes 29.2 hr; 175 → 87.5 takes 14.6 hr"]]}, {"t": "In a first order process the same fraction is lost every hour, so more drug is lost per hour when more drug is present. The rate is fastest at the top of the curve, where the concentration is highest, and slows as the concentration falls. In her practice table, from twelve to twenty four hours and again from twenty four to thirty six hours, the earlier level divided by the later one was two point eight three, yet the loss fell from eighty to about twenty eight milligrams per liter. On linear axes this is a curve, and on a semilog axis it is a straight line. Halving is always the same fraction, so it always takes the same time: the half-life is constant.", "s": "intro:21", "c": [[0, "first order: same fraction lost per hour"], [1, "rate highest at the top, falls with C"], [2, "e.g. 12 → 24 hr: 123.8 → 43.8 mg/L, lost 80.0, ratio 2.83"], [2, "24 → 36 hr: 43.8 → 15.5 mg/L, lost 28.3, ratio 2.83"], [3, "linear axes: curve   semilog axis: straight line"], [4, "t<sub>½</sub> constant: e.g. 350 → 175 and 175 → 87.5 mg/L both take 8 hr"]], "f": {"k": "orders_steps", "at": [[0, 1], [2, 2]]}}, {"t": "The rate and the rate constant are different quantities. For zero order, d C by d t equals minus k: the rate is k itself, with no C in it, so k is a rate, in concentration or amount per time. For first order, d C by d t equals minus k times C: k stays fixed while C falls, so the rate falls, and k is in reciprocal time, such as per hour. She said, If it's just K all by itself, it's independent of concentration. The minus sign shows only that the level is falling; k is never negative.", "s": "intro:21", "c": [[1, "zero: {{frac:dC|dt}} = −k; k in (mg/mL)/hr or mg/hr"], [2, "first: {{frac:dC|dt}} = −kC; k in hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [3, "k C in the rate: depends on C   k alone: does not"], [4, "k never negative; the minus sign = falling"]]}, {"t": "For first order, the half-life equals zero point six nine three over k. No concentration appears in it, so the half-life does not depend on concentration, and with k on the bottom a larger k gives a shorter half-life. She said, A constant divided by a constant is a constant. For zero order, the half-life equals C zero over two k, with the starting concentration, C zero, on the top, so a lower start gives a shorter half-life.", "s": "intro:22", "d": "For first order, the half-life equals zero point six nine three over k. No concentration appears in it, so the half-life does not depend on concentration, and with k on the bottom a larger k gives a shorter half-life. She said, A constant divided by a constant is a constant. For zero order, the half-life equals C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> over two k, with the starting concentration, C<sub>0</sub>, on the top, so a lower start gives a shorter half-life.", "c": [[0, "first: t<sub>½</sub> = {{frac:0.693|k}}"], [1, "no C → t<sub>½</sub> constant; larger k → shorter t<sub>½</sub>"], [3, "zero: t<sub>½</sub> = {{frac:C<sub>0</sub>|2k}}; lower C<sub>0</sub> → shorter t<sub>½</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Her practice set gives six concentrations over forty eight hours and asks for k, C zero, the half-life and the time to ninety percent decomposed. The order comes first: equal ratios over equal time spans mean a fixed fraction lost, first order, and equal differences per hour mean zero order. Here the earlier level divided by the later one is two point eight three from twelve to twenty four hours and again from twenty four to thirty six, so the table is first order. k comes from the natural log of C at two points, because the natural log of C falls along a straight line whose slope is minus k; the six and twenty four hour points give zero point zero eight six six per hour.", "s": "intro:21", "d": "Her practice set gives six concentrations over forty eight hours and asks for k, C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small>, the half-life and the time to ninety percent decomposed. The order comes first: equal ratios over equal time spans mean a fixed fraction lost, first order, and equal differences per hour mean zero order. Here the earlier level divided by the later one is two point eight three from twelve to twenty four hours and again from twenty four to thirty six, so the table is first order. k comes from the natural log of C at two points, because the natural log of C falls along a straight line whose slope is minus k; the six and twenty four hour points give zero point zero eight six six per hour.", "c": [[0, "t: 2, 6, 12, 24, 36, 48 hr"], [0, "C: 294.3, 208.1, 123.8, 43.8, 15.5, 5.5 mg/L"], [0, "find k, C<sub>0</sub>, t<sub>½</sub>, time to 90% decomposed"], [1, "equal ratios → first   equal differences per hr → zero"], [2, "{{frac:123.8|43.8}} = 2.83   {{frac:43.8|15.5}} = 2.83 → <b>first order</b>"], [3, "k = {{frac:ln C<sub>1</sub> − ln C<sub>2</sub>|t<sub>2</sub> − t<sub>1</sub>}}"], [3, "= {{frac:ln 208.1 − ln 43.8|24 hr − 6 hr}} = {{frac:5.3380 − 3.7796|18 hr}} = {{frac:1.5584|18 hr}} = 0.08658"], [3, "<b>k = 0.0866 hr<sup>−1</sup></b>"]], "f": {"k": "orders_steps", "at": [[1, 2]]}}, {"t": "C zero is the twelve hour level times e to the plus k t; the exponent is positive because C zero must exceed every later level, and the result is three hundred fifty milligrams per liter. The half-life, zero point six nine three over k, is eight hours. For ninety percent decomposed, ten percent remains, so C zero cancels and the time is the natural log of ten over k, twenty six point six hours. When the percent remaining is a power of one half, she counts half-lives instead: with a half-life of eight hours, losing eighty seven point five percent of seven hundred fifty milligrams leaves one eighth, which takes three half-lives, twenty four hours, whatever the starting amount.", "s": "intro:22", "d": "C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> is the twelve hour level times e to the plus k t; the exponent is positive because C<sub>0</sub> must exceed every later level, and the result is three hundred fifty milligrams per liter. The half-life, zero point six nine three over k, is eight hours. For ninety percent decomposed, ten percent remains, so C<sub>0</sub> cancels and the time is the natural log of ten over k, twenty six point six hours. When the percent remaining is a power of one half, she counts half-lives instead: with a half-life of eight hours, losing eighty seven point five percent of seven hundred fifty milligrams leaves one eighth, which takes three half-lives, twenty four hours, whatever the starting amount.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>0</sub> = C e<sup>+kt</sup>; kt = 0.0866 hr<sup>−1</sup> × 12 hr = 1.0392"], [0, "C<sub>0</sub> = 123.8 mg/L × e<sup>1.0392</sup> = 123.8 × 2.827 = 349.98"], [0, "<b>C<sub>0</sub> = 350 mg/L</b>   ✓ above 294.3"], [1, "t<sub>½</sub> = {{frac:0.693|0.0866 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = 8.002 → <b>8 hr</b>"], [2, "90% decomposed → 10% remains: C = 0.1 C<sub>0</sub>"], [2, "t = {{frac:ln C<sub>0</sub> − ln C|k}} = {{frac:ln 10|k}} = {{frac:2.3026|0.0866 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = 26.589 → <b>26.6 hr</b>"], [3, "87.5% lost = 3 t<sub>½</sub> = 3 × 8 hr = <b>24 hr</b>; the 750 mg is not used"]]}, {"t": "Her practice set also has a zero order table: from two to forty eight hours the level falls from three hundred thirty eight to sixty two milligrams per milliliter. From two to six hours the level falls by twenty four milligrams per milliliter, and from six to twelve hours by thirty six, both six milligrams per milliliter per hour, so the table is zero order. k is the fall in concentration over the time it took, six milligrams per milliliter per hour, and working back two hours adds twelve milligrams per milliliter to three hundred thirty eight, which gives C zero, three hundred fifty milligrams per milliliter. The half-life, C zero over two k, is twenty nine point two hours, and ninety percent decomposed takes fifty two point five hours.", "s": "intro:20", "d": "Her practice set also has a zero order table: from two to forty eight hours the level falls from three hundred thirty eight to sixty two milligrams per milliliter. From two to six hours the level falls by twenty four milligrams per milliliter, and from six to twelve hours by thirty six, both six milligrams per milliliter per hour, so the table is zero order. k is the fall in concentration over the time it took, six milligrams per milliliter per hour, and working back two hours adds twelve milligrams per milliliter to three hundred thirty eight, which gives C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small>, three hundred fifty milligrams per milliliter. The half-life, C<sub>0</sub> over two k, is twenty nine point two hours, and ninety percent decomposed takes fifty two point five hours.", "c": [[0, "t: 2, 6, 12, 24, 36, 48 hr"], [0, "C: 338, 314, 278, 206, 134, 62 mg/mL"], [1, "338 − 314 = 24 mg/mL in 4 hr   314 − 278 = 36 mg/mL in 6 hr"], [1, "both 6 (mg/mL)/hr → <b>zero order</b>"], [2, "k = {{frac:C<sub>1</sub> − C<sub>2</sub>|t<sub>2</sub> − t<sub>1</sub>}} = {{frac:338 − 206 mg/mL|24 hr − 2 hr}} = {{frac:132 mg/mL|22 hr}} → <b>k = 6 (mg/mL)/hr</b>"], [2, "C<sub>0</sub> = C + kt = 338 + 6 × 2 → <b>C<sub>0</sub> = 350 mg/mL</b>"], [3, "t<sub>½</sub> = {{frac:350 mg/mL|2 × 6 (mg/mL)/hr}} = {{frac:350|12}} = 29.17 → <b>29.2 hr</b>"], [3, "10% of 350 = 35 mg/mL; t = {{frac:350 − 35 mg/mL|6 (mg/mL)/hr}} = {{frac:315|6}} → <b>52.5 hr</b>"]], "f": {"k": "orders_steps", "at": [[0, 3], [1, 4], [3, 5]]}}, {"t": "Her Quiz one items match each order to its rate and its half-life. A rate that depends on concentration means first order: the half-life is independent of concentration, and d C by d t equals minus k C. A rate independent of concentration means zero order: the half-life depends on concentration, and d C by d t equals minus k. Her zero order item is keyed as a rate independent of the amount in the body; for first order she said, The rate is highest at the top of the curve when the concentration is highest. In her poll on first order, thirty eight percent chose the key, a constant half-life, and forty four percent chose units of k of concentration per time, which are zero order units.", "s": "intro:22", "c": [[1, "rate depends on C → first order: t<sub>½</sub> independent ✓   {{frac:dC|dt}} = −kC ✓"], [2, "rate independent of C → zero order: t<sub>½</sub> depends on C<sub>0</sub>   {{frac:dC|dt}} = −k"], [3, "first: rate highest at the top   zero: rate independent of the amount"], [4, "first order: constant t<sub>½</sub> ✓"], [4, "✗ constant rate   ✗ {{frac:dC|dt}} = −k   ✗ k in concentration per time: all zero order"]]}, {"t": "An axis marked one, ten, one hundred, one thousand is a log scale even when it is not labelled, so a straight line on it means first order. She said, I expect you to give me half-life in units of time. Working back to C zero, the k t term carries a plus sign, because C zero is higher than every later level, and k is kept to three or four decimal places. Both orders return in Module eight: some drugs are eliminated by first order at drug levels well below a concentration called K M, and near zero order well above it.", "s": "intro:24", "d": "An axis marked one, ten, one hundred, one thousand is a log scale even when it is not labelled, so a straight line on it means first order. She said, I expect you to give me half-life in units of time. Working back to C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small>, the k t term carries a plus sign, because C<sub>0</sub> is higher than every later level, and k is kept to three or four decimal places. Both orders return in Module eight: some drugs are eliminated by first order at drug levels well below a concentration called K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small>, and near zero order well above it.", "c": [[0, "1, 10, 100, 1000 → log axis; straight line → first order"], [1, "t<sub>½</sub> in hr or days, never hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [2, "C<sub>0</sub>: plus sign   k: 3 to 4 decimals"], [3, "Module 8: C<sub>p</sub> ≪ K<sub>M</sub> → first order   C<sub>p</sub> ≫ K<sub>M</sub> → near zero order"]]}], "mins": 7}, "gobj-m1-5": {"title": "Area under the curve by the trapezoidal rule", "segs": [{"t": "Module one, objective five. The area under the curve, by the trapezoidal rule. A U C is the area under the plot of plasma concentration against time. It tells you the extent of drug that is available for the body to use: how much drug, not how fast. Its units are a concentration times a time, such as microgram hours per milliliter. There is rarely an equation for the curve, only a table of measured points, so the area is estimated from those points.", "s": "intro:26", "d": "Module one, objective five. The area under the curve, by the trapezoidal rule. AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> is the area under the plot of plasma concentration against time. It tells you the extent of drug that is available for the body to use: how much drug, not how fast. Its units are a concentration times a time, such as microgram hours per milliliter. There is rarely an equation for the curve, only a table of measured points, so the area is estimated from those points.", "c": [[2, "AUC: area under C<sub>p</sub> against time"], [3, "AUC: extent of drug available (how much, not how fast)"], [4, "units: concentration × time, e.g. mcg·hr/mL"], [5, "table of points → estimate the area"]], "f": {"k": "auc_steps", "at": [[5, 1]]}}, {"t": "The trapezoidal rule joins each pair of neighbouring points with a straight line, which splits the area into strips. Each strip is a trapezoid, and its area is the average of its two concentrations times the time between them. She said, take this curve and break it up into little segments and then determine the area of each of those segments and add them up. The rule does not depend on the shape of the curve, so it works for any concentration time table, from any route. It does assume a straight line between points, so where the points are widely spaced on a curved stretch the estimate is less accurate, and the more points there are, the more accurate the area becomes.", "s": "intro:28", "c": [[1, "strip area = average height × width"], [3, "any curve shape, any route"], [4, "straight line between points: wide spacing → more error; more points → more accurate"]]}, {"t": "The A U C of one segment equals C n minus one plus C n, over two, times t n minus t n minus one, where C n minus one and C n are the concentrations at the two ends of the segment. Adding the two concentrations and dividing by two averages those two heights, so the two is a plain number, not hours. The time difference is the width, read from the time column, so a wider strip or higher concentrations give a larger area. A concentration in micrograms per milliliter times a width in hours gives microgram hours per milliliter. A U C total is the sum of the A U C of every segment.", "s": "intro:28", "d": "The AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> of one segment equals C n minus one plus C n, over two, times t n minus t n minus one, where C n minus one and C n are the concentrations at the two ends of the segment. Adding the two concentrations and dividing by two averages those two heights, so the two is a plain number, not hours. The time difference is the width, read from the time column, so a wider strip or higher concentrations give a larger area. A concentration in micrograms per milliliter times a width in hours gives microgram hours per milliliter. AUC total is the sum of the AUC of every segment.", "c": [[0, "AUC = {{frac:C<sub>n−1</sub> + C<sub>n</sub>|2}} × (t<sub>n</sub> − t<sub>n−1</sub>)"], [1, "the 2 averages two heights; it is not hours"], [2, "wider strip or higher C → larger area"], [3, "mcg/mL × hr = mcg·hr/mL"], [4, "AUC<sub>total</sub> = sum of the segments"]]}, {"t": "Her slide example gives plasma levels at six times, from half an hour to five hours, and asks for the A U C from hours two to four. The trapezoidal rule answers it, because only measured points are given. The first step is choosing the rows: only those inside two to four hours count, eighteen point four at two hours, eleven point one at three and six point seven seven at four, in micrograms per milliliter. The half hour and one hour rows are before the interval, and the five hour row is after it. That makes two segments, each one hour wide.", "s": "intro:29", "d": "Her slide example gives plasma levels at six times, from half an hour to five hours, and asks for the AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> from hours two to four. The trapezoidal rule answers it, because only measured points are given. The first step is choosing the rows: only those inside two to four hours count, eighteen point four at two hours, eleven point one at three and six point seven seven at four, in micrograms per milliliter. The half hour and one hour rows are before the interval, and the five hour row is after it. That makes two segments, each one hour wide.", "c": [[0, "t: 0.5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 hr"], [0, "C: 38.9, 30.3, 18.4, 11.1, 6.77, 4.10 mcg/mL"], [0, "AUC from 2 to 4 hr?"], [2, "2 hr: 18.4   3 hr: 11.1   4 hr: 6.77 mcg/mL"], [3, "✗ 0.5, 1 and 5 hr rows"], [4, "two segments, 1 hr each"]], "f": {"k": "auc_steps", "at": [[0, 1], [2, 2]]}}, {"t": "In her slide example, the strip from two to three hours has an area of eighteen point four plus eleven point one micrograms per milliliter, over two, times one hour: fourteen point seven five microgram hours per milliliter. The strip from three to four hours, from eleven point one to six point seven seven micrograms per milliliter, has an area of eight point nine three five microgram hours per milliliter, smaller because the concentrations are lower over the same width. The two areas add to twenty three point seven microgram hours per milliliter. This A U C is the extent of drug available between two and four hours only; the parts of the curve before two hours and after four hours would add more area.", "s": "intro:29", "d": "In her slide example, the strip from two to three hours has an area of eighteen point four plus eleven point one micrograms per milliliter, over two, times one hour: fourteen point seven five microgram hours per milliliter. The strip from three to four hours, from eleven point one to six point seven seven micrograms per milliliter, has an area of eight point nine three five microgram hours per milliliter, smaller because the concentrations are lower over the same width. The two areas add to twenty three point seven microgram hours per milliliter. This AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> is the extent of drug available between two and four hours only; the parts of the curve before two hours and after four hours would add more area.", "c": [[0, "2 → 3 hr: {{frac:18.4 + 11.1|2}} × 1 hr = 14.75 mcg·hr/mL"], [1, "3 → 4 hr: {{frac:11.1 + 6.77|2}} × 1 hr = 8.935 mcg·hr/mL"], [2, "14.75 + 8.935 = 23.685"], [2, "<b>AUC = 23.7 mcg·hr/mL</b>"], [3, "AUC from 2 to 4 hr only"]], "f": {"k": "auc_steps", "at": [[0, 3], [1, 4], [2, 5]]}}, {"t": "Her questions give a table and an interval named in hours. Use only the trapezoids between rows inside that interval, not the whole table and not an extrapolated tail. Check each width from the time column: where sampling times are uneven, as from half an hour to one hour, a segment is half an hour wide, not one hour. When a student asked whether the two on the bottom of the trapezoid formula is hours, she said, No, two is not hours. Give the units as concentration times time, microgram hours per milliliter. A concept question asks what A U C tells you: the extent of drug available, how much, while how fast shows in the peak concentration, C max, and the time of the peak, t max.", "s": "intro:29", "d": "Her questions give a table and an interval named in hours. Use only the trapezoids between rows inside that interval, not the whole table and not an extrapolated tail. Check each width from the time column: where sampling times are uneven, as from half an hour to one hour, a segment is half an hour wide, not one hour. When a student asked whether the two on the bottom of the trapezoid formula is hours, she said, No, two is not hours. Give the units as concentration times time, microgram hours per milliliter. A concept question asks what AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> tells you: the extent of drug available, how much, while how fast shows in the peak concentration, C<sub>max</sub>, and the time of the peak, t<sub>max</sub>.", "c": [[1, "✗ whole table   ✗ extrapolated tail"], [2, "0.5 → 1 hr: width 0.5 hr"], [3, "the 2 is not hours"], [4, "units: mcg·hr/mL"], [5, "AUC: extent   C<sub>max</sub>, t<sub>max</sub>: rate"]]}, {"t": "A U C returns later in the course. Bioavailability, F, the fraction of an oral dose that enters the plasma, equals A U C oral over A U C I V. The oral area is on the top, so the smaller it is compared with the I V area, the smaller the fraction that entered the plasma. When the doses differ, the equation sheet corrects for them: F equals A U C p o over A U C I V, times D I V over D p o, where p o means by mouth. Clearance, the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time, also links the dose to the A U C, in Module two.", "s": "intro:27", "d": "AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> returns later in the course. Bioavailability, F, the fraction of an oral dose that enters the plasma, equals AUC oral over AUC IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small>. The oral area is on the top, so the smaller it is compared with the IV area, the smaller the fraction that entered the plasma. When the doses differ, the equation sheet corrects for them: F equals AUC p o over AUC IV, times D IV over D p o, where p o means by mouth. Clearance, the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time, also links the dose to the AUC, in Module two.", "c": [[1, "F = {{frac:AUC<sub>oral</sub>|AUC<sub>IV</sub>}}"], [2, "smaller AUC<sub>oral</sub> relative to AUC<sub>IV</sub> → smaller F"], [3, "F = {{frac:AUC<sub>po</sub>|AUC<sub>IV</sub>}} × {{frac:D<sub>IV</sub>|D<sub>po</sub>}}"], [4, "clearance: links dose to AUC (Module 2)"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m2-1": {"title": "The one-compartment model after an IV bolus", "segs": [{"t": "Module two, objective one. The one compartment model after an I V bolus. An I V bolus is a whole dose injected into a vein at once, so all of the drug is in the body at time zero, with no absorption step. The one compartment open model treats the body as one uniform space that drug can enter and leave, which is what open means. The drug is assumed to spread through that space instantly and evenly, so the plasma concentration, C p, rises and falls with the drug in the whole body. She said, Assumptions, simplifications, this does not happen. In practice the spread takes a few minutes, the time the blood takes to circulate.", "s": "ivb:3", "d": "Module two, objective one. The one compartment model after an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus. An IV bolus is a whole dose injected into a vein at once, so all of the drug is in the body at time zero, with no absorption step. The one compartment open model treats the body as one uniform space that drug can enter and leave, which is what open means. The drug is assumed to spread through that space instantly and evenly, so the plasma concentration, C<sub>p</sub>, rises and falls with the drug in the whole body. She said, Assumptions, simplifications, this does not happen. In practice the spread takes a few minutes, the time the blood takes to circulate.", "c": [[2, "IV bolus: whole dose at t = 0, instantaneous input, no absorption rate constant"], [3, "one compartment, open: one uniform space, drug enters and leaves"], [4, "C<sub>p</sub> (mg/L): amount per volume of plasma; follows the drug in the whole body"], [6, "instant spread: a model assumption; in the body, a few minutes"]]}, {"t": "Elimination starts the moment the drug is in, and it is first order. First order means a fixed fraction of the drug present leaves per unit of time, so the amount removed per hour is largest at time zero, when the most drug is present, and becomes smaller as the drug falls. The fraction removed per hour stays the same throughout; that fraction is the elimination rate constant, k, in per hour. So the concentration starts at its highest value and falls quickly at first, then more slowly. On a log concentration axis the same values fall on a straight line, because equal times remove equal fractions. She said, If I tell you that it is an I V bolus dose that is administered, your expectation is first order.", "s": "ivb:4", "d": "Elimination starts the moment the drug is in, and it is first order. First order means a fixed fraction of the drug present leaves per unit of time, so the amount removed per hour is largest at time zero, when the most drug is present, and becomes smaller as the drug falls. The fraction removed per hour stays the same throughout; that fraction is the elimination rate constant, k, in per hour. So the concentration starts at its highest value and falls quickly at first, then more slowly. On a log concentration axis the same values fall on a straight line, because equal times remove equal fractions. She said, If I tell you that it is an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus dose that is administered, your expectation is first order.", "c": [[1, "first order: fraction removed per hr constant; amount removed per hr falls as the amount falls"], [1, "e.g. k = 0.1 hr<sup>−1</sup>: 1000 mg → 100 mg/hr; 500 mg → 50 mg/hr"], [2, "k: the fraction removed per unit time, hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [3, "C<sub>p</sub>: highest at t = 0, falls fast, then more slowly"], [4, "log axis: equal times, equal fractions → straight line"]], "f": {"k": "bolus1_steps", "at": [[4, 1]]}}, {"t": "Two quantities set the plasma level after a bolus: the volume the drug is spread in, and how fast it is eliminated. The first is the volume of distribution, V D, which links the amount of drug in the body, D B, to the plasma concentration at the same moment. At time zero after a bolus, D B equals the dose, D zero. The same amount in a larger V D gives a lower concentration. The second is k, the overall elimination rate constant: the sum of k m for metabolism and k e for excretion; a k with no subscript always means this overall constant. If the patient cannot excrete the drug as before, k e falls, so k falls and the level falls more slowly.", "s": "ivb:3", "d": "Two quantities set the plasma level after a bolus: the volume the drug is spread in, and how fast it is eliminated. The first is the volume of distribution, V<sub>D</sub>, which links the amount of drug in the body, D B, to the plasma concentration at the same moment. At time zero after a bolus, D B equals the dose, D<sub>0</sub>. The same amount in a larger V<sub>D</sub> gives a lower concentration. The second is k, the overall elimination rate constant: the sum of k m for metabolism and k e for excretion; a k with no subscript always means this overall constant. If the patient cannot excrete the drug as before, k e falls, so k falls and the level falls more slowly.", "c": [[1, "C<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>B</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}} &nbsp; mg ÷ L = mg/L"], [2, "t = 0: D<sub>B</sub> = D<sub>0</sub>"], [3, "same D<sub>B</sub>, larger V<sub>D</sub> → lower C<sub>p</sub>"], [4, "k = k<sub>m</sub> + k<sub>e</sub> (hr<sup>−1</sup>)"], [5, "k<sub>e</sub> ↓ → k ↓ → slower fall"]]}, {"t": "The model says the rate of loss is k times what is present: d C p by d t equals minus k C p. What is present is on the right side, so more drug gives a faster loss, and the minus sign says the level is falling. Integrated, this gives C p equals C p zero times e to the minus k t. At t equal to zero the exponential is one, so the curve starts at C p zero, the concentration at time zero, and a larger k gives a faster fall. In natural logs, l n C p equals minus k t plus l n C p zero, a straight line with slope minus k; she prefers the natural log forms, and the base ten form agrees.", "s": "ivb:4", "d": "The model says the rate of loss is k times what is present: d C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> by d t equals minus k C<sub>p</sub>. What is present is on the right side, so more drug gives a faster loss, and the minus sign says the level is falling. Integrated, this gives C<sub>p</sub> equals C<sub>p</sub> zero times e to the minus k t. At t equal to zero the exponential is one, so the curve starts at C<sub>p</sub> zero, the concentration at time zero, and a larger k gives a faster fall. In natural logs, l n C<sub>p</sub> equals minus k t plus l n C<sub>p</sub> zero, a straight line with slope minus k; she prefers the natural log forms, and the base ten form agrees.", "c": [[0, "{{frac:dC<sub>p</sub>|dt}} = −kC<sub>p</sub> &nbsp; {{frac:dD<sub>B</sub>|dt}} = −kD<sub>B</sub>"], [1, "more drug present → faster loss; minus sign: falling"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup>e<sup>−kt</sup>"], [3, "t = 0: e<sup>0</sup> = 1 → C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup>; larger k → faster fall"], [4, "ln C<sub>p</sub> = −kt + ln C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup>: slope −k"], [4, "log C<sub>p</sub> = −{{frac:kt|2.3}} + log C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup>"]], "f": {"k": "bolus1_steps", "at": [[4, 2]]}}, {"t": "About half of her stems state the model as a condition, such as one compartment, first order elimination. In her data table problems she leaves it out, because working out the order from the data is the task. On a log concentration axis, a single straight line after an I V bolus means one compartment; a steep early segment means two, which is objective four. Bolus means all at once; a continuous input is an infusion. The k m here is a rate constant for metabolism, not the K M of module eight, which is a concentration. Before calculating, sketch a falling curve, so a later concentration can be checked to come out lower. Objective three uses this curve to find k, C zero, V D and clearance.", "s": "ivb:10", "d": "About half of her stems state the model as a condition, such as one compartment, first order elimination. In her data table problems she leaves it out, because working out the order from the data is the task. On a log concentration axis, a single straight line after an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus means one compartment; a steep early segment means two, which is objective four. Bolus means all at once; a continuous input is an infusion. The k m here is a rate constant for metabolism, not the K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> of module eight, which is a concentration. Before calculating, sketch a falling curve, so a later concentration can be checked to come out lower. Objective three uses this curve to find k, C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small>, V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> and clearance.", "c": [[0, "stated in about half of her stems: one-compartment, first-order elimination; one-compartment open model"], [2, "log axis: one straight line → one compartment; steep start → two"], [3, "bolus: all at once; infusion: continuous"], [4, "k<sub>m</sub>: metabolism, hr<sup>−1</sup>; K<sub>M</sub> (module 8): a concentration"], [5, "later C<sub>p</sub> &lt; earlier C<sub>p</sub> ✓"]]}], "mins": 4}, "gobj-m2-2": {"title": "Volume of distribution and clearance", "segs": [{"t": "Module two, objective two. Volume of distribution and clearance. The apparent volume of distribution, V D, is the volume the amount of drug in the body would have to dissolve in to give the concentration measured in plasma. It is not a real body space, which is why it is called apparent. She said, It's not an actual volume, it depends on the drug. V D is a proportionality constant: the amount in the body equals V D times the plasma concentration at the same time. Clearance, Cl T, is the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit of time, in liters per hour. It measures elimination without saying how the drug is removed, and it is also called drug, systemic or total body clearance.", "s": "ivb:5", "d": "Module two, objective two. Volume of distribution and clearance. The apparent volume of distribution, V<sub>D</sub>, is the volume the amount of drug in the body would have to dissolve in to give the concentration measured in plasma. It is not a real body space, which is why it is called apparent. She said, It's not an actual volume, it depends on the drug. V<sub>D</sub> is a proportionality constant: the amount in the body equals V<sub>D</sub> times the plasma concentration at the same time. Clearance, Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small>, is the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit of time, in liters per hour. It measures elimination without saying how the drug is removed, and it is also called drug, systemic or total body clearance.", "c": [[2, "V<sub>D</sub> (L): the volume the amount in the body would dissolve in to give the measured C<sub>p</sub>"], [3, "apparent: not a real body space"], [5, "amount in body = V<sub>D</sub> × C<sub>p</sub>, at the same time"], [6, "Cl<sub>T</sub> (L/hr): volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time"], [7, "drug, systemic or total body clearance; mechanism not named"]]}, {"t": "Why does V D differ between drugs? Only plasma is sampled. If most of a dose leaves the blood for the tissues, little of it stays in plasma, so the measured concentration is low and the volume needed to explain it is large. A drug held in the blood, for example by binding to plasma proteins, keeps a high plasma concentration, so its V D is small; only unbound drug moves easily into the tissues. V D does not change with the dose. It changes only when the patient changes in a serious way, such as pregnancy or volume overload.", "s": "ivb:6", "d": "Why does V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> differ between drugs? Only plasma is sampled. If most of a dose leaves the blood for the tissues, little of it stays in plasma, so the measured concentration is low and the volume needed to explain it is large. A drug held in the blood, for example by binding to plasma proteins, keeps a high plasma concentration, so its V<sub>D</sub> is small; only unbound drug moves easily into the tissues. V<sub>D</sub> does not change with the dose. It changes only when the patient changes in a serious way, such as pregnancy or volume overload.", "c": [[1, "only plasma is sampled"], [2, "drug in tissues → low C<sub>p</sub> → large V<sub>D</sub>"], [3, "bound to plasma proteins → high C<sub>p</sub> → small V<sub>D</sub>"], [4, "dose ↑: V<sub>D</sub> unchanged"], [5, "V<sub>D</sub> changes: pregnancy, volume overload"]]}, {"t": "The relation is V D equals D B, the amount in the body, over C p. Turned around, C zero, the concentration at time zero, equals the dose, D zero, over V D: V D is on the bottom, so at the same dose a larger V D gives a lower starting concentration. Her lecture example puts one hundred milligrams into volumes of one, ten and one hundred liters, which give one hundred, ten and one milligram per liter. The amount is the same, but these would be three different drugs. The amount in the body is known only at time zero, when it is the dose, so V D is found as D zero over C zero, never as the dose over a later concentration.", "s": "ivb:5", "d": "The relation is V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> equals D B, the amount in the body, over C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small>. Turned around, C<sub>0</sub>, the concentration at time zero, equals the dose, D<sub>0</sub>, over V<sub>D</sub>: V<sub>D</sub> is on the bottom, so at the same dose a larger V<sub>D</sub> gives a lower starting concentration. Her lecture example puts one hundred milligrams into volumes of one, ten and one hundred liters, which give one hundred, ten and one milligram per liter. The amount is the same, but these would be three different drugs. The amount in the body is known only at time zero, when it is the dose, so V<sub>D</sub> is found as D<sub>0</sub> over C<sub>0</sub>, never as the dose over a later concentration.", "c": [[0, "V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>B</sub>|C<sub>p</sub>}}"], [1, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}}: V<sub>D</sub> ↑ → C<sub>0</sub> ↓"], [2, "{{frac:100 mg|1 L}} = <b>100 mg/L</b>; {{frac:100 mg|10 L}} = <b>10 mg/L</b>; {{frac:100 mg|100 L}} = <b>1 mg/L</b>"], [3, "same dose: V<sub>D</sub> × 10 → C<sub>0</sub> ÷ 10"], [4, "V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|C<sub>0</sub>}}, both at t = 0"]]}, {"t": "What sets clearance is the ability of the eliminating organs to remove the drug, and what sets V D is how the drug distributes; the elimination rate constant k follows from these two. Clearance is a fixed volume of plasma cleared per hour. A larger V D leaves the liters cleared each hour unchanged, but those liters then hold a smaller fraction of the drug in the body, so a smaller fraction is removed each hour. Only drug that is not bound to plasma proteins can be cleared; bound drug is part of the measured concentration but is not removed. The amount removed per hour, an amount per time, is clearance times the concentration, so it falls as the level falls, while clearance stays the same.", "s": "ivb:9", "d": "What sets clearance is the ability of the eliminating organs to remove the drug, and what sets V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> is how the drug distributes; the elimination rate constant k follows from these two. Clearance is a fixed volume of plasma cleared per hour. A larger V<sub>D</sub> leaves the liters cleared each hour unchanged, but those liters then hold a smaller fraction of the drug in the body, so a smaller fraction is removed each hour. Only drug that is not bound to plasma proteins can be cleared; bound drug is part of the measured concentration but is not removed. The amount removed per hour, an amount per time, is clearance times the concentration, so it falls as the level falls, while clearance stays the same.", "c": [[0, "Cl: set by the eliminating organs; V<sub>D</sub>: set by distribution"], [0, "Cl and V<sub>D</sub> set by the body → k follows"], [2, "V<sub>D</sub> ↑, same Cl: same L/hr cleared, smaller fraction of the drug"], [3, "only unbound drug is cleared"], [4, "amount removed per hr = Cl × C<sub>p</sub>: falls with C<sub>p</sub>; Cl constant"], [4, "e.g. Cl 4 L/hr: C<sub>p</sub> 10 mg/L → 40 mg/hr; 5 mg/L → 20 mg/hr"]]}, {"t": "The relation is Cl T equals k times V D. She said, I want you to take this one with you to your grave along with the half-life equation. Turned around, k equals Cl T over V D: the volume cleared per hour divided by the whole volume is the fraction removed per hour. Clearance is on the top, so at the same V D a higher clearance gives a larger k and a shorter half-life. V D is on the bottom, so at the same clearance a larger V D gives a smaller k and a longer half-life. Per hour times liters leaves liters per hour. She said, a constant times a constant is a constant. So clearance stays the same when the dose or the concentration changes.", "s": "ivb:8", "d": "The relation is Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small> equals k times V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>. She said, I want you to take this one with you to your grave along with the half-life equation. Turned around, k equals Cl<sub>T</sub> over V<sub>D</sub>: the volume cleared per hour divided by the whole volume is the fraction removed per hour. Clearance is on the top, so at the same V<sub>D</sub> a higher clearance gives a larger k and a shorter half-life. V<sub>D</sub> is on the bottom, so at the same clearance a larger V<sub>D</sub> gives a smaller k and a longer half-life. Per hour times liters leaves liters per hour. She said, a constant times a constant is a constant. So clearance stays the same when the dose or the concentration changes.", "c": [[0, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = k × V<sub>D</sub> &nbsp; (not on the sheet)"], [2, "k = {{frac:Cl<sub>T</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}}: fraction removed per hr"], [3, "e.g. V<sub>D</sub> 20 L: Cl 4 → k 0.2 hr<sup>−1</sup>, t½ 3.47 hr; Cl 8 → k 0.4 hr<sup>−1</sup>, t½ 1.73 hr"], [4, "e.g. Cl 4 L/hr: V<sub>D</sub> 20 → 40 L: k 0.2 → 0.1 hr<sup>−1</sup>, t½ 3.47 → 6.93 hr"], [5, "hr<sup>−1</sup> × L = L/hr"], [7, "dose or C<sub>p</sub> ↑: Cl unchanged"]]}, {"t": "A second equation for clearance is Cl T equals D zero, the dose, over A U C, the area under the plasma concentration time curve from zero to infinity. The dose is on the top and A U C on the bottom, and clearance does not change with the dose, so doubling the dose doubles the A U C. That is her second reason to value clearance, after its constancy: it links the dose directly to the area under the curve, which measures the extent of drug available to the body. Milligrams over milligram hours per liter leaves liters per hour. On the equation sheet the form has F, the fraction absorbed, on the top, and F is one for an I V dose.", "s": "ivb:8", "d": "A second equation for clearance is Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small> equals D<sub>0</sub>, the dose, over AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small>, the area under the plasma concentration time curve from zero to infinity. The dose is on the top and AUC on the bottom, and clearance does not change with the dose, so doubling the dose doubles the AUC. That is her second reason to value clearance, after its constancy: it links the dose directly to the area under the curve, which measures the extent of drug available to the body. Milligrams over milligram hours per liter leaves liters per hour. On the equation sheet the form has F, the fraction absorbed, on the top, and F is one for an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> dose.", "c": [[0, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|AUC<sub>0</sub><sup>∞</sup>}}"], [1, "D<sub>0</sub> × 2, Cl same → AUC × 2"], [3, "{{frac:mg|mg·hr/L}} = L/hr"], [4, "sheet: Cl<sub>T</sub> = {{frac:FD<sub>0</sub>|AUC}}, F = 1 for IV"]]}, {"t": "Her questions test which parameters stay the same when the dose or the concentration changes. When the I V bolus dose increases, only the plasma concentration increases; clearance, half-life and V D stay the same. If the dose is doubled, the half-life and the clearance do not change and the initial concentration doubles. The statement that clearance increases as concentration increases is false: the amount removed per hour rises with the concentration, but clearance does not. In a poll, the parameter that describes elimination as a volume of fluid cleared of drug per unit time is clearance, not biotransformation, elimination rate or excretion.", "s": "inf:1", "d": "Her questions test which parameters stay the same when the dose or the concentration changes. When the IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus dose increases, only the plasma concentration increases; clearance, half-life and V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> stay the same. If the dose is doubled, the half-life and the clearance do not change and the initial concentration doubles. The statement that clearance increases as concentration increases is false: the amount removed per hour rises with the concentration, but clearance does not. In a poll, the parameter that describes elimination as a volume of fluid cleared of drug per unit time is clearance, not biotransformation, elimination rate or excretion.", "c": [[1, "dose ↑ → C<sub>p</sub> ↑; Cl, t½, V<sub>D</sub> unchanged"], [2, "dose × 2: t½ same, Cl same, C<sub>0</sub> × 2"], [3, "clearance rises with concentration: <b>false</b>"], [4, "✓ clearance; not biotransformation, elimination rate or excretion"]]}, {"t": "In calculations the usual trap is the unit of V D. It can arrive in liters, per kilogram, as a percent of body weight, or not at all, to be found as dose over C zero. A per kilogram or percent value is multiplied by the weight in kilograms, taking one liter as one kilogram, and the answer is in liters: ten percent of an eighty kilogram patient is eight liters. She said, in kinetics, our body weight is always in kilograms. In her final review poll, four hundred milligrams with C p equal to forty milligrams per liter times e to the minus zero point nine t gives a V D of ten liters; the zero point nine is k and is not used.", "s": "ivb:6", "d": "In calculations the usual trap is the unit of V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>. It can arrive in liters, per kilogram, as a percent of body weight, or not at all, to be found as dose over C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small>. A per kilogram or percent value is multiplied by the weight in kilograms, taking one liter as one kilogram, and the answer is in liters: ten percent of an eighty kilogram patient is eight liters. She said, in kinetics, our body weight is always in kilograms. In her final review poll, four hundred milligrams with C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> equal to forty milligrams per liter times e to the minus zero point nine t gives a V<sub>D</sub> of ten liters; the zero point nine is k and is not used.", "c": [[1, "V<sub>D</sub>: L, L/kg, % body weight, or {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|C<sub>0</sub>}}"], [2, "1 L = 1 kg: 10% × 80 kg = <b>8 L</b>"], [4, "V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:400 mg|40 mg/L}} = <b>10 L</b>; 0.9 hr<sup>−1</sup> is k, not used"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m2-3": {"title": "Parameters from concentration versus time data", "segs": [{"t": "Module two, objective three. Calculating parameters from concentration versus time data. Given a dose and one or two measured plasma concentrations, you find the patient's elimination rate constant k, the half-life, the concentration at time zero, C zero, the volume of distribution, the clearance, and amounts at stated times. The half-life is the time for the concentration or the amount to fall by half; for a first order drug it is the same at any level. Each value is used to find the next, so the order is k, then half-life, then C zero, then V D, then clearance and amounts.", "s": "ivb:4", "d": "Module two, objective three. Calculating parameters from concentration versus time data. Given a dose and one or two measured plasma concentrations, you find the patient's elimination rate constant k, the half-life, the concentration at time zero, C<sub>0</sub>, the volume of distribution, the clearance, and amounts at stated times. The half-life is the time for the concentration or the amount to fall by half; for a first order drug it is the same at any level. Each value is used to find the next, so the order is k, then half-life, then C<sub>0</sub>, then V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>, then clearance and amounts.", "c": [[2, "given: D<sub>0</sub> and one or two C<sub>p</sub> values"], [3, "t½ (hr): time to fall by half; the same at any level"], [4, "k → t½ → C<sub>0</sub> → V<sub>D</sub> → Cl<sub>T</sub>, amounts"]]}, {"t": "Why does k come first? After a bolus the log of the concentration falls on a straight line, and k is the slope of that line with the sign changed, so any two points on the line give k. Two different pairs give nearly the same value. The two points must be of the same kind, two concentrations or two amounts. A dose in milligrams divided by a concentration in milligrams per liter leaves liters, not a fraction remaining, so it cannot give k. V D links the two kinds, because the amount equals V D times the concentration at the same time.", "s": "ivb:4", "d": "Why does k come first? After a bolus the log of the concentration falls on a straight line, and k is the slope of that line with the sign changed, so any two points on the line give k. Two different pairs give nearly the same value. The two points must be of the same kind, two concentrations or two amounts. A dose in milligrams divided by a concentration in milligrams per liter leaves liters, not a fraction remaining, so it cannot give k. V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> links the two kinds, because the amount equals V<sub>D</sub> times the concentration at the same time.", "c": [[1, "ln C<sub>p</sub> vs t: straight line, slope −k"], [3, "two concentrations, or two amounts"], [4, "{{frac:mg|mg/L}} = L, not a fraction"], [5, "amount = V<sub>D</sub> × C, same time"]], "f": {"k": "bolus1_steps", "at": [[1, 1], [2, 2]]}}, {"t": "The relation is k equals l n C one minus l n C two, over t two minus t one: the drop in log concentration divided by the time between the samples. A larger drop over the same time gives a larger k. With amounts, k equals l n D zero minus l n D B, over t. The half-life equals zero point six nine three over k, which she expects memorised. k is on the bottom, so a larger k gives a shorter half-life, and per hour on the bottom leaves hours.", "s": "ivb:4", "d": "The relation is k equals l n C one minus l n C two, over t two minus t one: the drop in log concentration divided by the time between the samples. A larger drop over the same time gives a larger k. With amounts, k equals l n D<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(dose)</small> minus l n D B, over t. The half-life equals zero point six nine three over k, which she expects memorised. k is on the bottom, so a larger k gives a shorter half-life, and per hour on the bottom leaves hours.", "c": [[0, "k = {{frac:ln C<sub>1</sub> − ln C<sub>2</sub>|t<sub>2</sub> − t<sub>1</sub>}}"], [1, "larger drop, same time → larger k"], [2, "k = {{frac:ln D<sub>0</sub> − ln D<sub>B</sub>|t}}"], [3, "t½ = {{frac:0.693|k}} (not on the sheet)"], [4, "k ↑ → t½ ↓; {{frac:1|hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = hr"]]}, {"t": "C zero is rarely measured, because a sample cannot be drawn at the instant of injection, so the line is extended back to time zero: C zero equals C t times e to the k t. Going back in time makes the exponent positive, so C zero is higher than every sampled concentration. Then V D equals D zero over C zero, both at time zero, because the times must match, and clearance is k times V D. In the calculation clearance comes last, but in the body clearance and V D are what is set, and k follows from them. An amount at a later time is D zero times e to the minus k t, or V D times the concentration then; she accepts either.", "s": "ivb:5", "d": "C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> is rarely measured, because a sample cannot be drawn at the instant of injection, so the line is extended back to time zero: C<sub>0</sub> equals C t times e to the k t. Going back in time makes the exponent positive, so C<sub>0</sub> is higher than every sampled concentration. Then V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> equals D<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(dose)</small> over C<sub>0</sub>, both at time zero, because the times must match, and clearance is k times V<sub>D</sub>. In the calculation clearance comes last, but in the body clearance and V<sub>D</sub> are what is set, and k follows from them. An amount at a later time is D<sub>0</sub> times e to the minus k t, or V<sub>D</sub> times the concentration then; she accepts either.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>0</sub> = C<sub>t</sub>e<sup>kt</sup>"], [1, "+kt: back in time, C<sub>0</sub> above every sample"], [2, "V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|C<sub>0</sub>}}; Cl<sub>T</sub> = k × V<sub>D</sub>"], [3, "in the body: Cl and V<sub>D</sub> set, k = {{frac:Cl|V<sub>D</sub>}} follows"], [4, "D<sub>B</sub> = D<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−kt</sup> or D<sub>B</sub> = V<sub>D</sub> × C<sub>t</sub>"]], "f": {"k": "bolus1_steps", "at": [[0, 3], [2, 4], [4, 5]]}}, {"t": "Her first practice problem gives two hundred milligrams by I V bolus to an eighty kilogram man, fifteen milligrams per liter at six hours, and a V D of ten percent of body weight. It asks the amount in the body at twelve hours and the half-life. The dose is an amount and the six hour value is a concentration, so one must be converted before k can be found. V D converts the concentration: ten percent of eighty kilograms, taking one liter as one kilogram, is eight liters, and eight liters times fifteen milligrams per liter is one hundred twenty milligrams in the body at six hours.", "s": "ivb:7", "d": "Her first practice problem gives two hundred milligrams by IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus to an eighty kilogram man, fifteen milligrams per liter at six hours, and a V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> of ten percent of body weight. It asks the amount in the body at twelve hours and the half-life. The dose is an amount and the six hour value is a concentration, so one must be converted before k can be found. V<sub>D</sub> converts the concentration: ten percent of eighty kilograms, taking one liter as one kilogram, is eight liters, and eight liters times fifteen milligrams per liter is one hundred twenty milligrams in the body at six hours.", "c": [[0, "D<sub>0</sub> = 200 mg; 80 kg; C = 15 mg/L at 6 hr; V<sub>D</sub> = 10% of body weight"], [2, "200 mg and 15 mg/L: not the same kind"], [3, "V<sub>D</sub> = 0.10 × 80 kg = 8 L"], [3, "D<sub>B</sub> = 8 L × 15 mg/L = <b>120 mg</b> at 6 hr"]]}, {"t": "Two amounts now give k: two hundred milligrams at time zero and one hundred twenty at six hours, so k is zero point zero eight five per hour. The amount at twelve hours comes from D zero times e to the minus k t, and is seventy two milligrams. A check: zero point six of the drug remains after six hours, so after twelve hours zero point six of zero point six remains, and zero point three six of two hundred milligrams is seventy two. The half-life is eight point one five hours. Her added question asks the concentration at twelve hours: seventy two milligrams in eight liters is nine milligrams per liter. Read whether the question asks for an amount or a concentration.", "s": "ivb:7", "d": "Two amounts now give k: two hundred milligrams at time zero and one hundred twenty at six hours, so k is zero point zero eight five per hour. The amount at twelve hours comes from D<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(dose)</small> times e to the minus k t, and is seventy two milligrams. A check: zero point six of the drug remains after six hours, so after twelve hours zero point six of zero point six remains, and zero point three six of two hundred milligrams is seventy two. The half-life is eight point one five hours. Her added question asks the concentration at twelve hours: seventy two milligrams in eight liters is nine milligrams per liter. Read whether the question asks for an amount or a concentration.", "c": [[0, "k = {{frac:ln 200 − ln 120|6 hr}} = 0.085 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [1, "D<sub>B</sub> = 200 mg × e<sup>−(0.085)(12)</sup> = 200 × 0.36 = <b>72 mg</b>"], [2, "{{frac:120|200}} = 0.6; 0.6 × 0.6 = 0.36; 0.36 × 200 mg = 72 mg ✓"], [3, "t½ = {{frac:0.693|0.085 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = <b>8.15 hr</b> (k unrounded 0.08514 → 8.14 hr)"], [4, "C = {{frac:72 mg|8 L}} = <b>9 mg/L</b>"], [5, "amount (mg) or concentration (mg/L)?"]]}, {"t": "Her exam one review problem gives a one hundred fifty four pound woman a fifteen milligram per kilogram I V bolus, with thirty two point eight five micrograms per milliliter at two hours and nine point three two at eight hours. Both values are concentrations, so k comes straight from them, zero point two one per hour, and the half-life is three point three hours. The dose needs the weight in kilograms, seventy, so the dose is one thousand fifty milligrams. Extending the line back two hours gives a C zero of fifty micrograms per milliliter, which is fifty milligrams per liter, since the two units give the same number. V D is twenty one liters, and clearance is four point four one liters per hour.", "s": "ivb:4", "d": "Her exam one review problem gives a one hundred fifty four pound woman a fifteen milligram per kilogram IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus, with thirty two point eight five micrograms per milliliter at two hours and nine point three two at eight hours. Both values are concentrations, so k comes straight from them, zero point two one per hour, and the half-life is three point three hours. The dose needs the weight in kilograms, seventy, so the dose is one thousand fifty milligrams. Extending the line back two hours gives a C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> of fifty micrograms per milliliter, which is fifty milligrams per liter, since the two units give the same number. V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> is twenty one liters, and clearance is four point four one liters per hour.", "c": [[0, "154 lb; 15 mg/kg; 32.85 mcg/mL at 2 hr, 9.32 mcg/mL at 8 hr"], [1, "k = {{frac:ln 32.85 − ln 9.32|8 − 2 hr}} = {{frac:1.2598|6 hr}} = <b>0.21 hr<sup>−1</sup></b>"], [1, "t½ = {{frac:0.693|0.21 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = <b>3.3 hr</b>"], [2, "{{frac:154 lb|2.2 lb/kg}} = 70 kg; D<sub>0</sub> = 15 mg/kg × 70 kg = 1050 mg"], [3, "C<sub>0</sub> = 32.85 × e<sup>(0.21)(2)</sup> = <b>50 mcg/mL = 50 mg/L</b> &gt; 32.85 ✓"], [4, "V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:1050 mg|50 mg/L}} = <b>21 L</b>"], [4, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = 0.21 hr<sup>−1</sup> × 21 L = <b>4.41 L/hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "Her questions run as one patient and a series of small parts, and on the exam an earlier part cannot be revisited. Weight in pounds with a milligram per kilogram dose tests the conversion of two point two pounds per kilogram, and every later part depends on it. Fifteen minutes is zero point two five hours. Ninety nine point nine percent eliminated means ten half-lives, because each half-life halves what is left. Keep k to three or four decimal places until the end, write a zero before a leading decimal point, and give units. The usual error is dividing the dose by a concentration to get k; convert one of them first.", "s": "ivb:10", "c": [[1, "lb ÷ 2.2 = kg, first"], [2, "15 min = 0.25 hr"], [3, "99.9% eliminated = 10 half-lives (0.5<sup>10</sup> ≈ 0.001)"], [4, "k to 3 or 4 decimals; 0.21, not .21; units"], [5, "wrong: k from {{frac:dose|concentration}}"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m2-4": {"title": "One compartment versus two compartments", "segs": [{"t": "Module two, objective four. One compartment versus two compartments. Both models describe a drug given by I V bolus. The difference is whether the drug spreads evenly through the body at once, which is one compartment, or reaches some tissues before others, which needs two or more. She said, if I tell you that we're giving an I V bolus dose, now what do I have to tell you? I have to tell you that it follows a one compartment model or a two compartment.", "s": "ivb:14", "d": "Module two, objective four. One compartment versus two compartments. Both models describe a drug given by IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus. The difference is whether the drug spreads evenly through the body at once, which is one compartment, or reaches some tissues before others, which needs two or more. She said, if I tell you that we're giving an IV bolus dose, now what do I have to tell you? I have to tell you that it follows a one compartment model or a two compartment.", "c": [[2, "both: IV bolus"], [3, "even spread at once → one compartment; some tissues first → two or more"], [4, "the model is stated or shown"]]}, {"t": "With one compartment the drug is evenly spread from the start, so the only process lowering the plasma concentration, C p, is elimination. A fixed fraction leaves per hour, so on a log concentration axis the points fall on one straight line, with the same slope from the first sample to the last. Her graphs in this module use a log axis even when the axis is not labelled log. So a single straight line on a log axis after an I V bolus means one compartment.", "s": "ivb:13", "d": "With one compartment the drug is evenly spread from the start, so the only process lowering the plasma concentration, C<sub>p</sub>, is elimination. A fixed fraction leaves per hour, so on a log concentration axis the points fall on one straight line, with the same slope from the first sample to the last. Her graphs in this module use a log axis even when the axis is not labelled log. So a single straight line on a log axis after an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus means one compartment.", "c": [[0, "one compartment: only elimination lowers C<sub>p</sub>"], [1, "log C<sub>p</sub>: one straight line, one slope"], [2, "unlabelled axis: still a log scale"], [3, "one straight line → one compartment"]], "f": {"k": "bolus1_steps", "at": [[1, 1]]}}, {"t": "With two compartments the drug starts in the plasma and the tissues it reaches quickly, and part of it then moves out into the slower tissues while elimination goes on. So in the early period the plasma level falls for two reasons, distribution and elimination, and the fall is steep: this is the distribution phase. Once drug moves into the tissues as fast as it moves back, the plasma and tissue levels fall together and only elimination lowers them, so the curve becomes a straight, shallower line: the elimination phase. From then on the body behaves as one compartment. If the blood is sampled too late, the distribution phase can be missed entirely, and the curve looks like one compartment.", "s": "ivb:20", "c": [[1, "early: distribution + elimination → steep fall, distribution phase"], [2, "later: elimination only → straight, shallower line, elimination phase"], [4, "sampled late: distribution phase missed, looks like one compartment"]], "f": {"k": "twocpt_steps", "at": [[1, 1], [2, 2]]}}, {"t": "The two equations show the same difference. One compartment: C p equals C p zero times e to the minus k t, one exponential term, so one straight line on a log axis. Two compartments: C p equals A e to the minus alpha t plus B e to the minus beta t, two exponential terms, so two phases. Alpha and beta are rate constants in per hour, and A and B are concentrations in milligrams per liter. The term with the larger constant, alpha, shrinks to almost nothing early, so the early segment is steep and the late segment follows the beta term alone. The more alpha and beta differ, the more the second compartment matters; when they are close, one compartment predicts nearly the same levels.", "s": "ivb:21", "d": "The two equations show the same difference. One compartment: C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> equals C<sub>p</sub> zero times e to the minus k t, one exponential term, so one straight line on a log axis. Two compartments: C<sub>p</sub> equals A e to the minus alpha t plus B e to the minus beta t, two exponential terms, so two phases. Alpha and beta are rate constants in per hour, and A and B are concentrations in milligrams per liter. The term with the larger constant, alpha, shrinks to almost nothing early, so the early segment is steep and the late segment follows the beta term alone. The more alpha and beta differ, the more the second compartment matters; when they are close, one compartment predicts nearly the same levels.", "c": [[1, "C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup>e<sup>−kt</sup>: one term, one line"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> = Ae<sup>−at</sup> + Be<sup>−bt</sup>: two terms, two phases"], [3, "a (alpha), b (beta), k: hr<sup>−1</sup>; A, B, C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup>: mg/L"], [4, "a ≫ b: the a term → 0 early; late curve = Be<sup>−bt</sup>"], [5, "a and b far apart → two compartments matter; close → one compartment nearly the same"]], "f": {"k": "twocpt_steps", "at": [[2, 1], [4, 3]]}}, {"t": "When data do not follow one compartment, A, B, alpha and beta are found by the method of residuals, also called feathering or peeling. The straight late segment is extended back to time zero; its intercept is B and its slope gives beta. Subtracting that extended line from the observed early points leaves the residuals, which fall on a second, steeper line with intercept A and slope alpha. She said, I want you to conceptually know what that is, but I'm not gonna ask you to do that. She gives A, B, alpha and beta, and asks what they represent and why they are used.", "s": "ivb:19", "c": [[1, "late line extended to t = 0: intercept B, slope b"], [2, "observed − extended line = residuals: intercept A, slope a"], [4, "given: A, B, a, b; asked: what they represent, why used"]], "f": {"k": "twocpt_steps", "at": [[1, 2], [2, 3]]}}, {"t": "Her questions test the one versus two compartment difference in two ways. One is a log axis graph after an I V bolus with the model withheld: one straight line is one compartment, and a steep early segment followed by a straight line is two. The other is a stem that never says two compartment but gives a two term equation, or A, B, alpha and beta; recognising the model is the first step of the calculation. When she says only I V bolus, with no graph and no second term, she means one compartment, first order. The steep early segment is distribution, not faster elimination. Objective five explains why some drugs need the second compartment, and objective six calculates with it.", "s": "ivb:13", "d": "Her questions test the one versus two compartment difference in two ways. One is a log axis graph after an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus with the model withheld: one straight line is one compartment, and a steep early segment followed by a straight line is two. The other is a stem that never says two compartment but gives a two term equation, or A, B, alpha and beta; recognising the model is the first step of the calculation. When she says only IV bolus, with no graph and no second term, she means one compartment, first order. The steep early segment is distribution, not faster elimination. Objective five explains why some drugs need the second compartment, and objective six calculates with it.", "c": [[1, "graph: one line → one; steep start, then line → two"], [2, "Ae<sup>−at</sup> + Be<sup>−bt</sup>, or A, B, a, b → two compartments"], [3, "IV bolus only → one compartment, first order"], [4, "steep start = distribution"]]}], "mins": 4}, "gobj-m2-5": {"title": "Why some drugs need two compartments", "segs": [{"t": "Module two, objective five. Why some drugs fit a model with more than one compartment. Perfusion is the blood supply to a tissue. Tissues receive very different amounts of blood, so a drug carried in the blood reaches the well perfused organs first and the slowly perfused tissues later, before it is evenly spread. The highly perfused group, heart, brain, hepatic portal system, kidney and endocrine glands, is nine percent of body weight. Skin and muscle are fifty percent, fat and marrow nineteen, and the slowly perfused group, bone, ligaments, tendons, cartilage, teeth and hair, twenty two percent. The brain is listed as highly perfused, but the blood brain barrier keeps many drugs out, so it is usually not part of the distribution phase.", "s": "ivb:15", "c": [[2, "perfusion: blood supply to a tissue"], [3, "well perfused first, slowly perfused later"], [4, "highly perfused: heart, brain, hepatic-portal, kidney, endocrine: 9%"], [5, "skin, muscle 50%; fat, marrow 19%; bone, ligament, tendon, cartilage, teeth, hair 22%"], [6, "brain: highly perfused, but the barrier keeps drugs out of the distribution phase"]]}, {"t": "When drug reaches tissues at different rates, the plasma level after a single bolus falls in two stages. Early, drug leaves the plasma both into the tissues and by elimination, so the plasma level falls steeply while the tissue level rises. When drug moves into the tissue as fast as it moves back out, the tissue level is at its peak; after that the plasma and tissue levels fall in parallel, and only elimination lowers them. On a log plot this is a steep segment followed by a straight line, not one straight line, so one compartment no longer describes the drug. The tissue curve is theoretical, because only plasma is sampled.", "s": "ivb:18", "c": [[1, "early: C<sub>p</sub> ↓ steeply, C<sub>t</sub> ↑"], [2, "in = out: C<sub>t</sub> peaks; then C<sub>p</sub> and C<sub>t</sub> fall in parallel"], [3, "log C<sub>p</sub>: steep segment, then straight line → two compartments"], [4, "tissue curve: theoretical, only plasma sampled"]]}, {"t": "For a drug that fits a two compartment model, distribution has to be faster than elimination. If elimination were fast relative to distribution, the drug would be removed before it built up in the tissues, and it would not reach concentrations high enough to work. She said, If the elimination phase is significantly fast relative to the distribution phase, then you're not going to build up appreciable levels. On the plasma curve this is why the early segment is steeper than the late one: alpha, the slope of the distribution phase, is larger than beta, the slope of the elimination phase.", "s": "ivb:20", "c": [[0, "distribution faster than elimination"], [1, "elimination faster → no useful tissue levels"], [3, "a (distribution-phase slope) &gt; b (elimination-phase slope): steep, then shallow"]], "f": {"k": "twocpt_steps", "at": [[3, 3]]}}, {"t": "In the two compartment model, the central compartment is the plasma and the tissues the drug reaches quickly; its amount, concentration and volume are D p, C p and V p, so V p is the central volume. The tissue compartment holds the slowly reached tissues, with D t, C t and V t. The transfer constant k one two moves drug from central to tissue, and k two one moves it back, both in per hour. Of the three wirings, with elimination from the central compartment, from the tissue, or from both, the course uses the first. The dose goes in and is eliminated, with the rate constant k, through the central compartment only, because the kidney and liver, the main eliminating organs, are highly perfused.", "s": "ivb:17", "d": "In the two compartment model, the central compartment is the plasma and the tissues the drug reaches quickly; its amount, concentration and volume are D p, C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> and V p, so V p is the central volume. The tissue compartment holds the slowly reached tissues, with D t, C t and V t. The transfer constant k one two moves drug from central to tissue, and k two one moves it back, both in per hour. Of the three wirings, with elimination from the central compartment, from the tissue, or from both, the course uses the first. The dose goes in and is eliminated, with the rate constant k, through the central compartment only, because the kidney and liver, the main eliminating organs, are highly perfused.", "c": [[0, "central: D<sub>p</sub>, C<sub>p</sub>, V<sub>p</sub> (plasma + fast tissues)"], [1, "tissue: D<sub>t</sub>, C<sub>t</sub>, V<sub>t</sub>"], [2, "k<sub>12</sub>: central → tissue; k<sub>21</sub>: tissue → central (hr<sup>−1</sup>)"], [3, "<b>course: eliminated from the central compartment</b>"], [4, "input and elimination k: central only"]]}, {"t": "The tissue concentration changes at k one two times C p minus k two one times C t: the tissue gains drug from plasma and loses it back. So while k one two times C p is larger than k two one times C t, the tissue level rises and the plasma level falls; when the two are equal, the tissue level is at its peak. The plasma concentration changes at k two one times C t minus k one two times C p minus k times C p, and the last term is the loss by elimination, which only the plasma has. She does not ask you to solve these; objective six has the equations you calculate with.", "s": "ivb:17", "d": "The tissue concentration changes at k one two times C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> minus k two one times C t: the tissue gains drug from plasma and loses it back. So while k one two times C<sub>p</sub> is larger than k two one times C t, the tissue level rises and the plasma level falls; when the two are equal, the tissue level is at its peak. The plasma concentration changes at k two one times C t minus k one two times C<sub>p</sub> minus k times C<sub>p</sub>, and the last term is the loss by elimination, which only the plasma has. She does not ask you to solve these; objective six has the equations you calculate with.", "c": [[0, "{{frac:dC<sub>t</sub>|dt}} = k<sub>12</sub>C<sub>p</sub> − k<sub>21</sub>C<sub>t</sub>"], [1, "k<sub>12</sub>C<sub>p</sub> &gt; k<sub>21</sub>C<sub>t</sub>: C<sub>t</sub> ↑; equal: C<sub>t</sub> at peak"], [2, "{{frac:dC<sub>p</sub>|dt}} = k<sub>21</sub>C<sub>t</sub> − k<sub>12</sub>C<sub>p</sub> − kC<sub>p</sub>"]]}, {"t": "She asks this objective in words, not as a calculation: what A, B, alpha and beta represent, and why the model is used. A and B are the intercepts, in milligrams per liter, and alpha and beta the slopes, in per hour, of the distribution and elimination phases. She called these four the microconstants. The tissue peak can be above or below the plasma level, so do not assume tissue is always lower. In her notation V p is the central volume and V t the tissue volume.", "s": "ivb:21", "c": [[0, "asked in words: what A, B, a, b represent; why two compartments"], [1, "A, B: intercepts, mg/L; a, b: slopes, hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [2, "her term: A, a, B, b = microconstants"], [3, "tissue peak: above or below C<sub>p</sub>"], [4, "V<sub>p</sub>: central volume; V<sub>t</sub>: tissue volume"]]}], "mins": 4}, "gobj-m2-6": {"title": "Concentrations in a two compartment model", "segs": [{"t": "Module two, objective six. Predicting concentrations after an I V bolus in a two compartment model. The plasma concentration is the sum of two falling first order terms, a fast one for distribution and a slow one for elimination. A and B are their intercepts on the log concentration axis, in milligrams per liter, and alpha and beta are their slopes, in per hour. She said, the A or alpha and the B or beta, those are what we call our microconstants. She gives you these four numbers, or the equation that contains them, and you substitute; you do not derive them.", "s": "ivb:21", "d": "Module two, objective six. Predicting concentrations after an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus in a two compartment model. The plasma concentration is the sum of two falling first order terms, a fast one for distribution and a slow one for elimination. A and B are their intercepts on the log concentration axis, in milligrams per liter, and alpha and beta are their slopes, in per hour. She said, the A or alpha and the B or beta, those are what we call our microconstants. She gives you these four numbers, or the equation that contains them, and you substitute; you do not derive them.", "c": [[2, "C<sub>p</sub> = distribution term + elimination term"], [3, "A, B: intercepts, mg/L; a (alpha), b (beta): slopes, hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [5, "given: A, B, a, b, or the equation"]], "f": {"k": "twocpt_steps", "at": [[2, 1]]}}, {"t": "Why does each term behave as it does? Late in the curve the plasma level falls on one straight line, the beta term alone, so its intercept is B and its slope beta, and the elimination half-life comes from beta alone. Early, the curve lies above that line, and the difference is the alpha term, with intercept A and slope alpha. Distribution is faster than elimination, so alpha is always the larger constant and the alpha term shrinks to almost nothing within the first hours; A and B can be either way round. Both intercepts are in proportion to the dose, so doubling the dose doubles A, B and every concentration, while alpha and beta do not change.", "s": "ivb:21", "c": [[1, "late: Be<sup>−bt</sup> alone → beta half-life from b"], [2, "early excess = Ae<sup>−at</sup>"], [3, "a &gt; b always; A, B either way"], [4, "dose × 2: A, B, every C<sub>p</sub> × 2; a, b unchanged"]], "f": {"k": "twocpt_steps", "at": [[1, 2], [2, 3]]}}, {"t": "The relation is C p equals A e to the minus alpha t plus B e to the minus beta t. At time zero both exponentials equal one, so C p zero equals A plus B. The beta half-life is zero point six nine three over beta, the first order half-life applied to the elimination phase; beta is on the bottom, so a smaller beta gives a longer half-life. Divide by beta directly. She said, Do not get all complicated. The overall rate constant k is a different number from beta, so zero point six nine three over k is not the beta half-life.", "s": "ivb:23", "d": "The relation is C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> equals A e to the minus alpha t plus B e to the minus beta t. At time zero both exponentials equal one, so C<sub>p</sub> zero equals A plus B. The beta half-life is zero point six nine three over beta, the first order half-life applied to the elimination phase; beta is on the bottom, so a smaller beta gives a longer half-life. Divide by beta directly. She said, Do not get all complicated. The overall rate constant k is a different number from beta, so zero point six nine three over k is not the beta half-life.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = Ae<sup>−at</sup> + Be<sup>−bt</sup>"], [1, "t = 0: e<sup>0</sup> = 1 → C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup> = A + B"], [2, "beta half-life: t½ = {{frac:0.693|b}}; b ↓ → t½ ↑"], [5, "wrong: {{frac:0.693|k}}; k is not b"]]}, {"t": "Her practice problem gives theophylline as C p equals twelve e to the minus five point eight t, plus eighteen e to the minus zero point one six t, and asks the plasma level three hours after the dose. The first check is the order of the terms: five point eight is much larger than zero point one six, so the first term is distribution and the second elimination. At time zero the level is thirty milligrams per liter. The beta half-life is four point three three hours. By three hours the distribution term is effectively zero, so the level is the elimination term alone, eleven point one four milligrams per liter.", "s": "ivb:22", "d": "Her practice problem gives theophylline as C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> equals twelve e to the minus five point eight t, plus eighteen e to the minus zero point one six t, and asks the plasma level three hours after the dose. The first check is the order of the terms: five point eight is much larger than zero point one six, so the first term is distribution and the second elimination. At time zero the level is thirty milligrams per liter. The beta half-life is four point three three hours. By three hours the distribution term is effectively zero, so the level is the elimination term alone, eleven point one four milligrams per liter.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = 12e<sup>−5.8t</sup> + 18e<sup>−0.16t</sup>"], [1, "A = 12 mg/L, a = 5.8 hr<sup>−1</sup>; B = 18 mg/L, b = 0.16 hr<sup>−1</sup>; 5.8 ≫ 0.16 ✓"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup> = 12 + 18 = <b>30 mg/L</b>"], [3, "t½ = {{frac:0.693|0.16 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = <b>4.33 hr</b>"], [4, "12e<sup>−(5.8)(3)</sup> = 12e<sup>−17.4</sup> ≈ 0 mg/L"], [4, "18e<sup>−(0.16)(3)</sup> = 18 × 0.6188 = 11.14 mg/L"], [4, "<b>C<sub>p</sub> = 0 + 11.14 = 11.14 mg/L</b>"]], "f": {"k": "twocpt_steps", "at": [[2, 1], [3, 2], [4, 4]]}}, {"t": "A, B, alpha and beta also give the rate constants of the two compartment model. k, the overall elimination rate constant, describes elimination from the central compartment. It is larger than beta, because in the elimination phase drug returning from the tissue enters the plasma, so the plasma level falls more slowly than k alone would make it. k equals alpha times beta times the sum A plus B, over the cross term, A times beta plus B times alpha. k two one, tissue to central, is the cross term over A plus B. k one two, central to tissue, is A times B times the square of beta minus alpha, over A plus B times the cross term.", "s": "ivb:24", "c": [[1, "k: elimination from the central compartment, hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [2, "k &gt; b: drug returning from tissue slows the fall"], [3, "cross term: Ab + Ba, each intercept × the other phase slope; work it out once"], [3, "k = {{frac:ab(A + B)|Ab + Ba}}"], [4, "k<sub>21</sub> = {{frac:Ab + Ba|A + B}}"], [5, "k<sub>12</sub> = {{frac:AB(b − a)<sup>2</sup>|(A + B)(Ab + Ba)}}"]]}, {"t": "The central volume, V p, is the dose divided by A plus B, which is D zero over C p zero, the same idea as V D in one compartment. At the same dose, a larger A plus B means a smaller V p. When a dose and an area under the curve are given instead, V p equals D zero over k times A U C. The tissue volume, V t, equals V p times k one two over k two one: the faster drug moves out to the tissue compared with back, the larger the tissue volume.", "s": "ivb:25", "d": "The central volume, V p, is the dose divided by A plus B, which is D<sub>0</sub> over C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> zero, the same idea as V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> in one compartment. At the same dose, a larger A plus B means a smaller V p. When a dose and an area under the curve are given instead, V p equals D<sub>0</sub> over k times AUC. The tissue volume, V t, equals V p times k one two over k two one: the faster drug moves out to the tissue compared with back, the larger the tissue volume.", "c": [[0, "V<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|A + B}} = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup>}}; {{frac:mg|mg/L}} = L"], [1, "A + B ↑, same D<sub>0</sub> → V<sub>p</sub> ↓"], [2, "V<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|k × AUC<sub>0</sub><sup>∞</sup>}}"], [3, "V<sub>t</sub> = {{frac:V<sub>p</sub>k<sub>12</sub>|k<sub>21</sub>}}: k<sub>12</sub> ↑ → V<sub>t</sub> ↑"]]}, {"t": "Her in class sheet gives a two hundred fifty milligram bolus, A eight point four five and B five point three two milligrams per liter, alpha two point one eight and beta zero point one one four per hour. The beta half-life is six point zero eight hours, and C p zero is thirteen point seven seven milligrams per liter. At four hours the alpha term is about one thousandth of a milligram per liter, so the level is nearly all beta term, three point three seven milligrams per liter. The central volume is eighteen point one six liters.", "s": "ivb:25", "d": "Her in class sheet gives a two hundred fifty milligram bolus, A eight point four five and B five point three two milligrams per liter, alpha two point one eight and beta zero point one one four per hour. The beta half-life is six point zero eight hours, and C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> zero is thirteen point seven seven milligrams per liter. At four hours the alpha term is about one thousandth of a milligram per liter, so the level is nearly all beta term, three point three seven milligrams per liter. The central volume is eighteen point one six liters.", "c": [[0, "250 mg; A = 8.45, B = 5.32 mg/L; a = 2.18, b = 0.114 hr<sup>−1</sup>; a ≫ b ✓"], [1, "t½ = {{frac:0.693|0.114 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = <b>6.08 hr</b>"], [1, "C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup> = 8.45 + 5.32 = <b>13.77 mg/L</b>"], [2, "8.45e<sup>−(2.18)(4)</sup> + 5.32e<sup>−(0.114)(4)</sup> = 0.001 + 3.372 = <b>3.37 mg/L</b>"], [3, "V<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:250 mg|13.77 mg/L}} = <b>18.16 L</b>"]]}, {"t": "In her in class sheet, with A eight point four five and B five point three two milligrams per liter, alpha two point one eight and beta zero point one one four per hour, the rate constants start from the cross term, A times beta plus B times alpha, which is twelve point five six. Then k is zero point two seven two per hour, more than twice beta, which is zero point one one four per hour, so k and beta are clearly different numbers. k two one is zero point nine one two per hour, and k one two is one point one zero nine per hour.", "s": "ivb:24", "c": [[0, "Ab + Ba = (8.45)(0.114) + (5.32)(2.18) = 0.9633 + 11.5976 = 12.5609"], [1, "k = {{frac:(2.18)(0.114)(13.77)|12.5609}} = {{frac:3.4221|12.5609}} = <b>0.272 hr<sup>−1</sup></b>"], [1, "k 0.272 vs b 0.114 hr<sup>−1</sup>: k is not b"], [2, "k<sub>21</sub> = {{frac:12.5609|13.77}} = <b>0.912 hr<sup>−1</sup></b>"], [2, "k<sub>12</sub> = {{frac:(8.45)(5.32)(0.114 − 2.18)<sup>2</sup>|(13.77)(12.5609)}} = {{frac:191.88|172.96}} = <b>1.109 hr<sup>−1</sup></b>"]]}, {"t": "She gives the equation, or A, B, alpha and beta, and does not say two compartment. Four parts recur in this order: the elimination half-life, the initial concentration, a concentration at a stated hour, and the central volume. The elimination half-life is always zero point six nine three over the smaller constant, beta; using alpha, or calculating k first, is the usual mistake. She said, It will be written as beta half-life, because I am going to ask you for either the beta half-life or the elimination half-life, so that there is no confusion. The overall elimination rate constant means k, not beta. Only her in-class sheet adds k, k one two and k two one to those four.", "s": "ivb:23", "c": [[1, "beta t½ → C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup> → C<sub>p</sub> at t → V<sub>p</sub>"], [1, "her review: 15e<sup>−3.4t</sup> + 7e<sup>−0.12t</sup>, 500 mg: t½ = {{frac:0.693|0.12 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = <b>5.8 hr</b>; V<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:500 mg|22 mg/L}} = <b>22.7 L</b>"], [2, "beta half-life: smaller constant; wrong: a, or k first"], [4, "overall elimination rate constant = k, not b"], [5, "in-class sheet only: also k, k<sub>12</sub>, k<sub>21</sub>"]]}], "mins": 7}, "gobj-m3-1": {"title": "The pharmacokinetics of an I V infusion", "segs": [{"t": "Module three, objective one. What happens to a drug given by I V infusion. An intravenous infusion runs drug into a vein at a constant rate for a set time, instead of giving it all at once as an I V bolus. The infusion rate, R, is the amount of drug put in per unit of time, most often milligrams per hour. A constant rate in is a zero order input, because it stays the same whatever the concentration. Elimination stays first order, exactly as after a bolus: a fixed fraction of the drug present leaves each hour, and that fraction is the elimination rate constant, k.", "s": "inf:3", "d": "Module three, objective one. What happens to a drug given by IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> infusion. An intravenous infusion runs drug into a vein at a constant rate for a set time, instead of giving it all at once as an IV bolus. The infusion rate, R, is the amount of drug put in per unit of time, most often milligrams per hour. A constant rate in is a zero order input, because it stays the same whatever the concentration. Elimination stays first order, exactly as after a bolus: a fixed fraction of the drug present leaves each hour, and that fraction is the elimination rate constant, k.", "c": [[2, "infusion: constant rate, set time; bolus: all at once"], [3, "R: amount per time, mg/hr"], [4, "input: zero order, R constant"], [5, "elimination: first order; k = fraction leaving per hour, hr<sup>−1</sup>"]], "f": {"k": "infusion_steps", "at": [[4, 1]]}}, {"t": "Comparing an infusion with an I V bolus, she said, the only thing that's changing here is the input. At the start no drug is in the body, so C p, the plasma concentration, is zero and almost nothing leaves. The amount leaving each hour is k times the amount in the body, so it grows as drug builds up. While R is larger than the amount leaving each hour, drug accumulates and C p rises. The rise slows as the amount leaving each hour approaches R, and when the two are equal the level stops changing: this plateau is steady state, and its level is C s s, the steady state concentration.", "s": "inf:3", "d": "Comparing an infusion with an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus, she said, the only thing that's changing here is the input. At the start no drug is in the body, so C<sub>p</sub>, the plasma concentration, is zero and almost nothing leaves. The amount leaving each hour is k times the amount in the body, so it grows as drug builds up. While R is larger than the amount leaving each hour, drug accumulates and C<sub>p</sub> rises. The rise slows as the amount leaving each hour approaches R, and when the two are equal the level stops changing: this plateau is steady state, and its level is C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, the steady state concentration.", "c": [[1, "start: C<sub>p</sub> = 0, almost nothing leaves"], [2, "amount out per hour = k × amount in the body: grows as C<sub>p</sub> grows"], [3, "R &gt; amount out per hour → C<sub>p</sub> rises"], [4, "R = amount out per hour → plateau, C<sub>ss</sub>"]], "f": {"k": "infusion_steps", "at": [[1, 1], [4, 2]]}}, {"t": "Her opening poll for this module asked what rises when an I V bolus dose is raised. Only the concentration does; the clearance, the volume of plasma emptied of drug each hour, the half-life and the apparent volume of distribution stay the same, because they describe how this patient handles the drug, not how much was given. The same holds for an infusion: a higher R gives higher concentrations, and at the plateau the amount eliminated each hour equals the new R. k and the clearance do not change with R.", "s": "inf:1", "d": "Her opening poll for this module asked what rises when an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus dose is raised. Only the concentration does; the clearance, the volume of plasma emptied of drug each hour, the half-life and the apparent volume of distribution stay the same, because they describe how this patient handles the drug, not how much was given. The same holds for an infusion: a higher R gives higher concentrations, and at the plateau the amount eliminated each hour equals the new R. k and the clearance do not change with R.", "c": [[0, "her poll: bolus dose raised → what rises?"], [1, "C<sub>p</sub> rises; Cl, t<sub>½</sub>, V<sub>D</sub> unchanged"], [2, "textbook: R 30 → 40 mg/hr; amount out per hour at the plateau 30 → 40 mg/hr"], [3, "k, Cl: unchanged by R"]]}, {"t": "Cessation means stopping the infusion. After it nothing comes in, so C p falls by first order elimination alone, the same fall as after a bolus. The fraction lost each hour is still k, so on a log plot the fall has the same slope whether the infusion stopped at steady state or before it. She said, Our input is zero order, constant in, first order out. When we stop the in, then it's just out.", "s": "inf:12", "d": "Cessation means stopping the infusion. After it nothing comes in, so C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> falls by first order elimination alone, the same fall as after a bolus. The fraction lost each hour is still k, so on a log plot the fall has the same slope whether the infusion stopped at steady state or before it. She said, Our input is zero order, constant in, first order out. When we stop the in, then it's just out.", "c": [[1, "after cessation: no input, first order fall"], [2, "stopped at or before steady state: same k, same slope"], [4, "zero order in, first order out; stop → only out"]], "f": {"k": "infusion_steps", "at": [[1, 3]]}}, {"t": "Why infuse at all? A constant input gives precise control of C p, and the textbook adds that for a drug with a narrow therapeutic window, such as heparin, it avoids wide swings between the highest and lowest levels. With R, k and the volume or the clearance known, C p can be predicted at any time during or after the infusion, and every calculation in this module does that.", "s": "inf:19", "d": "Why infuse at all? A constant input gives precise control of C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small>, and the textbook adds that for a drug with a narrow therapeutic window, such as heparin, it avoids wide swings between the highest and lowest levels. With R, k and the volume or the clearance known, C<sub>p</sub> can be predicted at any time during or after the infusion, and every calculation in this module does that.", "c": [[1, "infusion: precise control of C<sub>p</sub>"], [1, "narrow window, e.g. heparin: no wide peak and trough swings"], [2, "R, k, V<sub>D</sub> or Cl → C<sub>p</sub> at any time"]]}, {"t": "Her practice problem asks for the rate of an infusion in two forms. Two hundred milligrams of an analgesic are dissolved in five hundred milliliters of five percent dextrose and run over twenty four hours. R is an amount per time, so it is the amount infused divided by the time it runs: eight point three three milligrams per hour. A pump is set in volume per time, so the same division uses the five hundred milliliters instead, and the hours are converted to minutes. The result, zero point three five milliliters per minute, is the same infusion described by the volume of solution instead of the amount of drug.", "s": "inf:3", "c": [[1, "200 mg in 500 mL, over 24 hr"], [2, "R = {{frac:amount|time}}"], [2, "R = {{frac:200 mg|24 hr}} = <b>8.33 mg/hr</b>"], [3, "{{frac:500 mL|24 hr}} = 20.83 mL/hr"], [3, "{{frac:500 mL|24 hr}} × {{frac:1 hr|60 min}} = 0.347 mL/min"], [4, "<b>0.35 mL/min</b>"]]}, {"t": "Three rates in this module have three different units, and mixing them up is the common error. R is how much drug goes in each hour, in milligrams per hour; k is what fraction of the drug present leaves each hour, in per hour; and the clearance is how many liters of plasma are emptied of drug each hour, in liters per hour. A zero order input does not make elimination zero order: in her review poll the infusion was the zero order input, and elimination was first order after a bolus, an oral dose and an infusion alike. The words constant rate or continuous infusion signal zero order input with first order elimination.", "s": null, "c": [[1, "R: amount per time, mg/hr"], [1, "k: fraction per time, hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [1, "Cl: volume per time, L/hr"], [2, "bolus: all at once in; oral: first order in; infusion: zero order in"], [2, "all three: first order out"], [3, "constant rate, continuous infusion → zero order in, first order out"]]}, {"t": "Her stems also need unit work first. V D, the apparent volume of distribution, is the volume that relates the amount of drug in the body to C p, and it may be given per kilogram. In her solution one hundred ten pounds became fifty kilograms, since one kilogram is two point two pounds, and three liters per kilogram then became one hundred fifty liters. Objective two explains the plateau, objective seven the fall after stopping, and objectives four and five add a loading dose so the patient does not wait for the climb.", "s": null, "d": "Her stems also need unit work first. V<sub>D</sub>, the apparent volume of distribution, is the volume that relates the amount of drug in the body to C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small>, and it may be given per kilogram. In her solution one hundred ten pounds became fifty kilograms, since one kilogram is two point two pounds, and three liters per kilogram then became one hundred fifty liters. Objective two explains the plateau, objective seven the fall after stopping, and objectives four and five add a loading dose so the patient does not wait for the climb.", "c": [[1, "V<sub>D</sub>: links the amount in the body to C<sub>p</sub>; may be L/kg"], [2, "{{frac:110 lb|2.2 lb/kg}} = 50 kg"], [2, "V<sub>D</sub> = 3 L/kg × 50 kg = <b>150 L</b>"], [3, "→ plateau (obj. 2), fall after stopping (obj. 7), loading dose (obj. 4, 5)"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m3-2": {"title": "Steady state during an infusion", "segs": [{"t": "Module three, objective two. Steady state, and how it relates to continuous dosing. During an infusion, C p, the plasma concentration, climbs and then levels off. Steady state is that plateau: the rate of drug going in equals the rate going out, so the amount in the body stops changing, and the level there is C s s, the steady state concentration. R, the infusion rate, is an amount per time; the clearance is the volume of plasma emptied of drug per hour; k is the fraction of the drug present removed per hour; and the half-life is the time for the level to change by half.", "s": "inf:4", "d": "Module three, objective two. Steady state, and how it relates to continuous dosing. During an infusion, C<sub>p</sub>, the plasma concentration, climbs and then levels off. Steady state is that plateau: the rate of drug going in equals the rate going out, so the amount in the body stops changing, and the level there is C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, the steady state concentration. R, the infusion rate, is an amount per time; the clearance is the volume of plasma emptied of drug per hour; k is the fraction of the drug present removed per hour; and the half-life is the time for the level to change by half.", "c": [[2, "C<sub>p</sub>: climbs, then levels off"], [3, "steady state: rate in = rate out → C<sub>ss</sub>"], [4, "R: mg/hr; Cl: L/hr; k: hr<sup>−1</sup>; t<sub>½</sub>: hr"]], "f": {"k": "infusion_steps", "at": [[2, 1], [3, 2]]}}, {"t": "Why does the level stop rising? The amount eliminated each hour is the clearance times C p, so early in the infusion, with little drug in the body, little leaves. As C p rises, the amount leaving each hour rises with it, until it equals R. The approach is gradual: the curve comes closer and closer to C s s but in theory never reaches it, so in practice a level close to the plateau is called steady state.", "s": "inf:4", "d": "Why does the level stop rising? The amount eliminated each hour is the clearance times C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small>, so early in the infusion, with little drug in the body, little leaves. As C<sub>p</sub> rises, the amount leaving each hour rises with it, until it equals R. The approach is gradual: the curve comes closer and closer to C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> but in theory never reaches it, so in practice a level close to the plateau is called steady state.", "c": [[1, "amount out per hour = Cl × C<sub>p</sub>: small at first"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> rises → amount out per hour rises, until it equals R"], [3, "never reached exactly; close to C<sub>ss</sub> counts as steady state"]], "f": {"k": "infusion_steps", "at": [[1, 1], [2, 2]]}}, {"t": "At the plateau, R equals the clearance times C s s, so C s s equals R over clearance. R is on the top, so a higher rate gives a proportionally higher plateau: twice the rate, twice C s s. Clearance is on the bottom, so a patient who clears less drug, for example in renal failure, has a higher plateau on the same rate. V D, the apparent volume of distribution, which relates the amount in the body to C p, is not in R over clearance, so with clearance fixed a larger volume does not change the height of the plateau. C s s equals R over clearance contains no time, so it says nothing about when the plateau arrives.", "s": "inf:4", "d": "At the plateau, R equals the clearance times C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, so C<sub>ss</sub> equals R over clearance. R is on the top, so a higher rate gives a proportionally higher plateau: twice the rate, twice C<sub>ss</sub>. Clearance is on the bottom, so a patient who clears less drug, for example in renal failure, has a higher plateau on the same rate. V<sub>D</sub>, the apparent volume of distribution, which relates the amount in the body to C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small>, is not in R over clearance, so with clearance fixed a larger volume does not change the height of the plateau. C<sub>ss</sub> equals R over clearance contains no time, so it says nothing about when the plateau arrives.", "c": [[0, "R = Cl × C<sub>ss</sub>"], [0, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}} = {{frac:R|kV<sub>D</sub>}}"], [1, "R doubled → C<sub>ss</sub> doubled; her review: 50 → 100 mg/hr, 12 → 24 mg/L"], [2, "Cl falls → C<sub>ss</sub> rises at the same R"], [2, "e.g. 115.5 mg/hr, Cl 11.55 → 8.55 L/hr: C<sub>ss</sub> 10 → 13.5 mg/L"], [3, "no V<sub>D</sub> in {{frac:R|Cl}}: V<sub>D</sub> does not set the height"], [4, "no t: the height only"]]}, {"t": "How soon the plateau arrives depends only on the half-life. The fraction of C s s reached after infusing for a time t is one minus e to the minus k t, which holds only k and t, so R cannot change the timing. After one half-life the level is half of C s s, after three it is eighty seven point five percent, and after five about ninety seven percent. She said, if I ask you how long it takes to get steady state following I V infusion, the simplest answer is three to five half-lives.", "s": "inf:5", "d": "How soon the plateau arrives depends only on the half-life. The fraction of C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> reached after infusing for a time t is one minus e to the minus k t, which holds only k and t, so R cannot change the timing. After one half-life the level is half of C<sub>ss</sub>, after three it is eighty seven point five percent, and after five about ninety seven percent. She said, if I ask you how long it takes to get steady state following IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> infusion, the simplest answer is three to five half-lives.", "c": [[1, "fraction of C<sub>ss</sub> reached = 1 − e<sup>−kt</sup>: only k and t"], [2, "1 t<sub>½</sub> → 50%; 3 → 87.5%; 5 → 96.9%"], [3, "<b>time to steady state: 3 to 5 half-lives</b>"]], "f": {"k": "infusion_steps", "at": [[2, 2]]}}, {"t": "The half-life depends on the patient: k is the clearance divided by V D, so the half-life, zero point six nine three over k, equals zero point six nine three times V D over the clearance. Clearance is on the bottom, so a smaller clearance, as in renal failure, gives a longer half-life and a slower climb, and on the same rate a higher plateau. V D is on the top, so a larger volume with the same clearance also lengthens the half-life and the climb, while the height stays the same. R is in neither, so the rate changes the height of the plateau and never the time to reach it. She said, It does not change the time that it takes to get to that steady state concentration.", "s": "inf:4", "d": "The half-life depends on the patient: k is the clearance divided by V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>, so the half-life, zero point six nine three over k, equals zero point six nine three times V<sub>D</sub> over the clearance. Clearance is on the bottom, so a smaller clearance, as in renal failure, gives a longer half-life and a slower climb, and on the same rate a higher plateau. V<sub>D</sub> is on the top, so a larger volume with the same clearance also lengthens the half-life and the climb, while the height stays the same. R is in neither, so the rate changes the height of the plateau and never the time to reach it. She said, It does not change the time that it takes to get to that steady state concentration.", "c": [[0, "k = {{frac:Cl|V<sub>D</sub>}}; t<sub>½</sub> = {{frac:0.693|k}} = {{frac:0.693 V<sub>D</sub>|Cl}}"], [1, "Cl falls: t<sub>½</sub> longer, slower climb, higher C<sub>ss</sub>"], [2, "V<sub>D</sub> larger, Cl the same: t<sub>½</sub> longer, same C<sub>ss</sub>"], [3, "R: the height only, never the time"]]}, {"t": "The equation for the rise holds both the height and the timing: C p equals R over clearance, times one minus e to the minus k t. The first factor is C s s, set by R and the clearance; the bracket is the fraction of C s s reached, set by k and t, with t counted from the start of the infusion. Early on the bracket is a small fraction, and near five half-lives it approaches one. Counted in half-lives, the fraction after n half-lives is one minus one half to the power n, so two half-lives give seventy five percent and four give ninety three point seven five percent. About three point three two half-lives reach ninety percent, and four point three two reach ninety five percent.", "s": "inf:5", "d": "The equation for the rise holds both the height and the timing: C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> equals R over clearance, times one minus e to the minus k t. The first factor is C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, set by R and the clearance; the bracket is the fraction of C<sub>ss</sub> reached, set by k and t, with t counted from the start of the infusion. Early on the bracket is a small fraction, and near five half-lives it approaches one. Counted in half-lives, the fraction after n half-lives is one minus one half to the power n, so two half-lives give seventy five percent and four give ninety three point seven five percent. About three point three two half-lives reach ninety percent, and four point three two reach ninety five percent.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}}(1 − e<sup>−kt</sup>)"], [1, "{{frac:R|Cl}} = C<sub>ss</sub>: the height"], [1, "(1 − e<sup>−kt</sup>): the fraction reached, the timing"], [2, "early: small; near 5 half-lives: → 1"], [3, "after n half-lives: 1 − 0.5<sup>n</sup>"], [3, "1 → 50%, 2 → 75%, 3 → 87.5%, 4 → 93.75%"], [4, "3.32 half-lives → 90%; 4.32 → 95%"]], "f": {"k": "infusion_steps", "at": [[2, 2]]}}, {"t": "Her slide Example four asks how long an infusion must run to reach ninety percent of steady state, with k equal to zero point one five per hour. The fraction of C s s reached, one minus e to the minus k t, answers it, so it is set equal to zero point nine and solved for t. The ten percent of C s s not yet reached is not a power of one half, so the exponential is isolated and t is found with a natural log. The answer is fifteen point three five hours, which she states as about fifteen hours. As a check, the half-life is four point six two hours, so fifteen point three five hours is three point three two half-lives, her ninety percent shortcut.", "s": "inf:11", "d": "Her slide Example four asks how long an infusion must run to reach ninety percent of steady state, with k equal to zero point one five per hour. The fraction of C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> reached, one minus e to the minus k t, answers it, so it is set equal to zero point nine and solved for t. The ten percent of C<sub>ss</sub> not yet reached is not a power of one half, so the exponential is isolated and t is found with a natural log. The answer is fifteen point three five hours, which she states as about fifteen hours. As a check, the half-life is four point six two hours, so fifteen point three five hours is three point three two half-lives, her ninety percent shortcut.", "c": [[0, "k = 0.15 hr<sup>−1</sup>; 90% of C<sub>ss</sub>: t = ?"], [1, "0.9 = 1 − e<sup>−0.15t</sup>"], [2, "e<sup>−0.15t</sup> = 1 − 0.9 = 0.1"], [2, "−0.15t = ln 0.1 = −2.3026"], [2, "t = {{frac:−2.3026|−0.15 hr<sup>−1</sup>}}"], [3, "<b>t = 15.35 hr ≈ 15 hr</b>"], [4, "t<sub>½</sub> = {{frac:0.693|0.15 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = 4.62 hr; {{frac:15.35 hr|4.62 hr}} = 3.32 half-lives ✓"]]}, {"t": "Her graded quiz asked, Which of the following results when the rate of infusion is increased? The keyed answer is that the steady state concentration increases; the options that the time to steady state increases or decreases are wrong, because the rate sets the height of the plateau and the half-life sets how soon it arrives. Asked how long to steady state, answer three to five half-lives; asked for a stated percentage, calculate it. A renal failure part does change the time, because the half-life is longer: in her Practice three the new steady state takes four point three two of the new half-lives for ninety five percent.", "s": "inf:9", "c": [[0, "quiz: rate of infusion increased → ?"], [1, "✓ C<sub>ss</sub> increases"], [1, "wrong: the time to steady state increases or decreases"], [2, "how long? 3 to 5 half-lives; a stated %: calculate"], [2, "50, 75, 87.5% → count half-lives; other % → ln"], [3, "renal failure: 95% at 4.32 of the new, longer half-lives"]]}, {"t": "A concentration in the stem can stand for a percentage of C s s. In her Practice four the half-life is six hours and C s s is ten milligrams per liter, so a level of five milligrams per liter is fifty percent, one half-life, six hours. Eighty percent of C s s leaves twenty percent not yet reached, which is not a power of one half, so the log route gives thirteen point nine three hours. Objective six uses this plateau line to choose a rate, and objective five adds a loading dose to skip the wait.", "s": "inf:9", "d": "A concentration in the stem can stand for a percentage of C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>. In her Practice four the half-life is six hours and C<sub>ss</sub> is ten milligrams per liter, so a level of five milligrams per liter is fifty percent, one half-life, six hours. Eighty percent of C<sub>ss</sub> leaves twenty percent not yet reached, which is not a power of one half, so the log route gives thirteen point nine three hours. Objective six uses this plateau line to choose a rate, and objective five adds a loading dose to skip the wait.", "c": [[1, "k = {{frac:0.693|6 hr}} = 0.1155 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [1, "{{frac:5 mg/L|10 mg/L}} = 50% → <b>1 half-life = 6 hr</b>"], [2, "e<sup>−kt</sup> = 0.2; t = {{frac:ln 5|0.1155 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = <b>13.93 hr</b>"], [3, "→ R for a target (obj. 6); loading dose, no wait (obj. 5)"]]}], "mins": 7}, "gobj-m3-3": {"title": "Calculating infusion parameters", "segs": [{"t": "Module three, objective three. Determining optimum dosing for an infused drug by calculating its parameters. Dosing an infusion means choosing the infusion rate, R, and when needed a loading dose, so that C p, the plasma concentration, sits at a target. A loading dose is an I V bolus given as the infusion starts, sized to reach the target at once. Before either can be chosen, the stem's data have to be turned into three parameters: k, the fraction of drug removed per hour; V D, the apparent volume of distribution, which relates the amount in the body to C p; and the clearance, the volume of plasma emptied of drug per hour.", "s": "inf:7", "d": "Module three, objective three. Determining optimum dosing for an infused drug by calculating its parameters. Dosing an infusion means choosing the infusion rate, R, and when needed a loading dose, so that C<sub>p</sub>, the plasma concentration, sits at a target. A loading dose is an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus given as the infusion starts, sized to reach the target at once. Before either can be chosen, the stem's data have to be turned into three parameters: k, the fraction of drug removed per hour; V<sub>D</sub>, the apparent volume of distribution, which relates the amount in the body to C<sub>p</sub>; and the clearance, the volume of plasma emptied of drug per hour.", "c": [[2, "choices: R and a loading dose, for a target C<sub>p</sub>"], [3, "loading dose: I V bolus at the start"], [4, "k: hr<sup>−1</sup>; V<sub>D</sub>: L; Cl: L/hr"]]}, {"t": "Clearance and V D belong to the patient: the textbook treats them as the independent parameters, and k as depending on both. R and the loading dose are the clinician's choices. Each choice follows one patient parameter: the rate follows the clearance, because the plateau is R over clearance, and the loading dose follows V D, because it is the amount that brings that volume to the target level. The textbook describes the clinical routine: start a rate from published values, measure C p twice with the second sample near steady state, then recompute k and adjust the rate.", "s": "inf:7", "d": "Clearance and V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> belong to the patient: the textbook treats them as the independent parameters, and k as depending on both. R and the loading dose are the clinician's choices. Each choice follows one patient parameter: the rate follows the clearance, because the plateau is R over clearance, and the loading dose follows V<sub>D</sub>, because it is the amount that brings that volume to the target level. The textbook describes the clinical routine: start a rate from published values, measure C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> twice with the second sample near steady state, then recompute k and adjust the rate.", "c": [[0, "patient: Cl and V<sub>D</sub>; k depends on both"], [1, "clinician: R and D<sub>L</sub>"], [2, "R = C<sub>ss</sub> × Cl: no V<sub>D</sub>"], [2, "D<sub>L</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub> × V<sub>D</sub>: no Cl"], [3, "start R → two samples → recompute k → adjust R"]]}, {"t": "Clearance equals k times V D. k is the fraction of the volume emptied each hour, so multiplying it by the volume gives the liters emptied each hour. Read the other way, k is clearance over V D: a larger clearance removes a larger fraction each hour, and the same clearance acting on a larger volume removes a smaller fraction, so the half-life is longer. Any two of the three give the third, and this line is not on the equation sheet. When the stem gives the routes separately, total body clearance, Cl T, is renal plus metabolic clearance: in her Practice four, five plus six point five five is eleven point five five liters per hour.", "s": "inf:7", "d": "Clearance equals k times V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>. k is the fraction of the volume emptied each hour, so multiplying it by the volume gives the liters emptied each hour. Read the other way, k is clearance over V<sub>D</sub>: a larger clearance removes a larger fraction each hour, and the same clearance acting on a larger volume removes a smaller fraction, so the half-life is longer. Any two of the three give the third, and this line is not on the equation sheet. When the stem gives the routes separately, total body clearance, Cl<sub>T</sub>, is renal plus metabolic clearance: in her Practice four, five plus six point five five is eleven point five five liters per hour.", "c": [[0, "Cl = k × V<sub>D</sub>"], [1, "hr<sup>−1</sup> × L = L/hr"], [2, "k = {{frac:Cl|V<sub>D</sub>}}: Cl larger → k larger; V<sub>D</sub> larger → k smaller, t<sub>½</sub> longer"], [3, "any two → the third; not on the sheet"], [4, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = Cl<sub>renal</sub> + Cl<sub>metabolic</sub> = 5 + 6.55 = <b>11.55 L/hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "Her renal failure parts show what each parameter does. In Practice four the clearance falls from eleven point five five to eight point five five liters per hour while V D stays one hundred liters. So k falls from zero point one one five five to zero point zero eight five five per hour, and the half-life lengthens from six to about eight point one hours. To keep the same target of ten milligrams per liter, R falls in proportion to the clearance, from one hundred fifteen point five to eighty five point five milligrams per hour. The loading dose, ten milligrams per liter times one hundred liters, stays one thousand milligrams, because clearance is not in it.", "s": null, "d": "Her renal failure parts show what each parameter does. In Practice four the clearance falls from eleven point five five to eight point five five liters per hour while V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> stays one hundred liters. So k falls from zero point one one five five to zero point zero eight five five per hour, and the half-life lengthens from six to about eight point one hours. To keep the same target of ten milligrams per liter, R falls in proportion to the clearance, from one hundred fifteen point five to eighty five point five milligrams per hour. The loading dose, ten milligrams per liter times one hundred liters, stays one thousand milligrams, because clearance is not in it.", "c": [[1, "Cl<sub>T</sub>: 11.55 → 8.55 L/hr; V<sub>D</sub> 100 L, unchanged"], [2, "k = {{frac:Cl|V<sub>D</sub>}}: 0.1155 → 0.0855 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [2, "t<sub>½</sub>: 6 → 8.1 hr"], [3, "R = C<sub>ss</sub> × Cl = 10 × 8.55 = <b>85.5 mg/hr</b> (was 115.5)"], [4, "D<sub>L</sub> = 10 mg/L × 100 L = 1000 mg, both times"]], "f": {"k": "renal_steps", "at": [[1, 1], [2, 2], [3, 3]]}}, {"t": "Her slide Example one asks the expected C s s, the steady state concentration, for theophylline infused at fifty milligrams per hour, with a half-life of six hours and a V D of thirty liters. C s s is R over clearance, because the plateau is where the rate in equals the clearance times the level. The stem gives no clearance, so the half-life becomes k, and k times the volume becomes the clearance. Dividing the rate by that clearance gives fourteen point four three milligrams per liter, which she works with as fourteen point four. While working this aloud she called the thirty liters the clearance; thirty liters is V D, and the clearance is k times thirty liters.", "s": "inf:7", "d": "Her slide Example one asks the expected C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, the steady state concentration, for theophylline infused at fifty milligrams per hour, with a half-life of six hours and a V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> of thirty liters. C<sub>ss</sub> is R over clearance, because the plateau is where the rate in equals the clearance times the level. The stem gives no clearance, so the half-life becomes k, and k times the volume becomes the clearance. Dividing the rate by that clearance gives fourteen point four three milligrams per liter, which she works with as fourteen point four. While working this aloud she called the thirty liters the clearance; thirty liters is V<sub>D</sub>, and the clearance is k times thirty liters.", "c": [[0, "t<sub>½</sub> = 6 hr, V<sub>D</sub> = 30 L, R = 50 mg/hr; C<sub>ss</sub> = ?"], [1, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}}"], [2, "k = {{frac:0.693|6 hr}} = 0.1155 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [2, "Cl = 0.1155 hr<sup>−1</sup> × 30 L = 3.465 L/hr"], [3, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:50 mg/hr|3.465 L/hr}} = <b>14.43 mg/L</b>"], [4, "30 L = V<sub>D</sub>, not Cl"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Examples three and four give clearance directly: Cl T is four point five liters per hour, k is zero point one five per hour, and the rate is fifty milligrams per hour. C s s is fifty over four point five, eleven point one one milligrams per liter. C p eight hours after the start is that plateau times the fraction reached, one minus e to the minus k t. The result, seven point seven six milligrams per liter, is about seventy percent of the plateau, which fits: the half-life is four point six two hours, so eight hours lies between one and two half-lives, between fifty and seventy five percent. She did not say this value aloud; it is computed from her R, Cl T and k.", "s": "inf:10", "d": "Her slide Examples three and four give clearance directly: Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small> is four point five liters per hour, k is zero point one five per hour, and the rate is fifty milligrams per hour. C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> is fifty over four point five, eleven point one one milligrams per liter. C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> eight hours after the start is that plateau times the fraction reached, one minus e to the minus k t. The result, seven point seven six milligrams per liter, is about seventy percent of the plateau, which fits: the half-life is four point six two hours, so eight hours lies between one and two half-lives, between fifty and seventy five percent. She did not say this value aloud; it is computed from her R, Cl<sub>T</sub> and k.", "c": [[0, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = 4.5 L/hr, k = 0.15 hr<sup>−1</sup>, R = 50 mg/hr"], [1, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:50 mg/hr|4.5 L/hr}} = <b>11.11 mg/L</b>"], [2, "kt = 0.15 hr<sup>−1</sup> × 8 hr = 1.2"], [2, "1 − e<sup>−1.2</sup> = 0.6988"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> = 11.11 mg/L × 0.6988 = <b>7.76 mg/L</b>"], [3, "t<sub>½</sub> = 4.62 hr; 8 hr = 1.73 half-lives: between 50% and 75% ✓"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it: one patient in the stem, then six to nine chained parts, and later parts say at the rate you determined above, so an error in k carries into every later answer. Her graded quiz gave only a V D of twenty two liters and a clearance of three point two liters per hour, so k had to be built as clearance over V D before the level five hours after stopping could be found. Weight may come in pounds and V D per kilogram: one hundred ten pounds is fifty kilograms, and three liters per kilogram then gives one hundred fifty liters.", "s": null, "d": "How she tests it: one patient in the stem, then six to nine chained parts, and later parts say at the rate you determined above, so an error in k carries into every later answer. Her graded quiz gave only a V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> of twenty two liters and a clearance of three point two liters per hour, so k had to be built as clearance over V<sub>D</sub> before the level five hours after stopping could be found. Weight may come in pounds and V<sub>D</sub> per kilogram: one hundred ten pounds is fifty kilograms, and three liters per kilogram then gives one hundred fifty liters.", "c": [[0, "one patient → 6 to 9 chained parts"], [1, "quiz: k = {{frac:3.2 L/hr|22 L}} = 0.1455 hr<sup>−1</sup>; 14 mg/L → <b>6.8 mg/L</b> 5 hr after stopping"], [2, "110 lb ÷ 2.2 = 50 kg; 3 L/kg × 50 kg = 150 L"]]}, {"t": "Some data are never used, such as the age and sex in her quiz and the sixty five kilogram weight in Practice four. Contrasting scenarios are set in capitals, with the loading dose against without the loading dose, so check which one each part means. She computes to more precision than she reports and rounds to a practical value, for example fourteen point eight six milligrams per hour to fifteen. Micrograms per milliliter equal milligrams per liter, so a target in either unit goes straight into these lines. With k, V D and the clearance set, objective six chooses the rate, objective four the loading dose, and objective seven the level at any time.", "s": null, "d": "Some data are never used, such as the age and sex in her quiz and the sixty five kilogram weight in Practice four. Contrasting scenarios are set in capitals, with the loading dose against without the loading dose, so check which one each part means. She computes to more precision than she reports and rounds to a practical value, for example fourteen point eight six milligrams per hour to fifteen. Micrograms per milliliter equal milligrams per liter, so a target in either unit goes straight into these lines. With k, V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> and the clearance set, objective six chooses the rate, objective four the loading dose, and objective seven the level at any time.", "c": [[0, "not used: age and sex (quiz), 65 kg (Practice 4)"], [1, "WITH vs WITHOUT THE LOADING DOSE"], [2, "14.86 → 15 mg/hr; 454.5 → 455 mg"], [3, "1 mcg/mL = 1 mg/L"], [4, "→ R (obj. 6), D<sub>L</sub> (obj. 4), C<sub>p</sub> at any time (obj. 7)"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m3-4": {"title": "Loading doses with an infusion", "segs": [{"t": "Module three, objective four. Calculating a loading dose to give with an intravenous infusion. A loading dose, D L, is an I V bolus given at the same moment the infusion starts. Its aim is to put C p, the plasma concentration, at the target steady state concentration, C s s, from the start, instead of after three to five half-lives. V D, the apparent volume of distribution, is the volume that relates the amount of drug in the body to C p. R is the infusion rate, an amount per hour, and k is the fraction of the drug present eliminated each hour.", "s": "inf:15", "d": "Module three, objective four. Calculating a loading dose to give with an intravenous infusion. A loading dose, D L, is an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus given at the same moment the infusion starts. Its aim is to put C<sub>p</sub>, the plasma concentration, at the target steady state concentration, C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, from the start, instead of after three to five half-lives. V<sub>D</sub>, the apparent volume of distribution, is the volume that relates the amount of drug in the body to C<sub>p</sub>. R is the infusion rate, an amount per hour, and k is the fraction of the drug present eliminated each hour.", "c": [[2, "D<sub>L</sub>: I V bolus at the start of the infusion"], [3, "aim: C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub> from the start"], [4, "amount in the body = C<sub>p</sub> × V<sub>D</sub>"], [5, "R: mg/hr; k: hr<sup>−1</sup>"]]}, {"t": "At steady state the body holds a fixed amount of drug, C s s times V D. A loading dose of exactly that amount gives the body at once what the infusion alone would reach only after three to five half-lives. The bolus part of the level then falls by first order elimination. The infusion part rises toward C s s. Because the dose matches the amount at steady state, what the bolus part loses each hour the infusion part gains, so their sum stays flat at C s s. She said, we want our loading dose to look like the amount of drug that should be in the body at steady state.", "s": "inf:15", "d": "At steady state the body holds a fixed amount of drug, C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> times V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>. A loading dose of exactly that amount gives the body at once what the infusion alone would reach only after three to five half-lives. The bolus part of the level then falls by first order elimination. The infusion part rises toward C<sub>ss</sub>. Because the dose matches the amount at steady state, what the bolus part loses each hour the infusion part gains, so their sum stays flat at C<sub>ss</sub>. She said, we want our loading dose to look like the amount of drug that should be in the body at steady state.", "c": [[0, "amount at steady state = C<sub>ss</sub> × V<sub>D</sub>"], [1, "D<sub>L</sub> = that amount, at once"], [2, "bolus part: falls, first order"], [3, "infusion part: rises"], [4, "sum: flat at C<sub>ss</sub>"]], "f": {"k": "loading_steps", "at": [[2, 1], [3, 2], [4, 3]]}}, {"t": "The size of the loading dose is set by V D and the target concentration. A larger V D needs a larger dose to bring the larger volume to the same target, and a higher target needs a proportionally larger dose. Clearance is not in it, so when renal failure lowers the clearance, the rate must fall but the loading dose stays the same: in her Practice four it is one thousand milligrams both times. The loading dose does not move the plateau either, because the plateau is R over clearance: in question five of her in-class set, a seven hundred fifty milligram loading dose with seventy five milligrams per hour gives the same plateau as no loading dose, thirty six point one milligrams per liter.", "s": "inf:15", "d": "The size of the loading dose is set by V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> and the target concentration. A larger V<sub>D</sub> needs a larger dose to bring the larger volume to the same target, and a higher target needs a proportionally larger dose. Clearance is not in it, so when renal failure lowers the clearance, the rate must fall but the loading dose stays the same: in her Practice four it is one thousand milligrams both times. The loading dose does not move the plateau either, because the plateau is R over clearance: in question five of her in-class set, a seven hundred fifty milligram loading dose with seventy five milligrams per hour gives the same plateau as no loading dose, thirty six point one milligrams per liter.", "c": [[1, "V<sub>D</sub> larger → D<sub>L</sub> larger; target C<sub>ss</sub> doubled → D<sub>L</sub> doubled"], [2, "Cl falls: R falls, D<sub>L</sub> unchanged (1000 mg both times)"], [3, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}}: no D<sub>L</sub>"], [3, "her in-class set, question 5: 750 mg + 75 mg/hr → C<sub>ss</sub> 36.1 mg/L, as without it"]], "f": {"k": "loading_steps", "at": [[3, 4]]}}, {"t": "D L equals C s s times V D. A concentration times a volume gives an amount, so the result is in milligrams, a dose. This line holds no rate, so it applies whenever a target and a volume are given; it is on the equation sheet, under the R and k form. A second route is D L equals R over k. R over k is C s s times clearance over k, and clearance over k is V D, so the two routes agree only when R was itself chosen to give the target. She warned that a rate picked out of the air gives a poor loading dose.", "s": "inf:15", "d": "D L equals C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> times V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>. A concentration times a volume gives an amount, so the result is in milligrams, a dose. This line holds no rate, so it applies whenever a target and a volume are given; it is on the equation sheet, under the R and k form. A second route is D L equals R over k. R over k is C<sub>ss</sub> times clearance over k, and clearance over k is V<sub>D</sub>, so the two routes agree only when R was itself chosen to give the target. She warned that a rate picked out of the air gives a poor loading dose.", "c": [[0, "D<sub>L</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub> × V<sub>D</sub>"], [1, "mg/L × L = mg"], [2, "no rate in it; on the sheet"], [3, "D<sub>L</sub> = {{frac:R|k}}; {{frac:mg/hr|hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = mg"], [4, "{{frac:R|k}} = C<sub>ss</sub> × {{frac:Cl|k}} = C<sub>ss</sub> × V<sub>D</sub> only if R gives the target"]]}, {"t": "With both running, C p at a time t after the start of therapy is the bolus part plus the infusion part. The bolus part is D L over V D, times e to the minus k t, and it falls. The infusion part is R over k V D, times one minus e to the minus k t, and it rises. When D L over V D equals C s s, the two parts add to C s s at every time. The equation sheet carries the two parts separately, as the first order line and the infusion line.", "s": "inf:15", "d": "With both running, C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> at a time t after the start of therapy is the bolus part plus the infusion part. The bolus part is D L over V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>, times e to the minus k t, and it falls. The infusion part is R over k V<sub>D</sub>, times one minus e to the minus k t, and it rises. When D L over V<sub>D</sub> equals C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, the two parts add to C<sub>ss</sub> at every time. The equation sheet carries the two parts separately, as the first order line and the infusion line.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = bolus part + infusion part; t from the start of therapy"], [1, "bolus: {{frac:D<sub>L</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}}e<sup>−kt</sup>"], [2, "infusion: {{frac:R|kV<sub>D</sub>}}(1 − e<sup>−kt</sup>)"], [3, "{{frac:D<sub>L</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}} = C<sub>ss</sub>: C<sub>ss</sub>e<sup>−kt</sup> + C<sub>ss</sub>(1 − e<sup>−kt</sup>) = C<sub>ss</sub>"]], "f": {"k": "loading_steps", "at": [[1, 1], [2, 2], [3, 3]]}}, {"t": "Her slide Example seven: a physician wants to infuse at twenty milligrams per hour, k is zero point one six per hour, V D is ten liters, and the question asks the loading dose that reaches twelve point five micrograms per milliliter immediately. Both routes apply, and their agreement shows whether this rate suits the target. Rate over k gives one hundred twenty five milligrams. Twelve point five micrograms per milliliter is twelve point five milligrams per liter, and times ten liters it again gives one hundred twenty five milligrams. They agree because this rate gives a plateau of exactly twelve point five milligrams per liter.", "s": "inf:17", "d": "Her slide Example seven: a physician wants to infuse at twenty milligrams per hour, k is zero point one six per hour, V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> is ten liters, and the question asks the loading dose that reaches twelve point five micrograms per milliliter immediately. Both routes apply, and their agreement shows whether this rate suits the target. Rate over k gives one hundred twenty five milligrams. Twelve point five micrograms per milliliter is twelve point five milligrams per liter, and times ten liters it again gives one hundred twenty five milligrams. They agree because this rate gives a plateau of exactly twelve point five milligrams per liter.", "c": [[0, "R = 20 mg/hr, k = 0.16 hr<sup>−1</sup>, V<sub>D</sub> = 10 L"], [0, "target 12.5 mcg/mL: D<sub>L</sub> = ?"], [2, "D<sub>L</sub> = {{frac:20 mg/hr|0.16 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = 125 mg"], [3, "12.5 mcg/mL = 12.5 mg/L"], [3, "D<sub>L</sub> = 12.5 mg/L × 10 L = <b>125 mg</b>"], [4, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:20 mg/hr|0.16 hr<sup>−1</sup> × 10 L}} = 12.5 mg/L ✓"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it: she asks for the loading dose and the infusion rate together and accepts either route, joined with or. A loading dose is a dose, so the answer is in milligrams; she said, it's a dose, so we're looking for milligrams. In her review a C s s of twenty four milligrams per liter and a V D of about twenty two liters give five hundred twenty eight milligrams. In her in-class set the stem's five hundred milligram loading dose is deliberately not the right one; the right one is thirty six point one milligrams per liter times twelve liters, four hundred thirty three milligrams. The trap is R over k with a rate that was never matched to the target.", "s": null, "d": "How she tests it: she asks for the loading dose and the infusion rate together and accepts either route, joined with or. A loading dose is a dose, so the answer is in milligrams; she said, it's a dose, so we're looking for milligrams. In her review a C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> of twenty four milligrams per liter and a V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> of about twenty two liters give five hundred twenty eight milligrams. In her in-class set the stem's five hundred milligram loading dose is deliberately not the right one; the right one is thirty six point one milligrams per liter times twelve liters, four hundred thirty three milligrams. The trap is R over k with a rate that was never matched to the target.", "c": [[0, "D<sub>L</sub> and R together; either route"], [1, "D<sub>L</sub>: mg, not mg/L"], [2, "D<sub>L</sub> = 24 mg/L × 22 L = <b>528 mg</b>"], [3, "stem: 500 mg, not the right dose"], [3, "D<sub>L</sub> = 36.1 mg/L × 12 L = <b>433 mg</b>"], [4, "wrong: {{frac:R|k}} with an R not matched to the target"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m3-5": {"title": "The purpose of a loading dose", "segs": [{"t": "Module three, objective five. Why a loading dose is given. An infusion alone takes three to five half-lives to approach C s s, its steady state concentration, so the patient sits below the target for that time. A loading dose, D L, is an I V bolus that puts in at once the amount of drug the infusion would take that long to build up. So C p, the plasma concentration, reaches C s s almost immediately, and the infusion, at its rate R, then only has to replace what is eliminated each hour. She said, the loading dose helps us to reach that steady state concentration, like almost immediately.", "s": "inf:16", "d": "Module three, objective five. Why a loading dose is given. An infusion alone takes three to five half-lives to approach C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, its steady state concentration, so the patient sits below the target for that time. A loading dose, D L, is an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus that puts in at once the amount of drug the infusion would take that long to build up. So C<sub>p</sub>, the plasma concentration, reaches C<sub>ss</sub> almost immediately, and the infusion, at its rate R, then only has to replace what is eliminated each hour. She said, the loading dose helps us to reach that steady state concentration, like almost immediately.", "c": [[2, "infusion alone: 3 to 5 half-lives below target"], [3, "D<sub>L</sub>: that amount, at once"], [4, "C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub> almost at once; R replaces what leaves"]], "f": {"k": "loading_steps", "at": [[2, 4]]}}, {"t": "The loading dose changes when the target is reached, and nothing else. The plateau is R over clearance, and the dose is not in it, so the level the patient is held at is set by the infusion rate. The half-life depends on the clearance and V D, the apparent volume of distribution, so the loading dose does not change it either. The textbook adds the other side: a loading dose reaches the target quickly but cannot hold it, which needs the right infusion rate, and a higher infusion rate gives a higher plateau, not an earlier arrival at the same one.", "s": "inf:16", "d": "The loading dose changes when the target is reached, and nothing else. The plateau is R over clearance, and the dose is not in it, so the level the patient is held at is set by the infusion rate. The half-life depends on the clearance and V<sub>D</sub>, the apparent volume of distribution, so the loading dose does not change it either. The textbook adds the other side: a loading dose reaches the target quickly but cannot hold it, which needs the right infusion rate, and a higher infusion rate gives a higher plateau, not an earlier arrival at the same one.", "c": [[1, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}}: set by R, not by D<sub>L</sub>"], [2, "t<sub>½</sub>: unchanged by D<sub>L</sub>"], [3, "D<sub>L</sub> reaches the target; R holds it"], [3, "higher R: higher plateau, same time"]], "f": {"k": "loading_steps", "at": [[1, 4]]}}, {"t": "When D L is chosen correctly, the falling bolus curve and the rising infusion curve add to a flat line at C s s; adding the two at the same time is called superposition. Too large a loading dose starts the level above the plateau, and it falls to it. Too small a loading dose starts the level below the plateau, and it rises to it, though sooner than with no loading dose at all. The plateau itself is set by the infusion. She said, you got to choose wisely on the loading dose.", "s": "inf:16", "d": "When D L is chosen correctly, the falling bolus curve and the rising infusion curve add to a flat line at C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>; adding the two at the same time is called superposition. Too large a loading dose starts the level above the plateau, and it falls to it. Too small a loading dose starts the level below the plateau, and it rises to it, though sooner than with no loading dose at all. The plateau itself is set by the infusion. She said, you got to choose wisely on the loading dose.", "c": [[0, "bolus falls + infusion rises = flat at C<sub>ss</sub>"], [0, "superposition: add the two at the same time"], [1, "D<sub>L</sub> too large: starts above, falls to C<sub>ss</sub>"], [2, "D<sub>L</sub> too small: starts below, rises to C<sub>ss</sub>, sooner than with none"], [3, "plateau: set by the infusion"]], "f": {"k": "loading_steps", "at": [[0, 3]]}}, {"t": "C p is the bolus part plus the infusion part: D L over V D times e to the minus k t, plus C s s times one minus e to the minus k t, with k the fraction eliminated per hour. Rearranged, C p equals C s s plus the difference between the starting level, D L over V D, and C s s, times e to the minus k t. If the starting level equals C s s, the difference is zero and C p is C s s at every time. If it is larger, the difference is positive and shrinks by first order, so the level falls to the plateau; if it is smaller, the difference is negative and the level rises to it.", "s": "inf:16", "d": "C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> is the bolus part plus the infusion part: D L over V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> times e to the minus k t, plus C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> times one minus e to the minus k t, with k the fraction eliminated per hour. Rearranged, C<sub>p</sub> equals C<sub>ss</sub> plus the difference between the starting level, D L over V<sub>D</sub>, and C<sub>ss</sub>, times e to the minus k t. If the starting level equals C<sub>ss</sub>, the difference is zero and C<sub>p</sub> is C<sub>ss</sub> at every time. If it is larger, the difference is positive and shrinks by first order, so the level falls to the plateau; if it is smaller, the difference is negative and the level rises to it.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>L</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}}e<sup>−kt</sup> + C<sub>ss</sub>(1 − e<sup>−kt</sup>)"], [1, "C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub> + (C<sub>0</sub> − C<sub>ss</sub>)e<sup>−kt</sup>, C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>L</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}}"], [2, "C<sub>0</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub>: C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub> throughout"], [3, "C<sub>0</sub> &gt; C<sub>ss</sub>: falls to it; C<sub>0</sub> &lt; C<sub>ss</sub>: rises to it"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example eight: a two hundred eighty eight milligram loading dose is given with an infusion of fifty milligrams per hour, the half-life is four hours, V D is twelve liters, and the question asks C p at two and at four hours. The sum of the two parts answers it, and it shows whether this dose holds the plateau. k comes from the half-life: zero point one seven three two five per hour. The bolus part starts at twenty four milligrams per liter and has fallen to sixteen point nine seven by two hours. The plateau of the infusion is twenty four point zero five milligrams per liter, and by two hours the infusion part has reached seven point zero four.", "s": "inf:18", "d": "Her slide Example eight: a two hundred eighty eight milligram loading dose is given with an infusion of fifty milligrams per hour, the half-life is four hours, V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> is twelve liters, and the question asks C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> at two and at four hours. The sum of the two parts answers it, and it shows whether this dose holds the plateau. k comes from the half-life: zero point one seven three two five per hour. The bolus part starts at twenty four milligrams per liter and has fallen to sixteen point nine seven by two hours. The plateau of the infusion is twenty four point zero five milligrams per liter, and by two hours the infusion part has reached seven point zero four.", "c": [[0, "D<sub>L</sub> = 288 mg, R = 50 mg/hr, t<sub>½</sub> = 4 hr, V<sub>D</sub> = 12 L"], [0, "C<sub>p</sub> at 2 and 4 hr = ?"], [2, "k = {{frac:0.693|4 hr}} = 0.17325 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [3, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:288 mg|12 L}} = 24 mg/L"], [3, "e<sup>−(0.17325)(2)</sup> = 0.7072"], [3, "24 mg/L × 0.7072 = 16.97 mg/L"], [4, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:50 mg/hr|0.17325 hr<sup>−1</sup> × 12 L}} = 24.05 mg/L"], [4, "24.05 mg/L × (1 − 0.7072) = 24.05 × 0.2928 = 7.04 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "In Example eight, bolus part plus infusion part is twenty four milligrams per liter at two hours, the same as the plateau. Four hours is one half-life, so the bolus part is half of twenty four and the infusion part half of the plateau, and the sum is again twenty four. It stays flat because the dose matches the amount held at steady state: R over k is two hundred eighty eight point six milligrams. She said, We want the loading dose to look like the amount of drug that's in the body at steady state. In class she reworked it with three hundred eighty eight milligrams: the level started above the plateau of twenty four and was about thirty milligrams per liter at two hours, still falling toward it.", "s": "inf:18", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub>(2 hr) = 16.97 + 7.04 = 24.01 ≈ <b>24 mg/L</b>"], [1, "4 hr = 1 half-life: 12.00 + 12.02 = <b>24 mg/L</b>"], [2, "{{frac:R|k}} = {{frac:50 mg/hr|0.17325 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = 288.6 mg ≈ 288 mg ✓"], [4, "388 mg: C<sub>0</sub> = 32.3 mg/L; 2 hr: 24.05 + (32.33 − 24.05)(0.7072) = 29.9 ≈ 30 mg/L, falling to 24"]], "f": {"k": "loading_miss_steps", "at": [[0, 1], [4, 2]]}}, {"t": "Her graded quiz asked, Why is a loading dose used? The keyed answer is to immediately obtain a therapeutic plasma concentration; reducing adverse effects, improving adherence and reducing the half-life are wrong, because a loading dose changes when the target is reached, not the half-life and not the plateau. As a one sentence answer: the loading dose puts in at once the amount that would otherwise take three to five half-lives to accumulate. A renal failure part asks how long a new steady state takes; it is counted in the new, longer half-lives, four point three two of them for ninety five percent in her Practice three. Objective four gives the two routes to the dose itself.", "s": null, "c": [[1, "✓ to immediately obtain a therapeutic C<sub>p</sub>"], [1, "wrong: adverse effects, adherence, a shorter t<sub>½</sub>"], [2, "D<sub>L</sub>: at once, what takes 3 to 5 half-lives"], [3, "renal failure: 95% at 4.32 of the new, longer half-lives"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m3-6": {"title": "Choosing an infusion rate", "segs": [{"t": "Module three, objective six. Choosing the infusion rate that gives a desired steady state concentration. The infusion rate, R, is the amount of drug put in per hour; C s s is the plateau concentration, where the rate in equals the rate out; and the clearance is the volume of plasma emptied of drug per hour. The question is which R puts the plateau at a target level.", "s": "inf:4", "d": "Module three, objective six. Choosing the infusion rate that gives a desired steady state concentration. The infusion rate, R, is the amount of drug put in per hour; C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> is the plateau concentration, where the rate in equals the rate out; and the clearance is the volume of plasma emptied of drug per hour. The question is which R puts the plateau at a target level.", "c": [[2, "R: mg/hr"], [2, "C<sub>ss</sub>: the plateau, mg/L"], [2, "Cl: L/hr"], [3, "target C<sub>ss</sub> → R = ?"]]}, {"t": "At the plateau, the amount eliminated each hour is the clearance times the level, and it equals the amount going in. So the rate needed is the amount the patient eliminates each hour at the target level: R equals C s s times clearance. A higher target needs a proportionally higher rate, because at a higher level more drug leaves each hour. A patient with a lower clearance, as in renal failure, needs a proportionally lower rate for the same target, because less drug leaves each hour at that level. Once the clearance is known, V D, the apparent volume of distribution, adds nothing to this choice: the volume on its own decides the loading dose, not the rate.", "s": "inf:4", "d": "At the plateau, the amount eliminated each hour is the clearance times the level, and it equals the amount going in. So the rate needed is the amount the patient eliminates each hour at the target level: R equals C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> times clearance. A higher target needs a proportionally higher rate, because at a higher level more drug leaves each hour. A patient with a lower clearance, as in renal failure, needs a proportionally lower rate for the same target, because less drug leaves each hour at that level. Once the clearance is known, V<sub>D</sub>, the apparent volume of distribution, adds nothing to this choice: the volume on its own decides the loading dose, not the rate.", "c": [[0, "steady state: R = Cl × C<sub>ss</sub>"], [1, "R = C<sub>ss</sub> × Cl"], [2, "target doubled → R doubled"], [3, "Cl falls → R falls in proportion"], [3, "her Practice 4: Cl 11.55 → 8.55 L/hr, R 115.5 → 85.5 mg/hr"], [4, "V<sub>D</sub>: sets the loading dose, not R"]], "f": {"k": "infusion_steps", "at": [[0, 2]]}}, {"t": "Changing R moves the plateau and nothing else. Raising R raises C s s in proportion, but the plateau still arrives at the same time, because that time depends only on the half-life. She said, All we're doing is shifting that curve upward. She also said, changing the rate changes our steady state concentration, and said she repeated it five times on purpose. In her review, fifty milligrams per hour gave about twelve milligrams per liter and one hundred gave twenty four, each reached in three to five half-lives.", "s": "inf:4", "d": "Changing R moves the plateau and nothing else. Raising R raises C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> in proportion, but the plateau still arrives at the same time, because that time depends only on the half-life. She said, All we're doing is shifting that curve upward. She also said, changing the rate changes our steady state concentration, and said she repeated it five times on purpose. In her review, fifty milligrams per hour gave about twelve milligrams per liter and one hundred gave twenty four, each reached in three to five half-lives.", "c": [[1, "R raised → C<sub>ss</sub> raised, same time to the plateau"], [4, "her review: 50 → 100 mg/hr, C<sub>ss</sub> 12 → 24 mg/L, both in 3 to 5 half-lives"]]}, {"t": "The equation sheet gives C s s equals R over clearance; rearranged, R equals C s s times clearance. C s s is on the top, so the rate rises with the target, and the clearance is on the top, so the rate rises with the clearance. When the stem gives a half-life and a volume, the clearance is k, the fraction eliminated per hour, times V D, so R equals C s s times k times V D; V D appears only as part of the clearance. Milligrams per liter times liters per hour leaves milligrams per hour, an amount per time. If the stem gives a solution concentration, R is converted to milliliters per hour or per minute for the pump.", "s": "inf:4", "d": "The equation sheet gives C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> equals R over clearance; rearranged, R equals C<sub>ss</sub> times clearance. C<sub>ss</sub> is on the top, so the rate rises with the target, and the clearance is on the top, so the rate rises with the clearance. When the stem gives a half-life and a volume, the clearance is k, the fraction eliminated per hour, times V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>, so R equals C<sub>ss</sub> times k times V<sub>D</sub>; V<sub>D</sub> appears only as part of the clearance. Milligrams per liter times liters per hour leaves milligrams per hour, an amount per time. If the stem gives a solution concentration, R is converted to milliliters per hour or per minute for the pump.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}}"], [0, "R = C<sub>ss</sub> × Cl"], [1, "R follows C<sub>ss</sub> and Cl"], [2, "R = C<sub>ss</sub> × k × V<sub>D</sub>, with k × V<sub>D</sub> = Cl"], [3, "mg/L × L/hr = mg/hr"], [4, "pump: mL/hr or mL/min"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example two asks the rate that gives a C s s of twenty milligrams per liter for the Example one drug: half-life six hours, V D thirty liters. R equals C s s times clearance, and the stem gives no clearance, so it is built as k times thirty liters. Twenty milligrams per liter times that clearance, three point four six five liters per hour, is sixty nine point three milligrams per hour. As a check, sixty nine point three over the clearance gives back twenty milligrams per liter. In Example one, fifty milligrams per hour gave fourteen point four three milligrams per liter; rate and level are in proportion, so between the two examples both rose by the same factor, one point three eight six.", "s": "inf:8", "d": "Her slide Example two asks the rate that gives a C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> of twenty milligrams per liter for the Example one drug: half-life six hours, V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> thirty liters. R equals C<sub>ss</sub> times clearance, and the stem gives no clearance, so it is built as k times thirty liters. Twenty milligrams per liter times that clearance, three point four six five liters per hour, is sixty nine point three milligrams per hour. As a check, sixty nine point three over the clearance gives back twenty milligrams per liter. In Example one, fifty milligrams per hour gave fourteen point four three milligrams per liter; rate and level are in proportion, so between the two examples both rose by the same factor, one point three eight six.", "c": [[0, "t<sub>½</sub> = 6 hr, V<sub>D</sub> = 30 L; target C<sub>ss</sub> = 20 mg/L: R = ?"], [1, "k = {{frac:0.693|6 hr}} = 0.1155 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [1, "Cl = 0.1155 hr<sup>−1</sup> × 30 L = 3.465 L/hr"], [2, "R = 20 mg/L × 3.465 L/hr = <b>69.3 mg/hr</b>"], [3, "{{frac:69.3 mg/hr|3.465 L/hr}} = 20 mg/L ✓"], [4, "{{frac:69.3|50}} = {{frac:20|14.43}} = 1.386"]], "f": {"k": "two_rates_steps", "at": [[0, 1], [2, 2]]}}, {"t": "In her review the target is twenty four milligrams per liter, the half-life five hours and V D about twenty two liters. k is zero point one three eight six per hour, so R is twenty four times k times twenty two, seventy three point one eight milligrams per hour, which she gives as about seventy three. Her graded quiz asked for a rate to reach twenty three micrograms per milliliter with a V D of fifteen liters and a four hour half-life; micrograms per milliliter equal milligrams per liter, and the key is sixty milligrams per hour. The word recommend means round to a number a pump could take. The loading dose that goes with this rate, C s s times V D, is objective four.", "s": null, "d": "In her review the target is twenty four milligrams per liter, the half-life five hours and V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> about twenty two liters. k is zero point one three eight six per hour, so R is twenty four times k times twenty two, seventy three point one eight milligrams per hour, which she gives as about seventy three. Her graded quiz asked for a rate to reach twenty three micrograms per milliliter with a V<sub>D</sub> of fifteen liters and a four hour half-life; micrograms per milliliter equal milligrams per liter, and the key is sixty milligrams per hour. The word recommend means round to a number a pump could take. The loading dose that goes with this rate, C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> times V<sub>D</sub>, is objective four.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>ss</sub> = 24 mg/L, t<sub>½</sub> = 5 hr, V<sub>D</sub> ≈ 22 L"], [1, "k = {{frac:0.693|5 hr}} = 0.1386 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [1, "R = 24 × 0.1386 × 22 = <b>73.18 mg/hr</b> ≈ 73 mg/hr"], [2, "quiz: 23 mg/L × {{frac:0.693|4 hr}} × 15 L = 59.77 → <b>60 mg/hr</b>"], [3, "recommend → a number a pump could take"], [4, "→ D<sub>L</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub> × V<sub>D</sub> (obj. 4)"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it: a target C s s and two of the parameters, the half-life or k, and V D or the clearance. With a solution concentration in the stem, a parallel part asks the rate in volume: zero point three five milliliters per minute in her Practice one, and one point three two milliliters per hour from one hundred twenty five milligram per milliliter vials in Practice two. The distractor she flags is that a different rate reaches steady state sooner. It does not, because the time depends only on the half-life, and the half-life depends on the clearance and V D, not on the rate. While working Example two aloud she called the thirty liters the clearance; thirty liters is V D.", "s": null, "d": "How she tests it: a target C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> and two of the parameters, the half-life or k, and V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> or the clearance. With a solution concentration in the stem, a parallel part asks the rate in volume: zero point three five milliliters per minute in her Practice one, and one point three two milliliters per hour from one hundred twenty five milligram per milliliter vials in Practice two. The distractor she flags is that a different rate reaches steady state sooner. It does not, because the time depends only on the half-life, and the half-life depends on the clearance and V<sub>D</sub>, not on the rate. While working Example two aloud she called the thirty liters the clearance; thirty liters is V<sub>D</sub>.", "c": [[0, "target C<sub>ss</sub> + two parameters → R"], [1, "volume rate: 0.35 mL/min; 1.32 mL/hr from 125 mg/mL"], [2, "wrong: a new R reaches steady state sooner"], [3, "time to steady state: t<sub>½</sub> only"], [4, "30 L = V<sub>D</sub>, not Cl"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m3-7": {"title": "Plasma concentration at any time", "segs": [{"t": "Module three, objective seven. The plasma concentration, C p, at any time during or after an infusion. While the infusion runs, drug comes in at a constant rate, R, and C p rises toward C s s, the steady state concentration. Once it stops, which is called cessation, nothing comes in and C p falls by first order elimination: a fixed fraction, k, is lost each hour. The level at the moment the infusion stops, C peak, joins the two phases: it is the end of the rise and the start of the fall.", "s": "inf:9", "d": "Module three, objective seven. The plasma concentration, C<sub>p</sub>, at any time during or after an infusion. While the infusion runs, drug comes in at a constant rate, R, and C<sub>p</sub> rises toward C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, the steady state concentration. Once it stops, which is called cessation, nothing comes in and C<sub>p</sub> falls by first order elimination: a fixed fraction, k, is lost each hour. The level at the moment the infusion stops, C peak, joins the two phases: it is the end of the rise and the start of the fall.", "c": [[2, "during: C<sub>p</sub> rises toward C<sub>ss</sub>"], [3, "after cessation: first order fall"], [4, "C<sub>peak</sub>: the end of the rise, the start of the fall"]], "f": {"k": "infusion_steps", "at": [[2, 1], [3, 3]]}}, {"t": "How high C peak is depends on how long the infusion ran, counted in half-lives. One half-life of infusion reaches half of C s s, two reach seventy five percent, and only after three to five half-lives is C peak close to C s s. After cessation the fraction lost each hour is k whatever the starting level, so on a log plot the fall has the same slope whether the infusion stopped at steady state or before it. A higher C peak loses more milligrams per liter each hour, but the same fraction.", "s": "inf:12", "d": "How high C peak is depends on how long the infusion ran, counted in half-lives. One half-life of infusion reaches half of C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, two reach seventy five percent, and only after three to five half-lives is C peak close to C<sub>ss</sub>. After cessation the fraction lost each hour is k whatever the starting level, so on a log plot the fall has the same slope whether the infusion stopped at steady state or before it. A higher C peak loses more milligrams per liter each hour, but the same fraction.", "c": [[1, "infusion length 1 half-life → 50%; 2 → 75%; 3 to 5 → close to C<sub>ss</sub>"], [2, "after cessation: same k, same slope, from any C<sub>peak</sub>"], [3, "higher C<sub>peak</sub>: larger fall per hour, same fraction"]], "f": {"k": "infusion_steps", "at": [[1, 2], [2, 3]]}}, {"t": "During the infusion, C p equals R over clearance, times one minus e to the minus k t, with t counted from the start of the infusion. Putting t equal to the infusion length gives C peak. After cessation, C p equals C peak times e to the minus k t, with t now counted from cessation. C peak plays the part of C zero, the starting concentration, in the I V bolus equation, and on a plot of the natural log of C p against time the fall is a straight line. She said, Think of that C peak as your C zero as your starting point.", "s": "inf:12", "d": "During the infusion, C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> equals R over clearance, times one minus e to the minus k t, with t counted from the start of the infusion. Putting t equal to the infusion length gives C peak. After cessation, C<sub>p</sub> equals C peak times e to the minus k t, with t now counted from cessation. C peak plays the part of C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small>, the starting concentration, in the IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus equation, and on a plot of the natural log of C<sub>p</sub> against time the fall is a straight line. She said, Think of that C peak as your C<sub>0</sub> as your starting point.", "c": [[0, "during: C<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}}(1 − e<sup>−kt</sup>), t from the start"], [1, "t = infusion length → C<sub>peak</sub>"], [2, "after: C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>peak</sub>e<sup>−kt</sup>, t from cessation"], [3, "C<sub>peak</sub> plays C<sub>0</sub>; ln C<sub>p</sub> against time: straight, slope −k"]]}, {"t": "After cessation, the key decision is C peak, the level the fall starts from. If the infusion ran to steady state, C peak is C s s; if it ran for a stated shorter time, C peak must first be computed from the rising equation. Two adjacent parts of one practice set often differ only in this, so the infusion length in each part decides the starting point. When a time is a whole number of half-lives, halves can be counted instead of evaluating the exponential, and she shows both routes.", "s": "inf:12", "d": "After cessation, the key decision is C peak, the level the fall starts from. If the infusion ran to steady state, C peak is C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>; if it ran for a stated shorter time, C peak must first be computed from the rising equation. Two adjacent parts of one practice set often differ only in this, so the infusion length in each part decides the starting point. When a time is a whole number of half-lives, halves can be counted instead of evaluating the exponential, and she shows both routes.", "c": [[1, "ran to steady state: C<sub>peak</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub>"], [1, "shorter: C<sub>peak</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub>(1 − e<sup>−kt</sup>)"], [3, "whole half-lives: count halves"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example six: one hundred fifty milligrams is infused over six hours, k is zero point two three one per hour, V D, the apparent volume of distribution, is fifteen liters, and the question asks C p three hours after cessation. The fall needs a starting point, so the first question is whether six hours reached steady state. The rate is twenty five milligrams per hour, the clearance three point four six five liters per hour, and C s s seven point two one five milligrams per liter. The half-life is three hours, so six hours of infusion is only two half-lives, which reach seventy five percent of C s s.", "s": "inf:14", "d": "Her slide Example six: one hundred fifty milligrams is infused over six hours, k is zero point two three one per hour, V<sub>D</sub>, the apparent volume of distribution, is fifteen liters, and the question asks C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> three hours after cessation. The fall needs a starting point, so the first question is whether six hours reached steady state. The rate is twenty five milligrams per hour, the clearance three point four six five liters per hour, and C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> seven point two one five milligrams per liter. The half-life is three hours, so six hours of infusion is only two half-lives, which reach seventy five percent of C<sub>ss</sub>.", "c": [[0, "150 mg over 6 hr; k = 0.231 hr<sup>−1</sup>; V<sub>D</sub> = 15 L"], [0, "C<sub>p</sub> 3 hr after cessation = ?"], [2, "R = {{frac:150 mg|6 hr}} = 25 mg/hr"], [2, "Cl = 0.231 hr<sup>−1</sup> × 15 L = 3.465 L/hr"], [2, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:25 mg/hr|3.465 L/hr}} = 7.215 mg/L"], [3, "t<sub>½</sub> = {{frac:0.693|0.231 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = 3 hr; 6 hr = 2 half-lives: not at steady state"]]}, {"t": "In her Example six, C peak is C s s, seven point two one five milligrams per liter, times the fraction reached at six hours: five point four one milligrams per liter, seventy five percent of C s s. Three hours after cessation is one more half-life, so the level halves to two point seven one milligrams per liter, her stated two point seven. Assuming steady state instead would start the fall at seven point two one five and give about three point six milligrams per liter, which is wrong because six hours is only two half-lives.", "s": "inf:14", "d": "In her Example six, C peak is C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, seven point two one five milligrams per liter, times the fraction reached at six hours: five point four one milligrams per liter, seventy five percent of C<sub>ss</sub>. Three hours after cessation is one more half-life, so the level halves to two point seven one milligrams per liter, her stated two point seven. Assuming steady state instead would start the fall at seven point two one five and give about three point six milligrams per liter, which is wrong because six hours is only two half-lives.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>peak</sub> = 7.215 mg/L × (1 − e<sup>−(0.231)(6)</sup>) = 7.215 × 0.7499 = 5.41 mg/L"], [1, "C<sub>p</sub> = 5.41 mg/L × e<sup>−(0.231)(3)</sup> = 5.41 × 0.5 = 2.71 mg/L"], [1, "<b>C<sub>p</sub> ≈ 2.7 mg/L</b>"], [2, "wrong: from C<sub>ss</sub>, 7.215 × 0.5 = 3.61 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example five starts from steady state: an infusion that reached fifteen milligrams per liter, a five hour half-life, and C p twelve hours after cessation. Here C peak is C s s, fifteen milligrams per liter, and k is zero point one three eight six per hour. Fifteen times e to the minus k times twelve is two point eight four milligrams per liter. In class she changed twelve hours to ten, exactly two half-lives, and took a quarter of fifteen, three point seven five milligrams per liter; that written answer belongs to ten hours, and for the twelve hours asked the answer is two point eight four.", "s": "inf:13", "d": "Her slide Example five starts from steady state: an infusion that reached fifteen milligrams per liter, a five hour half-life, and C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> twelve hours after cessation. Here C peak is C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, fifteen milligrams per liter, and k is zero point one three eight six per hour. Fifteen times e to the minus k times twelve is two point eight four milligrams per liter. In class she changed twelve hours to ten, exactly two half-lives, and took a quarter of fifteen, three point seven five milligrams per liter; that written answer belongs to ten hours, and for the twelve hours asked the answer is two point eight four.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>ss</sub> = 15 mg/L, t<sub>½</sub> = 5 hr; C<sub>p</sub> 12 hr after cessation = ?"], [1, "C<sub>peak</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub> = 15 mg/L; k = {{frac:0.693|5 hr}} = 0.1386 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> = 15 mg/L × e<sup>−(0.1386)(12)</sup> = 15 × 0.1895 = <b>2.84 mg/L</b>"], [3, "10 hr = 2 half-lives: 0.25 × 15 mg/L = 3.75 mg/L"], [3, "12 hr, as asked: 2.84 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it: her stems give a number of hours after the cessation of the infusion and say whether a loading dose was given. In her review an infusion aimed at twenty four milligrams per liter stopped after five hours, with a five hour half-life. A student started the fall from eighteen milligrams per liter; one half-life of infusion reaches fifty percent of C s s, not seventy five, so C peak is twelve milligrams per liter, and three hours later the level is seven point nine milligrams per liter. The two equations count time differently: t in the rising one counts from the start, and t in the falling one counts from cessation.", "s": null, "d": "How she tests it: her stems give a number of hours after the cessation of the infusion and say whether a loading dose was given. In her review an infusion aimed at twenty four milligrams per liter stopped after five hours, with a five hour half-life. A student started the fall from eighteen milligrams per liter; one half-life of infusion reaches fifty percent of C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, not seventy five, so C peak is twelve milligrams per liter, and three hours later the level is seven point nine milligrams per liter. The two equations count time differently: t in the rising one counts from the start, and t in the falling one counts from cessation.", "c": [[1, "C<sub>ss</sub> = 24 mg/L; stopped at 5 hr; k = 0.1386 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [2, "1 half-life → 50%: C<sub>peak</sub> = 12 mg/L, not 18"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> = 12 mg/L × e<sup>−(0.1386)(3)</sup> = <b>7.9 mg/L</b>"], [3, "rising: t from the start; falling: t from cessation"]]}, {"t": "Her later review chained one more case. A two hour infusion of fifty milligrams per hour, with a three hour half-life and a V D of eighteen liters, ends at four point four five milligrams per liter. A second, identical infusion starts six hours after the first began, and the question asks the level four hours after it stops, twelve hours from the start. The first infusion's level falls from the moment it ends, at two hours. The second climbs exactly as the first did, on top of what is left of the first. At twelve hours the first has been falling for ten hours and the second for four, and the two levels add; her answer was two point two milligrams per liter.", "s": null, "d": "Her later review chained one more case. A two hour infusion of fifty milligrams per hour, with a three hour half-life and a V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> of eighteen liters, ends at four point four five milligrams per liter. A second, identical infusion starts six hours after the first began, and the question asks the level four hours after it stops, twelve hours from the start. The first infusion's level falls from the moment it ends, at two hours. The second climbs exactly as the first did, on top of what is left of the first. At twelve hours the first has been falling for ten hours and the second for four, and the two levels add; her answer was two point two milligrams per liter.", "c": [[1, "her 10/19 review: 50 mg/hr for 2 hr, t<sub>½</sub> 3 hr, V<sub>D</sub> 18 L"], [1, "k = {{frac:0.693|3 hr}} = 0.231 hr<sup>−1</sup>; C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:50 mg/hr|0.231 hr<sup>−1</sup> × 18 L}} = 12.02 mg/L"], [1, "end of infusion: 12.02 mg/L × (1 − e<sup>−0.231 × 2</sup>) = <b>4.45 mg/L</b>"], [2, "second infusion 6 to 8 hr; read at 12 hr, 4 hr after it stops"], [3, "first: 4.45 mg/L at 2 hr, then falls"], [4, "second: 4.45 mg/L at 8 hr"], [5, "4.45 e<sup>−0.231 × 10</sup> + 4.45 e<sup>−0.231 × 4</sup> = 0.44 + 1.77 ≈ <b>2.2 mg/L</b>"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m4-1": {"title": "Renal and hepatic routes of elimination", "segs": [{"t": "Module four, objective one. The main routes by which drugs leave the body, renal and hepatic. Elimination is the irreversible removal of drug from the body, by every route at once. Drug that moves into tissue and later comes back has only been distributed, so distribution is not elimination. There are two ways out. Excretion removes the drug intact, with no chemical change. Biotransformation, also called drug metabolism, converts the drug chemically into a metabolite.", "s": "cl:3", "c": [[2, "elimination: irreversible removal, all routes"], [3, "distribution: drug comes back, not eliminated"], [5, "excretion: drug removed intact, mainly by the kidney"], [6, "biotransformation (metabolism): drug → metabolite, mainly in the liver"]]}, {"t": "In general, which route a drug takes depends on how well it dissolves in water. The textbook explains that water-soluble drugs are eliminated more rapidly through the kidney, which excretes them into the urine. Many lipid-soluble drugs are metabolized in the liver into more polar, water-soluble metabolites, which the kidney or the bile then excretes. So biotransformation most often produces a compound the body can eliminate more readily than the parent drug. The kidney and the liver are the two major elimination organs: the kidney excretes drug, and the liver biotransforms it.", "s": "cl:3", "c": [[1, "water-soluble drug → kidney → urine"], [2, "lipid-soluble drug → liver → water-soluble metabolite → urine or bile"], [3, "metabolite: eliminated more readily than the parent drug"], [4, "kidney: excretion &nbsp; liver: biotransformation"]]}, {"t": "Each route has its own clearance, the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time. Each organ's elimination rate is its clearance times the same plasma concentration, so when the two rates are added, the total body clearance is the sum of the two clearances. Cl T equals Cl R plus Cl H: renal clearance plus hepatic clearance, also called metabolic clearance. A Cl written with no subscript means total body clearance. So when one clearance falls, the total falls by a smaller fraction than that clearance: in a textbook example, renal clearance is ten and nonrenal clearance five liters per hour, and when renal function halves, renal clearance falls to five, so the total falls from fifteen to ten liters per hour, by one third, not one half.", "s": "cl:8", "d": "Each route has its own clearance, the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time. Each organ's elimination rate is its clearance times the same plasma concentration, so when the two rates are added, the total body clearance is the sum of the two clearances. Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small> equals Cl<sub>R</sub> plus Cl H: renal clearance plus hepatic clearance, also called metabolic clearance. A Cl written with no subscript means total body clearance. So when one clearance falls, the total falls by a smaller fraction than that clearance: in a textbook example, renal clearance is ten and nonrenal clearance five liters per hour, and when renal function halves, renal clearance falls to five, so the total falls from fifteen to ten liters per hour, by one third, not one half.", "c": [[0, "clearance: volume of plasma cleared of drug per time, e.g. L/hr"], [1, "rate<sub>kidney</sub> + rate<sub>liver</sub> = (Cl<sub>R</sub> + Cl<sub>H</sub>) × C<sub>p</sub>"], [2, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = Cl<sub>R</sub> + Cl<sub>H</sub>"], [3, "Cl with no subscript = Cl<sub>T</sub>"], [4, "one route falls → Cl<sub>T</sub> falls by that route's share only"], [4, "e.g. (textbook) Cl<sub>R</sub> 10 → 5 L/hr, nonrenal 5 L/hr: Cl<sub>T</sub> 15 → <b>10 L/hr</b>, down ⅓"]]}, {"t": "Hepatic clearance is found by difference. The rate and extent of metabolism can rarely be measured directly, and the liver is not sampled, so hepatic clearance is what is left once renal clearance is taken from the total: Cl H equals Cl T minus Cl R. A second form uses f e, the fraction of the dose recovered unchanged in the urine, which has no units. f e is the kidney's share of the clearance, so one minus f e is the share that is not renal. Cl H equals one minus f e, times Cl T. The larger f e is, the smaller the hepatic part, and the two forms give the same answer.", "s": "cl:11", "d": "Hepatic clearance is found by difference. The rate and extent of metabolism can rarely be measured directly, and the liver is not sampled, so hepatic clearance is what is left once renal clearance is taken from the total: Cl H equals Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small> minus Cl<sub>R</sub>. A second form uses f<sub>e</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(fraction excreted unchanged)</small>, the fraction of the dose recovered unchanged in the urine, which has no units. f<sub>e</sub> is the kidney's share of the clearance, so one minus f<sub>e</sub> is the share that is not renal. Cl H equals one minus f<sub>e</sub>, times Cl<sub>T</sub>. The larger f<sub>e</sub> is, the smaller the hepatic part, and the two forms give the same answer.", "c": [[1, "Cl<sub>H</sub> = Cl<sub>T</sub> − Cl<sub>R</sub>"], [2, "f<sub>e</sub>: fraction excreted unchanged, no units"], [3, "1 − f<sub>e</sub>: the share that is not renal"], [4, "Cl<sub>H</sub> = (1 − f<sub>e</sub>) × Cl<sub>T</sub>"], [5, "f<sub>e</sub> up → Cl<sub>H</sub> down, at the same Cl<sub>T</sub>"]]}, {"t": "In the worked example, a drug's total body clearance is one point three liters per hour, and six tenths of the dose is excreted unchanged in the urine. Hepatic clearance is asked, and since f e and the total are both given, the line Cl H equals one minus f e, times Cl T, answers it. One minus f e is zero point four, and zero point four times one point three liters per hour is zero point five two liters per hour. As a check, renal clearance is zero point seven eight liters per hour, and one point three minus zero point seven eight gives the same zero point five two. So the liver accounts for four tenths of this drug's clearance, and the kidney for six tenths.", "s": "cl:11", "d": "In the worked example, a drug's total body clearance is one point three liters per hour, and six tenths of the dose is excreted unchanged in the urine. Hepatic clearance is asked, and since f<sub>e</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(fraction excreted unchanged)</small> and the total are both given, the line Cl H equals one minus f<sub>e</sub>, times Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small>, answers it. One minus f<sub>e</sub> is zero point four, and zero point four times one point three liters per hour is zero point five two liters per hour. As a check, renal clearance is zero point seven eight liters per hour, and one point three minus zero point seven eight gives the same zero point five two. So the liver accounts for four tenths of this drug's clearance, and the kidney for six tenths.", "c": [[0, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = 1.3 L/hr, &nbsp; f<sub>e</sub> = 0.6"], [1, "1 − f<sub>e</sub> = 1 − 0.6 = 0.4"], [2, "Cl<sub>H</sub> = (0.4)(1.3 L/hr) = <b>0.52 L/hr</b>"], [3, "Cl<sub>R</sub> = (0.6)(1.3 L/hr) = 0.78 L/hr"], [3, "1.3 L/hr − 0.78 L/hr = 0.52 L/hr ✓"], [4, "liver 0.4, kidney 0.6 of Cl<sub>T</sub>"]]}, {"t": "In her quizzes the routes are asked as vocabulary. Quiz three asked which term describes the process by which drug is chemically converted in the body, and the key is biotransformation, not distribution, excretion or absorption. Two features decide such items: whether the removal is irreversible, which distribution is not, and whether the drug is chemically changed or removed intact. A usual trap swaps the organs, putting excretion in the liver and biotransformation in the kidney. Her review poll stated that hepatic clearance is most readily approximated with creatinine clearance, and the key is false: creatinine clearance estimates the patient's kidney function, and hepatic clearance is total minus renal. Objective five calculates the renal and hepatic parts of a total clearance.", "s": "cl:25", "c": [[1, "Quiz 3: chemically converted → <b>biotransformation</b>"], [2, "irreversible? &nbsp; changed or intact?"], [3, "✗ excretion in the liver, biotransformation in the kidney"], [4, "✗ Cl<sub>H</sub> ≈ CrCl: <b>false</b>; Cl<sub>H</sub> = Cl<sub>T</sub> − Cl<sub>R</sub>"], [5, "objective 5: Cl<sub>R</sub> = f<sub>e</sub> × Cl<sub>T</sub>, Cl<sub>H</sub> = the rest"]]}], "mins": 4}, "gobj-m4-2": {"title": "Filtration, secretion and reabsorption", "segs": [{"t": "Module four, objective two. Glomerular filtration, tubular secretion and tubular reabsorption, the three processes by which the kidney handles a drug. In the nephron, drug passes from the blood across the glomerulus into the tubule, and the tubular lumen is the fluid space inside that tubule. The amount of drug that ends up in the urine is the net result of three processes: filtration and secretion add drug to the lumen, and reabsorption moves drug from the lumen back into the blood.", "s": "cl:14", "c": [[2, "blood → glomerulus → tubule (lumen)"], [3, "filtration, secretion → add drug to the lumen"], [3, "reabsorption → lumen back to the blood"]]}, {"t": "Glomerular filtration is passive diffusion across the glomerulus, so it needs no energy. The glomerular filtration rate, G F R, is the volume of fluid the glomeruli filter from the blood per unit time, and in normal kidneys it averages one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. Only drug that is not bound to plasma protein is filtered, so the textbook notes that a heavily bound drug is filtered less and has a longer half-life. Active tubular secretion uses a transporter and energy to move drug from the blood into the urine, so it removes drug on top of filtration. Tubular reabsorption moves some drug from the urine in the tubule back into the blood, so it takes away from what was removed.", "s": "cl:15", "d": "Glomerular filtration is passive diffusion across the glomerulus, so it needs no energy. The glomerular filtration rate, GFR, is the volume of fluid the glomeruli filter from the blood per unit time, and in normal kidneys it averages one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. Only drug that is not bound to plasma protein is filtered, so the textbook notes that a heavily bound drug is filtered less and has a longer half-life. Active tubular secretion uses a transporter and energy to move drug from the blood into the urine, so it removes drug on top of filtration. Tubular reabsorption moves some drug from the urine in the tubule back into the blood, so it takes away from what was removed.", "c": [[0, "filtration: passive, no energy"], [1, "GFR: average 120 mL/min, a volume per time"], [2, "only unbound drug is filtered: more binding → less filtered"], [3, "secretion: active, transporter → adds"], [4, "reabsorption: urine → blood → subtracts"]]}, {"t": "Renal clearance, Cl R, the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time by the kidney, is a volume per time like G F R, so the two can be compared. A drug that is only filtered is cleared at about the filtration rate, one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. If a transporter also secretes it, more plasma is cleared of drug than filtration alone can clear, so renal clearance rises above one hundred twenty. If some filtered drug returns to the blood, less is cleared, so renal clearance falls below one hundred twenty. Cl R equals filtration plus secretion minus reabsorption, in milliliters per minute, so the two added terms raise it and the subtracted term lowers it.", "s": "cl:20", "d": "Renal clearance, Cl<sub>R</sub>, the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time by the kidney, is a volume per time like GFR <small class=\"gwgl\">(glomerular filtration rate)</small>, so the two can be compared. A drug that is only filtered is cleared at about the filtration rate, one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. If a transporter also secretes it, more plasma is cleared of drug than filtration alone can clear, so renal clearance rises above one hundred twenty. If some filtered drug returns to the blood, less is cleared, so renal clearance falls below one hundred twenty. Cl<sub>R</sub> equals filtration plus secretion minus reabsorption, in milliliters per minute, so the two added terms raise it and the subtracted term lowers it.", "c": [[0, "Cl<sub>R</sub> and GFR: both volumes per time, mL/min"], [1, "filtration only: Cl<sub>R</sub> ≈ 120 mL/min"], [2, "+ secretion: Cl<sub>R</sub> &gt; 120 mL/min"], [3, "+ reabsorption: Cl<sub>R</sub> &lt; 120 mL/min"], [4, "Cl<sub>R</sub> = filtration + secretion − reabsorption"], [4, "a reasoning rule, not on the equation sheet"]]}, {"t": "Stated as a rule, reabsorption occurs if the renal clearance is less than the clearance by filtration. She allows a margin around the filtration value of one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. She said, I'm saying one hundred twenty. If it's one hundred nineteen, one hundred twenty one let's call that filtration. If it's two hundred fifty, then let's assume that we've got some active secretion going on. A renal clearance given in liters per hour has to be put in the same units before the comparison: one hundred twenty milliliters per minute is seven point two liters per hour.", "s": "cl:21", "c": [[0, "Cl<sub>R</sub> &lt; filtration clearance → reabsorption"], [3, "119 or 121 → filtration"], [4, "250 → secretion; her example 350 mL/min → secretion"], [5, "120 mL/min = 7.2 L/hr"]]}, {"t": "In her Module six practice set, a drug has a total body clearance of fifty two point nine five and a renal clearance of thirty four point seven two, both in milliliters per minute per seventy kilograms, and the probable mechanism of renal clearance is asked. The mechanism is read from renal clearance alone, so the total is set aside, and the renal clearance is already in milliliters per minute. A renal clearance of thirty four point seven two milliliters per minute is well below the filtration rate of one hundred twenty milliliters per minute, so the kidney clears less plasma of this drug than filtration alone would. Some filtered drug must be returning to the blood, and the mechanism is glomerular filtration with tubular reabsorption.", "s": "cl:21", "c": [[0, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = 52.95, &nbsp; Cl<sub>R</sub> = 34.72 mL/min per 70 kg"], [1, "use Cl<sub>R</sub> only; mL/min, no conversion"], [2, "34.72 mL/min &lt; 120 mL/min"], [3, "<b>filtration with reabsorption</b>"]]}, {"t": "Her review poll gave a renal clearance of forty five milliliters per minute for an antibiotic and asked what can be inferred. The key is that the drug is filtered and reabsorbed, because forty five is below the filtration rate. The option some evidence of renal impairment is the trap: a drug's renal clearance, read against the average one hundred twenty milliliters per minute, describes how the kidney handles that drug, while the patient's kidney function is judged from creatinine clearance, in objective three. For the same reason, a value below one hundred twenty does not mean reduced filtration. A second trap compares a renal clearance in liters per hour with one hundred twenty milliliters per minute without converting. Objective four explains what controls how much filtered drug is reabsorbed.", "s": "cl:25", "c": [[0, "poll: Cl<sub>R</sub> = 45 mL/min"], [1, "<b>filtered and reabsorbed</b> (45 &lt; 120 mL/min)"], [2, "✗ renal impairment: Cl<sub>R</sub> vs 120 mL/min describes the drug; the patient's kidneys → CrCl (objective 3)"], [3, "✗ below 120 read as reduced filtration"], [4, "✗ L/hr compared with mL/min"], [5, "objective 4: urine pH and pK<sub>a</sub> → reabsorption"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m4-3": {"title": "Creatinine clearance and its significance", "segs": [{"t": "Module four, objective three. Calculating creatinine clearance and what it means. The glomerular filtration rate, G F R, the volume of fluid the kidneys filter from the blood per unit time, shows how well a patient's kidneys are working. It is measured with a substance that is only filtered, neither secreted nor reabsorbed. Inulin is almost completely filtered, so it is the better marker, but it is not native to the body: it has to be given. Creatinine comes from the breakdown of muscle and is always in the blood, so creatinine clearance, C r C l, is the common clinical estimate of G F R. Creatinine is also secreted, which is why C r C l estimates G F R rather than measuring it.", "s": "cl:16", "d": "Module four, objective three. Calculating creatinine clearance and what it means. The glomerular filtration rate, GFR, the volume of fluid the kidneys filter from the blood per unit time, shows how well a patient's kidneys are working. It is measured with a substance that is only filtered, neither secreted nor reabsorbed. Inulin is almost completely filtered, so it is the better marker, but it is not native to the body: it has to be given. Creatinine comes from the breakdown of muscle and is always in the blood, so creatinine clearance, CrCl, is the common clinical estimate of GFR. Creatinine is also secreted, which is why CrCl estimates GFR rather than measuring it.", "c": [[2, "GFR: the patient's renal function, mL/min"], [3, "marker filtered only → its clearance = GFR"], [4, "inulin: better marker, must be given"], [5, "creatinine: from muscle, always present → CrCl"], [6, "also secreted → an estimate of GFR"]]}, {"t": "The creatinine clearance estimate uses serum creatinine, S C r, the creatinine concentration in the blood in milligrams per deciliter, together with age, sex and ideal body weight, I B W. A higher serum creatinine gives a lower estimate, in inverse proportion, so twice the serum creatinine gives half the estimate. An older patient gets a lower estimate. A taller patient has a larger I B W, which raises the estimate. A woman's estimate is multiplied by zero point eight five, which rests on the assumption that women are smaller and less muscular than men. Serum creatinine may not reflect renal function well in people with unusually low or unusually high muscle mass. A normal C r C l is one hundred twenty to one hundred thirty milliliters per minute.", "s": "cl:17", "d": "The creatinine clearance estimate uses serum creatinine, S C r, the creatinine concentration in the blood in milligrams per deciliter, together with age, sex and ideal body weight, IBW. A higher serum creatinine gives a lower estimate, in inverse proportion, so twice the serum creatinine gives half the estimate. An older patient gets a lower estimate. A taller patient has a larger IBW, which raises the estimate. A woman's estimate is multiplied by zero point eight five, which rests on the assumption that women are smaller and less muscular than men. Serum creatinine may not reflect renal function well in people with unusually low or unusually high muscle mass. A normal CrCl <small class=\"gwgl\">(creatinine clearance)</small> is one hundred twenty to one hundred thirty milliliters per minute.", "c": [[0, "inputs: S<sub>Cr</sub> (mg/dL), age (yr), sex, IBW (kg)"], [1, "S<sub>Cr</sub> up → CrCl down; S<sub>Cr</sub> × 2 → CrCl × ½"], [2, "age up → CrCl down"], [3, "taller → IBW up → CrCl up"], [4, "female: × 0.85"], [5, "unusual muscle mass: S<sub>Cr</sub> misleading"], [6, "normal: 120–130 mL/min"]]}, {"t": "The Cockcroft-Gault equation says this: C r C l equals one hundred forty minus age, times I B W, over seventy two times S C r, times zero point eight five if female. Age is subtracted on the top, so each added year makes the top smaller, and serum creatinine sits on the bottom, so a larger value makes the result smaller. I B W multiplies the top, so a larger ideal weight raises the result. The units do not cancel, since the algebra gives kilograms over milligrams per deciliter, but the answer is reported in milliliters per minute. She said, I also want you to recognize that the units of creatinine clearance should be in milliliters per minute.", "s": "cl:17", "d": "The Cockcroft-Gault equation says this: CrCl <small class=\"gwgl\">(creatinine clearance)</small> equals one hundred forty minus age, times IBW <small class=\"gwgl\">(ideal body weight)</small>, over seventy two times S C r, times zero point eight five if female. Age is subtracted on the top, so each added year makes the top smaller, and serum creatinine sits on the bottom, so a larger value makes the result smaller. IBW multiplies the top, so a larger ideal weight raises the result. The units do not cancel, since the algebra gives kilograms over milligrams per deciliter, but the answer is reported in milliliters per minute. She said, I also want you to recognize that the units of creatinine clearance should be in milliliters per minute.", "c": [[0, "CrCl = {{frac:(140 − age) × IBW|72 × S<sub>Cr</sub>}} &nbsp; (× 0.85 if female)"], [1, "age in (140 − age): top smaller &nbsp; S<sub>Cr</sub> on the bottom: result smaller"], [2, "IBW on the top: result larger"], [3, "{{frac:kg|mg/dL}}: units do not cancel; report mL/min"]]}, {"t": "I B W, in kilograms, comes from sex and height, and in this course it is always the weight used. For a male, I B W equals fifty plus two point three times the inches over five feet, and for a female, forty five point five plus two point three times the inches over five feet. The inches over five feet are the height in inches minus sixty. A height in centimeters is divided by two point five four, because one inch is two point five four centimeters, and a decimal height such as sixty four point five inches is read as written. Neither these lines nor Cockcroft-Gault are on the equation sheet. She said, this is an equation that I want you to know, to memorize.", "s": "cl:18", "d": "IBW <small class=\"gwgl\">(ideal body weight)</small>, in kilograms, comes from sex and height, and in this course it is always the weight used. For a male, IBW equals fifty plus two point three times the inches over five feet, and for a female, forty five point five plus two point three times the inches over five feet. The inches over five feet are the height in inches minus sixty. A height in centimeters is divided by two point five four, because one inch is two point five four centimeters, and a decimal height such as sixty four point five inches is read as written. Neither these lines nor Cockcroft-Gault are on the equation sheet. She said, this is an equation that I want you to know, to memorize.", "c": [[0, "IBW always; actual weight not used"], [1, "IBW (male) = 50 + 2.3 × (inches over 5 ft)"], [1, "IBW (female) = 45.5 + 2.3 × (inches over 5 ft)"], [2, "inches over 5 ft = height in inches − 60"], [3, "height in inches = {{frac:height in cm|2.54}}"], [3, "64.5 inches = 64.5 inches"], [5, "not on the sheet: memorize"]]}, {"t": "Her slide example asks for the C r C l, using the ideal body weight, of a forty five year old woman who weighs sixty one kilograms, has a serum creatinine of one point one milligrams per deciliter, and is one hundred sixty five centimeters tall. Cockcroft-Gault answers it, and it needs the I B W first, so the height goes to inches: one hundred sixty five centimeters divided by two point five four is sixty four point nine six inches, which she rounds to sixty five, five inches over five feet. With the female line, forty five point five plus two point three times five, the I B W is fifty seven kilograms, and the sixty one kilogram actual weight is not used.", "s": "cl:19", "d": "Her slide example asks for the CrCl <small class=\"gwgl\">(creatinine clearance)</small>, using the ideal body weight, of a forty five year old woman who weighs sixty one kilograms, has a serum creatinine of one point one milligrams per deciliter, and is one hundred sixty five centimeters tall. Cockcroft-Gault answers it, and it needs the IBW <small class=\"gwgl\">(ideal body weight)</small> first, so the height goes to inches: one hundred sixty five centimeters divided by two point five four is sixty four point nine six inches, which she rounds to sixty five, five inches over five feet. With the female line, forty five point five plus two point three times five, the IBW is fifty seven kilograms, and the sixty one kilogram actual weight is not used.", "c": [[0, "female, 45 yr, 61 kg, S<sub>Cr</sub> = 1.1 mg/dL, 165 cm"], [1, "{{frac:165 cm|2.54}} = 64.96 → 65 inches"], [1, "65 − 60 = 5 inches"], [2, "IBW = 45.5 + 2.3 × 5 = 45.5 + 11.5 = <b>57 kg</b>"], [2, "61 kg: not used"]]}, {"t": "In her slide example, Cockcroft-Gault takes the woman's age, forty five years, I B W, fifty seven kilograms, and serum creatinine, one point one milligrams per deciliter. The top, one hundred forty minus forty five, times fifty seven, is five thousand four hundred fifteen; the bottom, seventy two times one point one, is seventy nine point two. Their ratio is sixty eight point three seven, multiplied by zero point eight five because she is female. Her C r C l is fifty eight point one milliliters per minute. That is about half the normal one hundred twenty to one hundred thirty milliliters per minute, so her renal function is reduced. Working this example in class, a student answered fifty four, and she said that meant something had been done wrong.", "s": "cl:19", "d": "In her slide example, Cockcroft-Gault takes the woman's age, forty five years, IBW <small class=\"gwgl\">(ideal body weight)</small>, fifty seven kilograms, and serum creatinine, one point one milligrams per deciliter. The top, one hundred forty minus forty five, times fifty seven, is five thousand four hundred fifteen; the bottom, seventy two times one point one, is seventy nine point two. Their ratio is sixty eight point three seven, multiplied by zero point eight five because she is female. Her CrCl <small class=\"gwgl\">(creatinine clearance)</small> is fifty eight point one milliliters per minute. That is about half the normal one hundred twenty to one hundred thirty milliliters per minute, so her renal function is reduced. Working this example in class, a student answered fifty four, and she said that meant something had been done wrong.", "c": [[1, "top: (140 − 45) × 57 = 95 × 57 = 5415"], [1, "bottom: 72 × 1.1 = 79.2"], [2, "{{frac:5415|79.2}} = 68.37"], [3, "68.37 × 0.85 = <b>58.1 mL/min</b>"], [4, "58.1 &lt; 120–130 mL/min → reduced renal function"], [5, "54: something done wrong"]]}, {"t": "What a patient's C r C l means for a drug. A low C r C l means a drug eliminated by the kidney is excreted more slowly and may accumulate, so depending on the drug, its dose may need adjusting. She named patients with a C r C l of thirty or sixty milliliters per minute as ones who will have trouble excreting such a drug. In a textbook example, renal function halves, so the drug's renal clearance halves from ten to five liters per hour, while its nonrenal clearance stays at five. Total clearance falls from fifteen to ten liters per hour, so the daily dose is lowered by a third to keep the same exposure.", "s": "cl:17", "d": "What a patient's CrCl <small class=\"gwgl\">(creatinine clearance)</small> means for a drug. A low CrCl means a drug eliminated by the kidney is excreted more slowly and may accumulate, so depending on the drug, its dose may need adjusting. She named patients with a CrCl of thirty or sixty milliliters per minute as ones who will have trouble excreting such a drug. In a textbook example, renal function halves, so the drug's renal clearance halves from ten to five liters per hour, while its nonrenal clearance stays at five. Total clearance falls from fifteen to ten liters per hour, so the daily dose is lowered by a third to keep the same exposure.", "c": [[1, "CrCl low → renally eliminated drug excreted slower → may accumulate → adjust dose"], [2, "e.g. CrCl 30 or 60 mL/min"], [3, "e.g. (textbook) GFR halved: Cl<sub>R</sub> 10 → 5 L/hr, nonrenal 5 L/hr"], [4, "Cl<sub>T</sub> 15 → 10 L/hr: daily dose down ⅓ for the same AUC"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it: a one patient vignette gives age, sex, height, often in centimeters, actual weight and serum creatinine, and the answer is in milliliters per minute. Sex decides two things, the I B W constant, fifty or forty five point five, and whether the zero point eight five applies. The trap she names herself is confusing the patient's creatinine clearance with a drug's renal clearance: C r C l describes the patient's kidneys, while a drug's renal clearance, from objective five, describes one drug and is compared with the average one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. Her review poll also keyed as false the claim that hepatic clearance is most readily approximated with creatinine clearance.", "s": "cl:26", "d": "How she tests it: a one patient vignette gives age, sex, height, often in centimeters, actual weight and serum creatinine, and the answer is in milliliters per minute. Sex decides two things, the IBW <small class=\"gwgl\">(ideal body weight)</small> constant, fifty or forty five point five, and whether the zero point eight five applies. The trap she names herself is confusing the patient's creatinine clearance with a drug's renal clearance: CrCl describes the patient's kidneys, while a drug's renal clearance, from objective five, describes one drug and is compared with the average one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. Her review poll also keyed as false the claim that hepatic clearance is most readily approximated with creatinine clearance.", "c": [[0, "given: age, sex, height (cm), actual weight, S<sub>Cr</sub> → CrCl in mL/min"], [1, "sex: 50 or 45.5; × 0.85 or not"], [2, "CrCl: the patient's kidneys &nbsp; Cl<sub>R</sub>: one drug, compared with 120 mL/min"], [3, "✗ Cl<sub>H</sub> ≈ CrCl: false"]]}], "mins": 7}, "gobj-m4-4": {"title": "Ionization and renal excretion", "segs": [{"t": "Module four, objective four. How the degree of ionization affects the renal excretion of drugs. After drug is filtered into the tubule, some of it can cross back into the blood, which is tubular reabsorption. For weak acids and weak bases, how much crosses back depends on two things: the p H of the urine, the fluid in the renal tubule, and the drug's p K a. The p K a is a property of the drug that, together with the p H, fixes how much of the drug is ionized.", "s": "cl:22", "c": [[2, "tubular reabsorption: tubule → blood"], [3, "reabsorption depends on: urine pH, drug pK<sub>a</sub>"], [4, "pK<sub>a</sub> + pH → how much is ionized"]]}, {"t": "A weak acid or weak base exists in two forms at once, ionized and nonionized. Only the nonionized form is lipid soluble enough to cross membranes and be reabsorbed; the ionized form stays in the urine. The split between the two forms at a given p H is the degree of ionization. A weak acid is more ionized in alkaline urine, and a weak base is more ionized in acidic urine. So in more alkaline urine, more of a weak acid stays ionized in the tubule, less is reabsorbed, and its renal clearance rises. In more acidic urine, more of a weak base stays ionized, less is reabsorbed, and its renal clearance rises. Filtration does not change, because it is passive and does not depend on urine p H.", "s": "cl:22", "c": [[0, "two forms: ionized, nonionized"], [1, "nonionized: crosses, reabsorbed &nbsp; ionized: stays, excreted"], [2, "degree of ionization: the split at a given pH"], [3, "weak acid: more ionized in alkaline urine &nbsp; weak base: more ionized in acidic urine"], [4, "weak acid, urine more alkaline → more ionized → less reabsorbed → Cl<sub>R</sub> up"], [5, "weak base, urine more acidic → more ionized → less reabsorbed → Cl<sub>R</sub> up"], [6, "filtration: unchanged by urine pH"]]}, {"t": "For a weak acid, p H equals p K a plus the log of ionized over nonionized; for a weak base, the ratio is nonionized over ionized. When the urine p H equals the p K a, the log is zero, so ionized and nonionized drug are present in equal amounts. For a weak acid, each unit the p H rises above the p K a multiplies the ratio of ionized to nonionized drug by ten. For a weak base the ratio is turned over, so each unit the p H falls below the p K a multiplies the ratio of ionized to nonionized drug by ten. Neither line is on the equation sheet.", "s": "cl:22", "c": [[0, "weak acid: pH = pK<sub>a</sub> + log {{frac:ionized|nonionized}}"], [0, "weak base: pH = pK<sub>a</sub> + log {{frac:nonionized|ionized}}"], [1, "pH = pK<sub>a</sub>: log = 0, ionized = nonionized"], [2, "e.g. weak acid, pK<sub>a</sub> 5: pH 5 → 1 : 1, pH 6 → 10 : 1, pH 7 → 100 : 1 (ionized : nonionized)"], [3, "e.g. weak base, pK<sub>a</sub> 9: pH 8 → 10 : 1, pH 7 → 100 : 1 (ionized : nonionized)"], [4, "ratio of amounts: no units; not on the sheet"]]}, {"t": "Weak acids have p K a values of three to eight, and weak bases seven point five to ten point five. The ranges overlap between seven point five and eight, so a p K a alone does not always settle whether a drug is treated as an acid or a base. A drug's p K a is fixed, so in one patient it is the urine p H that changes the split between ionized and nonionized drug.", "s": "cl:22", "c": [[0, "pK<sub>a</sub>: weak acids 3–8, weak bases 7.5–10.5"], [1, "overlap: 7.5–8"], [2, "pK<sub>a</sub> fixed → urine pH changes the split"]]}, {"t": "The two factors that govern reabsorption are the urine p H and the drug's p K a. A trap option names plasma p H or the filtration rate, but it is the p H of the fluid in the tubule that matters, and filtration does not depend on it. Another trap swaps the p K a ranges, putting weak bases at three to eight. A third mixes up the forms: the nonionized form is reabsorbed, and the ionized form is excreted. Reabsorption of the nonionized form is one reason why, in objective two, a drug's renal clearance can fall below G F R: it returns part of the filtered drug to the blood.", "s": "cl:26", "d": "The two factors that govern reabsorption are the urine p H and the drug's p K a. A trap option names plasma p H or the filtration rate, but it is the p H of the fluid in the tubule that matters, and filtration does not depend on it. Another trap swaps the p K a ranges, putting weak bases at three to eight. A third mixes up the forms: the nonionized form is reabsorbed, and the ionized form is excreted. Reabsorption of the nonionized form is one reason why, in objective two, a drug's renal clearance can fall below GFR <small class=\"gwgl\">(glomerular filtration rate)</small>: it returns part of the filtered drug to the blood.", "c": [[0, "<b>urine pH + drug pK<sub>a</sub></b>"], [1, "✗ plasma pH &nbsp; ✗ filtration rate"], [2, "✗ weak bases at 3–8"], [3, "✗ ionized form reabsorbed"], [4, "objective 2: reabsorption → Cl<sub>R</sub> &lt; GFR"]]}], "mins": 3}, "gobj-m4-5": {"title": "Total, renal and hepatic clearance", "segs": [{"t": "Module four, objective five. Calculating total, renal and hepatic clearance. Clearance, Cl, is the volume of fluid cleared of drug per unit time, so its units are a volume per time, such as liters per hour or milliliters per minute. The textbook gives an example: with a clearance of fifteen milliliters per minute and a volume of distribution, V D, of twelve liters, fifteen milliliters of the twelve liters are cleared of drug each minute.", "s": "cl:4", "d": "Module four, objective five. Calculating total, renal and hepatic clearance. Clearance, Cl, is the volume of fluid cleared of drug per unit time, so its units are a volume per time, such as liters per hour or milliliters per minute. The textbook gives an example: with a clearance of fifteen milliliters per minute and a volume of distribution, V<sub>D</sub>, of twelve liters, fifteen milliliters of the twelve liters are cleared of drug each minute.", "c": [[2, "Cl: volume cleared of drug per time, L/hr or mL/min"], [3, "e.g. (textbook) Cl 15 mL/min, V<sub>D</sub> 12 L: 15 mL of the 12 L cleared each minute"]]}, {"t": "Clearance links the rate of elimination, an amount per time, to C p, the plasma concentration, an amount per volume: the rate equals clearance times C p, and the volumes cancel. In her penicillin example, fifteen milliliters per minute times five micrograms per milliliter gives seventy five micrograms per minute. As C p falls after a dose, the amount removed per minute falls with it, because the same volume is cleared each minute but it holds less drug. The clearance, the volume per minute, stays the same as long as elimination is first order. So the claim that clearance rises as concentration rises is false: a change in concentration changes the elimination rate, not the clearance.", "s": "cl:5", "d": "Clearance links the rate of elimination, an amount per time, to C<sub>p</sub>, the plasma concentration, an amount per volume: the rate equals clearance times C<sub>p</sub>, and the volumes cancel. In her penicillin example, fifteen milliliters per minute times five micrograms per milliliter gives seventy five micrograms per minute. As C<sub>p</sub> falls after a dose, the amount removed per minute falls with it, because the same volume is cleared each minute but it holds less drug. The clearance, the volume per minute, stays the same as long as elimination is first order. So the claim that clearance rises as concentration rises is false: a change in concentration changes the elimination rate, not the clearance.", "c": [[0, "rate of elimination = Cl × C<sub>p</sub>"], [1, "(15 mL/min)(5 mcg/mL) = <b>75 mcg/min</b>"], [1, "= (0.9 L/hr)(5 mg/L) = 4.5 mg/hr"], [2, "e.g. C<sub>p</sub> 5 → 2.5 mcg/mL: rate 75 → 37.5 mcg/min"], [3, "Cl constant (first order)"], [4, "✗ Cl rises as C<sub>p</sub> rises"]]}, {"t": "Total clearance comes from either of two relations. Cl T equals F times D zero over A U C, where F is the bioavailability factor, one for an I V dose, and A U C is the area under the plasma concentration time curve. The dose is on the top and A U C on the bottom, so for the same dose, a larger clearance goes with a smaller A U C, because the body removes the drug faster. Cl T also equals k times V D, the elimination rate constant times the volume of distribution. k is the fraction of the drug removed per hour, so k times the volume that holds the drug is the volume cleared per hour.", "s": "cl:7", "d": "Total clearance comes from either of two relations. Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small> equals F times D<sub>0</sub> over AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small>, where F is the bioavailability factor, one for an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> dose, and AUC is the area under the plasma concentration time curve. The dose is on the top and AUC on the bottom, so for the same dose, a larger clearance goes with a smaller AUC, because the body removes the drug faster. Cl<sub>T</sub> also equals k times V<sub>D</sub>, the elimination rate constant times the volume of distribution. k is the fraction of the drug removed per hour, so k times the volume that holds the drug is the volume cleared per hour.", "c": [[1, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = {{frac:F × D<sub>0</sub>|AUC}}"], [1, "{{frac:mg|mg·hr/L}} = L/hr &nbsp; (F = 1 for IV)"], [2, "same D<sub>0</sub>: Cl up → AUC down"], [3, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = k × V<sub>D</sub>, &nbsp; (hr<sup>−1</sup>)(L) = L/hr"], [4, "fraction per hour × volume = volume cleared per hour"], [4, "k × V<sub>D</sub>: not on the sheet, from memory"]]}, {"t": "Collecting urine until drug no longer appears gives the cumulative amount excreted unchanged, D u infinity. Divided by the dose that reached the body, F times D zero, it gives f e, the fraction excreted unchanged, which has no units. f e is also the kidney's share of the elimination rate constant: it equals k e over k, where k e is the excretion rate constant, so k e equals f e times k. The kidney's share of the clearance is the same fraction, so Cl R equals f e times Cl T, and hepatic clearance, Cl H, is the rest: one minus f e, times Cl T, or Cl T minus Cl R. She said, lowercase f e is our fraction excreted, capital F is our bioavailability factor.", "s": "cl:10", "d": "Collecting urine until drug no longer appears gives the cumulative amount excreted unchanged, D u infinity. Divided by the dose that reached the body, F times D<sub>0</sub>, it gives f<sub>e</sub>, the fraction excreted unchanged, which has no units. f<sub>e</sub> is also the kidney's share of the elimination rate constant: it equals k e over k, where k e is the excretion rate constant, so k e equals f<sub>e</sub> times k. The kidney's share of the clearance is the same fraction, so Cl<sub>R</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(renal clearance)</small> equals f<sub>e</sub> times Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small>, and hepatic clearance, Cl H, is the rest: one minus f<sub>e</sub>, times Cl<sub>T</sub>, or Cl<sub>T</sub> minus Cl<sub>R</sub>. She said, lowercase f<sub>e</sub> is our fraction excreted, capital F is our bioavailability factor.", "c": [[0, "D<sub>u</sub><sup>∞</sup>: total unchanged drug in the urine, mg"], [1, "f<sub>e</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>u</sub><sup>∞</sup>|F × D<sub>0</sub>}}"], [2, "f<sub>e</sub> = {{frac:k<sub>e</sub>|k}}"], [2, "k<sub>e</sub> = f<sub>e</sub> × k"], [3, "Cl<sub>R</sub> = f<sub>e</sub> × Cl<sub>T</sub>"], [3, "Cl<sub>H</sub> = (1 − f<sub>e</sub>) × Cl<sub>T</sub> = Cl<sub>T</sub> − Cl<sub>R</sub>"], [4, "f<sub>e</sub>: fraction excreted &nbsp; F: bioavailability"]]}, {"t": "Her slide example: five hundred milligrams of a drug was given by rapid I V injection, the V D is fifteen liters, and the elimination half-life is eight hours. Urine was collected for forty eight hours, and three hundred milligrams of unchanged drug was recovered. Asked are f e, k, k e, Cl T, Cl R and Cl H, and f e comes first because k e, Cl R and Cl H all use it. F is one for an I V dose, so f e is three hundred over five hundred milligrams, zero point six, with no units. The forty eight hours are not in the arithmetic, but forty eight hours is six half-lives, long enough that the amount collected counts as all the drug the kidney will excrete.", "s": "cl:12", "d": "Her slide example: five hundred milligrams of a drug was given by rapid IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> injection, the V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> is fifteen liters, and the elimination half-life is eight hours. Urine was collected for forty eight hours, and three hundred milligrams of unchanged drug was recovered. Asked are f<sub>e</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(fraction excreted unchanged)</small>, k, k e, Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small>, Cl<sub>R</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(renal clearance)</small> and Cl H, and f<sub>e</sub> comes first because k e, Cl<sub>R</sub> and Cl H all use it. F is one for an IV dose, so f<sub>e</sub> is three hundred over five hundred milligrams, zero point six, with no units. The forty eight hours are not in the arithmetic, but forty eight hours is six half-lives, long enough that the amount collected counts as all the drug the kidney will excrete.", "c": [[0, "D<sub>0</sub> = 500 mg IV (F = 1), V<sub>D</sub> = 15 L, t½ = 8 hr"], [1, "D<sub>u</sub><sup>∞</sup> = 300 mg in 48 hr"], [3, "f<sub>e</sub> = {{frac:300 mg|500 mg}} = <b>0.6</b>, no units"], [4, "48 hr = 6 × 8 hr: the amount counts as the total excreted (textbook: at least 3 to 4 half-lives)"]]}, {"t": "Next, k comes from the half-life, which equals zero point six nine three over k, so k is zero point six nine three over eight hours, zero point zero eight six six per hour. k e, the part of k due to the kidney, is f e times k, zero point zero five two per hour. Cl T is k times the fifteen liter V D, one point three liters per hour. Cl R is f e times Cl T, zero point seven eight liters per hour, and Cl H, by difference, is zero point five two liters per hour. So of the one point three liters of plasma cleared of drug each hour, the kidney clears six tenths and the liver four tenths.", "s": "cl:12", "d": "Next, k comes from the half-life, which equals zero point six nine three over k, so k is zero point six nine three over eight hours, zero point zero eight six six per hour. k e, the part of k due to the kidney, is f<sub>e</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(fraction excreted unchanged)</small> times k, zero point zero five two per hour. Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small> is k times the fifteen liter V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>, one point three liters per hour. Cl<sub>R</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(renal clearance)</small> is f<sub>e</sub> times Cl<sub>T</sub>, zero point seven eight liters per hour, and Cl H, by difference, is zero point five two liters per hour. So of the one point three liters of plasma cleared of drug each hour, the kidney clears six tenths and the liver four tenths.", "c": [[0, "k = {{frac:0.693|8 hr}} = <b>0.0866 hr<sup>−1</sup></b>"], [1, "k<sub>e</sub> = (0.6)(0.0866 hr<sup>−1</sup>) = <b>0.052 hr<sup>−1</sup></b>"], [2, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = (0.0866 hr<sup>−1</sup>)(15 L) = <b>1.3 L/hr</b>"], [3, "Cl<sub>R</sub> = (0.6)(1.3 L/hr) = <b>0.78 L/hr</b>"], [3, "Cl<sub>H</sub> = 1.3 L/hr − 0.78 L/hr = <b>0.52 L/hr</b>"], [4, "kidney 0.78 + liver 0.52 = 1.3 L/hr"]]}, {"t": "Clearance, half-life and volume are linked. Clearance is the volume cleared per hour and V D the volume holding the drug, so their ratio, k, is the fraction removed per hour. Putting this into the half-life equals zero point six nine three over k gives the half-life equals zero point six nine three times V D over Cl T. Cl T is on the bottom, so with the volume unchanged, a lower clearance gives a longer half-life in inverse proportion, and a higher clearance gives a shorter one. V D is on the top, so at the same clearance a larger volume gives a longer half-life; in a textbook example, twice the volume gives twice the half-life. V D and Cl T must share a volume unit.", "s": "cl:23", "d": "Clearance, half-life and volume are linked. Clearance is the volume cleared per hour and V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> the volume holding the drug, so their ratio, k, is the fraction removed per hour. Putting this into the half-life equals zero point six nine three over k gives the half-life equals zero point six nine three times V<sub>D</sub> over Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small>. Cl<sub>T</sub> is on the bottom, so with the volume unchanged, a lower clearance gives a longer half-life in inverse proportion, and a higher clearance gives a shorter one. V<sub>D</sub> is on the top, so at the same clearance a larger volume gives a longer half-life; in a textbook example, twice the volume gives twice the half-life. V<sub>D</sub> and Cl<sub>T</sub> must share a volume unit.", "c": [[1, "k = {{frac:Cl<sub>T</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}}"], [2, "t½ = {{frac:0.693|k}} = {{frac:0.693 × V<sub>D</sub>|Cl<sub>T</sub>}}"], [3, "V<sub>D</sub> same: Cl<sub>T</sub> down → t½ up; Cl<sub>T</sub> up → t½ down"], [4, "e.g. (textbook) Cl 125 mL/min both: V<sub>D</sub> 10 L → 55.44 min, 20 L → 110.88 min"], [5, "same volume unit; not on the sheet"]]}, {"t": "Her slide example: a new antibiotic, actively secreted by the kidney, has a V D of twenty five liters and a clearance of seven hundred fifty milliliters per minute, falling to one hundred fifty milliliters per minute in partial renal failure. The slide asks the usual half-life and the half-life in partial renal failure. The line, half-life equals zero point six nine three times V D over Cl T, answers both, because V D stays at twenty five liters while clearance changes. The usual half-life is twenty three point one minutes, and in renal failure it is one hundred fifteen point five minutes. Clearance fell to one fifth, so the half-life became five times longer.", "s": "cl:24", "d": "Her slide example: a new antibiotic, actively secreted by the kidney, has a V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> of twenty five liters and a clearance of seven hundred fifty milliliters per minute, falling to one hundred fifty milliliters per minute in partial renal failure. The slide asks the usual half-life and the half-life in partial renal failure. The line, half-life equals zero point six nine three times V<sub>D</sub> over Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small>, answers both, because V<sub>D</sub> stays at twenty five liters while clearance changes. The usual half-life is twenty three point one minutes, and in renal failure it is one hundred fifteen point five minutes. Clearance fell to one fifth, so the half-life became five times longer.", "c": [[0, "V<sub>D</sub> = 25 L; Cl<sub>T</sub> 750 → 150 mL/min"], [0, "750 mL/min = 0.75 L/min"], [3, "t½ = {{frac:0.693 × 25 L|0.75 L/min}} = <b>23.1 min</b>"], [3, "t½ = {{frac:0.693 × 25 L|0.15 L/min}} = <b>115.5 min</b>"], [4, "Cl<sub>T</sub> × ⅕ → t½ × 5"]]}, {"t": "Quiz three asked what happens to the elimination half-life if total body clearance increases due to renal dysfunction. The key is that the half-life decreases. Answer from the direction of clearance that the stem states, not from the cause it names: clearance up, half-life down, whatever the reason. Other traps are giving f e units, and putting the urinary fraction in place of capital F, which is one on an I V problem whatever the urine shows.", "s": "cl:25", "d": "Quiz three asked what happens to the elimination half-life if total body clearance increases due to renal dysfunction. The key is that the half-life decreases. Answer from the direction of clearance that the stem states, not from the cause it names: clearance up, half-life down, whatever the reason. Other traps are giving f<sub>e</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(fraction excreted unchanged)</small> units, and putting the urinary fraction in place of capital F, which is one on an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> problem whatever the urine shows.", "c": [[0, "Quiz 3: Cl<sub>T</sub> increases (stem: renal dysfunction) → t½ ?"], [1, "<b>t½ decreases</b>"], [2, "Cl<sub>T</sub> up → t½ down, whatever the cause"], [3, "✗ units on f<sub>e</sub> &nbsp; ✗ f<sub>e</sub> in place of F"]]}], "mins": 7}, "gobj-m5-1": {"title": "Kinetics after an oral dose", "segs": [{"t": "Module five, objective one. The kinetics of a drug given outside the blood vessels. Extravascular means given outside the blood vessels; in this course that means by mouth, so the drug must be absorbed before it reaches the blood. She said, first order means that the rate depends on how much is there. An I V bolus puts all the drug in at once, an I V infusion puts it in at a constant, zero order rate, and an oral dose goes in by a first order process: first order in, first order out.", "s": "oral:4", "d": "Module five, objective one. The kinetics of a drug given outside the blood vessels. Extravascular means given outside the blood vessels; in this course that means by mouth, so the drug must be absorbed before it reaches the blood. She said, first order means that the rate depends on how much is there. An IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus puts all the drug in at once, an IV infusion puts it in at a constant, zero order rate, and an oral dose goes in by a first order process: first order in, first order out.", "c": [[2, "extravascular: oral, absorbed first"], [3, "first order: the rate depends on the amount present"], [4, "IV bolus: all at once &nbsp; IV infusion: zero order in"], [4, "oral: first order in, first order out"]]}, {"t": "Three amounts follow the dose: D G I in the gut, D B in the body, and D E already eliminated. The amount absorbed per hour is k a, the absorption rate constant, times the amount in the gut, so as the gut empties, the rate in falls. The amount eliminated per hour is k, the elimination rate constant, times the amount in the body, so the rate out rises as the body fills. The change in the amount in the body is the rate in minus the rate out: d D B over d t equals d D G I over d t minus d D E over d t. The rate constants, fractions per hour, stay the same, while the rates, amounts per hour, change with the amounts.", "s": "oral:7", "d": "Three amounts follow the dose: D G I in the gut, D B in the body, and D E already eliminated. The amount absorbed per hour is k<sub>a</sub>, the absorption rate constant, times the amount in the gut, so as the gut empties, the rate in falls. The amount eliminated per hour is k, the elimination rate constant, times the amount in the body, so the rate out rises as the body fills. The change in the amount in the body is the rate in minus the rate out: d D B over d t equals d D G I over d t minus d D E over d t. The rate constants, fractions per hour, stay the same, while the rates, amounts per hour, change with the amounts.", "c": [[0, "D<sub>GI</sub> → D<sub>B</sub> → D<sub>E</sub> (mg)"], [1, "rate in = k<sub>a</sub> × D<sub>GI</sub>: falls as the gut empties"], [2, "rate out = k × D<sub>B</sub>: rises as D<sub>B</sub> rises"], [3, "{{frac:dD<sub>B</sub>|dt}} = {{frac:dD<sub>GI</sub>|dt}} − {{frac:dD<sub>E</sub>|dt}} &nbsp; (rate in − rate out)"], [4, "rate (mg/hr) changes; rate constant (hr<sup>−1</sup>) does not"]], "f": {"k": "oral_steps", "at": [[1, 1]]}}, {"t": "Because the drug enters gradually while it is also being eliminated, the plasma curve starts at zero, rises to a peak and then falls. In the absorption phase, the rising part, the rate in is larger than the rate out, though both processes are going on. She said, hear me when I say both processes are going on. At the peak, where the level reaches C max at the time t max, the rate in equals the rate out, so for that moment the amount in the body does not change. Just after the peak, in the post absorption phase, some drug is still being absorbed, but the rate out is now larger. Once the gut is empty, only elimination is left, and that is the elimination phase.", "s": "oral:8", "d": "Because the drug enters gradually while it is also being eliminated, the plasma curve starts at zero, rises to a peak and then falls. In the absorption phase, the rising part, the rate in is larger than the rate out, though both processes are going on. She said, hear me when I say both processes are going on. At the peak, where the level reaches C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small> at the time t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small>, the rate in equals the rate out, so for that moment the amount in the body does not change. Just after the peak, in the post absorption phase, some drug is still being absorbed, but the rate out is now larger. Once the gut is empty, only elimination is left, and that is the elimination phase.", "c": [[0, "starts at zero → peak → falls"], [1, "absorption phase: rate in &gt; rate out, both going on"], [3, "peak (C<sub>max</sub> at t<sub>max</sub>): rate in = rate out"], [4, "post-absorption phase: rate out &gt; rate in, still absorbing"], [5, "elimination phase: rate out only"]], "f": {"k": "oral_steps", "at": [[1, 1], [3, 2], [4, 3]]}}, {"t": "Before it reaches the body, the tablet has to disintegrate, dissolve, cross membranes and pass through the liver, and k a combines all of those steps in one first order constant, per hour. Usually only part of the dose arrives, because some of it is eliminated before it becomes available, and the fraction that arrives is F, the bioavailability, between zero and one with no units. For an I V dose F is taken as one. F and k a are independent: the textbook notes that a higher k a does not mean more drug is absorbed, only that it is absorbed faster. She said, when you see a capital F, You should think oral.", "s": "oral:7", "d": "Before it reaches the body, the tablet has to disintegrate, dissolve, cross membranes and pass through the liver, and k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> combines all of those steps in one first order constant, per hour. Usually only part of the dose arrives, because some of it is eliminated before it becomes available, and the fraction that arrives is F, the bioavailability, between zero and one with no units. For an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> dose F is taken as one. F and k<sub>a</sub> are independent: the textbook notes that a higher k<sub>a</sub> does not mean more drug is absorbed, only that it is absorbed faster. She said, when you see a capital F, You should think oral.", "c": [[0, "k<sub>a</sub>: all absorption steps in one constant, hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [1, "F: fraction that arrives, 0 to 1"], [2, "IV: F = 1"], [3, "k<sub>a</sub> up: faster, not more (F unchanged)"], [4, "capital F → oral"]]}, {"t": "The absorption half-life is the time it takes for half of the drug still in the gut to be absorbed. It equals zero point six nine three over k a, so a larger k a gives a shorter absorption half-life. Stems usually give the absorption half-life, so it is turned into k a before any oral equation is used, and this line is not on the equation sheet. The textbook adds that absorption is generally complete after five to seven absorption half-lives.", "s": "oral:6", "d": "The absorption half-life is the time it takes for half of the drug still in the gut to be absorbed. It equals zero point six nine three over k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small>, so a larger k<sub>a</sub> gives a shorter absorption half-life. Stems usually give the absorption half-life, so it is turned into k<sub>a</sub> before any oral equation is used, and this line is not on the equation sheet. The textbook adds that absorption is generally complete after five to seven absorption half-lives.", "c": [[1, "t½<sub>a</sub> = {{frac:0.693|k<sub>a</sub>}}"], [1, "k<sub>a</sub> up → t½<sub>a</sub> down"], [2, "given t½<sub>a</sub> → k<sub>a</sub> first; not on the sheet"], [3, "absorption complete: 5 to 7 × t½<sub>a</sub>"]]}, {"t": "She shows a curve and asks for the route. A curve that starts at zero, peaks and falls is oral; an I V bolus curve starts at its highest point; an I V infusion curve rises from zero to a plateau. She said, instantaneous in, first order out is our bolus, first order in, first order out is our oral. Oral input is usually first order, not always: a modified release product that releases drug at a constant rate gives a curve shaped like an infusion. Quiz three asked what is true at C max, and the key is that the rate of absorption equals the rate of elimination; the option that drug at the absorption site has become depleted describes the elimination phase.", "s": "oral:22", "d": "She shows a curve and asks for the route. A curve that starts at zero, peaks and falls is oral; an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus curve starts at its highest point; an IV infusion curve rises from zero to a plateau. She said, instantaneous in, first order out is our bolus, first order in, first order out is our oral. Oral input is usually first order, not always: a modified release product that releases drug at a constant rate gives a curve shaped like an infusion. Quiz three asked what is true at C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small>, and the key is that the rate of absorption equals the rate of elimination; the option that drug at the absorption site has become depleted describes the elimination phase.", "c": [[1, "peak → oral"], [1, "highest at the start → IV bolus &nbsp; plateau → IV infusion"], [3, "modified release, zero order in → infusion shape"], [4, "Quiz 3: C<sub>max</sub> → <b>rate of absorption = rate of elimination</b>"], [4, "✗ absorption site depleted: elimination phase"]]}], "mins": 4}, "gobj-m5-2": {"title": "Plasma concentration after a single oral dose", "segs": [{"t": "Module five, objective two. The plasma concentration after a single oral dose. C p is the plasma concentration of drug at a time t after the dose, in milligrams per liter. One equation gives it at any time from five inputs: F, the fraction of the dose absorbed; the dose, D zero; V D, the volume of distribution; k a, the absorption rate constant; and k, the elimination rate constant. It applies to a single oral dose in one compartment, with first order absorption and first order elimination.", "s": "oral:10", "d": "Module five, objective two. The plasma concentration after a single oral dose. C<sub>p</sub> is the plasma concentration of drug at a time t after the dose, in milligrams per liter. One equation gives it at any time from five inputs: F, the fraction of the dose absorbed; the dose, D<sub>0</sub>; V<sub>D</sub>, the volume of distribution; k<sub>a</sub>, the absorption rate constant; and k, the elimination rate constant. It applies to a single oral dose in one compartment, with first order absorption and first order elimination.", "c": [[2, "C<sub>p</sub>: plasma concentration at time t, mg/L"], [3, "F: fraction absorbed &nbsp; D<sub>0</sub>: dose, mg &nbsp; V<sub>D</sub>: L"], [3, "k<sub>a</sub>: absorption &nbsp; k: elimination, hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [4, "one dose, one compartment, first order in and out"]]}, {"t": "She describes the single oral dose equation for C p as drug in minus drug out. Inside its bracket are two exponentials that start equal, so at time zero the bracket is zero and the oral curve starts at zero concentration. k a is the larger constant, so the absorption term, e to the minus k a t, shrinks fast, and the elimination term, e to the minus k t, shrinks slowly. Early on the difference between them grows, which is the rise; once the absorption term is nearly gone, the curve follows the elimination term alone, which is the slow fall.", "s": "oral:10", "d": "She describes the single oral dose equation for C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> as drug in minus drug out. Inside its bracket are two exponentials that start equal, so at time zero the bracket is zero and the oral curve starts at zero concentration. k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> is the larger constant, so the absorption term, e to the minus k<sub>a</sub> t, shrinks fast, and the elimination term, e to the minus k t, shrinks slowly. Early on the difference between them grows, which is the rise; once the absorption term is nearly gone, the curve follows the elimination term alone, which is the slow fall.", "c": [[0, "bracket: e<sup>−kt</sup> − e<sup>−k<sub>a</sub>t</sup> = drug in minus drug out"], [1, "t = 0: e<sup>0</sup> − e<sup>0</sup> = 0, so C<sub>p</sub> starts at 0"], [2, "e<sup>−k<sub>a</sub>t</sup>: falls fast &nbsp; e<sup>−kt</sup>: falls slowly"], [3, "rise, then the fall follows k alone"]], "f": {"k": "oral_steps", "at": [[2, 4]]}}, {"t": "The fraction in front combines F, the dose, k a, V D and the difference of the rate constants into one number. It is not C zero, the starting concentration of an I V bolus, because the bracket is always less than one, so no point on the oral curve reaches it. She said, this big portion right here does not represent C zero. F and the dose sit on the top, so doubling the dose doubles C p at every time, and V D sits on the bottom, so a larger volume gives a lower curve. The textbook adds that the timing of the curve, when it rises and falls, is set by k a and k alone.", "s": "oral:10", "d": "The fraction in front combines F, the dose, k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small>, V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> and the difference of the rate constants into one number. It is not C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small>, the starting concentration of an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus, because the bracket is always less than one, so no point on the oral curve reaches it. She said, this big portion right here does not represent C<sub>0</sub>. F and the dose sit on the top, so doubling the dose doubles C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> at every time, and V<sub>D</sub> sits on the bottom, so a larger volume gives a lower curve. The textbook adds that the timing of the curve, when it rises and falls, is set by k<sub>a</sub> and k alone.", "c": [[0, "{{frac:F k<sub>a</sub> D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub> (k<sub>a</sub> − k)}}: one number"], [1, "not C<sub>0</sub>: the bracket is always &lt; 1"], [3, "D<sub>0</sub> × 2 → every C<sub>p</sub> × 2; e.g. her Example 1, 500 → 1000 mg: front number 25.76 → 51.52 mg/L"], [3, "V<sub>D</sub> up → every C<sub>p</sub> down"], [4, "timing: k<sub>a</sub> and k only"]], "f": {"k": "oral_steps", "at": [[1, 4]]}}, {"t": "The equation reads: C p equals F times k a times D zero, over V D times k a minus k, all times e to the minus k t minus e to the minus k a t. C p comes out in milligrams per liter with the dose in milligrams, V D in liters, both rate constants per hour and t in hours since the dose, and the line is on the equation sheet, page one.", "s": "oral:10", "d": "The equation reads: C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> equals F times k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> times D<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(dose)</small>, over V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> times k<sub>a</sub> minus k, all times e to the minus k t minus e to the minus k<sub>a</sub> t. C<sub>p</sub> comes out in milligrams per liter with the dose in milligrams, V<sub>D</sub> in liters, both rate constants per hour and t in hours since the dose, and the line is on the equation sheet, page one.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:F k<sub>a</sub> D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub> (k<sub>a</sub> − k)}} × (e<sup>−kt</sup> − e<sup>−k<sub>a</sub>t</sup>)"], [1, "C<sub>p</sub> mg/L, D<sub>0</sub> mg, V<sub>D</sub> L, k<sub>a</sub> and k hr<sup>−1</sup>, t hr; on the sheet, p. 1"]]}, {"t": "In her problems the single oral dose equation often comes with the fraction in front of the bracket worked out as a leading number, P, in milligrams per liter, and a question asks for a parameter inside that fraction. Her slide Example two gives C p equals seventy five e to the minus zero point two two t, minus seventy five e to the minus two point seven five t. She expects the larger exponent to be read as k a, and the textbook explains why: most oral drugs have shorter absorption half-lives than elimination half-lives. To find V D, P is set equal to the fraction and the line is rearranged: V D equals F times k a times D zero, over P times k a minus k.", "s": "oral:15", "d": "In her problems the single oral dose equation often comes with the fraction in front of the bracket worked out as a leading number, P, in milligrams per liter, and a question asks for a parameter inside that fraction. Her slide Example two gives C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> equals seventy five e to the minus zero point two two t, minus seventy five e to the minus two point seven five t. She expects the larger exponent to be read as k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small>, and the textbook explains why: most oral drugs have shorter absorption half-lives than elimination half-lives. To find V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>, P is set equal to the fraction and the line is rearranged: V<sub>D</sub> equals F times k<sub>a</sub> times D<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(dose)</small>, over P times k<sub>a</sub> minus k.", "c": [[0, "P: the leading number, mg/L"], [1, "C<sub>p</sub> = 75e<sup>−0.22t</sup> − 75e<sup>−2.75t</sup>: P = 75"], [2, "larger exponent = k<sub>a</sub> = 2.75 hr<sup>−1</sup>; smaller = k = 0.22 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [3, "P = {{frac:F k<sub>a</sub> D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub> (k<sub>a</sub> − k)}}"], [3, "V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:F k<sub>a</sub> D<sub>0</sub>|P (k<sub>a</sub> − k)}}"]]}, {"t": "Her in class practice two: a seven hundred fifty milligram oral dose, eighty four percent bioavailable, gives C p equals twenty three point two times, e to the minus zero point one eight two t, minus e to the minus zero point eight seven two t, and V D and the half-life of elimination are asked. The exponents give k a, zero point eight seven two per hour, and k, zero point one eight two per hour. With twenty three point two as P, the leading number, V D equals F times k a times D zero, over P times k a minus k: thirty four point three liters. So each milligram per liter in the plasma corresponds to about thirty four milligrams of drug in the body.", "s": "oral:10", "d": "Her in class practice two: a seven hundred fifty milligram oral dose, eighty four percent bioavailable, gives C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> equals twenty three point two times, e to the minus zero point one eight two t, minus e to the minus zero point eight seven two t, and V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> and the half-life of elimination are asked. The exponents give k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small>, zero point eight seven two per hour, and k, zero point one eight two per hour. With twenty three point two as P, the leading number, V<sub>D</sub> equals F times k<sub>a</sub> times D<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(dose)</small>, over P times k<sub>a</sub> minus k: thirty four point three liters. So each milligram per liter in the plasma corresponds to about thirty four milligrams of drug in the body.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = 23.2(e<sup>−0.182t</sup> − e<sup>−0.872t</sup>) mg/L, D<sub>0</sub> = 750 mg, F = 0.84"], [1, "k<sub>a</sub> = 0.872 hr<sup>−1</sup>, k = 0.182 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [1, "0.872 − 0.182 = 0.690 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [2, "V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:(0.84)(750 mg)(0.872 hr<sup>−1</sup>)|(23.2 mg/L)(0.690 hr<sup>−1</sup>)}} = {{frac:549.36|16.008}} L"], [2, "= 34.32 L → <b>V<sub>D</sub> = 34.3 L</b>"], [3, "amount in body = C<sub>p</sub> × V<sub>D</sub>"]]}, {"t": "The second question in her in class practice two asks the half-life, and with no qualifier that means the elimination half-life, so it uses k, the smaller exponent, zero point one eight two per hour. She said, If it's just T one half, then your assumption is that I'm looking for the half-life of elimination. The half-life equals zero point six nine three over k, a line she says is never on the equation sheet, and here it is three point eight hours.", "s": "oral:15", "c": [[0, "no qualifier: elimination half-life, use k"], [2, "t½ = {{frac:0.693|k}}"], [2, "t½ = {{frac:0.693|0.182 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = <b>3.8 hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "In her slide Example two, with a leading number of seventy five, five hundred milligrams at eighty seven percent bioavailability gives a V D of six point three liters. Quiz three gave thirty times, e to the minus zero point one three three t, minus e to the minus zero point nine three four t, a five hundred milligram dose and eighty two percent bioavailability, and keyed fifteen point nine liters. Dividing the dose by thirty, as if thirty were C zero, gives sixteen point seven liters, close enough to fifteen point nine to look right, and that is the trap. The other traps are leaving out V D and losing the k a minus k term when rearranging.", "s": "oral:15", "d": "In her slide Example two, with a leading number of seventy five, five hundred milligrams at eighty seven percent bioavailability gives a V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> of six point three liters. Quiz three gave thirty times, e to the minus zero point one three three t, minus e to the minus zero point nine three four t, a five hundred milligram dose and eighty two percent bioavailability, and keyed fifteen point nine liters. Dividing the dose by thirty, as if thirty were C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small>, gives sixteen point seven liters, close enough to fifteen point nine to look right, and that is the trap. The other traps are leaving out V<sub>D</sub> and losing the k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> minus k term when rearranging.", "c": [[0, "Example 2: V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:(0.87)(500)(2.75)|(75)(2.53)}} = <b>6.3 L</b>"], [1, "Quiz 3: V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:(0.82)(500)(0.934)|(30)(0.934 − 0.133)}} = <b>15.9 L</b>"], [2, "✗ 500 ÷ 30 = 16.7 (P read as C<sub>0</sub>)"], [3, "✗ V<sub>D</sub> left out &nbsp; ✗ (k<sub>a</sub> − k) lost"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m5-3": {"title": "Peak concentration and time to peak after one oral dose", "segs": [{"t": "Module five, objective three. The peak concentration and the time to peak after a single oral dose. t max is the time after the dose at which the plasma concentration is highest, and C max is that highest concentration. Before the peak, absorption is faster than elimination, so the level rises. At the peak the rate in equals the rate out. After it, elimination is faster, so the level falls.", "s": "oral:12", "d": "Module five, objective three. The peak concentration and the time to peak after a single oral dose. t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> is the time after the dose at which the plasma concentration is highest, and C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small> is that highest concentration. Before the peak, absorption is faster than elimination, so the level rises. At the peak the rate in equals the rate out. After it, elimination is faster, so the level falls.", "c": [[2, "t<sub>max</sub>: time of the peak, hr &nbsp; C<sub>max</sub>: the peak, mg/L"], [3, "before t<sub>max</sub>: rate in &gt; rate out"], [4, "at t<sub>max</sub>: rate in = rate out"], [5, "after t<sub>max</sub>: rate out &gt; rate in"]], "f": {"k": "oral_steps", "at": [[3, 1], [4, 2], [5, 3]]}}, {"t": "The time of the peak depends only on the two rate constants, k a and k. The dose, F and V D multiply every point of the curve by the same number, so they raise or lower the whole curve together and cannot move the time of its peak. She said, t max depends solely on k and k a. A larger k a brings drug in faster, so the peak comes earlier and is higher; in a textbook problem, the formulation with the larger k a reaches its peak about an hour sooner. A larger k also brings the peak earlier, but lower, because drug leaves faster and the level cannot build as high.", "s": "oral:19", "d": "The time of the peak depends only on the two rate constants, k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> and k. The dose, F and V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> multiply every point of the curve by the same number, so they raise or lower the whole curve together and cannot move the time of its peak. She said, t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> depends solely on k and k<sub>a</sub>. A larger k<sub>a</sub> brings drug in faster, so the peak comes earlier and is higher; in a textbook problem, the formulation with the larger k<sub>a</sub> reaches its peak about an hour sooner. A larger k also brings the peak earlier, but lower, because drug leaves faster and the level cannot build as high.", "c": [[0, "t<sub>max</sub>: k<sub>a</sub> and k only"], [1, "D<sub>0</sub>, F, V<sub>D</sub>: scale the whole curve; t<sub>max</sub> unchanged"], [3, "k<sub>a</sub> up → t<sub>max</sub> earlier, C<sub>max</sub> higher"], [3, "e.g. (Ch 16) k 0.3 hr<sup>−1</sup>: k<sub>a</sub> 1 hr<sup>−1</sup> → ≈ 1.7 hr; k<sub>a</sub> 0.45 hr<sup>−1</sup> → ≈ 2.7 hr"], [4, "k up → t<sub>max</sub> earlier, C<sub>max</sub> lower"]]}, {"t": "Because t max depends only on k a and k, swapping their values gives the same t max. In the textbook table, a k a of zero point one with a k of zero point two per hour, and a k a of zero point two with a k of zero point one, both peak at six point nine three hours. C max and A U C differ, though: clearance equals k times V D, so the pair with k at zero point two per hour has twice the clearance and half the A U C, fifty against one hundred microgram hours per milliliter, and its C max is two point five against five micrograms per milliliter.", "s": "oral:12", "d": "Because t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> depends only on k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> and k, swapping their values gives the same t<sub>max</sub>. In the textbook table, a k<sub>a</sub> of zero point one with a k of zero point two per hour, and a k<sub>a</sub> of zero point two with a k of zero point one, both peak at six point nine three hours. C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small> and AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> differ, though: clearance equals k times V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>, so the pair with k at zero point two per hour has twice the clearance and half the AUC, fifty against one hundred microgram hours per milliliter, and its C<sub>max</sub> is two point five against five micrograms per milliliter.", "c": [[0, "swap k<sub>a</sub> and k → same t<sub>max</sub>"], [1, "k<sub>a</sub> 0.1, k 0.2 and k<sub>a</sub> 0.2, k 0.1 hr<sup>−1</sup>: t<sub>max</sub> 6.93 hr both (Ch 16 Table 16-2)"], [2, "C<sub>max</sub> 2.50 vs 5.00 mcg/mL; AUC 50 vs 100 mcg·hr/mL"]]}, {"t": "t max equals the natural log of k a over k, divided by k a minus k. Only rate constants appear, so a dose change cannot move t max, and swapping k a and k turns both the log and the difference negative, so the signs cancel and t max is the same. There is no separate C max line for a single oral dose: t max is put in for t in the C p equation, which is why t max always comes first. She said, If you are asked for C max, and I will ask you for C max, you must find t max first. Both lines need rate constants, not half-lives, in the same time unit.", "s": "oral:12", "d": "t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> equals the natural log of k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> over k, divided by k<sub>a</sub> minus k. Only rate constants appear, so a dose change cannot move t<sub>max</sub>, and swapping k<sub>a</sub> and k turns both the log and the difference negative, so the signs cancel and t<sub>max</sub> is the same. There is no separate C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small> line for a single oral dose: t<sub>max</sub> is put in for t in the C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> equation, which is why t<sub>max</sub> always comes first. She said, If you are asked for C<sub>max</sub>, and I will ask you for C<sub>max</sub>, you must find t<sub>max</sub> first. Both lines need rate constants, not half-lives, in the same time unit.", "c": [[0, "t<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:ln(k<sub>a</sub> ÷ k)|k<sub>a</sub> − k}}"], [1, "no D<sub>0</sub>, F or V<sub>D</sub>"], [1, "swap: ln(k ÷ k<sub>a</sub>) = −ln(k<sub>a</sub> ÷ k), &nbsp; k − k<sub>a</sub> = −(k<sub>a</sub> − k)"], [2, "C<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:F k<sub>a</sub> D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub> (k<sub>a</sub> − k)}} × (e<sup>−k t<sub>max</sub></sup> − e<sup>−k<sub>a</sub> t<sub>max</sub></sup>)"], [4, "rate constants, same time unit (hr)"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example one: V D is twenty two liters, the absorption half-life forty five minutes, the elimination half-life three hours, and F eighty five percent, and the peak after a single five hundred milligram dose, and its time, are asked. t max comes first, so both half-lives go to hours, forty five minutes being zero point seven five hours, and then to rate constants: k a is zero point nine two four and k is zero point two three one per hour. k a is four times k, so t max is the natural log of four, one point three eight six, over k a minus k, zero point six nine three per hour: two hours.", "s": "oral:13", "d": "Her slide Example one: V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> is twenty two liters, the absorption half-life forty five minutes, the elimination half-life three hours, and F eighty five percent, and the peak after a single five hundred milligram dose, and its time, are asked. t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> comes first, so both half-lives go to hours, forty five minutes being zero point seven five hours, and then to rate constants: k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> is zero point nine two four and k is zero point two three one per hour. k<sub>a</sub> is four times k, so t<sub>max</sub> is the natural log of four, one point three eight six, over k<sub>a</sub> minus k, zero point six nine three per hour: two hours.", "c": [[0, "V<sub>D</sub> = 22 L, t½<sub>a</sub> = 45 min, t½ = 3 hr, F = 0.85, D<sub>0</sub> = 500 mg"], [0, "find t<sub>max</sub>, then C<sub>max</sub>"], [1, "45 min = 0.75 hr"], [1, "k<sub>a</sub> = {{frac:0.693|0.75 hr}} = 0.924 hr<sup>−1</sup> &nbsp; k = {{frac:0.693|3 hr}} = 0.231 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [2, "t<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:ln(0.924 ÷ 0.231)|(0.924 − 0.231) hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = {{frac:ln 4|0.693 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = {{frac:1.386|0.693}} hr"], [2, "<b>t<sub>max</sub> = 2 hr</b>"]], "f": {"k": "oral_steps", "at": [[1, 1], [2, 2]]}}, {"t": "In Example one, C max is the C p equation at the two hour t max, and the fraction in front needs V D. She said, Do not forget the volume of distribution. The fraction is twenty five point seven six milligrams per liter, and the bracket at two hours is zero point four seven two four. C max is twelve point one seven milligrams per liter, two hours after the dose. The peak is below the fraction in front, as on every oral curve, because the bracket, drug in minus drug out, is less than one.", "s": "oral:13", "d": "In Example one, C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small> is the C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> equation at the two hour t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small>, and the fraction in front needs V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>. She said, Do not forget the volume of distribution. The fraction is twenty five point seven six milligrams per liter, and the bracket at two hours is zero point four seven two four. C<sub>max</sub> is twelve point one seven milligrams per liter, two hours after the dose. The peak is below the fraction in front, as on every oral curve, because the bracket, drug in minus drug out, is less than one.", "c": [[0, "{{frac:(0.85)(500 mg)(0.924 hr<sup>−1</sup>)|(22 L)(0.693 hr<sup>−1</sup>)}} = {{frac:392.7|15.246}} = 25.76 mg/L"], [2, "e<sup>−(0.231)(2)</sup> − e<sup>−(0.924)(2)</sup> = 0.6300 − 0.1576 = 0.4724"], [3, "C<sub>max</sub> = (25.76 mg/L)(0.4724) = <b>12.17 mg/L</b>"], [4, "12.17 &lt; 25.76 mg/L: bracket &lt; 1"]], "f": {"k": "oral_steps", "at": [[3, 2], [4, 4]]}}, {"t": "When the C p equation is given already worked out, its exponents give k a and k, and t max and C max follow in that order. In her Example two, k a is two point seven five and k zero point two two per hour, so t max is one hour, and one hour in the given equation gives a C max of fifty five point four milligrams per liter. Her in class practice two, twenty three point two times, e to the minus zero point one eight two t, minus e to the minus zero point eight seven two t, gives a t max of two point two seven hours and a C max of twelve point one four milligrams per liter.", "s": "oral:15", "d": "When the C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> equation is given already worked out, its exponents give k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> and k, and t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> and C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small> follow in that order. In her Example two, k<sub>a</sub> is two point seven five and k zero point two two per hour, so t<sub>max</sub> is one hour, and one hour in the given equation gives a C<sub>max</sub> of fifty five point four milligrams per liter. Her in class practice two, twenty three point two times, e to the minus zero point one eight two t, minus e to the minus zero point eight seven two t, gives a t<sub>max</sub> of two point two seven hours and a C<sub>max</sub> of twelve point one four milligrams per liter.", "c": [[0, "given equation: exponents → k<sub>a</sub>, k"], [1, "t<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:ln(2.75 ÷ 0.22)|2.53 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = 1 hr"], [1, "C<sub>max</sub> = 75(e<sup>−0.22</sup> − e<sup>−2.75</sup>) = 75(0.8025 − 0.0639) = <b>55.4 mg/L</b>"], [2, "k<sub>a</sub> = 0.872, k = 0.182 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [2, "t<sub>max</sub> = 2.27 hr, &nbsp; C<sub>max</sub> = <b>12.14 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "Quiz three asked what is true of the time to reach the maximum concentration; the key: it depends on the absorption and elimination rate constants, not the dose. Another quiz three item gave a dose, F and V D that were not needed; its absorption half-life of zero point eight hours and elimination half-life of five point six hours give a t max of two point six hours. A trap in her practice one is using the one point five hour absorption half-life as a rate constant: k a is zero point six nine three over one point five hours, zero point four six two per hour. Absorption half-lives given in minutes go to hours first, and leaving V D out of the fraction is another common error.", "s": "oral:12", "d": "Quiz three asked what is true of the time to reach the maximum concentration; the key: it depends on the absorption and elimination rate constants, not the dose. Another quiz three item gave a dose, F and V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> that were not needed; its absorption half-life of zero point eight hours and elimination half-life of five point six hours give a t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> of two point six hours. A trap in her practice one is using the one point five hour absorption half-life as a rate constant: k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> is zero point six nine three over one point five hours, zero point four six two per hour. Absorption half-lives given in minutes go to hours first, and leaving V<sub>D</sub> out of the fraction is another common error.", "c": [[0, "Quiz 3: t<sub>max</sub> <b>depends on k<sub>a</sub> and k</b>"], [0, "✗ t<sub>max</sub> changes with the dose"], [1, "Quiz 3: k<sub>a</sub> 0.866, k 0.124 hr<sup>−1</sup>: t<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:ln 7|0.7425 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = <b>2.6 hr</b>; dose, F, V<sub>D</sub> not used"], [2, "✗ half-life as a rate constant: k<sub>a</sub> = {{frac:0.693|1.5 hr}} = 0.462 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [3, "minutes → hours first"], [3, "✗ V<sub>D</sub> left out"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m5-4": {"title": "Changing parameters after an oral dose", "segs": [{"t": "Module five, objective four. What happens after an oral dose when one parameter changes. The question is how C max, t max and A U C change, and the answers are directions: up, down or unchanged. A U C, the area under the plasma concentration time curve, measures the extent of exposure: how much drug got in and how long it stayed. Two relations decide every direction. t max uses only k a and k, and A U C equals F times the dose over clearance, where clearance equals k times V D. k a is in neither A U C line, so A U C depends on the dose, F and the clearance, but not on k a.", "s": "oral:18", "d": "Module five, objective four. What happens after an oral dose when one parameter changes. The question is how C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small>, t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> and AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> change, and the answers are directions: up, down or unchanged. AUC, the area under the plasma concentration time curve, measures the extent of exposure: how much drug got in and how long it stayed. Two relations decide every direction. t<sub>max</sub> uses only k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> and k, and AUC equals F times the dose over clearance, where clearance equals k times V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>. k<sub>a</sub> is in neither AUC line, so AUC depends on the dose, F and the clearance, but not on k<sub>a</sub>.", "c": [[2, "one change → C<sub>max</sub>, t<sub>max</sub>, AUC: up, down or unchanged"], [3, "AUC: extent of exposure, mg·hr/L"], [5, "t<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:ln(k<sub>a</sub> ÷ k)|k<sub>a</sub> − k}}"], [5, "AUC = {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|Cl}}, &nbsp; Cl = k × V<sub>D</sub>"], [6, "AUC: D<sub>0</sub>, F, Cl (k × V<sub>D</sub>); not k<sub>a</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Raise the dose, and C max and A U C rise in proportion, while t max stays where it was. More drug means larger amounts absorbed and eliminated per hour, but the rate constants, the fractions per hour, do not change, so the peak comes at the same time. She said, If I increase the dose, what happens to t max? Nothing, right? In her Example one, doubling the dose from five hundred to one thousand milligrams keeps t max at two hours, doubles C max from twelve point one seven to twenty four point three four milligrams per liter, and doubles A U C.", "s": "oral:18", "d": "Raise the dose, and C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small> and AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> rise in proportion, while t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> stays where it was. More drug means larger amounts absorbed and eliminated per hour, but the rate constants, the fractions per hour, do not change, so the peak comes at the same time. She said, If I increase the dose, what happens to t<sub>max</sub>? Nothing, right? In her Example one, doubling the dose from five hundred to one thousand milligrams keeps t<sub>max</sub> at two hours, doubles C<sub>max</sub> from twelve point one seven to twenty four point three four milligrams per liter, and doubles AUC.", "c": [[0, "dose up: C<sub>max</sub> and AUC up in proportion, t<sub>max</sub> unchanged"], [1, "rates (mg/hr) up; k and k<sub>a</sub> (hr<sup>−1</sup>) unchanged"], [4, "t<sub>max</sub> = 2 hr, unchanged"], [4, "C<sub>max</sub> = (12.17 mg/L)({{frac:1000 mg|500 mg}}) = <b>24.34 mg/L</b>"], [4, "e.g. AUC = {{frac:(0.85)(500 mg)|(0.231 hr<sup>−1</sup>)(22 L)}} = 83.6 → 167.3 mg·hr/L"]]}, {"t": "Raise k a with k unchanged, and the drug gets in faster, so C max is higher and t max earlier. A U C stays about the same, because k a is not part of the clearance. In the textbook table, with k at zero point one per hour, raising k a from zero point two to zero point six per hour moves t max from six point nine three to three point five eight hours and raises C max from five to six point nine nine micrograms per milliliter, while A U C stays at one hundred. She said, looking at the curve, you might not be able to really ascertain, but this comes from your textbook, and this is tabulated, and the A U C is relatively unchanged.", "s": "oral:19", "d": "Raise k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> with k unchanged, and the drug gets in faster, so C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small> is higher and t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> earlier. AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> stays about the same, because k<sub>a</sub> is not part of the clearance. In the textbook table, with k at zero point one per hour, raising k<sub>a</sub> from zero point two to zero point six per hour moves t<sub>max</sub> from six point nine three to three point five eight hours and raises C<sub>max</sub> from five to six point nine nine micrograms per milliliter, while AUC stays at one hundred. She said, looking at the curve, you might not be able to really ascertain, but this comes from your textbook, and this is tabulated, and the AUC is relatively unchanged.", "c": [[0, "k<sub>a</sub> up, k fixed: C<sub>max</sub> up, t<sub>max</sub> earlier"], [1, "AUC about unchanged: Cl unchanged"], [2, "k<sub>a</sub> 0.2 → 0.6 hr<sup>−1</sup>: t<sub>max</sub> 6.93 → 3.58 hr"], [2, "C<sub>max</sub> 5.00 → 6.99 mcg/mL; AUC 100 mcg·hr/mL throughout"], [3, "AUC for a k<sub>a</sub> change: from the table, not the curves"]]}, {"t": "Raise k with k a unchanged, and the drug leaves faster, so C max is lower, t max is earlier, and A U C falls. A U C falls because clearance equals k times V D, so a larger k is a larger clearance. She said, if we change the K, we are effectively changing the clearance. The trap is to expect a later peak with faster elimination, but t max falls when either constant rises. Swapping the values of k a and k gives the same t max, but the pair with the larger k has the larger clearance, and so the smaller A U C.", "s": "oral:19", "d": "Raise k with k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> unchanged, and the drug leaves faster, so C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small> is lower, t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> is earlier, and AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> falls. AUC falls because clearance equals k times V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>, so a larger k is a larger clearance. She said, if we change the K, we are effectively changing the clearance. The trap is to expect a later peak with faster elimination, but t<sub>max</sub> falls when either constant rises. Swapping the values of k<sub>a</sub> and k gives the same t<sub>max</sub>, but the pair with the larger k has the larger clearance, and so the smaller AUC.", "c": [[0, "k up, k<sub>a</sub> fixed: C<sub>max</sub> down, t<sub>max</sub> earlier, AUC down"], [1, "Cl = k × V<sub>D</sub> up → AUC down"], [1, "e.g. (Ch 16 Table 16-2, k<sub>a</sub> 0.3 hr<sup>−1</sup>) k 0.1 → 0.5 hr<sup>−1</sup>: t<sub>max</sub> 5.49 → 2.55 hr, C<sub>max</sub> 5.77 → 2.79 mcg/mL, AUC 100 → 20 mcg·hr/mL"], [3, "✗ k up → later peak"], [4, "k<sub>a</sub> 0.1, k 0.2 vs k<sub>a</sub> 0.2, k 0.1 hr<sup>−1</sup>: t<sub>max</sub> 6.93 hr both; AUC 50 vs 100 mcg·hr/mL"]]}, {"t": "Two terms say which process is slower. Disposition rate limiting, the usual case, means the absorption half-life is much shorter than the elimination half-life, so k a is much larger than k. Absorption rate limiting means the absorption half-life is much longer than the elimination half-life. She once said distribution limited for the usual case, but the term to use is disposition rate limiting. The textbook notes that in an extended release product absorption is slower than elimination, so the rate constant read from the last, falling part of the curve represents absorption, not elimination; this is called flip flop, and the true k then has to come from I V data.", "s": "oral:20", "d": "Two terms say which process is slower. Disposition rate limiting, the usual case, means the absorption half-life is much shorter than the elimination half-life, so k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> is much larger than k. Absorption rate limiting means the absorption half-life is much longer than the elimination half-life. She once said distribution limited for the usual case, but the term to use is disposition rate limiting. The textbook notes that in an extended release product absorption is slower than elimination, so the rate constant read from the last, falling part of the curve represents absorption, not elimination; this is called flip flop, and the true k then has to come from IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> data.", "c": [[1, "disposition rate limiting: t½<sub>a</sub> ≪ t½, k<sub>a</sub> ≫ k (usual)"], [2, "absorption rate limiting: t½<sub>a</sub> ≫ t½"], [3, "✗ distribution limited"], [4, "flip-flop: the falling part shows k<sub>a</sub>; true k from IV data"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example two asks which case an antibiotic is. Its exponents give k a, two point seven five, and k, zero point two two per hour. The absorption half-life is zero point two five two hours and the elimination half-life three point one five hours, twelve point five times longer, so absorption is twelve point five times faster than elimination. The drug is disposition rate limiting, the usual case. A question may give two exponents to compare directly: with e to the minus zero point one eight against e to the minus zero point eight seven, the larger, zero point eight seven, is k a, so absorption is much faster than elimination.", "s": "oral:15", "d": "Her slide Example two asks which case an antibiotic is. Its exponents give k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small>, two point seven five, and k, zero point two two per hour. The absorption half-life is zero point two five two hours and the elimination half-life three point one five hours, twelve point five times longer, so absorption is twelve point five times faster than elimination. The drug is disposition rate limiting, the usual case. A question may give two exponents to compare directly: with e to the minus zero point one eight against e to the minus zero point eight seven, the larger, zero point eight seven, is k<sub>a</sub>, so absorption is much faster than elimination.", "c": [[1, "k<sub>a</sub> = 2.75 hr<sup>−1</sup> &nbsp; k = 0.22 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [2, "t½<sub>a</sub> = {{frac:0.693|2.75 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = 0.252 hr &nbsp; t½ = {{frac:0.693|0.22 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = 3.15 hr"], [2, "{{frac:3.15 hr|0.252 hr}} = 12.5"], [3, "<b>disposition rate limiting</b>"], [4, "e<sup>−0.18</sup> vs e<sup>−0.87</sup>: absorption faster"]]}, {"t": "Her exam review asked what to expect with a two-fold increase in oral dose, select all that apply, and by her rule the answers are increased A U C and increased C max; both t max options are wrong. Her attendance poll asked what results when an oral dose is increased, and she was looking for no change in t max. k a, t max and C max matter for designing multiple dose regimens, for bioequivalence, for choosing the route and dosage form, for the onset of action, and for relating concentration to effect.", "s": "oral:21", "d": "Her exam review asked what to expect with a two-fold increase in oral dose, select all that apply, and by her rule the answers are increased AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> and increased C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small>; both t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> options are wrong. Her attendance poll asked what results when an oral dose is increased, and she was looking for no change in t<sub>max</sub>. k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small>, t<sub>max</sub> and C<sub>max</sub> matter for designing multiple dose regimens, for bioequivalence, for choosing the route and dosage form, for the onset of action, and for relating concentration to effect.", "c": [[0, "2 × dose: <b>AUC up, C<sub>max</sub> up</b>"], [0, "✗ any t<sub>max</sub> change"], [1, "poll: dose up → <b>no change in t<sub>max</sub></b>"], [2, "uses: regimens, bioequivalence, route and form, onset, effect"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m6-1": {"title": "Superposition and accumulation", "segs": [{"t": "Module six, objective one. Superposition, and why repeated doses build up. The dosing interval, tau, is the time between doses, so three times a day is a tau of eight hours. The peak is the highest plasma concentration, C p, in an interval, and the trough is the lowest, just before the next dose. Given at a fixed interval, each dose adds to what is left of the earlier ones, so the peaks and troughs climb to a plateau called steady state, marked by an infinity sign. First order elimination removes a fixed fraction of the drug present per unit of time, set by the rate constant k.", "s": "rep:3", "d": "Module six, objective one. Superposition, and why repeated doses build up. The dosing interval, tau, is the time between doses, so three times a day is a tau of eight hours. The peak is the highest plasma concentration, C<sub>p</sub>, in an interval, and the trough is the lowest, just before the next dose. Given at a fixed interval, each dose adds to what is left of the earlier ones, so the peaks and troughs climb to a plateau called steady state, marked by an infinity sign. First order elimination removes a fixed fraction of the drug present per unit of time, set by the rate constant k.", "c": [[2, "τ: the time between doses, hr; TID → τ = 8 hr"], [3, "peak = highest C<sub>p</sub> in an interval; trough = lowest, just before the next dose"], [4, "each dose adds to the leftover → climb → steady state (∞)"], [5, "first order: a fixed fraction of the drug present removed per hr; k in hr<sup>−1</sup>"]], "f": {"k": "md_bolus_steps", "at": [[3, 1], [4, 2], [4, 3]]}}, {"t": "Drug builds up when the next dose comes before the last one is completely eliminated; with an interval long enough for each dose to be completely eliminated, nothing is left over and nothing builds up. The climb stops because, with first order elimination, the fraction removed per interval stays the same while the amount removed grows with the amount in the body. When the amount removed over one interval equals one dose, every interval repeats. For I V bolus doses the peak minus the trough is then one dose: in amount, the dose itself, and in milligrams per liter, C zero, the concentration one dose produces on its own.", "s": "rep:3", "d": "Drug builds up when the next dose comes before the last one is completely eliminated; with an interval long enough for each dose to be completely eliminated, nothing is left over and nothing builds up. The climb stops because, with first order elimination, the fraction removed per interval stays the same while the amount removed grows with the amount in the body. When the amount removed over one interval equals one dose, every interval repeats. For IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus doses the peak minus the trough is then one dose: in amount, the dose itself, and in milligrams per liter, C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small>, the concentration one dose produces on its own.", "c": [[0, "next dose before the last is eliminated → leftover + new dose → higher peak"], [0, "interval longer than the time to eliminate a dose → no build-up"], [1, "per interval: fraction removed constant; amount removed grows with the amount present"], [2, "amount removed per τ = one dose → steady state"], [3, "IV bolus at steady state: D<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> − D<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup> = D<sub>0</sub>; C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> − C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup> = C<sub>0</sub>"]], "f": {"k": "md_bolus_steps", "at": [[0, 2], [1, 3], [2, 4]]}}, {"t": "Steady state takes three to five half lives, the half life being the time for the level to fall by half. That time depends on the half life only: after n doses the level has reached one minus e to the minus n k tau of its plateau, which holds only k and n times tau, the length of n dosing intervals, and no dose. A larger dose raises the plateau in proportion but does not move the time. She said, not three to five doses, three to five half lives. After the last dose, most of the drug is gone in another three to five half lives.", "s": "rep:3", "c": [[0, "steady state: 3 to 5 t<sub>½</sub>"], [1, "fraction of the plateau after n doses = 1 − e<sup>−nkτ</sup>; nτ = n intervals (hr); no D<sub>0</sub>"], [1, "e.g. 3.3 t<sub>½</sub>: 1 − 0.5<sup>3.3</sup> = 0.90; 5 t<sub>½</sub>: 1 − 0.5<sup>5</sup> = 0.97"], [2, "dose × 2: plateau × 2, same time"], [4, "after the last dose: most gone in 3 to 5 t<sub>½</sub>"]], "f": {"k": "md_bolus_steps", "at": [[0, 4], [4, 5]]}}, {"t": "Superposition says C p at any time is the sum of what is left of every dose given so far, each following its own single dose curve. It assumes first order elimination, and pharmacokinetics after one dose that are not altered by later doses, so the half life and the clearance, the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit of time, stay the same. Then each dose loses the same fraction per hour whatever else is present, so each leftover can be worked out on its own and the leftovers add. Saturation of a carrier, enzyme induction or inhibition, or a changing disease state break superposition. She said, First order kinetics equals linear pharmacokinetics.", "s": "rep:4", "d": "Superposition says C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> at any time is the sum of what is left of every dose given so far, each following its own single dose curve. It assumes first order elimination, and pharmacokinetics after one dose that are not altered by later doses, so the half life and the clearance, the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit of time, stay the same. Then each dose loses the same fraction per hour whatever else is present, so each leftover can be worked out on its own and the leftovers add. Saturation of a carrier, enzyme induction or inhibition, or a changing disease state break superposition. She said, First order kinetics equals linear pharmacokinetics.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = the sum of what is left of each dose"], [1, "assumes: 1. first-order elimination; 2. t<sub>½</sub> and Cl unchanged by later doses"], [2, "same fraction lost per hr → each leftover on its own → leftovers add"], [3, "fails: carrier saturation, induction or inhibition, changing pathophysiology"], [4, "first order = linear"]], "f": {"k": "md_bolus_steps", "at": [[0, 2]]}}, {"t": "In symbols, C equals C zero e to the minus k t applies after every dose, at steady state as after the first; only the starting value is higher. With t equal to tau, e to the minus k tau is the fraction of a dose left one interval later. The accumulation factor, r, one over one minus e to the minus k tau, is the steady state peak divided by the first dose peak. A shorter tau leaves more behind, so one minus e to the minus k tau, under the one, is smaller and r is larger. The dose is not in r, so doubling the dose doubles every level but leaves r unchanged.", "s": "mo:16", "d": "In symbols, C equals C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> e to the minus k t applies after every dose, at steady state as after the first; only the starting value is higher. With t equal to tau, e to the minus k tau is the fraction of a dose left one interval later. The accumulation factor, r, one over one minus e to the minus k tau, is the steady state peak divided by the first dose peak. A shorter tau leaves more behind, so one minus e to the minus k tau, under the one, is smaller and r is larger. The dose is not in r, so doubling the dose doubles every level but leaves r unchanged.", "c": [[0, "C = C<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−kt</sup> after every dose"], [1, "t = τ: e<sup>−kτ</sup> = fraction left after one interval"], [2, "r = {{frac:1|1 − e<sup>−kτ</sup>}} = {{frac:C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup>|C<sub>0</sub>}}"], [3, "τ shorter → e<sup>−kτ</sup> larger → 1 − e<sup>−kτ</sup> smaller → r larger"], [3, "τ = t<sub>½</sub>: r = {{frac:1|0.5}} = 2; e.g. τ = 2 t<sub>½</sub>: r = {{frac:1|0.75}} = 1.33"], [4, "no D<sub>0</sub> in r: dose × 2, levels × 2, r unchanged"]]}, {"t": "Since each dose follows the same curve, the area under the curve, A U C, of the first dose from zero to infinity equals the area of one interval at steady state. The average at steady state is that one interval area divided by tau, in milligrams per liter. This area form is not on the equation sheet.", "s": "rep:4", "d": "Since each dose follows the same curve, the area under the curve, AUC, of the first dose from zero to infinity equals the area of one interval at steady state. The average at steady state is that one interval area divided by tau, in milligrams per liter. This area form is not on the equation sheet.", "c": [[0, "[AUC]<sub>0</sub><sup>∞</sup> of dose 1 = [AUC]<sub>t1</sub><sup>t2</sup> of one interval at steady state"], [1, "C<sub>av</sub><sup>∞</sup> = {{frac:[AUC]<sub>t1</sub><sup>t2</sup>|τ}}: {{frac:mg hr/L|hr}} = mg/L"], [2, "not on the sheet"]]}, {"t": "For her Example one regimen, a half life of four hours, a volume of distribution of zero point two five liters per kilogram, and ten milligrams per kilogram by I V bolus every eight hours, find the peak after the second dose and the time to steady state. By superposition, the second peak is the second dose's forty plus the ten milligrams per liter left of the first after eight hours, two half lives: fifty milligrams per liter. Steady state comes in three to five half lives, twelve to twenty hours. At steady state the peak is one dose's forty plus the trough, a quarter of the peak, so forty is three quarters of the peak: fifty three point three milligrams per liter, and the trough is thirteen point three.", "s": "rep:11", "d": "For her Example one regimen, a half life of four hours, a volume of distribution of zero point two five liters per kilogram, and ten milligrams per kilogram by IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus every eight hours, find the peak after the second dose and the time to steady state. By superposition, the second peak is the second dose's forty plus the ten milligrams per liter left of the first after eight hours, two half lives: fifty milligrams per liter. Steady state comes in three to five half lives, twelve to twenty hours. At steady state the peak is one dose's forty plus the trough, a quarter of the peak, so forty is three quarters of the peak: fifty three point three milligrams per liter, and the trough is thirteen point three.", "c": [[0, "t<sub>½</sub> = 4 hr, V<sub>D</sub> = 0.25 L/kg, 10 mg/kg IV bolus every 8 hr"], [1, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:10 mg/kg|0.25 L/kg}} = 40 mg/L; 8 hr = 2 t<sub>½</sub>: 40 → 20 → 10 mg/L"], [1, "<b>peak 2 = 40 + 10 = 50 mg/L</b>"], [2, "3 × 4 hr = 12 hr to 5 × 4 hr = 20 hr: <b>steady state within 12 to 20 hr</b>"], [3, "P = 40 mg/L + 0.25P → P = {{frac:40 mg/L|0.75}} = <b>53.3 mg/L</b>"], [3, "trough = 53.3 × 0.25 = <b>13.3 mg/L</b>; 53.3 − 13.3 = 40 mg/L = C<sub>0</sub> ✓"], [3, "{{frac:53.3|40}} = 1.33 = r for τ = 2 t<sub>½</sub>"]], "f": {"k": "md_bolus_steps", "at": [[1, 1], [1, 2], [2, 3], [3, 4]]}}, {"t": "Her items test the wording. Of the two assumptions of superposition, the second is the one that names the half life and the clearance, and a review item states it as early doses having no effect on the pharmacokinetics of later doses, which is true. Steady state takes three to five half lives, not three to five doses, and does not depend on the dose. e to the minus k tau is the fraction of one dose left after one interval, while the accumulation factor is the steady state peak divided by the first dose peak. First order and linear mean the same, and objectives two and three build on superposition.", "s": "rep:4", "c": [[1, "✓ early doses do not change the PK of later doses (True)"], [2, "✓ 3 to 5 t<sub>½</sub>; not 3 to 5 doses; not dose-dependent"], [3, "e<sup>−kτ</sup>: left after one τ; r = {{frac:C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup>|C<sub>0</sub>}}"], [4, "first order = linear"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m6-2": {"title": "Concentrations after repeated IV bolus doses", "segs": [{"t": "Module six, objective two. Concentrations at any time after repeated I V bolus doses. An I V bolus puts the whole dose into the plasma at once, so each peak comes at the moment of dosing. One dose on its own gives C zero, the dose, D zero, divided by V D, the volume of distribution that relates the amount in the body to C p. Between doses the level falls by first order elimination with rate constant k, and the fraction of a dose still in the body one interval later is e to the minus k tau, tau being the time between doses. That leftover is what accumulates.", "s": "rep:6", "d": "Module six, objective two. Concentrations at any time after repeated IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus doses. An IV bolus puts the whole dose into the plasma at once, so each peak comes at the moment of dosing. One dose on its own gives C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small>, the dose, D<sub>0</sub>, divided by V<sub>D</sub>, the volume of distribution that relates the amount in the body to C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small>. Between doses the level falls by first order elimination with rate constant k, and the fraction of a dose still in the body one interval later is e to the minus k tau, tau being the time between doses. That leftover is what accumulates.", "c": [[2, "IV bolus: peak at the moment of dosing"], [3, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}} (mg/L)"], [4, "fraction left after τ: e<sup>−kτ</sup>; TID → τ = 8 hr"], [5, "D<sub>B</sub> = D<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−kτ</sup>: the leftover"]], "f": {"k": "md_bolus_steps", "at": [[2, 1]]}}, {"t": "Why does the level settle where it does? At steady state the drug eliminated over one interval equals one dose, so the drop from peak to trough equals one dose's C zero. That drop is also the peak times the fraction lost over one interval, one minus e to the minus k tau, so the peak must be C zero divided by the fraction lost. A shorter interval loses a smaller fraction, so the peak has to climb higher before the loss matches one dose. A larger dose raises peak, trough and drop in proportion; the fraction lost holds only k and tau.", "s": "rep:8", "d": "Why does the level settle where it does? At steady state the drug eliminated over one interval equals one dose, so the drop from peak to trough equals one dose's C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small>. That drop is also the peak times the fraction lost over one interval, one minus e to the minus k tau, so the peak must be C<sub>0</sub> divided by the fraction lost. A shorter interval loses a smaller fraction, so the peak has to climb higher before the loss matches one dose. A larger dose raises peak, trough and drop in proportion; the fraction lost holds only k and tau.", "c": [[1, "steady state: C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> − C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup> = C<sub>0</sub>"], [2, "drop = C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup>(1 − e<sup>−kτ</sup>) = C<sub>0</sub> → C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> = {{frac:C<sub>0</sub>|1 − e<sup>−kτ</sup>}}"], [3, "τ shorter → fraction lost smaller → C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> higher"], [4, "D<sub>0</sub> × 2 → peak, trough, drop × 2; fraction lost unchanged"]], "f": {"k": "md_bolus_steps", "at": [[1, 4]]}}, {"t": "In symbols, C max at steady state equals C zero over one minus e to the minus k tau. One over one minus e to the minus k tau is the accumulation factor, r, which holds only k and tau; k times tau has no units. A shorter tau leaves more behind, so the bottom, one minus e to the minus k tau, is smaller and the peak larger. The bottom is always less than one, so C max at steady state must come out above C zero. C min at steady state is the peak carried down one full interval, C max times e to the minus k tau, so the trough comes at the end of the interval.", "s": "rep:8", "d": "In symbols, C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small> at steady state equals C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> over one minus e to the minus k tau. One over one minus e to the minus k tau is the accumulation factor, r, which holds only k and tau; k times tau has no units. A shorter tau leaves more behind, so the bottom, one minus e to the minus k tau, is smaller and the peak larger. The bottom is always less than one, so C<sub>max</sub> at steady state must come out above C<sub>0</sub>. C min at steady state is the peak carried down one full interval, C<sub>max</sub> times e to the minus k tau, so the trough comes at the end of the interval.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> = {{frac:C<sub>0</sub>|1 − e<sup>−kτ</sup>}}"], [1, "r = {{frac:1|1 − e<sup>−kτ</sup>}}; kτ has no units"], [2, "τ shorter → e<sup>−kτ</sup> larger → bottom smaller → C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> larger"], [3, "bottom &lt; 1 → C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> &gt; C<sub>0</sub>"], [4, "C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup> = C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup>e<sup>−kτ</sup>, at the end of τ"], [4, "amounts: D<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup>, D<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup>; C = {{frac:D|V<sub>D</sub>}}"]], "f": {"k": "md_bolus_steps", "at": [[3, 4]]}}, {"t": "Next, the steady state average. The concentration falls exponentially between doses, faster while it is high, so for more than half of each interval it is below the midpoint of peak and trough, and the average is below that midpoint. She said, I want you to realize that the average is not the max plus the min divided by two. C average at steady state equals F D zero over Cl T tau, where Cl T, the total clearance, the volume of plasma cleared per unit of time, equals V D times k. F, the fraction of the dose reaching the circulation, is one for I V dosing. The dose is on top, so a larger dose raises the average; a longer tau or a larger clearance, underneath, lowers it.", "s": "rep:8", "d": "Next, the steady state average. The concentration falls exponentially between doses, faster while it is high, so for more than half of each interval it is below the midpoint of peak and trough, and the average is below that midpoint. She said, I want you to realize that the average is not the max plus the min divided by two. C average at steady state equals F D<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(dose)</small> over Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small> tau, where Cl<sub>T</sub>, the total clearance, the volume of plasma cleared per unit of time, equals V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> times k. F, the fraction of the dose reaching the circulation, is one for IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> dosing. The dose is on top, so a larger dose raises the average; a longer tau or a larger clearance, underneath, lowers it.", "c": [[1, "exponential fall → more time at low levels → C<sub>avg</sub><sup>∞</sup> below {{frac:max + min|2}}"], [3, "C<sub>avg</sub><sup>∞</sup> = {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>kτ}} = {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|Cl<sub>T</sub>τ}}"], [4, "IV: F = 1"], [5, "D<sub>0</sub> larger → C<sub>avg</sub><sup>∞</sup> larger; τ or Cl<sub>T</sub> larger → C<sub>avg</sub><sup>∞</sup> smaller; no e<sup>−kτ</sup>, no r"]], "f": {"k": "md_bolus_steps", "at": [[1, 4]]}}, {"t": "Her Example one: an antibiotic with a half life of about four hours and a V D of twenty five percent of body weight is given at ten milligrams per kilogram every eight hours by I V bolus to a sixty five kilogram female. She asks the maximum, minimum and average at steady state. Dose and volume go on the same basis first: twenty five percent of body weight is zero point two five liters per kilogram, so C zero, ten milligrams per kilogram over zero point two five liters per kilogram, is forty milligrams per liter. The eight hour interval is two half lives of four hours, so a quarter of each dose is left when the next is given.", "s": "rep:9", "d": "Her Example one: an antibiotic with a half life of about four hours and a V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> of twenty five percent of body weight is given at ten milligrams per kilogram every eight hours by IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus to a sixty five kilogram female. She asks the maximum, minimum and average at steady state. Dose and volume go on the same basis first: twenty five percent of body weight is zero point two five liters per kilogram, so C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small>, ten milligrams per kilogram over zero point two five liters per kilogram, is forty milligrams per liter. The eight hour interval is two half lives of four hours, so a quarter of each dose is left when the next is given.", "c": [[0, "t<sub>½</sub> 4 hr, V<sub>D</sub> 25% of body weight, 10 mg/kg every 8 hr, 65 kg"], [2, "V<sub>D</sub> = 0.25 L/kg; C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:10 mg/kg|0.25 L/kg}} = 40 mg/L"], [2, "or per patient: {{frac:650 mg|16.25 L}} = 40 mg/L"], [3, "k = {{frac:0.693|4 hr}} = 0.1733 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [3, "kτ = (0.1733 hr<sup>−1</sup>)(8 hr) = 1.386; e<sup>−1.386</sup> = 0.25"]]}, {"t": "In her Example one, the steady state peak is forty milligrams per liter divided by one minus zero point two five, fifty three point three milligrams per liter, above the first dose peak of forty, as her check requires. She said, if you got a value less than forty milligrams per liter, you've done something wrong. The trough, a quarter of the peak, is thirteen point three milligrams per liter, and peak minus trough is forty, one dose's C zero. The average, C zero over k tau, forty divided by one point three eight six, is twenty eight point nine milligrams per liter, below the midpoint of peak and trough, thirty three point three, as the exponential fall predicts.", "s": "rep:11", "d": "In her Example one, the steady state peak is forty milligrams per liter divided by one minus zero point two five, fifty three point three milligrams per liter, above the first dose peak of forty, as her check requires. She said, if you got a value less than forty milligrams per liter, you've done something wrong. The trough, a quarter of the peak, is thirteen point three milligrams per liter, and peak minus trough is forty, one dose's C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small>. The average, C<sub>0</sub> over k tau, forty divided by one point three eight six, is twenty eight point nine milligrams per liter, below the midpoint of peak and trough, thirty three point three, as the exponential fall predicts.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> = {{frac:40 mg/L|1 − 0.25}} = {{frac:40 mg/L|0.75}} = <b>53.3 mg/L</b> ✓ above 40"], [2, "C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup> = (53.3 mg/L)(0.25) = <b>13.3 mg/L</b>; 53.3 − 13.3 = 40 mg/L = C<sub>0</sub>"], [3, "C<sub>avg</sub><sup>∞</sup> = {{frac:(1)(40 mg/L)|1.386}} = <b>28.9 mg/L</b>"], [3, "midpoint {{frac:53.3 + 13.3|2}} = 33.3 mg/L &gt; 28.9"]], "f": {"k": "md_bolus_steps", "at": [[0, 4]]}}, {"t": "Before steady state, a dose number, n, tracks the climb. With f the fraction left after one interval, just after dose n the body holds one plus f plus f squared, up to f to the n minus one, doses: the build up. In symbols, C p equals D zero over V D, times the build up, one minus e to the minus n k tau over one minus e to the minus k tau, times e to the minus k t, with t the time since dose n, not since the first dose. As n grows, e to the minus n k tau shrinks toward zero, the build up becomes the accumulation factor r, and the equation becomes the steady state line.", "s": "rep:14", "d": "Before steady state, a dose number, n, tracks the climb. With f the fraction left after one interval, just after dose n the body holds one plus f plus f squared, up to f to the n minus one, doses: the build up. In symbols, C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> equals D<sub>0</sub> over V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>, times the build up, one minus e to the minus n k tau over one minus e to the minus k tau, times e to the minus k t, with t the time since dose n, not since the first dose. As n grows, e to the minus n k tau shrinks toward zero, the build up becomes the accumulation factor r, and the equation becomes the steady state line.", "c": [[1, "1 + f + f<sup>2</sup> + ... + f<sup>n−1</sup> = {{frac:1 − f<sup>n</sup>|1 − f}}, f = e<sup>−kτ</sup>"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}} × {{frac:1 − e<sup>−nkτ</sup>|1 − e<sup>−kτ</sup>}} × e<sup>−kt</sup>"], [2, "t = time since dose n"], [3, "n large: e<sup>−nkτ</sup> → 0, build-up → r"]], "f": {"k": "md_bolus_steps", "at": [[1, 2], [3, 3]]}}, {"t": "Her Example two asks the level three hours after the second dose of the Example one regimen. Two doses is not steady state, so the n dose line answers it, with n equal to two and t equal to three hours. The build up after two doses is one dose plus the quarter left of the first, one point two five, so the second peak is one point two five times one dose's forty milligrams per liter, fifty milligrams per liter, as superposition gave in objective one. Three hours of decline multiplies fifty by e to the minus zero point one seven three three times three, zero point five nine four five, which leaves twenty nine point seven milligrams per liter.", "s": "rep:16", "c": [[1, "n = 2, t = 3 hr, {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}} = 40 mg/L"], [2, "{{frac:1 − e<sup>−2(1.386)</sup>|1 − 0.25}} = {{frac:1 − 0.0625|0.75}} = 1.25; 40 × 1.25 = 50 mg/L"], [3, "e<sup>−(0.1733)(3)</sup> = e<sup>−0.520</sup> = 0.5945"], [3, "C<sub>p</sub> = (40 mg/L)(1.25)(0.5945) = <b>29.7 mg/L</b>"]], "f": {"k": "md_bolus_steps", "at": [[2, 2]]}}, {"t": "Within a steady state interval, and after the last dose, C p is C max at steady state times e to the minus k t, t hours after the latest dose; she prefers this to searching the sheet. Her final review poll asked the steady state peak for three hundred milligrams every twelve hours, a four hour half life and a V D of sixteen liters. Its key is twenty one point four milligrams per liter; eighteen point seven, the first dose peak, is the answer without accumulation. A review item asks when the minimum occurs: at the end of the dosing interval, not in three to five half lives, the time to steady state. T I D, three times a day, must become a tau of eight hours.", "s": "rep:19", "d": "Within a steady state interval, and after the last dose, C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> is C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small> at steady state times e to the minus k t, t hours after the latest dose; she prefers this to searching the sheet. Her final review poll asked the steady state peak for three hundred milligrams every twelve hours, a four hour half life and a V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> of sixteen liters. Its key is twenty one point four milligrams per liter; eighteen point seven, the first dose peak, is the answer without accumulation. A review item asks when the minimum occurs: at the end of the dosing interval, not in three to five half lives, the time to steady state. T I D, three times a day, must become a tau of eight hours.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup>e<sup>−kt</sup>, t since the last dose"], [1, "300 mg every 12 hr, t<sub>½</sub> 4 hr, V<sub>D</sub> 16 L: C<sub>0</sub> = 18.75 mg/L"], [2, "r = {{frac:1|1 − 0.125}} = 1.143 → <b>C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> = 21.4 mg/L</b>, not 18.7"], [3, "C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup>: end of τ, not 3 to 5 t<sub>½</sub>"], [4, "TID → τ = 8 hr; dose and V<sub>D</sub> on the same basis"]], "f": {"k": "md_bolus_steps", "at": [[0, 5]]}}], "mins": 7}, "gobj-m6-3": {"title": "Repeated intermittent IV infusions", "segs": [{"t": "Module six, objective three. Concentrations after repeated intermittent I V infusions. An intermittent infusion gives each dose at a constant rate over a set time, such as two hours, and then stops until the next dose. The reason is to prevent high concentrations and the side effects that go with them, since many drugs are better tolerated infused slowly than given as an I V bolus. In this objective R is the infusion rate, the dose divided by the infusion time, in milligrams per hour; the stem gives a dose and a duration, so R comes first. No new equation is needed.", "s": "rep:21", "d": "Module six, objective three. Concentrations after repeated intermittent IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> infusions. An intermittent infusion gives each dose at a constant rate over a set time, such as two hours, and then stops until the next dose. The reason is to prevent high concentrations and the side effects that go with them, since many drugs are better tolerated infused slowly than given as an IV bolus. In this objective R is the infusion rate, the dose divided by the infusion time, in milligrams per hour; the stem gives a dose and a duration, so R comes first. No new equation is needed.", "c": [[2, "each dose at a constant rate over a set time, then off"], [3, "slow input: no transient high levels, fewer side effects"], [4, "R = {{frac:dose|infusion time}} (mg/hr): here R is the infusion rate"], [5, "pump on: Module 3 infusion line; pump off: first-order fall"]]}, {"t": "While the pump runs, drug enters at a constant rate, and the amount leaving per hour grows as the level grows, because elimination is first order. So the level climbs, more slowly as it goes, toward C s s, the steady state concentration a continuous infusion would reach, where rate in equals rate out. The fraction of C s s reached depends on how long the pump runs compared with the half life: half of it after one half life, so a short infusion stops well below C s s. A faster rate raises C s s and every level on the climb in proportion, without changing the time course. When the pump stops, nothing comes in, and the level falls by first order elimination from where it stopped.", "s": "rep:22", "d": "While the pump runs, drug enters at a constant rate, and the amount leaving per hour grows as the level grows, because elimination is first order. So the level climbs, more slowly as it goes, toward C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, the steady state concentration a continuous infusion would reach, where rate in equals rate out. The fraction of C<sub>ss</sub> reached depends on how long the pump runs compared with the half life: half of it after one half life, so a short infusion stops well below C<sub>ss</sub>. A faster rate raises C<sub>ss</sub> and every level on the climb in proportion, without changing the time course. When the pump stops, nothing comes in, and the level falls by first order elimination from where it stopped.", "c": [[0, "rate in constant; amount out per hr grows with C<sub>p</sub>"], [1, "climb toward C<sub>ss</sub>, where rate in = rate out"], [2, "fraction of C<sub>ss</sub> reached = 1 − e<sup>−kt</sup>; 1 t<sub>½</sub> → ½; short infusion: C<sub>end</sub> ≪ C<sub>ss</sub>"], [3, "R × 2 → C<sub>ss</sub> × 2, same time course"], [4, "pump off: first-order fall"]], "f": {"k": "infusion_steps", "at": [[0, 1], [1, 2], [4, 3]]}}, {"t": "In symbols, during an infusion C p equals R over V D k, times one minus e to the minus k t, with t the time the pump has run. V D, the volume of distribution, times k is the clearance, Cl, the volume of plasma cleared per hour, so R over V D k is C s s. R is on top and clearance underneath, so a faster rate raises the level in proportion and a larger clearance lowers it. After the pump stops, C equals C end, the level at the end of the infusion, times e to the minus k t, with t counted from that end. She said, So that C zero is gonna be the concentration at the end of the infusion, right?", "s": "rep:22", "d": "In symbols, during an infusion C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> equals R over V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> k, times one minus e to the minus k t, with t the time the pump has run. V<sub>D</sub>, the volume of distribution, times k is the clearance, Cl, the volume of plasma cleared per hour, so R over V<sub>D</sub> k is C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>. R is on top and clearance underneath, so a faster rate raises the level in proportion and a larger clearance lowers it. After the pump stops, C equals C end, the level at the end of the infusion, times e to the minus k t, with t counted from that end. She said, So that C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> is gonna be the concentration at the end of the infusion, right?", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:R|V<sub>D</sub>k}} (1 − e<sup>−kt</sup>), t = the time the pump has run"], [1, "V<sub>D</sub>k = Cl (L/hr); {{frac:mg/hr|L/hr}} = mg/L; C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}}"], [2, "R larger → C<sub>p</sub> larger; Cl larger → C<sub>p</sub> smaller"], [3, "C = C<sub>end</sub>e<sup>−kt</sup>, t since that infusion ended"]], "f": {"k": "infusion_steps", "at": [[2, 2]]}}, {"t": "The same drug at the same rate for the same duration reaches the same end of infusion concentration, C end, every time. Because elimination is first order, each infusion follows its own curve and the contributions add, which is superposition from objective one. She said, the concentrations from each administration of the dose will be additive over time. For two infusions, C equals C end e to the minus k t one, plus C end e to the minus k t two, with t one the time since the first infusion ended and t two the time since the second ended.", "s": "rep:25", "c": [[0, "same R, same duration → same C<sub>end</sub>"], [1, "first order → contributions add"], [3, "C = C<sub>end</sub>e<sup>−kt<sub>1</sub></sup> + C<sub>end</sub>e<sup>−kt<sub>2</sub></sup>"], [3, "t<sub>1</sub>, t<sub>2</sub>: each from the end of its own infusion"]], "f": {"k": "two_infusions_steps", "at": [[0, 1], [0, 2], [1, 3]]}}, {"t": "Her Example four: three hundred milligrams over two hours, twice, the second starting six hours after the first started; k is zero point one five per hour, V D fifteen liters. She asks the level at the end of the first infusion and four hours after the second stops. Part a uses the infusion line with t equal to the two hour infusion time, R one hundred fifty milligrams per hour and C s s sixty six point six seven milligrams per liter. Two hours is under one four point six two hour half life, so under half of C s s is reached: zero point two five nine two of it, seventeen point two eight milligrams per liter. The identical second infusion also ends at seventeen point two eight.", "s": "rep:24", "d": "Her Example four: three hundred milligrams over two hours, twice, the second starting six hours after the first started; k is zero point one five per hour, V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> fifteen liters. She asks the level at the end of the first infusion and four hours after the second stops. Part a uses the infusion line with t equal to the two hour infusion time, R one hundred fifty milligrams per hour and C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> sixty six point six seven milligrams per liter. Two hours is under one four point six two hour half life, so under half of C<sub>ss</sub> is reached: zero point two five nine two of it, seventeen point two eight milligrams per liter. The identical second infusion also ends at seventeen point two eight.", "c": [[0, "300 mg over 2 hr; second 300 mg starts at 6 hr; k = 0.15 hr<sup>−1</sup>, V<sub>D</sub> = 15 L"], [2, "R = {{frac:300 mg|2 hr}} = 150 mg/hr"], [2, "V<sub>D</sub>k = (15 L)(0.15 hr<sup>−1</sup>) = 2.25 L/hr; C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:150 mg/hr|2.25 L/hr}} = 66.67 mg/L"], [3, "t<sub>½</sub> = {{frac:0.693|0.15 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = 4.62 hr; 1 − e<sup>−(0.15)(2)</sup> = 1 − 0.7408 = 0.2592"], [3, "<b>C<sub>end</sub> = (66.67 mg/L)(0.2592) = 17.28 mg/L</b>"], [4, "infusion 2 also ends at 17.28 mg/L"]], "f": {"k": "two_infusions_steps", "at": [[3, 1]]}}, {"t": "For part b, a number line of start and stop times gives each infusion's decay time, the hours since that infusion ended. Infusion one runs from zero to two hours and infusion two from six to eight, so four hours after the second stops is twelve hours. The first infusion has been declining for ten hours and the second for four. The first leaves three point eight six milligrams per liter and the second nine point four eight, and their sum is thirteen point three four milligrams per liter. Her stated answer is thirteen point three three, and the difference is rounding. The second infusion gives most of the total because it has had less time to decline.", "s": "rep:25", "c": [[1, "infusion 1: 0 to 2 hr; infusion 2: 6 to 8 hr; asked: 8 + 4 = 12 hr"], [2, "t<sub>1</sub> = 12 − 2 = 10 hr; t<sub>2</sub> = 12 − 8 = 4 hr"], [3, "17.28 e<sup>−(0.15)(10)</sup> = 17.28 × 0.2231 = 3.86 mg/L"], [3, "17.28 e<sup>−(0.15)(4)</sup> = 17.28 × 0.5488 = 9.48 mg/L"], [3, "<b>C = 3.86 + 9.48 = 13.34 mg/L</b>"], [4, "her answer: 13.33 mg/L, rounding"]], "f": {"k": "two_infusions_steps", "at": [[3, 4]]}}, {"t": "Her stems start the second infusion a stated number of hours after the first starts, so the spacing is start to start, while each decay time runs from the end of its own infusion. The usual trap is one decay time for both infusions; a second trap puts the six hour spacing of Example four into the infusion line in place of the infusion time. The elimination parameter may come as k, as a half life, or as a clearance with a volume, so convert to k and R first. A quiz item asks the assumption behind intermittent infusions: the concentrations from repeated infusions are additive, and the option that infusion is the best way to reach high levels quickly is the opposite of its purpose.", "s": "rep:23", "c": [[0, "spacing: start to start; each t: from the end of its own infusion"], [1, "not one t for both; not the 6 hr spacing in the infusion line"], [2, "k, t<sub>½</sub>, or Cl with V<sub>D</sub> → k and R first"], [3, "✓ additive; not the fastest way to high levels"]]}, {"t": "This objective joins two earlier ones: the climb during each infusion is the Module three infusion line, and the adding of leftovers is the superposition of objective one. A closing part may ask what a continuous infusion at the same rate would reach, which is R over Cl: sixty six point six seven milligrams per liter in Example four, nearly four times the seventeen point two eight its two hour infusion reaches. The textbook adds that when infusions are repeated at a fixed interval until steady state, the accumulation factor from objective two, one over one minus e to the minus k tau, multiplies the peak of a single infusion to give the steady state peak.", "s": "rep:26", "c": [[0, "Module 3 infusion line + superposition (objective 1)"], [1, "continuous: C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}} = 66.67 mg/L ≫ 17.28 mg/L"], [2, "repeated to steady state: steady-state peak = single-infusion peak × {{frac:1|1 − e<sup>−kτ</sup>}} (textbook)"]], "f": {"k": "infusion_steps", "at": [[1, 2]]}}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m6a-1": {"title": "Concentrations after multiple oral doses", "segs": [{"t": "Module six A, objective one. Concentrations after multiple oral doses. Extravascular means outside a blood vessel, here by mouth, so each dose is absorbed before it reaches the plasma, and the level rises to a peak and then falls. F, the bioavailability, is the fraction of the dose, D zero, that reaches the circulation; k a is the absorption rate constant and k the elimination rate constant. The peak comes at t max, when the absorption rate has fallen to equal the elimination rate, and C max is the level at that time, so t max is found before C max. She said, max for an oral dose, you have to find t max before you can find C max.", "s": "mo:3", "d": "Module six A, objective one. Concentrations after multiple oral doses. Extravascular means outside a blood vessel, here by mouth, so each dose is absorbed before it reaches the plasma, and the level rises to a peak and then falls. F, the bioavailability, is the fraction of the dose, D<sub>0</sub>, that reaches the circulation; k<sub>a</sub> is the absorption rate constant and k the elimination rate constant. The peak comes at t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small>, when the absorption rate has fallen to equal the elimination rate, and C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small> is the level at that time, so t<sub>max</sub> is found before C<sub>max</sub>. She said, max for an oral dose, you have to find t<sub>max</sub> before you can find C<sub>max</sub>.", "c": [[2, "oral: absorbed first → rise to a peak, then fall"], [3, "F (no units); k<sub>a</sub> and k in hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [4, "peak: absorption rate = elimination rate → t<sub>max</sub> first"], [5, "IV bolus peak = C<sub>0</sub>, no t<sub>max</sub>"]], "f": {"k": "md_oral_steps", "at": [[2, 1]]}}, {"t": "The single dose line from Module five is C p equals P, times e to the minus k t minus e to the minus k a t. P, the first dose prefactor, F k a D zero over V D times k a minus k, is in milligrams per liter, the same for every dose, and neither C zero nor the peak. t max equals one over k a minus k, times the natural log of k a over k. It holds only k and k a, so a larger dose does not move it. e to the minus k t follows elimination and falls slowly; e to the minus k a t follows the unabsorbed drug and falls fast, because k a is larger.", "s": "mo:3", "d": "The single dose line from Module five is C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> equals P, times e to the minus k t minus e to the minus k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> t. P, the first dose prefactor, F k<sub>a</sub> D<sub>0</sub> over V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> times k<sub>a</sub> minus k, is in milligrams per liter, the same for every dose, and neither C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> nor the peak. t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> equals one over k<sub>a</sub> minus k, times the natural log of k<sub>a</sub> over k. It holds only k and k<sub>a</sub>, so a larger dose does not move it. e to the minus k t follows elimination and falls slowly; e to the minus k<sub>a</sub> t follows the unabsorbed drug and falls fast, because k<sub>a</sub> is larger.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = P (e<sup>−kt</sup> − e<sup>−k<sub>a</sub>t</sup>)"], [1, "P = {{frac:F k<sub>a</sub> D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>(k<sub>a</sub> − k)}} (mg/L)"], [2, "t<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:1|k<sub>a</sub> − k}} ln({{frac:k<sub>a</sub>|k}})"], [3, "no D<sub>0</sub>: dose larger, t<sub>max</sub> unchanged"], [4, "e<sup>−kt</sup>: elimination, slow; e<sup>−k<sub>a</sub>t</sup>: unabsorbed drug, fast"]], "f": {"k": "oral_steps", "at": [[2, 2], [4, 4]]}}, {"t": "Repeated oral doses add the same way as bolus doses: each dose adds its own curve to what is left of the earlier ones, so the levels climb to a steady state. The time to get there depends on the half life alone, so with a ten hour half life it is thirty to fifty hours. At steady state the peak is expected to be higher, because drug from earlier doses is still in the body. It also comes earlier: the peak is where absorption into the plasma has fallen to equal elimination, and at steady state elimination starts each interval already high, from the trough, so the two rates become equal sooner. She said, that t max is going to decrease.", "s": "mo:5", "d": "Repeated oral doses add the same way as bolus doses: each dose adds its own curve to what is left of the earlier ones, so the levels climb to a steady state. The time to get there depends on the half life alone, so with a ten hour half life it is thirty to fifty hours. At steady state the peak is expected to be higher, because drug from earlier doses is still in the body. It also comes earlier: the peak is where absorption into the plasma has fallen to equal elimination, and at steady state elimination starts each interval already high, from the trough, so the two rates become equal sooner. She said, that t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> is going to decrease.", "c": [[0, "each dose adds its curve to the leftover → climb → steady state"], [1, "time to steady state: t<sub>½</sub> alone; 10 hr → 30 to 50 hr"], [2, "C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> expected &gt; first-dose C<sub>max</sub>: drug accumulated"], [3, "t<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> &lt; t<sub>max</sub>: elimination already high at the trough → absorption rate = elimination rate sooner"]], "f": {"k": "md_oral_steps", "at": [[0, 2], [1, 3], [2, 4]]}}, {"t": "In symbols, repeated doses add two accumulation factors, with tau the dosing interval, the time between doses: r k equals one over one minus e to the minus k tau, and r a is the same with k a in place of k. e to the minus k tau is the fraction of a dose left one interval later, so one minus it is less than one and both factors are at least one. A longer tau leaves a smaller fraction, so it makes both factors smaller.", "s": "mo:5", "d": "In symbols, repeated doses add two accumulation factors, with tau the dosing interval, the time between doses: r k equals one over one minus e to the minus k tau, and r a is the same with k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> in place of k. e to the minus k tau is the fraction of a dose left one interval later, so one minus it is less than one and both factors are at least one. A longer tau leaves a smaller fraction, so it makes both factors smaller.", "c": [[0, "r<sub>k</sub> = {{frac:1|1 − e<sup>−kτ</sup>}}; r<sub>a</sub> = {{frac:1|1 − e<sup>−k<sub>a</sub>τ</sup>}}"], [1, "e<sup>−kτ</sup>: fraction left after τ; r at least 1; τ longer → r smaller"], [1, "steady state, t after the latest dose: C<sub>p</sub><sup>∞</sup> = P (r<sub>k</sub>e<sup>−kt</sup> − r<sub>a</sub>e<sup>−k<sub>a</sub>t</sup>)"], [1, "before steady state, n doses: {{frac:1 − e<sup>−nkτ</sup>|1 − e<sup>−kτ</sup>}}, same with k<sub>a</sub>; n large → r"]], "f": {"k": "md_oral_steps", "at": [[0, 3]]}}, {"t": "t max at steady state is the single dose t max with one more fraction inside the log, one minus e to the minus k tau over one minus e to the minus k a tau. Since k a is larger than k, the bottom is the larger, so the fraction is less than one and t max at steady state is shorter than the first dose t max. It holds tau, so a new interval means a new t max at steady state, while a new dose changes neither t max.", "s": "mo:7", "d": "t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> at steady state is the single dose t<sub>max</sub> with one more fraction inside the log, one minus e to the minus k tau over one minus e to the minus k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> tau. Since k<sub>a</sub> is larger than k, the bottom is the larger, so the fraction is less than one and t<sub>max</sub> at steady state is shorter than the first dose t<sub>max</sub>. It holds tau, so a new interval means a new t<sub>max</sub> at steady state, while a new dose changes neither t<sub>max</sub>.", "c": [[0, "t<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> = {{frac:1|k<sub>a</sub> − k}} ln[{{frac:k<sub>a</sub>|k}} × {{frac:1 − e<sup>−kτ</sup>|1 − e<sup>−k<sub>a</sub>τ</sup>}}]"], [1, "k<sub>a</sub> &gt; k → correction &lt; 1 → t<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> &lt; t<sub>max</sub>"], [2, "depends on k, k<sub>a</sub>, τ; not on D<sub>0</sub>"]], "f": {"k": "md_oral_steps", "at": [[1, 4]]}}, {"t": "C max at steady state equals F D zero over V D, the absorbed dose spread through the volume of distribution, times r k for drug left from earlier doses, times e to the minus k t max at steady state. F and t max mark this as the oral line; without them it is the repeated bolus peak. C min at steady state is P times r k times e to the minus k tau, read at the end of the interval, starting from P, not F D zero over V D. C average at steady state is F D zero over Cl T tau, Cl T being the total clearance, with no t max and no accumulation factor.", "s": "mo:6", "d": "C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small> at steady state equals F D<sub>0</sub> over V<sub>D</sub>, the absorbed dose spread through the volume of distribution, times r k for drug left from earlier doses, times e to the minus k t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> at steady state. F and t<sub>max</sub> mark this as the oral line; without them it is the repeated bolus peak. C min at steady state is P times r k times e to the minus k tau, read at the end of the interval, starting from P, not F D<sub>0</sub> over V<sub>D</sub>. C average at steady state is F D<sub>0</sub> over Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small> tau, Cl<sub>T</sub> being the total clearance, with no t<sub>max</sub> and no accumulation factor.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> = {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}} × r<sub>k</sub> × e<sup>−kt<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup></sup>"], [1, "F and t<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup>: the oral line; bolus: {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}} × r<sub>k</sub>"], [2, "C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup> = P × r<sub>k</sub> × e<sup>−kτ</sup> (P, not {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}})"], [3, "C<sub>avg</sub><sup>∞</sup> = {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|Cl<sub>T</sub>τ}} = {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>kτ}}"]]}, {"t": "Her Example one: a seventy five kilogram man takes two hundred fifty milligrams of tetracycline by mouth every eight hours, with F about seventy five percent, V D one point five liters per kilogram, a half life of about ten hours and k a zero point nine per hour. She asks the first dose peak, then the steady state peak, trough and average. Converted, k is zero point zero six nine three per hour and V D one hundred twelve point five liters; the first dose t max is three point zero nine hours, written three point one. P is one point eight zero six milligrams per liter, and the first dose peak is one point three five milligrams per liter, below P, because P multiplies a bracket below one.", "s": "mo:8", "d": "Her Example one: a seventy five kilogram man takes two hundred fifty milligrams of tetracycline by mouth every eight hours, with F about seventy five percent, V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> one point five liters per kilogram, a half life of about ten hours and k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> zero point nine per hour. She asks the first dose peak, then the steady state peak, trough and average. Converted, k is zero point zero six nine three per hour and V<sub>D</sub> one hundred twelve point five liters; the first dose t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> is three point zero nine hours, written three point one. P is one point eight zero six milligrams per liter, and the first dose peak is one point three five milligrams per liter, below P, because P multiplies a bracket below one.", "c": [[0, "75 kg; 250 mg every 8 hr; F 75%, V<sub>D</sub> 1.5 L/kg, t<sub>½</sub> 10 hr, k<sub>a</sub> 0.9 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [2, "k = {{frac:0.693|10 hr}} = 0.0693 hr<sup>−1</sup>; V<sub>D</sub> = 1.5 L/kg × 75 kg = 112.5 L; F = 0.75"], [2, "<b>t<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:ln(12.99)|0.9 − 0.0693}} = {{frac:2.564|0.8307 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = 3.09 hr</b>"], [3, "P = {{frac:(0.75)(0.9)(250 mg)|(112.5 L)(0.8307)}} = {{frac:168.75|93.45}} = <b>1.806 mg/L</b>"], [3, "<b>C<sub>max</sub> = 1.806 × (e<sup>−0.0693 × 3.09</sup> − e<sup>−0.9 × 3.09</sup>) = 1.806 × (0.807 − 0.062) = 1.35 mg/L</b>"]], "f": {"k": "md_oral_steps", "at": [[2, 1]]}}, {"t": "For steady state, eight hours is less than one ten hour half life, so about fifty seven percent of each dose is still in the body when the next dose is given, and r k, one over one minus zero point five seven four, is two point three five. Almost nothing is left unabsorbed after eight hours, so r a is one. t max at steady state is two point zero six hours, about an hour earlier than after the first dose. The steady state peak is three point four zero milligrams per liter.", "s": "mo:8", "d": "For steady state, eight hours is less than one ten hour half life, so about fifty seven percent of each dose is still in the body when the next dose is given, and r k, one over one minus zero point five seven four, is two point three five. Almost nothing is left unabsorbed after eight hours, so r a is one. t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> at steady state is two point zero six hours, about an hour earlier than after the first dose. The steady state peak is three point four zero milligrams per liter.", "c": [[0, "e<sup>−kτ</sup> = e<sup>−0.0693 × 8</sup> = 0.574; r<sub>k</sub> = {{frac:1|1 − 0.574}} = {{frac:1|0.426}} = 2.35"], [1, "e<sup>−k<sub>a</sub>τ</sup> = e<sup>−0.9 × 8</sup> = 0.0007; r<sub>a</sub> = {{frac:1|0.999}} = 1.00"], [2, "<b>t<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> = {{frac:1|0.8307}} ln[12.99 × {{frac:0.426|0.999}}] = {{frac:ln(5.539)|0.8307}} = {{frac:1.712|0.8307}} = 2.06 hr</b>"], [3, "{{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}} = {{frac:(0.75)(250 mg)|112.5 L}} = 1.667 mg/L"], [3, "<b>C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> = 1.667 × 2.35 × e<sup>−0.0693 × 2.06</sup> = 1.667 × 2.35 × 0.867 = 3.40 mg/L</b>"]], "f": {"k": "md_oral_steps", "at": [[0, 3], [2, 4]]}}, {"t": "The steady state trough is two point four four milligrams per liter, and the average, the absorbed dose over the volume cleared in one interval, is three point zero one milligrams per liter. The pattern checks: the steady state peak is higher than the first dose peak and comes earlier, and even the trough is above the first dose peak. The average is an average over time, not the midpoint of peak and trough: here it is above the midpoint, two point nine two milligrams per liter, while for her repeated I V bolus example it was below the midpoint.", "s": "mo:8", "d": "The steady state trough is two point four four milligrams per liter, and the average, the absorbed dose over the volume cleared in one interval, is three point zero one milligrams per liter. The pattern checks: the steady state peak is higher than the first dose peak and comes earlier, and even the trough is above the first dose peak. The average is an average over time, not the midpoint of peak and trough: here it is above the midpoint, two point nine two milligrams per liter, while for her repeated IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus example it was below the midpoint.", "c": [[0, "<b>C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup> = 1.806 × 2.35 × 0.574 = 2.44 mg/L</b>"], [0, "<b>C<sub>avg</sub><sup>∞</sup> = {{frac:(0.75)(250 mg)|(112.5 L)(0.0693 hr<sup>−1</sup>)(8 hr)}} = {{frac:187.5 mg|62.37 L}} = 3.01 mg/L</b>"], [1, "3.40 &gt; 1.35 mg/L; 2.06 &lt; 3.09 hr; 2.44 &gt; 1.35 mg/L ✓"], [2, "midpoint {{frac:3.40 + 2.44|2}} = 2.92 mg/L &lt; 3.01"], [2, "her spoken 3.3; inputs give 3.40 (3.39 with t<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> = 2.1 hr)"]], "f": {"k": "md_oral_steps", "at": [[0, 4]]}}, {"t": "Her traps are about which line to use: the steady state peak needs t max at steady state, not the first dose t max, and the trough line starts from P, the first dose prefactor, not from F D zero over V D. A quiz item asks when the steady state maximum occurs; the key, two point six hours, uses only k, k a and tau, not F, V D, the dose or the weight. Another asks what a larger oral dose changes: a higher steady state C max, not a later t max.", "s": "mo:6", "d": "Her traps are about which line to use: the steady state peak needs t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> at steady state, not the first dose t<sub>max</sub>, and the trough line starts from P, the first dose prefactor, not from F D<sub>0</sub> over V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small>. A quiz item asks when the steady state maximum occurs; the key, two point six hours, uses only k, k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> and tau, not F, V<sub>D</sub>, the dose or the weight. Another asks what a larger oral dose changes: a higher steady state C<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(peak concentration)</small>, not a later t<sub>max</sub>.", "c": [[0, "oral line: F and t<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup>; not {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}} × r<sub>k</sub>"], [0, "C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> with t<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> 2.06 hr, not 3.09 hr; C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup> from P"], [1, "750 mg every 8 hr, t<sub>½</sub> 5 hr, absorption t<sub>½</sub> 1.5 hr: <b>t<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> = 2.6 hr</b>; F, V<sub>D</sub>, dose, weight unused"], [2, "dose larger → C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> higher; t<sub>max</sub> unchanged"]]}], "mins": 7}, "gobj-m6a-2": {"title": "Changing the dose or the interval", "segs": [{"t": "Module six A, objective two. Changing the dose or the dosing interval. A dosage regimen is the dose, D zero, and the dosing interval, tau, the time between doses, and these are the only two things a prescriber adjusts. The clearance, the half life, the elimination rate constant and the volume of distribution belong to the drug and the patient, not to the regimen. Fluctuation is the difference between the peak and the trough within one interval at steady state, and compliance is how reliably the patient takes each dose as prescribed.", "s": "mo:11", "d": "Module six A, objective two. Changing the dose or the dosing interval. A dosage regimen is the dose, D<sub>0</sub>, and the dosing interval, tau, the time between doses, and these are the only two things a prescriber adjusts. The clearance, the half life, the elimination rate constant and the volume of distribution belong to the drug and the patient, not to the regimen. Fluctuation is the difference between the peak and the trough within one interval at steady state, and compliance is how reliably the patient takes each dose as prescribed.", "c": [[2, "regimen = D<sub>0</sub> + τ"], [3, "fixed by drug and patient: Cl<sub>T</sub>, t<sub>½</sub>, k, V<sub>D</sub>"], [4, "fluctuation: C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> − C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup>; compliance: doses taken as prescribed"]]}, {"t": "The average steady state level is proportional to the dosing rate, the dose divided by the interval. Over one interval the body clears a volume equal to the clearance times tau, and the absorbed dose spread through that volume is the average. In symbols, C average at steady state equals F D zero over Cl T tau, with F the fraction of the dose that reaches the circulation. D zero is on top, so a larger dose at the same interval raises the average in proportion. Tau is underneath, so a longer interval at the same dose lowers it, because each dose has to cover more hours.", "s": "mo:13", "d": "The average steady state level is proportional to the dosing rate, the dose divided by the interval. Over one interval the body clears a volume equal to the clearance times tau, and the absorbed dose spread through that volume is the average. In symbols, C average at steady state equals F D<sub>0</sub> over Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small> tau, with F the fraction of the dose that reaches the circulation. D<sub>0</sub> is on top, so a larger dose at the same interval raises the average in proportion. Tau is underneath, so a longer interval at the same dose lowers it, because each dose has to cover more hours.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>avg</sub><sup>∞</sup> follows {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|τ}} (mg/hr)"], [1, "volume cleared per interval = Cl<sub>T</sub>τ (L)"], [2, "C<sub>avg</sub><sup>∞</sup> = {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|Cl<sub>T</sub>τ}}"], [3, "D<sub>0</sub> × 2 → C<sub>avg</sub><sup>∞</sup> × 2"], [4, "τ × 2 → C<sub>avg</sub><sup>∞</sup> × ½"]]}, {"t": "The dosing interval also sets how far the level falls between doses. For repeated I V bolus doses the trough is the peak times e to the minus k tau, the fraction of a dose left one interval later. A longer interval lets more of each dose be eliminated, so the trough is a smaller fraction of the peak: the larger fluctuation she lists for a longer interval. With equal bolus doses the gap between peak and trough is one dose's C zero at any interval: forty milligrams per liter in her Module six repeated I V bolus Example one, both every eight hours and every four, while the trough is a quarter of the peak every eight hours and half of it every four.", "s": "rep:12", "d": "The dosing interval also sets how far the level falls between doses. For repeated IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus doses the trough is the peak times e to the minus k tau, the fraction of a dose left one interval later. A longer interval lets more of each dose be eliminated, so the trough is a smaller fraction of the peak: the larger fluctuation she lists for a longer interval. With equal bolus doses the gap between peak and trough is one dose's C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> at any interval: forty milligrams per liter in her Module six repeated IV bolus Example one, both every eight hours and every four, while the trough is a quarter of the peak every eight hours and half of it every four.", "c": [[1, "C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup> = C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup>e<sup>−kτ</sup> → {{frac:C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup>|C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup>}} = e<sup>−kτ</sup>"], [2, "τ longer → e<sup>−kτ</sup> smaller → trough a smaller fraction of the peak → larger fluctuation"], [3, "equal doses: C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> − C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup> = C<sub>0</sub> at any τ"], [3, "every 8 hr: 53.3 − 13.3 = 40 mg/L; trough = 0.25 × peak"], [3, "every 4 hr: e<sup>−0.1733 × 4</sup> = 0.5; {{frac:40|0.5}} = 80, 80 × 0.5 = 40; 80 − 40 = 40 mg/L; trough = 0.5 × peak"]]}, {"t": "So her smaller fluctuation for a shorter interval means the trough sits closer to the peak, as a fraction of it. It does not mean lower peaks or troughs: with a shorter interval the peak, the trough and the average all rise. She said, we're not going all the way to the bottom of that curve. Tau is also inside t max at steady state, so a new interval means a new steady state peak time and peak.", "s": "mo:18", "d": "So her smaller fluctuation for a shorter interval means the trough sits closer to the peak, as a fraction of it. It does not mean lower peaks or troughs: with a shorter interval the peak, the trough and the average all rise. She said, we're not going all the way to the bottom of that curve. Tau is also inside t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small> at steady state, so a new interval means a new steady state peak time and peak.", "c": [[0, "τ shorter: C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup> closer to C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup>, as a fraction → smaller fluctuation"], [1, "τ shorter: C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup>, C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup>, C<sub>avg</sub><sup>∞</sup> all rise"], [3, "new τ → new t<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> and C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup>"]]}, {"t": "A larger dose at the same interval raises every level in proportion: the peak, the trough and the average. So the gap between peak and trough in milligrams per liter grows with the dose, and that is the larger fluctuation she lists for a larger dose. In her repeated I V bolus Example one every eight hours, doubling the dose doubles the gap from forty to eighty milligrams per liter, while the trough stays a quarter of the peak. She said, if we increase the dose, we expect increased concentration and increased fluctuation between peaks and troughs. Compliance usually does not change, and neither does t max.", "s": "mo:13", "d": "A larger dose at the same interval raises every level in proportion: the peak, the trough and the average. So the gap between peak and trough in milligrams per liter grows with the dose, and that is the larger fluctuation she lists for a larger dose. In her repeated IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus Example one every eight hours, doubling the dose doubles the gap from forty to eighty milligrams per liter, while the trough stays a quarter of the peak. She said, if we increase the dose, we expect increased concentration and increased fluctuation between peaks and troughs. Compliance usually does not change, and neither does t<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(time of the peak)</small>.", "c": [[0, "D<sub>0</sub> × 2: C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup>, C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup>, C<sub>avg</sub><sup>∞</sup> × 2"], [1, "gap in mg/L × 2 → larger fluctuation"], [2, "20 mg/kg every 8 hr: C<sub>0</sub> = 80 mg/L; C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> = {{frac:80|0.75}} = 106.7; C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup> = 26.7 mg/L"], [2, "106.7 − 26.7 = 80 mg/L; trough = 0.25 × peak"], [4, "compliance usually unchanged; t<sub>max</sub>, t<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> unchanged"]]}, {"t": "Her question to the room: five hundred milligrams every four hours is changed to five hundred milligrams every eight hours, so what is the expectation at steady state? The interval doubles at the same dose, so the average halves: a lower level, the part she confirmed in class. Each dose now has eight hours instead of four to be eliminated, so the trough falls to a smaller fraction of the peak, a larger fluctuation. Three doses a day instead of six is easier to keep to, so compliance improves. The area under the curve of each dose is unchanged; what halves is the area per day, and so the average.", "s": "mo:15", "c": [[0, "500 mg every 4 hr → 500 mg every 8 hr"], [1, "2τ in {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|Cl<sub>T</sub>τ}} → average × ½"], [2, "e<sup>−kτ</sup> smaller → trough a smaller fraction of the peak"], [3, "3 doses a day, not 6"], [3, "<b>lower level, larger fluctuation, better compliance</b>"], [4, "AUC per dose unchanged; AUC per day × ½"]]}, {"t": "Take equal oral doses every six hours and every eight hours, with k a and k the same, followed as the amount of drug in the body. The six hour curve has the higher plateau, and both reach steady state in the same time, because the half life alone sets that. The six hour curve also fluctuates less in her sense: its trough sits closer to its peak, as a fraction of it, because less of each dose is eliminated before the next arrives.", "s": "mo:17", "d": "Take equal oral doses every six hours and every eight hours, with k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> and k the same, followed as the amount of drug in the body. The six hour curve has the higher plateau, and both reach steady state in the same time, because the half life alone sets that. The six hour curve also fluctuates less in her sense: its trough sits closer to its peak, as a fraction of it, because less of each dose is eliminated before the next arrives.", "c": [[0, "equal doses every 6 hr and every 8 hr; same k<sub>a</sub>, k"], [1, "every 6 hr: higher plateau; same time to steady state"], [2, "every 6 hr: trough a larger fraction of the peak → smaller fluctuation"]]}, {"t": "Asked the best way to raise the steady state level, the key is to decrease the dosing interval; clearance and half life are the patient's, not the prescriber's. Asked what a shorter interval at the same dose gives, the key is higher steady state levels, since larger fluctuation belongs to a longer interval. Pairing a larger dose with a smaller fluctuation is wrong, because a larger dose raises both the level and the gap. Expecting a longer interval to raise the level because each dose has more time to be absorbed is wrong too: absorption is complete either way, and the extra time goes to elimination.", "s": "mo:14", "c": [[0, "✓ decrease τ; not increase τ; not Cl or t<sub>½</sub>"], [1, "τ shorter, same D<sub>0</sub>: ✓ higher levels"], [2, "D<sub>0</sub> larger: level and gap both larger"], [3, "τ longer: extra time → elimination, not absorption"]]}, {"t": "The aim is a regimen whose peak and trough both sit inside the therapeutic range, between the lowest effective level and the level where toxicity starts; the average alone does not show that. The interval and the tablet strength must also be ones a patient can use: a calculated interval of three point seven two hours makes no sense to a patient, and a calculated oral dose of seventeen point two nine milligrams is rounded, for example to twenty milligrams. She said, Don't tell me seventeen point two nine milligrams for an oral dose.", "s": "mo:19", "c": [[0, "C<sub>max</sub><sup>∞</sup> and C<sub>min</sub><sup>∞</sup> inside the therapeutic range"], [1, "interval: 3.72 hr makes no sense to the patient; oral dose: 17.29 mg → <b>20 mg</b>"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m7-1": {"title": "Product performance, bioavailability and bioequivalence", "segs": [{"t": "Module seven A, objective one. Drug product performance, bioavailability and bioequivalence. Drug product performance is the release of the drug substance from the drug product, the step that leads to its bioavailability. It comes first because drug that stays in the tablet is never absorbed. She said, If the drug does not release from dosage form, then the drug will not be available for the body to use.", "s": "ba:3", "c": [[2, "drug product performance: release of the drug substance from the product"], [3, "release → absorption → systemic circulation"], [4, "no release: no drug available"]]}, {"t": "Bioavailability is the rate and extent to which the active ingredient is absorbed from a product and becomes available at the site of action. Extent is how much of the dose gets into the systemic circulation, and rate is how fast. Extent is read from the area under the plasma level against time curve, A U C, a concentration times a time. The area is proportional to the amount of unchanged drug that reaches the circulation, so in the same person more drug in gives more area. F, the fraction of the dose that reaches the circulation, is the extent half; it has no units and is one for an I V dose.", "s": "ba:4", "d": "Bioavailability is the rate and extent to which the active ingredient is absorbed from a product and becomes available at the site of action. Extent is how much of the dose gets into the systemic circulation, and rate is how fast. Extent is read from the area under the plasma level against time curve, AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small>, a concentration times a time. The area is proportional to the amount of unchanged drug that reaches the circulation, so in the same person more drug in gives more area. F, the fraction of the dose that reaches the circulation, is the extent half; it has no units and is one for an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> dose.", "c": [[0, "bioavailability = rate and extent of absorption"], [1, "extent: how much gets in. rate: how fast"], [2, "extent: AUC, in (mg/L)hr"], [3, "same patient: more drug in → larger AUC"], [4, "F = fraction reaching the circulation, no units; F<sub>IV</sub> = 1"]]}, {"t": "Rate is read from t max, the time of the peak. At first absorption brings drug in faster than elimination removes it, so the level rises. At the peak the two rates are equal, so faster absorption gets there sooner, at a smaller t max. More drug absorbed at the same speed gives a larger area, the same t max. t max equals the natural log of k a over k, divided by k a minus k, with k a and k the absorption and elimination rate constants: no F, no dose. A U C equals F times the dose, D zero, over k times the volume of distribution, V D, with F on top and no k a, so changing k a alone moves the peak, not the area.", "s": "ba:4", "d": "Rate is read from t<sub>max</sub>, the time of the peak. At first absorption brings drug in faster than elimination removes it, so the level rises. At the peak the two rates are equal, so faster absorption gets there sooner, at a smaller t<sub>max</sub>. More drug absorbed at the same speed gives a larger area, the same t<sub>max</sub>. t<sub>max</sub> equals the natural log of k<sub>a</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(absorption rate constant)</small> over k, divided by k<sub>a</sub> minus k, with k<sub>a</sub> and k the absorption and elimination rate constants: no F, no dose. AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> equals F times the dose, D<sub>0</sub>, over k times the volume of distribution, V<sub>D</sub>, with F on top and no k<sub>a</sub>, so changing k<sub>a</sub> alone moves the peak, not the area.", "c": [[0, "rate: t<sub>max</sub>, in hr"], [2, "at t<sub>max</sub>: rate of absorption = rate of elimination"], [2, "faster absorption → smaller t<sub>max</sub>"], [3, "more absorbed, same speed: AUC larger, t<sub>max</sub> unchanged"], [4, "t<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:ln(k<sub>a</sub>/k)|k<sub>a</sub> − k}}: no F, no dose"], [5, "AUC = {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|k V<sub>D</sub>}}: F on top, no k<sub>a</sub> → k<sub>a</sub> alone moves t<sub>max</sub>, not AUC"]], "f": {"k": "oral_steps", "at": [[1, 1], [2, 2]]}}, {"t": "Bioequivalence compares the same active ingredient in two products, a test product and the reference, the standard or originator it is compared with. They are bioequivalent when there is no significant difference in the rate and extent of availability at the site of action, at the same molar dose under similar conditions. Since bioavailability has two parts, both must match: the area and the peak time. She said, It's the absence of a significant difference. So they don't have to be exactly the same, but they just can't be too different.", "s": "ba:12", "c": [[0, "test against reference: same active ingredient"], [1, "no significant difference in rate and extent"], [1, "same molar dose, similar conditions"], [2, "bioequivalent: AUC similar and t<sub>max</sub> similar"], [4, "similar, not identical"]]}, {"t": "Her bioequivalence example puts three formulations of one drug, A, B and C, on one plot. A and B enclose the same area, so the same amount got in, but B peaks later, so it got in more slowly. A and C peak at the same time, but C encloses half the area, so C delivered half as much drug at the same speed. So A and B differ in rate, A and C in extent, and no pair is bioequivalent. A fourth curve she drew close to A, with about the same peak time and area, is the one she called bioequivalent.", "s": "ba:13", "c": [[1, "A, B: AUC<sub>A</sub> = AUC<sub>B</sub>; B peaks later"], [2, "A, C: same t<sub>max</sub>; AUC<sub>C</sub> = ½ AUC<sub>A</sub>"], [3, "<b>none of A, B and C is bioequivalent to another</b>"], [4, "D: close to A in AUC and t<sub>max</sub> ✓"]]}, {"t": "In her items, the rate and extent of absorption from one product is bioavailability, a comparison of two products is bioequivalence, and the release step is drug product performance. Two products with similar F values are not shown to be bioequivalent, because F and A U C give extent only. She said, these two have similar F's, but you don't know if they're bioequivalent because you don't know about the, the rate. A bioequivalence study is a specialized relative bioavailability study, the comparison of objective two, that compares rate as well as extent.", "s": "ba:12", "d": "In her items, the rate and extent of absorption from one product is bioavailability, a comparison of two products is bioequivalence, and the release step is drug product performance. Two products with similar F values are not shown to be bioequivalent, because F and AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> give extent only. She said, these two have similar F's, but you don't know if they're bioequivalent because you don't know about the, the rate. A bioequivalence study is a specialized relative bioavailability study, the comparison of objective two, that compares rate as well as extent.", "c": [[0, "one product: bioavailability. two products: bioequivalence. release: product performance"], [1, "similar F: extent only, rate unknown → bioequivalence not shown"], [3, "bioequivalence study: relative bioavailability study + rate"]]}], "mins": 4}, "gobj-m7-2": {"title": "Absolute and relative bioavailability", "segs": [{"t": "Module seven A, objective two. Relative against absolute bioavailability. F, the bioavailability factor, is the fraction of a dose that reaches the systemic circulation, with no units. Absolute bioavailability compares a dose given by an extravascular route, any route other than directly into the blood, here oral, with the same drug given intravenously, I V. An I V dose is placed entirely in the blood, so its F is one, and it is the standard. Relative bioavailability compares two formulations of the same drug, such as a tablet against an oral solution; neither is I V, so the reference product is the standard.", "s": "ba:5", "d": "Module seven A, objective two. Relative against absolute bioavailability. F, the bioavailability factor, is the fraction of a dose that reaches the systemic circulation, with no units. Absolute bioavailability compares a dose given by an extravascular route, any route other than directly into the blood, here oral, with the same drug given intravenously, IV. An IV dose is placed entirely in the blood, so its F is one, and it is the standard. Relative bioavailability compares two formulations of the same drug, such as a tablet against an oral solution; neither is IV, so the reference product is the standard.", "c": [[2, "F: fraction of the dose reaching the circulation, no units"], [3, "absolute: extravascular (here oral, po) against IV"], [4, "IV: the whole dose in the blood → F = 1, the standard"], [5, "relative: two formulations, neither IV; reference product = standard"]]}, {"t": "Absolute and relative bioavailability both compare extent, so both compare areas under the curve, A U C. The I V dose is usually smaller than the oral dose, so each area is divided by its own dose, and F is the oral area per milligram over the I V area per milligram. So a larger oral area at the same doses gives a larger F. A larger oral dose that gives the same area gives a smaller F, because each milligram delivered less.", "s": "ba:6", "d": "Absolute and relative bioavailability both compare extent, so both compare areas under the curve, AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small>. The IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> dose is usually smaller than the oral dose, so each area is divided by its own dose, and F is the oral area per milligram over the IV area per milligram. So a larger oral area at the same doses gives a larger F. A larger oral dose that gives the same area gives a smaller F, because each milligram delivered less.", "c": [[1, "F = {{frac:AUC<sub>po</sub>/D<sub>po</sub>|AUC<sub>IV</sub>/D<sub>IV</sub>}}, area per mg against area per mg"], [2, "AUC<sub>po</sub> higher, same doses → F higher"], [3, "D<sub>po</sub> higher, same AUC<sub>po</sub> → F lower"]]}, {"t": "Written out, F abs equals A U C p o over A U C I V, times D I V over D p o, where p o means oral. The I V area is underneath because it is the comparator, and the I V dose is on top because the I V area was divided by it. Areas cancel with areas and milligrams with milligrams, so F has no units. She said, I want you to remember that the I V A U C is going to be in the denominator, right? That is what you are comparing it to.", "s": "ba:6", "d": "Written out, F abs equals AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> p o over AUC IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small>, times D IV over D p o, where p o means oral. The IV area is underneath because it is the comparator, and the IV dose is on top because the IV area was divided by it. Areas cancel with areas and milligrams with milligrams, so F has no units. She said, I want you to remember that the IV AUC is going to be in the denominator, right? That is what you are comparing it to.", "c": [[0, "F<sub>abs</sub> = {{frac:AUC<sub>po</sub>|AUC<sub>IV</sub>}} × {{frac:D<sub>IV</sub>|D<sub>po</sub>}}"], [1, "IV area underneath, IV dose on top"], [2, "{{frac:(mcg/mL)hr|(mcg/mL)hr}} × {{frac:mg|mg}}: no units"]]}, {"t": "For relative bioavailability, F rel equals A U C A over A U C B, times D B over D A. A is the test product, and B the reference, whose area goes underneath; compared to the oral solution makes the solution B. An absolute F cannot be above one, because the I V reference already places the whole dose in the blood. A relative F can be above one, because the test product can do better than the reference. The textbook names a property or excipient of the test formulation, and her example was an old product, Bayer aspirin, that a newer one could exceed. And an F rel of one does not mean an absolute F of one, since the reference is not I V.", "s": "ba:9", "d": "For relative bioavailability, F rel equals AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> A over AUC B, times D B over D A. A is the test product, and B the reference, whose area goes underneath; compared to the oral solution makes the solution B. An absolute F cannot be above one, because the IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> reference already places the whole dose in the blood. A relative F can be above one, because the test product can do better than the reference. The textbook names a property or excipient of the test formulation, and her example was an old product, Bayer aspirin, that a newer one could exceed. And an F rel of one does not mean an absolute F of one, since the reference is not IV.", "c": [[0, "F<sub>rel</sub> = {{frac:AUC<sub>A</sub>|AUC<sub>B</sub>}} × {{frac:D<sub>B</sub>|D<sub>A</sub>}}"], [1, "A = test; B = reference, in the denominator"], [2, "F<sub>abs</sub>: never above 1"], [3, "F<sub>rel</sub>: can be above 1"], [4, "her example: Bayer aspirin, an old product a newer one could exceed"], [5, "F<sub>rel</sub> = 1 does not mean F<sub>abs</sub> = 1"]]}, {"t": "Her first practice problem asks the absolute bioavailability of a two hundred fifty milligram oral tablet. In six volunteers it gave an average A U C of one hundred one point five microgram hours per milliliter, and a one hundred milligram I V bolus gave seventy three point eight. The comparator is I V, so it is absolute, with the I V area underneath and the I V dose on top. The oral area is larger only because its dose is two and a half times as large; per milligram the tablet gives less. F abs is zero point five five, or fifty five percent: each milligram of the tablet delivers fifty five percent of what a milligram given I V delivers.", "s": "ba:7", "d": "Her first practice problem asks the absolute bioavailability of a two hundred fifty milligram oral tablet. In six volunteers it gave an average AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> of one hundred one point five microgram hours per milliliter, and a one hundred milligram IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus gave seventy three point eight. The comparator is IV, so it is absolute, with the IV area underneath and the IV dose on top. The oral area is larger only because its dose is two and a half times as large; per milligram the tablet gives less. F abs is zero point five five, or fifty five percent: each milligram of the tablet delivers fifty five percent of what a milligram given IV delivers.", "c": [[0, "tablet: D<sub>po</sub> = 250 mg, AUC<sub>po</sub> = 101.5 (mcg/mL)hr"], [1, "IV: D<sub>IV</sub> = 100 mg, AUC<sub>IV</sub> = 73.8 (mcg/mL)hr"], [2, "F<sub>abs</sub> = {{frac:101.5 (mcg/mL)hr|73.8 (mcg/mL)hr}} × {{frac:100 mg|250 mg}}"], [2, "= 1.375 × 0.4 = 0.5501"], [3, "per mg: oral {{frac:101.5|250}} = 0.406; IV {{frac:73.8|100}} = 0.738 (mcg/mL)hr per mg"], [3, "{{frac:0.406|0.738}} = 0.55"], [4, "<b>F<sub>abs</sub> = 0.55, or 55%</b>"]]}, {"t": "Her second problem compares the same tablet with five milliliters of an oral solution holding two hundred fifty milligrams, average A U C ninety eight point seven six microgram hours per milliliter. It asks the bioavailability of the tablet compared to the oral solution, so it is relative, and the solution is B, underneath. The doses are equal, so only the area ratio is left, and F rel is one point zero three: the tablet delivers about three percent more than the solution. A solution has little or no formulation effect, so solution against I V shows the drug's own absorption, and tablet against solution isolates the formulation.", "s": "ba:10", "d": "Her second problem compares the same tablet with five milliliters of an oral solution holding two hundred fifty milligrams, average AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> ninety eight point seven six microgram hours per milliliter. It asks the bioavailability of the tablet compared to the oral solution, so it is relative, and the solution is B, underneath. The doses are equal, so only the area ratio is left, and F rel is one point zero three: the tablet delivers about three percent more than the solution. A solution has little or no formulation effect, so solution against IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> shows the drug's own absorption, and tablet against solution isolates the formulation.", "c": [[0, "solution, B: D<sub>B</sub> = 250 mg, AUC<sub>B</sub> = 98.76 (mcg/mL)hr"], [1, "both oral, compared to the solution → F<sub>rel</sub>, solution = B"], [2, "F<sub>rel</sub> = {{frac:101.5 (mcg/mL)hr|98.76 (mcg/mL)hr}} × {{frac:250 mg|250 mg}} = 1.0277 × 1"], [2, "<b>F<sub>rel</sub> = 1.03</b>"], [3, "solution vs IV: the drug's absorption. tablet vs solution: the formulation"]]}, {"t": "The stem may only ask the oral bioavailability, or the bioavailability compared to another product. An I V comparator makes it absolute, and two products, neither of them I V, make it relative. Inverting the ratios is the usual wrong answer; for her first problem it gives one point eight two, and an absolute F above one shows the inversion. Asked in class which F can exceed one, she answered relative. The relative F of one point zero three shows similar extent, not bioequivalence, because no peak times were given, and the peak time is the rate in objective one. Absolute F is what turns an I V dose into an oral dose with the same area, in objective four, and she said she would focus more on absolute bioavailability.", "s": "ba:9", "d": "The stem may only ask the oral bioavailability, or the bioavailability compared to another product. An IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> comparator makes it absolute, and two products, neither of them IV, make it relative. Inverting the ratios is the usual wrong answer; for her first problem it gives one point eight two, and an absolute F above one shows the inversion. Asked in class which F can exceed one, she answered relative. The relative F of one point zero three shows similar extent, not bioequivalence, because no peak times were given, and the peak time is the rate in objective one. Absolute F is what turns an IV dose into an oral dose with the same area, in objective four, and she said she would focus more on absolute bioavailability.", "c": [[0, "the word absolute may be missing"], [1, "IV comparator → F<sub>abs</sub>; two non-IV products → F<sub>rel</sub>"], [2, "wrong: {{frac:73.8|101.5}} × {{frac:250|100}} = 1.82"], [3, "F above 1: relative only"], [4, "F<sub>rel</sub> = 1.03: extent similar, rate unknown → bioequivalence not shown"], [5, "F<sub>abs</sub> → equivalent oral dose (objective four)"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m7-3": {"title": "Factors that influence bioavailability", "segs": [{"t": "Module seven A, objective three. Factors that can influence bioavailability. F is the fraction of a dose that reaches the systemic circulation, with no units, and it is one for an I V dose. An oral dose has to leave the product, dissolve, survive the stomach and gut, cross the gut wall and pass the liver before it reaches the circulation, and F counts only what arrives. These factors change how much arrives, so they can lower F or raise it. Her list has nine entries: in the product, on the way into the circulation, and in the patient.", "s": "ba:14", "d": "Module seven A, objective three. Factors that can influence bioavailability. F is the fraction of a dose that reaches the systemic circulation, with no units, and it is one for an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> dose. An oral dose has to leave the product, dissolve, survive the stomach and gut, cross the gut wall and pass the liver before it reaches the circulation, and F counts only what arrives. These factors change how much arrives, so they can lower F or raise it. Her list has nine entries: in the product, on the way into the circulation, and in the patient.", "c": [[2, "F = fraction reaching the systemic circulation; F<sub>IV</sub> = 1"], [3, "product → dissolved → gut → gut wall → liver → circulation: F counts what arrives"], [4, "factors change how much arrives: F can go down or up"], [5, "the product; on the way in; the patient"]]}, {"t": "First, the product. The physicochemical properties of the drug and the formulation, such as solubility, dissolution rate and particle size, decide how much drug dissolves and can be absorbed, and a better formulation can raise F. A drug that breaks down in acid is partly destroyed in the low p H of the stomach, so stability and p H effects lower its oral F. A prodrug is usually an inactive form that the body converts to the active drug, used to improve solubility or stability, or to raise the active drug's availability by decreasing first pass metabolism. She singled out the physical and chemical properties of the drug, what p H does to it in the body, first pass metabolism, and stability.", "s": "ba:14", "c": [[1, "properties, formulation: how much dissolves and can be absorbed"], [2, "acid-labile drug: degraded in the stomach → F lower"], [3, "prodrug: usually inactive → active; for solubility, stability, or less first-pass"], [4, "most often: properties, pH, first-pass, stability"]]}, {"t": "Next, the way in. Pre systemic metabolism is any metabolism before the drug reaches the systemic circulation, and first pass metabolism is the liver acting on an oral dose on its way from the gut. Absorbed is not the same as available: the textbook's propranolol is almost completely absorbed, but first pass metabolism leaves only about a quarter of it to reach the circulation. Food can lower F, raise it, or leave it unchanged, and a second drug that inhibits the metabolizing enzymes raises F while one that induces them lowers it. Efflux transporters are membrane proteins that pump absorbed drug back out of the cell, so they reduce how much gets through.", "s": "ba:14", "c": [[1, "pre-systemic: any loss before the systemic circulation"], [1, "first-pass: the liver, on an oral dose"], [2, "propranolol: almost all absorbed, about 25% reaches the circulation"], [3, "food: lower, higher or no change"], [3, "enzyme inhibitor → F higher; inducer → F lower"], [4, "efflux transporters: pump drug back out → less gets through"]]}, {"t": "Last, the patient: age and disease state. She said, Yeah, because our bodies change as we age, and yes, everything slows down. The textbook says bioavailability can change with age and with disease state, so these patient factors can raise F or lower it. Her summary names the two causes of low bioavailability she stresses, formulation factors and the first pass effect, and ties bioavailability to the drug's safety and efficacy.", "s": "ba:15", "c": [[2, "age, disease state: can raise or lower F (textbook)"], [3, "low F: formulation factors, first-pass effect"], [3, "→ safety and efficacy"]]}, {"t": "Her first practice problem in objective two gave a tablet an F of zero point five five. So from a one thousand milligram tablet about five hundred fifty milligrams reaches the circulation, while one thousand milligrams given I V is all available. The oral problems earlier in the course had F values of about zero point seven to zero point nine. She said, we're losing almost half the drug giving it orally, right? A low F raises the question of whether oral is the best route, and it raises the oral dose needed to match an I V dose, because that dose must make up for what is lost.", "s": "ba:7", "d": "Her first practice problem in objective two gave a tablet an F of zero point five five. So from a one thousand milligram tablet about five hundred fifty milligrams reaches the circulation, while one thousand milligrams given IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> is all available. The oral problems earlier in the course had F values of about zero point seven to zero point nine. She said, we're losing almost half the drug giving it orally, right? A low F raises the question of whether oral is the best route, and it raises the oral dose needed to match an IV dose, because that dose must make up for what is lost.", "c": [[0, "F = 0.55 (objective two)"], [1, "oral: 0.55 × 1000 mg = <b>550 mg</b>"], [1, "IV: F = 1 → all 1000 mg"], [2, "earlier oral problems: F about 0.7 to 0.9"], [4, "low F → larger oral dose to match IV (objective four)"]]}, {"t": "Her items keep F apart from what happens after the drug arrives. A low F is keyed to first pass metabolism and formulation factors, not to the half life, the volume of distribution, or a low clearance. F is the fraction that gets into the circulation, while clearance and half life act after it gets there, so they change the area under the curve, not F. Write F as zero point five five or fifty five percent. She said, just a little reminder, there should be no leading decimals, OK?", "s": "ba:14", "c": [[1, "low F: first-pass, formulation. not t<sub>½</sub>, V<sub>D</sub> or clearance"], [2, "F: how much enters. Cl, t<sub>½</sub>: act after → change AUC, not F"], [3, "write 0.55 or 55%"]]}], "mins": 4}, "gobj-m7-4": {"title": "Estimating the bioavailability of a dose", "segs": [{"t": "Module seven A, objective four. Estimating the bioavailability of a dose. Clearance, Cl, is the volume of plasma cleared of drug per hour, and it ties a dose to its area under the curve, A U C: the amount that reached the blood equals clearance times the area. For an I V dose that amount is the whole dose, and for an oral dose it is F, the fraction absorbed, times the dose. In one person clearance is the same for both routes, so dividing one line by the other cancels it and leaves F abs equals A U C p o over A U C I V, times D I V over D p o. She said, it doesn't matter which approach you use.", "s": "ba:6", "d": "Module seven A, objective four. Estimating the bioavailability of a dose. Clearance, Cl, is the volume of plasma cleared of drug per hour, and it ties a dose to its area under the curve, AUC: the amount that reached the blood equals clearance times the area. For an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> dose that amount is the whole dose, and for an oral dose it is F, the fraction absorbed, times the dose. In one person clearance is the same for both routes, so dividing one line by the other cancels it and leaves F abs equals AUC p o over AUC IV, times D IV over D p o. She said, it doesn't matter which approach you use.", "c": [[2, "amount reaching the blood = Cl × AUC"], [3, "D<sub>IV</sub> = Cl × AUC<sub>IV</sub>"], [3, "F<sub>abs</sub> × D<sub>po</sub> = Cl × AUC<sub>po</sub>"], [4, "same patient, same Cl → F<sub>abs</sub> = {{frac:AUC<sub>po</sub>|AUC<sub>IV</sub>}} × {{frac:D<sub>IV</sub>|D<sub>po</sub>}}"]]}, {"t": "Because the area is the amount in the blood over clearance, three behaviors follow. With linear kinetics the area is proportional to the dose, so half the dose gives half the area. A higher clearance at the same dose gives a smaller area, because the drug is removed faster, but F does not change, because F is about how much entered. A lower F means less of the oral dose entered, so the oral area is smaller at the same clearance.", "s": "ba:6", "c": [[1, "linear: AUC proportional to dose; ½ dose → ½ AUC"], [2, "Cl higher → AUC smaller; F unchanged"], [3, "F lower → AUC<sub>po</sub> smaller"]]}, {"t": "When a stem gives only the oral area, the I V area is built from clearance. For a one compartment drug clearance is k times V D, the elimination rate constant times the volume of distribution, and k is zero point six nine three over the half life. So A U C I V equals D I V over k times V D, and a half life and a volume are enough. Milligrams over liters per hour gives milligram hours per liter, the unit of an area. This is the Module four line, Cl T equals F D zero over A U C, with F equal to one.", "s": "ba:6", "d": "When a stem gives only the oral area, the IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> area is built from clearance. For a one compartment drug clearance is k times V<sub>D</sub>, the elimination rate constant times the volume of distribution, and k is zero point six nine three over the half life. So AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> IV equals D IV over k times V<sub>D</sub>, and a half life and a volume are enough. Milligrams over liters per hour gives milligram hours per liter, the unit of an area. This is the Module four line, Cl<sub>T</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(total body clearance)</small> equals F D<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(dose)</small> over AUC, with F equal to one.", "c": [[1, "Cl = k × V<sub>D</sub>, k = {{frac:0.693|t<sub>½</sub>}}"], [2, "AUC<sub>IV</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>IV</sub>|k × V<sub>D</sub>}}"], [3, "{{frac:mg|L/hr}} = (mg/L)hr"], [4, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|AUC}}, F = 1"]], "f": {"k": "bolus1_steps", "at": [[1, 4]]}}, {"t": "Her in class question one asks the F of a five hundred milligram oral tablet with an area of seventy milligram hours per liter, and the area after a two hundred fifty milligram I V bolus. The same volunteer's five hundred milligram I V bolus had a half life of three hours and a V D of twenty five liters but no area, so the I V area comes from clearance. The doses are equal, so F is the area ratio, zero point eight zero eight five, about eighty one percent, and the clearance route gives the same. Half the I V dose gives half the area, forty three point two nine milligram hours per liter, because the kinetics are linear.", "s": "ba:6", "d": "Her in class question one asks the F of a five hundred milligram oral tablet with an area of seventy milligram hours per liter, and the area after a two hundred fifty milligram IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus. The same volunteer's five hundred milligram IV bolus had a half life of three hours and a V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> of twenty five liters but no area, so the IV area comes from clearance. The doses are equal, so F is the area ratio, zero point eight zero eight five, about eighty one percent, and the clearance route gives the same. Half the IV dose gives half the area, forty three point two nine milligram hours per liter, because the kinetics are linear.", "c": [[0, "tablet: D<sub>po</sub> = 500 mg, AUC<sub>po</sub> = 70 (mg/L)hr"], [1, "IV: D<sub>IV</sub> = 500 mg, t<sub>½</sub> = 3 hr, V<sub>D</sub> = 25 L"], [1, "k = {{frac:0.693|3 hr}} = 0.231 hr<sup>−1</sup>; Cl = 25 L × 0.231 hr<sup>−1</sup> = 5.775 L/hr"], [1, "AUC<sub>IV</sub> = {{frac:500 mg|5.775 L/hr}} = 86.58 (mg/L)hr"], [2, "<b>F = {{frac:70|86.58}} = 0.8085, about 81%</b>"], [2, "F = {{frac:5.775 L/hr × 70 (mg/L)hr|500 mg}} = 0.8085 ✓"], [3, "<b>AUC = {{frac:250 mg|5.775 L/hr}} = 43.29 (mg/L)hr</b>, half of 86.58"]]}, {"t": "Question three gives the I V curve itself: after a five hundred milligram bolus, the plasma concentration, C p, equals fifty e to the minus zero point two t. Matching the I V bolus line, C zero is fifty milligrams per liter and k is zero point two per hour, so V D is ten liters and clearance two liters per hour. The I V area is two hundred fifty milligram hours per liter, so a five hundred milligram tablet with an area of one hundred eighty eight milligram hours per liter has an F of zero point seven five two, seventy five percent.", "s": "ba:6", "d": "Question three gives the IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> curve itself: after a five hundred milligram bolus, the plasma concentration, C<sub>p</sub>, equals fifty e to the minus zero point two t. Matching the IV bolus line, C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> is fifty milligrams per liter and k is zero point two per hour, so V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> is ten liters and clearance two liters per hour. The IV area is two hundred fifty milligram hours per liter, so a five hundred milligram tablet with an area of one hundred eighty eight milligram hours per liter has an F of zero point seven five two, seventy five percent.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = 50e<sup>−0.2t</sup> → C<sub>0</sub> = 50 mg/L, k = 0.2 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [1, "V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:500 mg|50 mg/L}} = 10 L; Cl = 0.2 hr<sup>−1</sup> × 10 L = 2 L/hr"], [2, "AUC<sub>IV</sub> = {{frac:500 mg|2 L/hr}} = 250 (mg/L)hr"], [2, "<b>F = {{frac:188 (mg/L)hr|250 (mg/L)hr}} = 0.752 = 75%</b>"]]}, {"t": "Turned around, F gives the oral dose that matches an I V dose. Same extent means equal areas, and with the same clearance equal areas mean the same amount reached the blood, so F times the oral dose equals the I V dose. So the oral dose is the I V dose divided by F. F is underneath, so the lower F is, the larger the oral dose, and an F of one makes the two doses equal. She said, You expect that you're gonna have a larger, um, P O dose, right?", "s": "ba:6", "d": "Turned around, F gives the oral dose that matches an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> dose. Same extent means equal areas, and with the same clearance equal areas mean the same amount reached the blood, so F times the oral dose equals the IV dose. So the oral dose is the IV dose divided by F. F is underneath, so the lower F is, the larger the oral dose, and an F of one makes the two doses equal. She said, You expect that you're gonna have a larger, um, P O dose, right?", "c": [[1, "AUC<sub>po</sub> = AUC<sub>IV</sub> → F × D<sub>po</sub> = D<sub>IV</sub>"], [2, "D<sub>po</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>IV</sub>|F}}"], [3, "F lower → D<sub>po</sub> larger; F = 1 → D<sub>po</sub> = D<sub>IV</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Her first example took the tablet from her practice problem, with an F of zero point five five, and asked the oral dose equivalent to one hundred milligrams I V. It is about one hundred eighty two milligrams, almost twice the I V dose, because almost half of each oral dose is lost. That number is then rounded to a dose that makes sense. She said, We might round it to two hundred if there's a one seventy five or something that makes sense as opposed to one eighty one.", "s": "ba:7", "d": "Her first example took the tablet from her practice problem, with an F of zero point five five, and asked the oral dose equivalent to one hundred milligrams IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small>. It is about one hundred eighty two milligrams, almost twice the IV dose, because almost half of each oral dose is lost. That number is then rounded to a dose that makes sense. She said, We might round it to two hundred if there's a one seventy five or something that makes sense as opposed to one eighty one.", "c": [[0, "D<sub>IV</sub> = 100 mg, F = 0.55"], [1, "D<sub>po</sub> = {{frac:100 mg|0.55}} = 181.8 mg ≈ 182 mg"], [3, "<b>≈ 200 mg</b>, a dose that makes sense"]]}, {"t": "Question two: ciprofloxacin has an oral immediate release F of seventy percent, and the oral dose with the same extent as a four hundred milligram I V bolus is asked. Four hundred milligrams over zero point seven zero is five hundred seventy one point four three milligrams, not a real tablet, since the strengths she looked up were two hundred fifty and five hundred. She wrote five hundred seventy five, six hundred or five hundred seventy beside it, and when a stem asks for the nearest five, five hundred seventy is the answer, as in her review. The rounding instruction in the stem comes first, and a tablet strength that exists is used only when the stem gives no rounding instruction.", "s": "ba:6", "d": "Question two: ciprofloxacin has an oral immediate release F of seventy percent, and the oral dose with the same extent as a four hundred milligram IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus is asked. Four hundred milligrams over zero point seven zero is five hundred seventy one point four three milligrams, not a real tablet, since the strengths she looked up were two hundred fifty and five hundred. She wrote five hundred seventy five, six hundred or five hundred seventy beside it, and when a stem asks for the nearest five, five hundred seventy is the answer, as in her review. The rounding instruction in the stem comes first, and a tablet strength that exists is used only when the stem gives no rounding instruction.", "c": [[0, "F = 0.70; D<sub>IV</sub> = 400 mg"], [1, "D<sub>po</sub> = {{frac:400 mg|0.70}} = 571.43 mg"], [1, "marketed: 250 and 500 mg"], [2, "<b>D<sub>po</sub> ≈ 575, 600 or 570 mg; nearest 5: 570 mg</b>"], [3, "rounding: as the stem says"]]}, {"t": "Her poll is the model item: a five hundred milligram tablet with an area of one hundred fifteen milligram hours per liter, against a four hundred milligram I V bolus with one hundred thirty two. With the I V area underneath and the I V dose on top, F is zero point six nine seven, seventy percent. Inverting the dose ratio gives one point zero eight nine, and the area ratio alone zero point eight seven one; both are wrong. When a stem has two different doses, as in her Exam two review, F uses the oral dose that made the oral area, five hundred milligrams, and the equivalent oral dose is the replaced I V dose, nine hundred milligrams, divided by F: twelve hundred milligrams.", "s": "ba:6", "d": "Her poll is the model item: a five hundred milligram tablet with an area of one hundred fifteen milligram hours per liter, against a four hundred milligram IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus with one hundred thirty two. With the IV area underneath and the IV dose on top, F is zero point six nine seven, seventy percent. Inverting the dose ratio gives one point zero eight nine, and the area ratio alone zero point eight seven one; both are wrong. When a stem has two different doses, as in her Exam two review, F uses the oral dose that made the oral area, five hundred milligrams, and the equivalent oral dose is the replaced IV dose, nine hundred milligrams, divided by F: twelve hundred milligrams.", "c": [[0, "tablet: 500 mg, 115 (mg/L)hr; IV: 400 mg, 132 (mg/L)hr"], [1, "<b>F = {{frac:115|132}} × {{frac:400|500}} = 0.697 = 70%</b>"], [2, "wrong: {{frac:115|132}} × {{frac:500|400}} = 1.089; {{frac:115|132}} = 0.871"], [3, "her review: AUC<sub>IV</sub> for 500 mg = {{frac:500 mg|1.86 L/hr}} = 268.8 (mg/L)hr; F = {{frac:202|268.8}} = 0.75"], [3, "IV dose replaced 900 mg: {{frac:900 mg|0.75}} = 1200 mg"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m7b-1": {"title": "Response rises with dose, up to a maximum", "segs": [{"t": "Module seven B, objective one. How the response rises with dose, up to a maximum. Pharmacokinetics, P K, relates the drug concentration to time, and pharmacodynamics, P D, relates the concentration at the site of action, the receptor, to the pharmacologic response that is observed. She said, Pharmacodynamics is the relationship between drug concentration and pharmacological response. The link between them is also called the exposure response, concentration response or concentration effect relationship, and it is used in drug approval to judge safety, efficacy and benefit risk, and to derive dosing information.", "s": "pd:3", "c": [[2, "PK: concentration against time"], [2, "PD: concentration at the receptor → observed response"], [4, "PK–PD = exposure-response = concentration-response = concentration-effect"], [4, "drug approval: safety, efficacy, benefit-risk, dosing"]]}, {"t": "As the dose increases, the concentration at the receptor site increases, and the response increases, but only up to a maximum effect, E max. The maximum comes when all the receptors are saturated, that is, all are occupied by drug. Past that point a higher dose still raises the concentration at the receptor site, but with no free receptors left the response does not rise. Plotted against dose on an ordinary axis, the response rises steeply at low doses and then flattens. So the same added dose gives a large gain in response at low doses, where many receptors are free, and the smallest gain near the maximum.", "s": "pd:4", "c": [[0, "dose higher → concentration at the receptor higher → response higher, up to E<sub>max</sub>"], [1, "E<sub>max</sub>: all receptors saturated"], [2, "past saturation: concentration still rises, response does not"], [3, "ordinary dose axis: steep, then flat"], [4, "same dose step: large gain low, smallest gain near E<sub>max</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Plotted against the log of the dose, the dose response data form a sigmoid, an S shaped curve, flat at the bottom, steep in the middle and flat at the top. The top of the S is the maximum, where the receptors are saturated. The middle of the S is close to a straight line, so there the effect, E, is a straight line function of the log of the concentration, C: E equals m log C plus e, with m the slope and e a constant. A larger m means a larger change in effect for each tenfold change in concentration.", "s": "pd:5", "c": [[0, "log dose axis: sigmoid (S) curve"], [1, "top: all receptors saturated"], [2, "middle: E = m log C + e, slope m"], [3, "m larger → more effect per tenfold change in C"]]}, {"t": "After an I V bolus the concentration falls by the same fraction each hour, so its log falls in a straight line. In the straight middle of the S curve, the effect is m log C plus a constant, so it also falls in a straight line, the same amount each hour. The relation is E equals E zero minus k m t over two point three, where E is the effect at t hours, E zero the effect at time zero, k the elimination rate constant per hour, and m the slope. k and m are both on the top, so faster elimination or a steeper slope makes the effect fall by more each hour. The two point three is there because the effect line uses base ten logs.", "s": "pd:5", "d": "After an IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus the concentration falls by the same fraction each hour, so its log falls in a straight line. In the straight middle of the S curve, the effect is m log C plus a constant, so it also falls in a straight line, the same amount each hour. The relation is E equals E zero minus k m t over two point three, where E is the effect at t hours, E zero the effect at time zero, k the elimination rate constant per hour, and m the slope. k and m are both on the top, so faster elimination or a steeper slope makes the effect fall by more each hour. The two point three is there because the effect line uses base ten logs.", "c": [[0, "log C = log C<sub>0</sub> − {{frac:kt|2.3}}"], [1, "E = m log C + e → a straight line in time"], [2, "E = E<sub>0</sub> − {{frac:kmt|2.3}}"], [2, "k in hr<sup>−1</sup>, t in hr, m = slope of E against log C"], [3, "k or m larger → effect falls faster"], [4, "2.3: base-10 logs"]]}, {"t": "On a plasma level against time curve, the effect is read against the minimum effective concentration, M E C or C eff, the lowest level at which it appears. Onset is the time at which the rising level first reaches the M E C. She said, when does the drug reach a therapeutic concentration? That's our onset. Duration is how long the level stays at or above the M E C, a time, and intensity is how far the peak rises above it, a difference of two concentrations. Above the minimum toxic concentration, M T C, toxicity appears, and the range from the M E C to the M T C is the therapeutic window.", "s": "pd:4", "c": [[0, "read against the MEC (C<sub>eff</sub>), a concentration"], [1, "onset: the level first reaches the MEC"], [4, "duration (t<sub>eff</sub>): time at or above the MEC, in hr"], [4, "intensity: peak concentration minus MEC, in mg/L"], [5, "therapeutic window: MEC to MTC"]]}, {"t": "In the concept items, the response is not proportional to the dose; it rises to a maximum, and the same added dose gives the smallest gain near that maximum. The response stops rising because all receptors are saturated, not because the concentration stops rising or elimination speeds up. Against log dose the curve is an S; rising steeply and then flattening is the shape against dose on an ordinary axis. In the effect line, m is a slope, not a mass or a concentration, and the effect falls in a straight line, not by a fixed fraction each hour. Absolute bioavailability is a P K comparison, not a use of exposure response information. Onset, intensity and duration depend on the dose and the pharmacokinetics; objective two works out duration.", "s": "pd:11", "c": [[0, "not proportional at every dose: up to a maximum"], [0, "smallest gain: near the maximum"], [1, "reason: receptors saturated"], [2, "log axis: S. ordinary axis: steep, then flat"], [3, "m: a slope. effect: straight line in time"], [4, "absolute bioavailability: a PK comparison"], [5, "onset, intensity, duration: depend on dose and PK → objective two"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m7b-2": {"title": "Dose, half life and duration of activity", "segs": [{"t": "Module seven B, objective two. How dose and half life change the duration of the response. The duration of activity, t eff, is how long a single I V bolus keeps the plasma level at or above C eff, the minimum effective concentration, also called the M E C. Every one of her problems is a one compartment open model with first order elimination, so after the bolus the level falls by the same fraction each hour. C zero is the level just after the bolus, the dose D zero divided by the volume of distribution, V D.", "s": "pd:6", "d": "Module seven B, objective two. How dose and half life change the duration of the response. The duration of activity, t eff, is how long a single IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus keeps the plasma level at or above C eff, the minimum effective concentration, also called the M E C. Every one of her problems is a one compartment open model with first order elimination, so after the bolus the level falls by the same fraction each hour. C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> is the level just after the bolus, the dose D<sub>0</sub> divided by the volume of distribution, V<sub>D</sub>.", "c": [[2, "t<sub>eff</sub>: time the level stays at or above C<sub>eff</sub> after one IV bolus"], [3, "one compartment, first order: the same fraction lost each hour"], [4, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}}"]]}, {"t": "With first order elimination the level halves every half life. So t eff is the number of halvings from C zero down to C eff, times the half life. Doubling the dose doubles C zero, and a level twice as high needs exactly one more halving to reach the same C eff. So each doubling of the dose adds one half life to t eff; it does not double t eff, and four times the dose adds two half lives. She wrote, each time we double the dose, we'll get an additional half life. A larger V D gives a lower C zero, and a higher C eff leaves fewer halvings, so either one shortens t eff.", "s": "pd:9", "d": "With first order elimination the level halves every half life. So t eff is the number of halvings from C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> down to C eff, times the half life. Doubling the dose doubles C<sub>0</sub>, and a level twice as high needs exactly one more halving to reach the same C eff. So each doubling of the dose adds one half life to t eff; it does not double t eff, and four times the dose adds two half lives. She wrote, each time we double the dose, we'll get an additional half life. A larger V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> gives a lower C<sub>0</sub>, and a higher C eff leaves fewer halvings, so either one shortens t eff.", "c": [[0, "every t<sub>½</sub>: the level halves"], [1, "t<sub>eff</sub> = number of halvings from C<sub>0</sub> to C<sub>eff</sub> × t<sub>½</sub>"], [2, "2 × D<sub>0</sub> → 2 × C<sub>0</sub> → one more halving"], [3, "each doubling: + 1 t<sub>½</sub>; 4 × D<sub>0</sub>: + 2 t<sub>½</sub>"], [5, "V<sub>D</sub> larger or C<sub>eff</sub> higher → t<sub>eff</sub> shorter"]], "f": {"k": "bolus1_steps", "at": [[0, 5]]}}, {"t": "The half life sets the length of each halving. Doubling the half life at the same dose keeps the same number of halvings, each twice as long, so t eff doubles. Liver or kidney disease that lowers elimination prolongs the half life, so the drug is retained longer, and both the duration of activity and the possibility of toxicity increase. The dose works differently: plotted against dose, t eff rises steeply at low doses and then flattens, because each added milligram adds less duration, and it takes a doubling of the dose to add one more half life.", "s": "pd:10", "c": [[1, "2 × t<sub>½</sub>, same dose → 2 × t<sub>eff</sub>"], [1, "e.g. textbook table: 2 × t<sub>½</sub>: 3.24 → 6.48 hr; 2 × dose: 3.24 → 3.98 hr"], [2, "liver or kidney disease → t<sub>½</sub> longer → t<sub>eff</sub> longer, toxicity risk higher"], [3, "t<sub>eff</sub> against dose: steep, then flat; each doubling adds one t<sub>½</sub>"]]}, {"t": "t eff is when the I V bolus line, C zero e to the minus k t, reaches C eff. In natural logs, t eff is the natural log of C zero over C eff, divided by k. With the half life, t eff is one point four four, one over zero point six nine three, times t half times the natural log of D zero over V D times C eff. The dose is inside the log, so doubling it adds the natural log of two over k, one half life, while the half life multiplies everything, so t eff is in proportion to it. The base ten form gives slightly less than the natural log form, because its two point three rounds two point three zero three.", "s": "pd:6", "d": "t eff is when the IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus line, C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> e to the minus k t, reaches C eff. In natural logs, t eff is the natural log of C<sub>0</sub> over C eff, divided by k. With the half life, t eff is one point four four, one over zero point six nine three, times t half times the natural log of D<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(dose)</small> over V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> times C eff. The dose is inside the log, so doubling it adds the natural log of two over k, one half life, while the half life multiplies everything, so t eff is in proportion to it. The base ten form gives slightly less than the natural log form, because its two point three rounds two point three zero three.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>eff</sub> = C<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−kt</sup>, solve for t"], [1, "t<sub>eff</sub> = {{frac:ln(C<sub>0</sub>/C<sub>eff</sub>)|k}}, in hr"], [2, "t<sub>eff</sub> = 1.44 t<sub>½</sub> ln({{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub> C<sub>eff</sub>}}), 1.44 = {{frac:1|0.693}}"], [3, "2 × D<sub>0</sub> → + {{frac:ln 2|k}} = + {{frac:0.693|k}} = + 1 t<sub>½</sub>"], [3, "t<sub>eff</sub> proportional to t<sub>½</sub>"], [4, "t<sub>eff</sub> = {{frac:2.3(log C<sub>0</sub> − log C<sub>eff</sub>)|k}}: 26.32 hr against 26.35 hr"]]}, {"t": "Her review poll: a one gram I V bolus, a V D of twelve point five liters, a half life of four hours and an M E C of twenty milligrams per liter. C zero is eighty milligrams per liter, which halves to forty and then to twenty, two halvings, so t eff is eight hours, her key. With five hundred milligrams, C zero is forty milligrams per liter, one halving, four hours, her key. Halving the dose halved the duration only because forty is exactly twice the M E C. A two gram dose, which she did not ask, would start at one hundred sixty milligrams per liter, three halvings, twelve hours, not sixteen, so doubling the dose does not double t eff, as her poll key says.", "s": "pd:12", "d": "Her review poll: a one gram IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus, a V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> of twelve point five liters, a half life of four hours and an M E C of twenty milligrams per liter. C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> is eighty milligrams per liter, which halves to forty and then to twenty, two halvings, so t eff is eight hours, her key. With five hundred milligrams, C<sub>0</sub> is forty milligrams per liter, one halving, four hours, her key. Halving the dose halved the duration only because forty is exactly twice the M E C. A two gram dose, which she did not ask, would start at one hundred sixty milligrams per liter, three halvings, twelve hours, not sixteen, so doubling the dose does not double t eff, as her poll key says.", "c": [[0, "D<sub>0</sub> = 1000 mg, V<sub>D</sub> = 12.5 L, t<sub>½</sub> = 4 hr, MEC = 20 mg/L"], [1, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:1000 mg|12.5 L}} = 80 mg/L"], [1, "80 → 40 → 20: 2 halvings × 4 hr = <b>8 hr</b>"], [2, "500 mg: C<sub>0</sub> = 40 mg/L → 20: 1 halving = <b>4 hr</b>"], [3, "40 mg/L = 2 × MEC: the 500 mg t<sub>eff</sub> is exactly 1 t<sub>½</sub>"], [4, "e.g. 2 g: C<sub>0</sub> = 160 mg/L, 3 halvings = 12 hr, not 16"], [4, "2 × dose → 2 × t<sub>eff</sub>: <b>False</b>"]], "f": {"k": "teff_halvings_steps", "at": [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 3], [4, 4]]}}, {"t": "Her slide practice problem gives an antibiotic an M E C of zero point two micrograms per milliliter, the same number in milligrams per liter, a V D of twenty four liters and a k of zero point one five per hour. For a two hundred fifty milligram dose, k is given, so the natural log form is quickest. C zero is ten point four two milligrams per liter, about fifty two times the M E C, and t eff is twenty six point three five hours. Five hundred milligrams gives thirty point nine seven hours, four point six two hours more, which is one half life, and one thousand milligrams adds a second half life, to about thirty five point six hours.", "s": "pd:7", "d": "Her slide practice problem gives an antibiotic an M E C of zero point two micrograms per milliliter, the same number in milligrams per liter, a V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> of twenty four liters and a k of zero point one five per hour. For a two hundred fifty milligram dose, k is given, so the natural log form is quickest. C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> is ten point four two milligrams per liter, about fifty two times the M E C, and t eff is twenty six point three five hours. Five hundred milligrams gives thirty point nine seven hours, four point six two hours more, which is one half life, and one thousand milligrams adds a second half life, to about thirty five point six hours.", "c": [[0, "MEC 0.2 mcg/mL = 0.2 mg/L; V<sub>D</sub> 24 L; k 0.15 hr<sup>−1</sup>"], [1, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:250 mg|24 L}} = 10.42 mg/L; {{frac:10.42|0.2}} = 52.08"], [2, "<b>t<sub>eff</sub> = {{frac:ln 52.08|0.15 hr<sup>−1</sup>}} = {{frac:3.953|0.15}} = 26.35 hr</b>"], [3, "500 mg: C<sub>0</sub> = 20.83 mg/L → 30.97 hr; + 4.62 hr = {{frac:0.693|0.15}} = 1 t<sub>½</sub>"], [3, "1000 mg: 35.59 hr (her working 35.60) = 26.35 + 2 × 4.62"]]}, {"t": "Her follow up on the slide problem asks the dose that doubles t eff, from twenty six point three five to fifty two point seven hours. Solved for the dose, D zero equals C eff times V D times e to the plus k t eff. The exponent is positive because the dose must be larger than C eff times V D, four point eight milligrams, the amount that would give exactly the M E C at the start. The target time is in the exponent, so each added half life of duration doubles the dose. The answer is about thirteen grams, fifty two times the two hundred fifty milligram dose, for twice the duration; doubling the half life at two hundred fifty milligrams would also double t eff.", "s": "pd:7", "d": "Her follow up on the slide problem asks the dose that doubles t eff, from twenty six point three five to fifty two point seven hours. Solved for the dose, D<sub>0</sub> equals C eff times V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> times e to the plus k t eff. The exponent is positive because the dose must be larger than C eff times V<sub>D</sub>, four point eight milligrams, the amount that would give exactly the M E C at the start. The target time is in the exponent, so each added half life of duration doubles the dose. The answer is about thirteen grams, fifty two times the two hundred fifty milligram dose, for twice the duration; doubling the half life at two hundred fifty milligrams would also double t eff.", "c": [[0, "target t<sub>eff</sub> = 2 × 26.35 = 52.7 hr"], [1, "D<sub>0</sub> = C<sub>eff</sub> V<sub>D</sub> e<sup>kt<sub>eff</sub></sup>"], [2, "C<sub>eff</sub> V<sub>D</sub> = (0.2 mg/L)(24 L) = 4.8 mg"], [3, "e<sup>(0.15)(52.7)</sup> = 2710.8"], [4, "<b>D<sub>0</sub> = 4.8 mg × 2710.8 = 13,012 mg ≈ 13 g</b>, about 52 × 250 mg"], [4, "2 × t<sub>½</sub> at 250 mg: t<sub>eff</sub> = 52.7 hr"]]}, {"t": "Her practice three uses the half life form: a twenty milligram per kilogram I V bolus, a V D of zero point three liters per kilogram, a half life of three point six hours and an M E C of seven point two milligrams per liter. The kilograms cancel, so C zero is sixty six point six seven milligrams per liter, and t eff is eleven point five six hours. The doubled dose gives fifteen point one six hours, three point six hours more, one half life.", "s": "pd:9", "d": "Her practice three uses the half life form: a twenty milligram per kilogram IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus, a V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> of zero point three liters per kilogram, a half life of three point six hours and an M E C of seven point two milligrams per liter. The kilograms cancel, so C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> is sixty six point six seven milligrams per liter, and t eff is eleven point five six hours. The doubled dose gives fifteen point one six hours, three point six hours more, one half life.", "c": [[0, "20 mg/kg, V<sub>D</sub> 0.3 L/kg, t<sub>½</sub> 3.6 hr, MEC 7.2 mcg/mL = 7.2 mg/L"], [1, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:20 mg/kg|0.3 L/kg}} = 66.67 mg/L"], [1, "<b>t<sub>eff</sub> = ln({{frac:66.67|7.2}}) × {{frac:3.6 hr|0.693}} = 2.226 × 5.195 = 11.56 hr</b>"], [2, "2 × dose: C<sub>0</sub> = 133.3 mg/L → <b>15.16 hr</b>, + 3.6 hr = 1 t<sub>½</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Her poll asked, true or false, whether doubling the I V bolus dose of a linear, first order drug gives a two fold increase in the duration of activity, and her key is false. On her quiz, doubling the dose adds one half life to the duration; it doubles neither the duration nor the half life. C zero does double with the dose, but because the dose is inside the log, the duration rises by only one half life. Other wrong answers she sees: changing the number when converting the M E C from micrograms per milliliter to milligrams per liter, e to the minus k t instead of e to the plus k t in the dose equation, and a base ten log in the natural log form.", "s": "pd:12", "d": "Her poll asked, true or false, whether doubling the IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus dose of a linear, first order drug gives a two fold increase in the duration of activity, and her key is false. On her quiz, doubling the dose adds one half life to the duration; it doubles neither the duration nor the half life. C<sub>0</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(concentration at time zero)</small> does double with the dose, but because the dose is inside the log, the duration rises by only one half life. Other wrong answers she sees: changing the number when converting the M E C from micrograms per milliliter to milligrams per liter, e to the minus k t instead of e to the plus k t in the dose equation, and a base ten log in the natural log form.", "c": [[0, "poll: 2 × dose → 2 × t<sub>eff</sub>? <b>False</b>"], [1, "2 × dose: + 1 t<sub>½</sub>; t<sub>½</sub> unchanged"], [2, "C<sub>0</sub> doubles, e.g. 10.42 → 20.83 mg/L"], [3, "wrong: MEC number changed; e<sup>−kt</sup> in the D<sub>0</sub> line; log in the ln form"]]}], "mins": 7}, "gobj-m8-1": {"title": "Linear versus nonlinear pharmacokinetics", "segs": [{"t": "Module eight, objective one. Linear versus nonlinear pharmacokinetics. In linear pharmacokinetics the parameters, such as the half life and the clearance, do not change when different or multiple doses of a drug are given. In nonlinear pharmacokinetics, increased doses or chronic administration cause deviations from the profile seen with single doses, and the half life and the clearance change. She said, Linear, our parameters don't change, nonlinear, they do. Phenytoin is the prototype nonlinear drug in this course.", "s": "nonlin:3", "c": [[2, "linear: t<sub>½</sub>, Cl unchanged with dose"], [3, "nonlinear: higher or repeated doses → t<sub>½</sub>, Cl change"], [5, "Ex. phenytoin"]]}, {"t": "Most drugs are eliminated by a first order process, which is linear. The amount removed per unit of time is proportional to the amount present, so the fraction removed per unit of time stays the same at every level. Because that fraction is fixed, the half life and the clearance are the same at every dose. So twice the dose gives twice the level at every time and twice the A U C, the area under the curve of level against time, and repeated doses can be predicted from the first dose by superposition: each new dose adds its own curve to what is left of the earlier ones.", "s": "nonlin:5", "d": "Most drugs are eliminated by a first order process, which is linear. The amount removed per unit of time is proportional to the amount present, so the fraction removed per unit of time stays the same at every level. Because that fraction is fixed, the half life and the clearance are the same at every dose. So twice the dose gives twice the level at every time and twice the AUC, the area under the curve of level against time, and repeated doses can be predicted from the first dose by superposition: each new dose adds its own curve to what is left of the earlier ones.", "c": [[1, "first order: amount removed per time ∝ amount present; fraction per time constant"], [2, "→ t<sub>½</sub>, Cl the same at every dose"], [3, "2 × dose → 2 × level, 2 × AUC (AUC ∝ dose); superposition"]]}, {"t": "Nonlinear pharmacokinetics comes from saturation of an enzyme or carrier system that can only work so fast. V max, the fastest elimination rate it can reach, is a rate: an amount or concentration per unit of time. K M is the concentration at which the rate is half of V max, and the drug level, C p, is low or high only compared with K M. Well below K M, the rate is a small part of V max, so the enzymes have spare capacity, and twice the level gives about twice the rate: first order. Well above K M, the enzymes are saturated: more drug raises the rate only a little, so it stays near V max: zero order. She said, That's as fast as it can go.", "s": "nonlin:7", "d": "Nonlinear pharmacokinetics comes from saturation of an enzyme or carrier system that can only work so fast. V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small>, the fastest elimination rate it can reach, is a rate: an amount or concentration per unit of time. K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> is the concentration at which the rate is half of V<sub>max</sub>, and the drug level, C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small>, is low or high only compared with K<sub>M</sub>. Well below K<sub>M</sub>, the rate is a small part of V<sub>max</sub>, so the enzymes have spare capacity, and twice the level gives about twice the rate: first order. Well above K<sub>M</sub>, the enzymes are saturated: more drug raises the rate only a little, so it stays near V<sub>max</sub>: zero order. She said, That's as fast as it can go.", "c": [[0, "cause: a saturable enzyme or carrier"], [1, "V<sub>max</sub>: the fastest elimination rate, a rate (her example 12 mg/kg/day)"], [2, "K<sub>M</sub>: the concentration at which rate = ½ V<sub>max</sub> (her example 10 mg/L)"], [3, "C<sub>p</sub> ≪ K<sub>M</sub>: twice C<sub>p</sub>, about twice the rate → first order"], [4, "C<sub>p</sub> ≫ K<sub>M</sub>: rate ≈ V<sub>max</sub> → zero order"]], "f": {"k": "nl_rate_steps", "at": [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 3], [3, 4], [4, 5]]}}, {"t": "When a higher dose raises the level toward saturation of the enzymes, the amount removed per day still rises, but by a smaller fraction than the level rises, so the fraction of the drug in the body removed each day falls. With a smaller fraction removed each day, the half life lengthens and the clearance falls as the dose increases. More of each dose stays in the body, so on her plot of A U C against dose, curve A, the saturable drug, follows the straight line of a drug whose A U C is proportional to dose, curve C, up to a certain dose and then rises above it. Saturation can occur in absorption, distribution, metabolism or excretion, and this module deals with elimination.", "s": "nonlin:5", "d": "When a higher dose raises the level toward saturation of the enzymes, the amount removed per day still rises, but by a smaller fraction than the level rises, so the fraction of the drug in the body removed each day falls. With a smaller fraction removed each day, the half life lengthens and the clearance falls as the dose increases. More of each dose stays in the body, so on her plot of AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> against dose, curve A, the saturable drug, follows the straight line of a drug whose AUC is proportional to dose, curve C, up to a certain dose and then rises above it. Saturation can occur in absorption, distribution, metabolism or excretion, and this module deals with elimination.", "c": [[0, "into saturation: rate rises less than C<sub>p</sub> → fraction per day ↓"], [1, "t<sub>½</sub> ↑, Cl ↓ as dose ↑"], [2, "curve A: above the AUC ∝ dose line past a certain dose"], [3, "saturable steps: absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion; here: elimination"]]}, {"t": "On her log plot of level against time, curves A and B are one saturable drug given as a single I V bolus at a high and a low dose, and curve C is a different drug with first order elimination. B is a straight line like C, because at levels well below K M elimination is first order. A starts at levels where the enzymes are saturated, so at first a smaller fraction is removed each hour and A falls less steeply than B; as the level falls, the fraction removed each hour rises and A curves downward. Once A has fallen to levels well below K M it becomes straight, with the same terminal slope as B.", "s": "nonlin:4", "d": "On her log plot of level against time, curves A and B are one saturable drug given as a single IV <small class=\"gwgl\">(intravenous)</small> bolus at a high and a low dose, and curve C is a different drug with first order elimination. B is a straight line like C, because at levels well below K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> elimination is first order. A starts at levels where the enzymes are saturated, so at first a smaller fraction is removed each hour and A falls less steeply than B; as the level falls, the fraction removed each hour rises and A curves downward. Once A has fallen to levels well below K<sub>M</sub> it becomes straight, with the same terminal slope as B.", "c": [[0, "A: high dose; B: low dose; C: a first-order drug"], [1, "B straight, like C: C<sub>p</sub> ≪ K<sub>M</sub>, first order"], [2, "A: saturated at first, smaller fraction per hour → bends"], [3, "terminal slopes A = B"]]}, {"t": "Her slide example shows what this does at steady state, where the daily dose rate equals the elimination rate. At six milligrams per kilogram per day, the steady state level was ten milligrams per liter. Raising the dose rate to eight is one third more, so for a linear drug the level would also rise by one third, to about thirteen milligrams per liter. Instead it rose to twenty milligrams per liter, double, because the enzymes were close to saturated: to remove eight instead of six milligrams per kilogram each day, the level had to climb much higher. She said, Small changes in dose will lead to big changes in concentration.", "s": "nonlin:14", "c": [[0, "steady state: dose rate = elimination rate"], [1, "6 mg/kg/day → C<sub>ss</sub> 10 mg/L"], [2, "dose rate × {{frac:8|6}} = × 1.33; linear: 10 mg/L × 1.33 = 13.3 mg/L"], [3, "<b>8 mg/kg/day → 20 mg/L: dose rate × 1.33, level × 2</b>"], [4, "small dose change → large level change"]], "f": {"k": "nl_css_steps", "at": [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 3], [3, 4]]}}, {"t": "Her written question asks the difference between linear and nonlinear pharmacokinetics. The answer is the parameters: in linear pharmacokinetics they do not change with dose, and in nonlinear pharmacokinetics increased doses or chronic administration change them. Her signs of saturation are a half life that changes with dose and an A U C that is not in proportion to the dose, plus two more: other drugs needing the same enzyme or carrier can change the saturation, and the ratio of the metabolites can change with dose. In her quiz the true statement was that the half life changes as the dose is increased; parameters that do not change, superposition, and an A U C in proportion to the dose each describe a linear drug.", "s": "nonlin:6", "d": "Her written question asks the difference between linear and nonlinear pharmacokinetics. The answer is the parameters: in linear pharmacokinetics they do not change with dose, and in nonlinear pharmacokinetics increased doses or chronic administration change them. Her signs of saturation are a half life that changes with dose and an AUC <small class=\"gwgl\">(area under the curve)</small> that is not in proportion to the dose, plus two more: other drugs needing the same enzyme or carrier can change the saturation, and the ratio of the metabolites can change with dose. In her quiz the true statement was that the half life changes as the dose is increased; parameters that do not change, superposition, and an AUC in proportion to the dose each describe a linear drug.", "c": [[1, "linear: parameters fixed; nonlinear: parameters change"], [2, "signs: t<sub>½</sub> changes, AUC out of proportion, competing drugs, metabolite ratio changes"], [3, "<b>nonlinear: t<sub>½</sub> changes as dose ↑</b>; ✗ fixed parameters, superposition, AUC ∝ dose"]]}, {"t": "Her poll said a saturable drug often shows zero order kinetics at low concentrations and first order kinetics at high ones; her key is false, because the orders are the other way round: first order at concentrations well below K M and zero order well above K M. Low and high mean relative to K M, a concentration, never relative to V max, which is a rate. A second trap is to mix up the names: linear pharmacokinetics means first order elimination, not zero order, and a nonlinear drug is first order only at levels well below K M. The summary statement that clearance and half life are usually not constant in nonlinear processes is true. Objective two explains why the order changes with the level.", "s": "nonlin:19", "d": "Her poll said a saturable drug often shows zero order kinetics at low concentrations and first order kinetics at high ones; her key is false, because the orders are the other way round: first order at concentrations well below K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> and zero order well above K<sub>M</sub>. Low and high mean relative to K<sub>M</sub>, a concentration, never relative to V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small>, which is a rate. A second trap is to mix up the names: linear pharmacokinetics means first order elimination, not zero order, and a nonlinear drug is first order only at levels well below K<sub>M</sub>. The summary statement that clearance and half life are usually not constant in nonlinear processes is true. Objective two explains why the order changes with the level.", "c": [[0, "poll: zero order at low C, first order at high C? <b>False</b>"], [1, "low or high: relative to K<sub>M</sub> (mg/L), never V<sub>max</sub> (a rate)"], [2, "✗ linear = zero order; ✗ nonlinear = first order"], [3, "Cl and t<sub>½</sub> usually not constant: true"]], "f": {"k": "nl_rate_steps", "at": [[0, 5]]}}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m8-2": {"title": "K M, C p and the order of elimination", "segs": [{"t": "Module eight, objective two. How K M and C p decide the order of elimination. C p is the plasma concentration, the drug level, and the rate of a saturable elimination depends on C p and on two constants, V max and K M. V max, the maximum elimination rate, is a rate: an amount or concentration per unit of time. K M, the Michaelis constant, reflects the capacity of the enzyme system; it is a concentration, the level at which the elimination rate is half of V max. Both are constants for one drug in one patient and differ between patients.", "s": "nonlin:7", "d": "Module eight, objective two. How K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> and C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> decide the order of elimination. C<sub>p</sub> is the plasma concentration, the drug level, and the rate of a saturable elimination depends on C<sub>p</sub> and on two constants, V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> and K<sub>M</sub>. V<sub>max</sub>, the maximum elimination rate, is a rate: an amount or concentration per unit of time. K<sub>M</sub>, the Michaelis constant, reflects the capacity of the enzyme system; it is a concentration, the level at which the elimination rate is half of V<sub>max</sub>. Both are constants for one drug in one patient and differ between patients.", "c": [[2, "C<sub>p</sub>: the plasma concentration, mg/L"], [3, "V<sub>max</sub>: maximum elimination rate, a rate (mg/day, mg/kg/day)"], [4, "K<sub>M</sub>: Michaelis constant, a concentration (mg/L); at C<sub>p</sub> = K<sub>M</sub>, rate = ½ V<sub>max</sub>"], [5, "constants in one patient; differ between patients"]], "f": {"k": "nl_rate_steps", "at": [[2, 1], [3, 2], [4, 3]]}}, {"t": "On a graph of elimination rate against C p, K M is marked on the concentration axis and V max on the rate axis. So C p, a concentration in milligrams per liter, is compared with K M, also in milligrams per liter, never with V max. She said, We can compare because they've got the same units. V max is a rate, so a level cannot be low or high relative to V max, even though the units of V max on her slide, concentration per time, contain the word concentration.", "s": "nonlin:8", "d": "On a graph of elimination rate against C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small>, K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> is marked on the concentration axis and V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> on the rate axis. So C<sub>p</sub>, a concentration in milligrams per liter, is compared with K<sub>M</sub>, also in milligrams per liter, never with V<sub>max</sub>. She said, We can compare because they've got the same units. V<sub>max</sub> is a rate, so a level cannot be low or high relative to V<sub>max</sub>, even though the units of V<sub>max</sub> on her slide, concentration per time, contain the word concentration.", "c": [[0, "K<sub>M</sub>: on the C<sub>p</sub> axis (mg/L); V<sub>max</sub>: on the rate axis (mg/kg/day)"], [1, "<b>compare C<sub>p</sub> with K<sub>M</sub>: both mg/L</b>"], [3, "V<sub>max</sub>: concentration per time, a rate, not a level"]], "f": {"k": "nl_rate_steps", "at": [[0, 3]]}}, {"t": "The elimination rate is the amount of drug removed per unit of time, so when her quiz says the rate depends on concentration, it means this amount changes when C p changes. Well below K M, the rate is a small part of V max, so the enzymes have spare capacity, and twice the level gives about twice the rate: the rate depends on concentration, first order. Well above K M, the enzymes are saturated: more drug raises the rate only a little, so it stays near V max, independent of concentration: zero order. She said, That's as fast as it can go. In between, the rate still rises with C p, but by a smaller fraction than C p rises.", "s": "nonlin:8", "d": "The elimination rate is the amount of drug removed per unit of time, so when her quiz says the rate depends on concentration, it means this amount changes when C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> changes. Well below K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small>, the rate is a small part of V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small>, so the enzymes have spare capacity, and twice the level gives about twice the rate: the rate depends on concentration, first order. Well above K<sub>M</sub>, the enzymes are saturated: more drug raises the rate only a little, so it stays near V<sub>max</sub>, independent of concentration: zero order. She said, That's as fast as it can go. In between, the rate still rises with C<sub>p</sub>, but by a smaller fraction than C<sub>p</sub> rises.", "c": [[0, "rate = amount removed per time"], [1, "C<sub>p</sub> ≪ K<sub>M</sub>: rate ∝ C<sub>p</sub>, depends on C<sub>p</sub> → first order"], [1, "e.g. (K<sub>M</sub> 10, V<sub>max</sub> 12) C<sub>p</sub> 0.5 → 1 mg/L: rate 0.57 → 1.09 mg/kg/day (× 1.9)"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> ≫ K<sub>M</sub>: rate ≈ V<sub>max</sub>, independent of C<sub>p</sub> → zero order"], [2, "e.g. C<sub>p</sub> 100 → 200 mg/L (beyond the graph): rate 10.9 → 11.4 mg/kg/day (× 1.05)"], [4, "e.g. her Solutions 2 (K<sub>M</sub> 9 mg/L, V<sub>max</sub> 500 mg/day): C<sub>p</sub> 6 → 21 mg/L (× 3.5), rate 200 → 350 mg/day (× 1.75)"]], "f": {"k": "nl_rate_steps", "at": [[1, 4], [2, 5]]}}, {"t": "The elimination rate, the fall in C p with time, equals V max times C p, divided by K M plus C p. C p on the top raises the rate, and C p on the bottom, added to K M, limits its rise; the two can be added because both are concentrations. When C p is much smaller than K M, the bottom stays about K M, so the rate is about k prime times C p, where k prime, V max over K M, is a constant: first order. When C p is much larger than K M, the bottom is about C p, the C p on top and bottom cancel, and the rate is about V max: zero order.", "s": "nonlin:8", "d": "The elimination rate, the fall in C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> with time, equals V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> times C<sub>p</sub>, divided by K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> plus C<sub>p</sub>. C<sub>p</sub> on the top raises the rate, and C<sub>p</sub> on the bottom, added to K<sub>M</sub>, limits its rise; the two can be added because both are concentrations. When C<sub>p</sub> is much smaller than K<sub>M</sub>, the bottom stays about K<sub>M</sub>, so the rate is about k prime times C<sub>p</sub>, where k prime, V<sub>max</sub> over K<sub>M</sub>, is a constant: first order. When C<sub>p</sub> is much larger than K<sub>M</sub>, the bottom is about C<sub>p</sub>, the C<sub>p</sub> on top and bottom cancel, and the rate is about V<sub>max</sub>: zero order.", "c": [[0, "−{{frac:dC<sub>p</sub>|dt}} = {{frac:V<sub>max</sub> C<sub>p</sub>|K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>p</sub>}}"], [0, "C<sub>p</sub> = K<sub>M</sub>: rate = {{frac:V<sub>max</sub> K<sub>M</sub>|2 K<sub>M</sub>}} = ½ V<sub>max</sub>"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> ≪ K<sub>M</sub>: rate ≈ {{frac:V<sub>max</sub>|K<sub>M</sub>}} C<sub>p</sub> = k′C<sub>p</sub> → first order"], [3, "C<sub>p</sub> ≫ K<sub>M</sub>: K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>p</sub> ≈ C<sub>p</sub>, rate ≈ V<sub>max</sub> → zero order"]], "f": {"k": "nl_rate_steps", "at": [[0, 6]]}}, {"t": "Well below K M, the amount removed per day rises in proportion to C p, so the fraction removed per day stays nearly constant, and so do the half life and the clearance, as in first order. The textbook puts it this way: in first order the rate is a function of the concentration, the half life is not; in zero order the half life depends on the concentration. As C p rises toward and above K M, the amount removed per day rises by a smaller fraction than C p, so the fraction removed per day falls, the half life lengthens and the clearance falls. She said, Higher concentration, that half life starts to depend on concentration, looks like zero order.", "s": "nonlin:17", "d": "Well below K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small>, the amount removed per day rises in proportion to C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small>, so the fraction removed per day stays nearly constant, and so do the half life and the clearance, as in first order. The textbook puts it this way: in first order the rate is a function of the concentration, the half life is not; in zero order the half life depends on the concentration. As C<sub>p</sub> rises toward and above K<sub>M</sub>, the amount removed per day rises by a smaller fraction than C<sub>p</sub>, so the fraction removed per day falls, the half life lengthens and the clearance falls. She said, Higher concentration, that half life starts to depend on concentration, looks like zero order.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> ≪ K<sub>M</sub>: amount per time follows C<sub>p</sub>; fraction per time, t<sub>½</sub>, Cl nearly constant"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> ↑ toward K<sub>M</sub> and above: fraction per time ↓ → t<sub>½</sub> ↑, Cl<sub>T</sub> ↓"]], "f": {"k": "nl_frac_steps", "at": [[0, 1], [2, 2]]}}, {"t": "The half life equals V D, the volume of distribution, times zero point six nine three over V max, times K M plus C p. C p is on the top, added to K M, so a higher level gives a longer half life. The total body clearance, Cl T, equals V max divided by K M plus C p. C p is on the bottom, so the clearance falls as the level rises. V max is on the top of the clearance line and on the bottom of the half life line, so with the same K M, a higher V max gives a higher clearance and a shorter half life, and the drug is removed faster.", "s": "nonlin:18", "d": "The half life equals V<sub>D</sub>, the volume of distribution, times zero point six nine three over V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small>, times K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> plus C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small>. C<sub>p</sub> is on the top, added to K<sub>M</sub>, so a higher level gives a longer half life. The total body clearance, Cl<sub>T</sub>, equals V<sub>max</sub> divided by K<sub>M</sub> plus C<sub>p</sub>. C<sub>p</sub> is on the bottom, so the clearance falls as the level rises. V<sub>max</sub> is on the top of the clearance line and on the bottom of the half life line, so with the same K<sub>M</sub>, a higher V<sub>max</sub> gives a higher clearance and a shorter half life, and the drug is removed faster.", "c": [[0, "t<sub>½</sub> = V<sub>D</sub>({{frac:0.693|V<sub>max</sub>}})(K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>p</sub>)"], [1, "C<sub>p</sub> ↑ → t<sub>½</sub> ↑"], [2, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = {{frac:V<sub>max</sub>|K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>p</sub>}}"], [3, "C<sub>p</sub> ↑ → Cl<sub>T</sub> ↓"], [4, "V<sub>max</sub> ↑ (same K<sub>M</sub>): Cl<sub>T</sub> ↑, t<sub>½</sub> ↓; first order: t<sub>½</sub> = {{frac:0.693|k}}, Cl = kV<sub>D</sub>, no C<sub>p</sub>"]], "f": {"k": "nl_frac_steps", "at": [[1, 4]]}}, {"t": "Her Solutions two asks the half life and the total body clearance at fifteen and at twenty one milligrams per liter. The half life line and the clearance line answer it, because each takes a stated plasma level. The patient weighs seventy five kilograms, with a V D of zero point seven five liters per kilogram, which she converts first to fifty six point two five liters, a K M of nine milligrams per liter and a V max of five hundred milligrams per day. At fifteen milligrams per liter, the half life is one point eight seven days and the clearance twenty point eight liters per day.", "s": "nonlin:17", "d": "Her Solutions two asks the half life and the total body clearance at fifteen and at twenty one milligrams per liter. The half life line and the clearance line answer it, because each takes a stated plasma level. The patient weighs seventy five kilograms, with a V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> of zero point seven five liters per kilogram, which she converts first to fifty six point two five liters, a K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> of nine milligrams per liter and a V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> of five hundred milligrams per day. At fifteen milligrams per liter, the half life is one point eight seven days and the clearance twenty point eight liters per day.", "c": [[2, "75 kg; V<sub>D</sub> 0.75 L/kg, K<sub>M</sub> 9 mg/L, V<sub>max</sub> 500 mg/day"], [2, "V<sub>D</sub> = 0.75 L/kg × 75 kg = 56.25 L"], [3, "t<sub>½</sub> = (56.25 L)({{frac:0.693|500 mg/day}})(9 + 15 mg/L) = <b>1.87 days</b>"], [3, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = {{frac:500 mg/day|(9 + 15) mg/L}} = <b>20.8 L/day</b>"]]}, {"t": "For the same Solutions two patient at twenty one milligrams per liter, the half life is two point three four days and the clearance sixteen point seven liters per day. So from fifteen to twenty one milligrams per liter the half life rose from one point eight seven days and the clearance fell from twenty point eight liters per day, which answers her written question three: at higher concentrations the half life increases and the clearance decreases. The elimination rate still rose, from three hundred twelve point five to three hundred fifty milligrams per day, twelve percent, while the level rose forty percent, so the clearance, the rate divided by the level, fell. Milligrams per day divided by milligrams per liter leaves liters per day.", "s": "nonlin:18", "c": [[0, "t<sub>½</sub> = (56.25)({{frac:0.693|500}})(9 + 21) = <b>2.34 days</b>"], [0, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = {{frac:500|9 + 21}} = <b>16.7 L/day</b>"], [1, "C<sub>p</sub> 15 → 21 mg/L: t<sub>½</sub> 1.87 → 2.34 days ↑, Cl<sub>T</sub> 20.8 → 16.7 L/day ↓"], [2, "rate {{frac:500 × 15|24}} = 312.5 → {{frac:500 × 21|30}} = 350 mg/day (× 1.12); C<sub>p</sub> × 1.4"], [2, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = {{frac:rate|C<sub>p</sub>}}: {{frac:312.5|15}} = 20.8, {{frac:350|21}} = 16.7 L/day ✓"], [3, "{{frac:mg/day|mg/L}} = L/day; L × {{frac:1|mg/day}} × mg/L = day"]]}, {"t": "Her quiz question five asked which statement is true for a nonlinear drug; each option paired low concentrations, relative to V max or to K M, with an elimination rate that depends on the concentration or is independent of it. The key: at low concentrations relative to K M, the elimination rate depends on the concentration. Any option relative to V max is wrong, because V max is a rate. In the key, depends on the concentration describes the elimination rate, the amount removed per unit of time, not the half life, which hardly changes with C p well below K M. Her poll statement, zero order at low concentrations and first order at high ones, is false.", "s": "nonlin:8", "d": "Her quiz question five asked which statement is true for a nonlinear drug; each option paired low concentrations, relative to V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> or to K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small>, with an elimination rate that depends on the concentration or is independent of it. The key: at low concentrations relative to K<sub>M</sub>, the elimination rate depends on the concentration. Any option relative to V<sub>max</sub> is wrong, because V<sub>max</sub> is a rate. In the key, depends on the concentration describes the elimination rate, the amount removed per unit of time, not the half life, which hardly changes with C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small> well below K<sub>M</sub>. Her poll statement, zero order at low concentrations and first order at high ones, is false.", "c": [[1, "<b>C<sub>p</sub> low relative to K<sub>M</sub>: rate depends on C<sub>p</sub></b>"], [2, "✗ relative to V<sub>max</sub>: a rate, not a level"], [3, "depends on C<sub>p</sub>: the rate (amount per time); t<sub>½</sub> nearly constant well below K<sub>M</sub>"], [4, "poll: zero order at low C, first order at high C? <b>False</b>"]], "f": {"k": "nl_rate_steps", "at": [[1, 4], [4, 5]]}}, {"t": "Definition items ask which term is the maximum elimination rate, V max, and which is the constant in concentration units that reflects enzyme capacity, K M; K M is not a rate. Her sheets ask the half life and the clearance at a stated level: fifteen milligrams per liter, or each dose's own steady state level. The unit checks are V D converted to liters first, the half life in days and the clearance in liters per day.", "s": "nonlin:7", "d": "Definition items ask which term is the maximum elimination rate, V<sub>max</sub>, and which is the constant in concentration units that reflects enzyme capacity, K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small>; K<sub>M</sub> is not a rate. Her sheets ask the half life and the clearance at a stated level: fifteen milligrams per liter, or each dose's own steady state level. The unit checks are V<sub>D</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(volume of distribution)</small> converted to liters first, the half life in days and the clearance in liters per day.", "c": [[0, "V<sub>max</sub>: rate; K<sub>M</sub>: concentration; ✗ K<sub>M</sub> as a rate"], [1, "t<sub>½</sub>, Cl<sub>T</sub> at a stated C<sub>p</sub> (15 mg/L, or each dose's C<sub>ss</sub>)"], [2, "V<sub>D</sub> in L; t<sub>½</sub> in days; Cl<sub>T</sub> in L/day"]]}], "mins": 7}, "gobj-m8-3": {"title": "Finding V max and K M in a patient", "segs": [{"t": "Module eight, objective three. Finding V max and K M in a patient. C p is the plasma concentration, the drug level. V max, the fastest rate at which the enzymes can eliminate the drug, is a rate: an amount or concentration per unit of time. K M is the drug level at which the elimination rate is half of V max. Since C p and K M are both concentrations, a level is low or high only when compared with K M. She said, We can compare because they've got the same units. V max and K M are constant in one patient but differ between patients. Her slide example gives K M ten milligrams per liter and V max twelve milligrams per kilogram per day.", "s": "nonlin:7", "d": "Module eight, objective three. Finding V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> and K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> in a patient. C<sub>p</sub> is the plasma concentration, the drug level. V<sub>max</sub>, the fastest rate at which the enzymes can eliminate the drug, is a rate: an amount or concentration per unit of time. K<sub>M</sub> is the drug level at which the elimination rate is half of V<sub>max</sub>. Since C<sub>p</sub> and K<sub>M</sub> are both concentrations, a level is low or high only when compared with K<sub>M</sub>. She said, We can compare because they've got the same units. V<sub>max</sub> and K<sub>M</sub> are constant in one patient but differ between patients. Her slide example gives K<sub>M</sub> ten milligrams per liter and V<sub>max</sub> twelve milligrams per kilogram per day.", "c": [[2, "C<sub>p</sub>: the plasma concentration, mg/L"], [3, "V<sub>max</sub>: the fastest elimination rate, a rate, e.g. mg/kg/day"], [4, "K<sub>M</sub>: the concentration at which rate = ½ V<sub>max</sub>, mg/L"], [4, "K<sub>M</sub> on the C<sub>p</sub> axis; V<sub>max</sub> on the rate axis"], [5, "compare C<sub>p</sub> with K<sub>M</sub> (both mg/L)"], [7, "constants in one patient, different between patients"], [8, "her example: K<sub>M</sub> 10 mg/L, V<sub>max</sub> 12 mg/kg/day"]], "f": {"k": "nl_rate_steps", "at": [[2, 1], [3, 2], [4, 3]]}}, {"t": "Why does the order of elimination change with the level? The elimination rate is the amount removed per unit of time; it depends on concentration when this amount changes with the level, even if the fraction removed does not. Well below K M, the rate is a small part of V max, so the enzymes have spare capacity, and twice the level gives about twice the rate: first order. Well above K M, the enzymes are saturated: more drug raises the rate only a little, so it stays near V max: zero order. Low and high always mean relative to K M, a concentration, never to V max, a rate. The half life is nearly constant well below K M and lengthens as the level rises.", "s": "nonlin:8", "d": "Why does the order of elimination change with the level? The elimination rate is the amount removed per unit of time; it depends on concentration when this amount changes with the level, even if the fraction removed does not. Well below K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small>, the rate is a small part of V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small>, so the enzymes have spare capacity, and twice the level gives about twice the rate: first order. Well above K<sub>M</sub>, the enzymes are saturated: more drug raises the rate only a little, so it stays near V<sub>max</sub>: zero order. Low and high always mean relative to K<sub>M</sub>, a concentration, never to V<sub>max</sub>, a rate. The half life is nearly constant well below K<sub>M</sub> and lengthens as the level rises.", "c": [[1, "rate = amount removed per time; depends on concentration = this amount changes with C<sub>p</sub>"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> ≪ K<sub>M</sub>: twice C<sub>p</sub>, about twice the rate → first order"], [2, "e.g. (K<sub>M</sub> 10, V<sub>max</sub> 12) C<sub>p</sub> 0.5 → 1 mg/L: rate 0.57 → 1.09 mg/kg/day (× 1.9); 0.57 is about 5% of V<sub>max</sub>"], [3, "C<sub>p</sub> ≫ K<sub>M</sub>: saturated, rate ≈ V<sub>max</sub> → zero order"], [3, "e.g. C<sub>p</sub> 100 → 200 mg/L (beyond the graph): rate 10.9 → 11.4 mg/kg/day (× 1.05)"], [4, "<b>low or high: compared with K<sub>M</sub> (mg/L). V<sub>max</sub> is a rate (mg/kg/day), never compared with a level.</b>"], [5, "C<sub>p</sub> ≪ K<sub>M</sub>: the amount removed per time follows C<sub>p</sub>; the fraction and t<sub>½</sub> stay nearly constant. t<sub>½</sub> lengthens as C<sub>p</sub> rises."]], "f": {"k": "nl_rate_steps", "at": [[2, 4], [3, 5]]}}, {"t": "The elimination rate equals V max times C p, divided by K M plus C p. C p on the top raises the rate, and C p on the bottom, added to K M, limits how far it can rise. When C p is much smaller than K M, adding it hardly changes the bottom, which stays about K M, so the rate is about V max over K M times C p: a constant times the level, which is why twice the level gave about twice the rate. When C p is much larger than K M, the bottom is about C p, the C p on the top and the bottom cancel, and the rate is about V max whatever the level: zero order.", "s": "nonlin:8", "d": "The elimination rate equals V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> times C<sub>p</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(plasma concentration)</small>, divided by K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> plus C<sub>p</sub>. C<sub>p</sub> on the top raises the rate, and C<sub>p</sub> on the bottom, added to K<sub>M</sub>, limits how far it can rise. When C<sub>p</sub> is much smaller than K<sub>M</sub>, adding it hardly changes the bottom, which stays about K<sub>M</sub>, so the rate is about V<sub>max</sub> over K<sub>M</sub> times C<sub>p</sub>: a constant times the level, which is why twice the level gave about twice the rate. When C<sub>p</sub> is much larger than K<sub>M</sub>, the bottom is about C<sub>p</sub>, the C<sub>p</sub> on the top and the bottom cancel, and the rate is about V<sub>max</sub> whatever the level: zero order.", "c": [[0, "rate = {{frac:V<sub>max</sub> C<sub>p</sub>|K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>p</sub>}}"], [0, "C<sub>p</sub> = K<sub>M</sub>: rate = {{frac:V<sub>max</sub> K<sub>M</sub>|2 K<sub>M</sub>}} = ½ V<sub>max</sub>"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> ≪ K<sub>M</sub>: K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>p</sub> ≈ K<sub>M</sub> (e.g. 10 + 0.5 = 10.5 ≈ 10)"], [2, "rate ≈ {{frac:V<sub>max</sub>|K<sub>M</sub>}} C<sub>p</sub>: a constant × C<sub>p</sub>, like first order"], [3, "C<sub>p</sub> ≫ K<sub>M</sub>: K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>p</sub> ≈ C<sub>p</sub>, so rate ≈ V<sub>max</sub>, a constant, like zero order"]], "f": {"k": "nl_rate_steps", "at": [[0, 6]]}}, {"t": "At steady state the drug going in each day equals the drug eliminated each day, so the daily dose rate R equals the elimination rate, and the level is C s s, the steady state concentration. This gives the dose rate equation: R equals V max times C s s, over K M plus C s s. Well below K M, C s s rises nearly in proportion to R. As R nears V max, the enzymes are close to saturated and the elimination rate rises only a little as the level rises, so a small rise in dose needs a large rise in C s s. For a drug like this, steady state takes two to three weeks, not three to five half lives.", "s": "nonlin:11", "d": "At steady state the drug going in each day equals the drug eliminated each day, so the daily dose rate R equals the elimination rate, and the level is C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, the steady state concentration. This gives the dose rate equation: R equals V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> times C<sub>ss</sub>, over K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> plus C<sub>ss</sub>. Well below K<sub>M</sub>, C<sub>ss</sub> rises nearly in proportion to R. As R nears V<sub>max</sub>, the enzymes are close to saturated and the elimination rate rises only a little as the level rises, so a small rise in dose needs a large rise in C<sub>ss</sub>. For a drug like this, steady state takes two to three weeks, not three to five half lives.", "c": [[0, "steady state: rate in = rate out, so R = elimination rate"], [1, "R = {{frac:V<sub>max</sub> C<sub>ss</sub>|K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>ss</sub>}}"], [1, "turned around: C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:R K<sub>M</sub>|V<sub>max</sub> − R}}"], [3, "R near V<sub>max</sub>: a small rise in R, a large rise in C<sub>ss</sub>"], [4, "steady state: 2 to 3 weeks"]], "f": {"k": "nl_css_steps", "at": [[2, 1]]}}, {"t": "Her slide example, a seventy two kilogram patient, shows what saturation does to the steady state level. A pair is one daily dose rate and the steady state level it produced. On six milligrams per kilogram per day, the steady state level was ten milligrams per liter. Raising the dose rate to eight milligrams per kilogram per day is one third more, so for a linear drug the level would also rise by one third, to about thirteen milligrams per liter. Instead it rose to twenty milligrams per liter, double, because the enzymes were close to saturated: to remove eight instead of six milligrams per kilogram each day, the level had to climb much higher.", "s": "nonlin:14", "c": [[2, "pair 1: R<sub>1</sub> = 6 mg/kg/day → C<sub>1</sub> = 10 mg/L"], [3, "linear: {{frac:10 × 8|6}} = 13.3 mg/L"], [4, "pair 2: R<sub>2</sub> = 8 mg/kg/day → C<sub>2</sub> = 20 mg/L"]], "f": {"k": "nl_css_steps", "at": [[0, 1], [2, 2], [3, 3], [4, 4]]}}, {"t": "One pair cannot give V max and K M, because the dose rate equation has two unknowns, V max and K M. Two pairs from the same patient share one V max and one K M. Solving each pair's equation for V max and setting the two equal removes V max and leaves K M, which is why K M comes first. This is the direct method. With R one and C one the first pair's dose rate and steady state level, and R two and C two the second pair's, K M equals the difference R two minus R one, divided by the difference between R one over C one and R two over C two.", "s": "nonlin:13", "d": "One pair cannot give V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> and K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small>, because the dose rate equation has two unknowns, V<sub>max</sub> and K<sub>M</sub>. Two pairs from the same patient share one V<sub>max</sub> and one K<sub>M</sub>. Solving each pair's equation for V<sub>max</sub> and setting the two equal removes V<sub>max</sub> and leaves K<sub>M</sub>, which is why K<sub>M</sub> comes first. This is the direct method. With R one and C one the first pair's dose rate and steady state level, and R two and C two the second pair's, K<sub>M</sub> equals the difference R two minus R one, divided by the difference between R one over C one and R two over C two.", "c": [[1, "two pairs, one patient: one V<sub>max</sub>, one K<sub>M</sub>"], [2, "V<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:R<sub>1</sub>|C<sub>1</sub>}}K<sub>M</sub> + R<sub>1</sub> = {{frac:R<sub>2</sub>|C<sub>2</sub>}}K<sub>M</sub> + R<sub>2</sub>"], [4, "K<sub>M</sub> = {{frac:R<sub>2</sub> − R<sub>1</sub>|(R<sub>1</sub>/C<sub>1</sub>) − (R<sub>2</sub>/C<sub>2</sub>)}}"]], "f": {"k": "nl_css_steps", "at": [[0, 4]]}}, {"t": "In the K M equation, pair one is the lower dose rate. Each ratio on the bottom, a dose rate over its own level, is the patient's clearance per kilogram at that level. Clearance falls as the level rises, so the lower dose has the larger ratio. The top takes the higher dose rate minus the lower, and the bottom the lower dose's ratio minus the higher dose's, so both differences are positive and so is K M. Dose rate units on the top, divided by dose rate units per milligram per liter on the bottom, leave milligrams per liter. Her ratios are six over ten, zero point six, and eight over twenty, zero point four, so K M is two over zero point two, ten milligrams per liter.", "s": "nonlin:13", "d": "In the K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> equation, pair one is the lower dose rate. Each ratio on the bottom, a dose rate over its own level, is the patient's clearance per kilogram at that level. Clearance falls as the level rises, so the lower dose has the larger ratio. The top takes the higher dose rate minus the lower, and the bottom the lower dose's ratio minus the higher dose's, so both differences are positive and so is K<sub>M</sub>. Dose rate units on the top, divided by dose rate units per milligram per liter on the bottom, leave milligrams per liter. Her ratios are six over ten, zero point six, and eight over twenty, zero point four, so K<sub>M</sub> is two over zero point two, ten milligrams per liter.", "c": [[1, "{{frac:R|C<sub>ss</sub>}} = {{frac:V<sub>max</sub>|K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>ss</sub>}} = Cl<sub>T</sub>, clearance (per kg here)"], [2, "{{frac:6|10}} = 0.6 &gt; {{frac:8|20}} = 0.4"], [3, "top 8 − 6 &gt; 0, bottom 0.6 − 0.4 &gt; 0"], [4, "{{frac:mg/kg/day|(mg/kg/day) per (mg/L)}} = mg/L"], [5, "<b>K<sub>M</sub> = {{frac:8 − 6|0.6 − 0.4}} = {{frac:2|0.2}} = 10 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "With K M known, V max comes from the dose rate equation rearranged: V max equals R times the sum K M plus C s s, all divided by C s s, with one pair's R and that same pair's level. From the second pair, eight times thirty, over twenty, is twelve milligrams per kilogram per day. The first pair, six times twenty over ten, also gives twelve. V max takes the units of the dose rate, so here it is per kilogram. She said, I wrote this twice because you should get the same answer regardless of which way you use it.", "s": "nonlin:15", "d": "With K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> known, V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> comes from the dose rate equation rearranged: V<sub>max</sub> equals R times the sum K<sub>M</sub> plus C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, all divided by C<sub>ss</sub>, with one pair's R and that same pair's level. From the second pair, eight times thirty, over twenty, is twelve milligrams per kilogram per day. The first pair, six times twenty over ten, also gives twelve. V<sub>max</sub> takes the units of the dose rate, so here it is per kilogram. She said, I wrote this twice because you should get the same answer regardless of which way you use it.", "c": [[0, "V<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:R (K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>ss</sub>)|C<sub>ss</sub>}}"], [1, "<b>pair 2: {{frac:(8)(10 + 20)|20}} = {{frac:240|20}} = 12 mg/kg/day</b>"], [2, "pair 1: {{frac:(6)(10 + 10)|10}} = 12 ✓"], [3, "V<sub>max</sub> in the units of R: mg/kg/day"]]}, {"t": "Putting K M, ten milligrams per liter, and V max, twelve milligrams per kilogram per day, back into the dose rate equation shows why a dose change matters so much. At a steady state level of ten milligrams per liter, the level equals K M, so the elimination rate is half of V max, six milligrams per kilogram per day, the first dose rate she gave. At twenty milligrams per liter, the elimination rate is eight milligrams per kilogram per day, two thirds of V max. So the closer the daily dose comes to V max, the larger the rise in level for each added milligram per kilogram per day, and objective four uses K M and V max, not a simple proportion, to choose a new dose.", "s": "nonlin:15", "d": "Putting K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small>, ten milligrams per liter, and V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small>, twelve milligrams per kilogram per day, back into the dose rate equation shows why a dose change matters so much. At a steady state level of ten milligrams per liter, the level equals K<sub>M</sub>, so the elimination rate is half of V<sub>max</sub>, six milligrams per kilogram per day, the first dose rate she gave. At twenty milligrams per liter, the elimination rate is eight milligrams per kilogram per day, two thirds of V<sub>max</sub>. So the closer the daily dose comes to V<sub>max</sub>, the larger the rise in level for each added milligram per kilogram per day, and objective four uses K<sub>M</sub> and V<sub>max</sub>, not a simple proportion, to choose a new dose.", "c": [[1, "C<sub>ss</sub> = 10 mg/L = K<sub>M</sub>: R = {{frac:12 × 10|10 + 10}} = 6 = ½ V<sub>max</sub>"], [2, "C<sub>ss</sub> = 20 mg/L: R = {{frac:12 × 20|10 + 20}} = 8 = ⅔ V<sub>max</sub>"]], "f": {"k": "nl_rate_steps", "at": [[0, 6], [2, 7]]}}, {"t": "The usual errors in this objective come from the K M line and the V max line. K M is a concentration, so a rate unit on K M is wrong. A level called low relative to V max is wrong in any option, because V max is a rate, read on the rate axis, and a level is compared only with K M, on the concentration axis. Subtracting the ratios in the wrong order makes K M negative. In the V max line, one pair's dose rate goes with that same pair's level, never with the other pair's level. The seventy two kilograms are used only when a dose for this patient is asked, in objective four.", "s": "nonlin:15", "d": "The usual errors in this objective come from the K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> line and the V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> line. K<sub>M</sub> is a concentration, so a rate unit on K<sub>M</sub> is wrong. A level called low relative to V<sub>max</sub> is wrong in any option, because V<sub>max</sub> is a rate, read on the rate axis, and a level is compared only with K<sub>M</sub>, on the concentration axis. Subtracting the ratios in the wrong order makes K<sub>M</sub> negative. In the V<sub>max</sub> line, one pair's dose rate goes with that same pair's level, never with the other pair's level. The seventy two kilograms are used only when a dose for this patient is asked, in objective four.", "c": [[1, "K<sub>M</sub> in mg/L, never a rate unit"], [2, "✗ low relative to V<sub>max</sub>: a rate, not a level"], [3, "(R<sub>1</sub>/C<sub>1</sub>) − (R<sub>2</sub>/C<sub>2</sub>), in that order"], [4, "V<sub>max</sub>: R and C<sub>ss</sub> from the same pair"]]}], "mins": 8}, "gobj-m8-4": {"title": "Dosing a nonlinear drug such as phenytoin", "segs": [{"t": "Module eight, objective four. Estimating the dose of a nonlinear drug such as phenytoin. The dose here is a dose rate, R, an amount per unit of time such as milligrams per day. C s s is the steady state concentration, the level once dosing and elimination balance. The patient's V max and K M are known, given or found as in objective three. Phenytoin is dosed with care because it is nonlinear and has a narrow therapeutic window, the range between the minimum effective and the minimum toxic level. She said, The range for phenytoin, I think, is ten to twenty milligrams per liter.", "s": "nonlin:11", "d": "Module eight, objective four. Estimating the dose of a nonlinear drug such as phenytoin. The dose here is a dose rate, R, an amount per unit of time such as milligrams per day. C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> is the steady state concentration, the level once dosing and elimination balance. The patient's V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> and K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> are known, given or found as in objective three. Phenytoin is dosed with care because it is nonlinear and has a narrow therapeutic window, the range between the minimum effective and the minimum toxic level. She said, The range for phenytoin, I think, is ten to twenty milligrams per liter.", "c": [[2, "R: dose rate, amount per time (mg/day, mg/kg/day)"], [3, "C<sub>ss</sub>: steady-state level, mg/L"], [4, "V<sub>max</sub>, K<sub>M</sub>: given, or from objective 3"], [6, "phenytoin window: about 10 to 20 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "At steady state the drug going in each day equals the drug eliminated each day, so R equals the elimination rate at that level. The elimination rate rises with the level but never past V max, so a higher target level needs a higher R, and R must stay below V max. At a daily dose at or above V max, drug goes in faster than it is removed at every level, so the level keeps rising and no steady state is reached. Close to V max, the elimination rate rises only a little as the level rises, so a small rise in R needs a large rise in C s s. Steady state takes two to three weeks for such a drug, not three to five half lives.", "s": "nonlin:11", "d": "At steady state the drug going in each day equals the drug eliminated each day, so R equals the elimination rate at that level. The elimination rate rises with the level but never past V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small>, so a higher target level needs a higher R, and R must stay below V<sub>max</sub>. At a daily dose at or above V<sub>max</sub>, drug goes in faster than it is removed at every level, so the level keeps rising and no steady state is reached. Close to V<sub>max</sub>, the elimination rate rises only a little as the level rises, so a small rise in R needs a large rise in C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>. Steady state takes two to three weeks for such a drug, not three to five half lives.", "c": [[0, "steady state: rate in = rate out, so R = elimination rate"], [1, "C<sub>ss</sub> ↑ → R ↑, always R &lt; V<sub>max</sub>"], [2, "R ≥ V<sub>max</sub>: no steady state"], [3, "R near V<sub>max</sub>: small R ↑ → large C<sub>ss</sub> ↑"], [4, "steady state: 2 to 3 weeks"]], "f": {"k": "nl_css_steps", "at": [[1, 1]]}}, {"t": "In symbols, the dose rate equation is R equals V max times C s s, divided by K M plus C s s. C s s on the top raises R, and C s s on the bottom, added to K M, keeps R below V max however high the target. The milligrams per liter on the top and the bottom cancel, so R comes out in the units of V max. When V max is per kilogram, R comes out per kilogram, and multiplying by the patient's weight gives the patient's daily dose.", "s": "nonlin:11", "d": "In symbols, the dose rate equation is R equals V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> times C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>, divided by K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> plus C<sub>ss</sub>. C<sub>ss</sub> on the top raises R, and C<sub>ss</sub> on the bottom, added to K<sub>M</sub>, keeps R below V<sub>max</sub> however high the target. The milligrams per liter on the top and the bottom cancel, so R comes out in the units of V<sub>max</sub>. When V<sub>max</sub> is per kilogram, R comes out per kilogram, and multiplying by the patient's weight gives the patient's daily dose.", "c": [[0, "R = {{frac:V<sub>max</sub> C<sub>ss</sub>|K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>ss</sub>}}"], [1, "C<sub>ss</sub> ↑ → R ↑ toward V<sub>max</sub>"], [2, "{{frac:(mg/day)(mg/L)|mg/L}} = mg/day: R in the units of V<sub>max</sub>"], [3, "V<sub>max</sub> per kg → R per kg → × weight"]], "f": {"k": "nl_rate_steps", "at": [[0, 1], [1, 2]]}}, {"t": "Turned around, the dose rate equation gives the level a chosen dose rate will produce: C s s equals R times K M, divided by V max minus R. She worked this on the board from the R equation; it is not on the equation sheet. R is on the top and is also subtracted from V max on the bottom, so as R approaches V max the bottom shrinks and C s s climbs steeply. In her slide example, with K M ten milligrams per liter and V max twelve milligrams per kilogram per day, six milligrams per kilogram per day gives ten milligrams per liter, and eight gives twenty milligrams per liter, not the thirteen milligrams per liter a linear drug would give.", "s": "nonlin:11", "d": "Turned around, the dose rate equation gives the level a chosen dose rate will produce: C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> equals R times K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small>, divided by V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> minus R. She worked this on the board from the R equation; it is not on the equation sheet. R is on the top and is also subtracted from V<sub>max</sub> on the bottom, so as R approaches V<sub>max</sub> the bottom shrinks and C<sub>ss</sub> climbs steeply. In her slide example, with K<sub>M</sub> ten milligrams per liter and V<sub>max</sub> twelve milligrams per kilogram per day, six milligrams per kilogram per day gives ten milligrams per liter, and eight gives twenty milligrams per liter, not the thirteen milligrams per liter a linear drug would give.", "c": [[0, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:R K<sub>M</sub>|V<sub>max</sub> − R}}"], [1, "R(K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>ss</sub>) = V<sub>max</sub>C<sub>ss</sub> → RK<sub>M</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub>(V<sub>max</sub> − R)"], [2, "R → V<sub>max</sub>: V<sub>max</sub> − R → 0, C<sub>ss</sub> climbs"], [3, "6 → 10 mg/L; 8 → 20 mg/L (linear: 13.3)"]], "f": {"k": "nl_css_steps", "at": [[2, 1], [3, 2], [3, 3], [3, 4]]}}, {"t": "Her first slide example asks the daily dose rate for an average level of twenty four milligrams per liter in a sixty five kilogram patient, with K M five milligrams per liter and V max eight milligrams per kilogram per day. A target level is given, so the R equation answers it. R is six point six two milligrams per kilogram per day, and four hundred thirty milligrams per day for this patient. The target is almost five times K M, so R is already most of V max, about eighty three percent of it.", "s": "nonlin:12", "d": "Her first slide example asks the daily dose rate for an average level of twenty four milligrams per liter in a sixty five kilogram patient, with K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> five milligrams per liter and V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> eight milligrams per kilogram per day. A target level is given, so the R equation answers it. R is six point six two milligrams per kilogram per day, and four hundred thirty milligrams per day for this patient. The target is almost five times K<sub>M</sub>, so R is already most of V<sub>max</sub>, about eighty three percent of it.", "c": [[0, "K<sub>M</sub> 5 mg/L, V<sub>max</sub> 8 mg/kg/day; target C<sub>ss</sub> 24 mg/L; 65 kg"], [1, "R = {{frac:(8 mg/kg/day)(24 mg/L)|(5 + 24) mg/L}} = {{frac:192|29}}"], [2, "<b>R = 6.62 mg/kg/day × 65 kg = 430 mg/day</b>"], [3, "{{frac:24|5}} = 4.8; {{frac:6.62|8}} = 0.83 of V<sub>max</sub>"]]}, {"t": "A dose rate left per kilogram is not the answer for a patient. She said, And if I'm your patient, please don't tell me six point six two milligrams per kilogram per day. Tell me how many, tell me what I need. A per kilogram V max needs the patient's weight at the end; a per day V max does not, and the weight is then used only to turn the volume of distribution from liters per kilogram into liters. An amount with no time is not a dose rate either. She said, I don't wanna just see one thousand milligrams.", "s": "nonlin:12", "d": "A dose rate left per kilogram is not the answer for a patient. She said, And if I'm your patient, please don't tell me six point six two milligrams per kilogram per day. Tell me how many, tell me what I need. A per kilogram V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> needs the patient's weight at the end; a per day V<sub>max</sub> does not, and the weight is then used only to turn the volume of distribution from liters per kilogram into liters. An amount with no time is not a dose rate either. She said, I don't wanna just see one thousand milligrams.", "c": [[0, "✗ per-kg rate left for a patient"], [3, "V<sub>max</sub> per kg: × weight at the end; per day: weight only for V<sub>D</sub>"], [5, "✗ 1000 mg with no interval"]]}, {"t": "Her second slide example continues the patient of objective three: with K M ten milligrams per liter and V max twelve milligrams per kilogram per day, she asks the dose rate for fifteen milligrams per liter in the seventy two kilogram patient. R is seven point two milligrams per kilogram per day, or five hundred eighteen point four milligrams per day, which she rounded to five hundred twenty on her slide. A check: fifteen milligrams per liter lies between her two measured levels, ten and twenty milligrams per liter, so R must lie between the dose rates that produced them, six and eight milligrams per kilogram per day, and seven point two does.", "s": "nonlin:15", "d": "Her second slide example continues the patient of objective three: with K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> ten milligrams per liter and V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> twelve milligrams per kilogram per day, she asks the dose rate for fifteen milligrams per liter in the seventy two kilogram patient. R is seven point two milligrams per kilogram per day, or five hundred eighteen point four milligrams per day, which she rounded to five hundred twenty on her slide. A check: fifteen milligrams per liter lies between her two measured levels, ten and twenty milligrams per liter, so R must lie between the dose rates that produced them, six and eight milligrams per kilogram per day, and seven point two does.", "c": [[0, "K<sub>M</sub> 10 mg/L, V<sub>max</sub> 12 mg/kg/day; target 15 mg/L; 72 kg"], [1, "R = {{frac:(12)(15)|10 + 15}} = 7.2 mg/kg/day"], [1, "<b>7.2 mg/kg/day × 72 kg = 518.4 ≈ 520 mg/day</b>"], [2, "10 &lt; 15 &lt; 20 mg/L → 6 &lt; 7.2 &lt; 8 mg/kg/day ✓"]], "f": {"k": "nl_rate_steps", "at": [[2, 7]]}}, {"t": "Her Solutions one uses the K M of five point nine one milligrams per liter and the V max of six hundred forty seven point eight three milligrams per day from objective three. First, the dose for fifteen milligrams per liter, from the R equation: four hundred sixty four point seven three milligrams per day, which she gave as four hundred sixty five. The weight is not needed, because V max is per day. Then the level on four hundred fifty milligrams daily, from the C s s equation, R times K M over V max minus R, because the dose is now fixed: thirteen point four four milligrams per liter, written thirteen point five on her sheet.", "s": "nonlin:11", "d": "Her Solutions one uses the K<sub>M</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(Michaelis constant)</small> of five point nine one milligrams per liter and the V<sub>max</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(maximum elimination rate)</small> of six hundred forty seven point eight three milligrams per day from objective three. First, the dose for fifteen milligrams per liter, from the R equation: four hundred sixty four point seven three milligrams per day, which she gave as four hundred sixty five. The weight is not needed, because V<sub>max</sub> is per day. Then the level on four hundred fifty milligrams daily, from the C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small> equation, R times K<sub>M</sub> over V<sub>max</sub> minus R, because the dose is now fixed: thirteen point four four milligrams per liter, written thirteen point five on her sheet.", "c": [[0, "K<sub>M</sub> 5.91 mg/L, V<sub>max</sub> 647.83 mg/day"], [1, "R = {{frac:(647.83 mg/day)(15 mg/L)|(5.91 + 15) mg/L}}"], [1, "<b>R = 464.73 ≈ 465 mg/day</b>"], [3, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:(450 mg/day)(5.91 mg/L)|(647.83 − 450) mg/day}} = {{frac:2659.5|197.83}}"], [3, "<b>C<sub>ss</sub> = 13.44 ≈ 13.5 mg/L</b> (13.45 with K<sub>M</sub>, V<sub>max</sub> unrounded)"]]}, {"t": "In her Solutions one, four hundred fifty milligrams per day is about ninety seven percent of four hundred sixty five milligrams per day, the dose that gives fifteen milligrams per liter. For a linear drug the level would also be about ninety seven percent of fifteen, about fourteen point five milligrams per liter, but here it is thirteen point five milligrams per liter, a tenth lower than fifteen. Near saturation, the level changes by a larger fraction than the dose, whether the dose goes up or down, because the elimination rate changes only a little when the level changes. That is why levels are measured and the dose adjusted for the individual patient.", "s": "nonlin:11", "c": [[1, "{{frac:450|465}} ≈ 0.97, but {{frac:13.5|15}} = 0.90"], [2, "near V<sub>max</sub>: C<sub>ss</sub> changes by a larger fraction than R"]], "f": {"k": "nl_css_steps", "at": [[2, 1]]}}, {"t": "How she tests it. Most sheets ask a daily dose for fifteen milligrams per liter at steady state, and some then ask the level at another dose, such as eleven point five milligrams per liter on four hundred milligrams daily in her In Class Practice. She said, Don't panic if I ask you to round to the nearest ten. Concept items ask what R means, how long steady state takes, and what a small dose increase near saturation does: a disproportionately large rise in C s s. Her sheets also ask the half life and the clearance at the target level, or at each dose's own steady state level, as in objective two.", "s": "nonlin:14", "d": "How she tests it. Most sheets ask a daily dose for fifteen milligrams per liter at steady state, and some then ask the level at another dose, such as eleven point five milligrams per liter on four hundred milligrams daily in her In Class Practice. She said, Don't panic if I ask you to round to the nearest ten. Concept items ask what R means, how long steady state takes, and what a small dose increase near saturation does: a disproportionately large rise in C<sub>ss</sub> <small class=\"gwgl\">(steady-state concentration)</small>. Her sheets also ask the half life and the clearance at the target level, or at each dose's own steady state level, as in objective two.", "c": [[1, "dose for C<sub>ss</sub> 15 mg/L → C<sub>ss</sub> at another dose (400 mg/day → 11.5 mg/L)"], [2, "round when asked: 518.4 → 520 mg/day (nearest ten)"], [3, "small dose ↑ near saturation → large C<sub>ss</sub> ↑"]]}], "mins": 7}};
